window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"signature-exemplar.html","t":"Overview – Start Here","secs":[{"s":"Overview – Start Here","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 1"]},{"s":"How to Use This Section","a":"section-resources-heading","k":["How to Use This Section","Overview – Start Here — The possible diagnoses, by day and body system, and what is ruled out.","The possible diagnoses, by day and body system, and what is ruled out.","System pagesHematologicCardiovascularRenalGI and HepaticNeuro — Compare tab: the system's diagnoses side by side, the patient picture and the course.One tab per diagnosis taught at length: a completed example of the whole assessment, from t…","Hematologic","Cardiovascular","Renal","GI and Hepatic","Neuro","Compare tab: the system's diagnoses side by side, the patient picture and the course.","One tab per diagnosis taught at length: a completed example of the whole assessment, from the HPI to the complication in another system.","Priority Problem Bank — Every diagnosis's priority problems, key cues and pathophysiology links, one table per system.","Every diagnosis's priority problems, key cues and pathophysiology links, one table per system."]},{"s":"Possible Diagnoses, Days 1 to 3","a":"possible-diagnoses","k":["Possible Diagnoses, Days 1 to 3","Day 1 — Hematologic — DICThe bleeding patient and anemia — Thrombocytopenia and HITThe immunocompromised patient","DIC","The bleeding patient and anemia","Thrombocytopenia and HIT","The immunocompromised patient","GI and Hepatic — Acute pancreatitis","Acute pancreatitis","Day 2 — Cardiovascular — Acute coronary syndromeAcute heart failure — Aortic aneurysmCardiac tamponadePericarditisValve disease and infective endocarditisAV blocks","Acute coronary syndrome","Acute heart failure","Aortic aneurysm","Cardiac tamponade","Pericarditis","Valve disease and infective endocarditis","AV blocks","Renal — Acute kidney injury","Acute kidney injury","Day 3 — GI and Hepatic — GI bleed, including varicealHepatic failure — Ischemic bowelBowel obstruction and paralytic ileusAbdominal compartment syndrome","GI bleed, including variceal","Hepatic failure","Ischemic bowel","Bowel obstruction and paralytic ileus","Abdominal compartment syndrome","Neuro — TBI with increased ICPStrokeSpinal cord injury — MeningitisSeizures","TBI with increased ICP","Stroke","Spinal cord injury","Meningitis","Seizures","Ruled out as the diagnosis given: the respiratory diagnoses, pulmonary embolism included, and aortic dissection. The respiratory diagnoses still turn up as complications."]}]},{"f":"signature-hematologic.html","t":"Hematologic","secs":[{"s":"Hematologic","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 2"]},{"s":"The Hematologic Diagnoses","a":"compare-diagnoses","k":["The Hematologic Diagnoses","DIC — Always set off by another condition: vessel injury from a disease state, tissue injury, or a foreign body in the bloodstream. Trauma, burns and sepsis are the most common triggers. Infections and inflammatory states disrupt coagulatio…","Always set off by another condition: vessel injury from a disease state, tissue injury, or a foreign body in the bloodstream.","Trauma, burns and sepsis are the most common triggers. Infections and inflammatory states disrupt coagulation.","Clots and bleeds at once: microemboli in the small vessels, and any area that can bleed will bleed.","Bleeding and oozing from insertion sites.","Organ ischemia: skin, lungs and kidneys most often. Skin signs sometimes only on the hands, the feet and the tips of the toes.","Very painful: organs and tissues are dying.","Diagnosed by the risk factors, the presentation and the coags: a really prolonged bleeding time (PT, PTT).","Fibrinogen and the platelets used up in the clots.","A TEG, to examine the coagulopathy. Never interpreted.","Bleeding: the platelets and clotting factors are used up.","Ineffective peripheral tissue perfusion: microclots clog the small vessels of the hands and feet.","Microclots lodge: an organ dies when they clog its circulation. In septic shock, patients can lose hands and feet.","Clotting factors depleted: a really prolonged bleeding time.","Hypotension from low volume. Hypoxemia from fewer RBCs. Acidosis from tissue ischemia.","Goal: preserve organ function. Treat the underlying cause, such as sepsis or trauma.","Correct the hypotension, hypoxemia and acidosis.","Platelets first, the highest priority. FFP for fibrinogen and other clotting factors.","Heparin in very low doses blocks the clotting that started it. Counterintuitive.","Treat the pain. Monitor the labs very closely.","Often all at once: thrombophlebitis, pulmonary embolism, stroke, GI bleeding, renal failure.","The bleeding patient and anemia — Blood loss, the most common: post-op, trauma, a GI bleed. Also RBC production (chronic kidney disease, radiation) and RBC destruction (autoimmune, the wrong blood transfused). On a blood thinner: heparin, w…","Blood loss, the most common: post-op, trauma, a GI bleed.","Also RBC production (chronic kidney disease, radiation) and RBC destruction (autoimmune, the wrong blood transfused).","On a blood thinner: heparin, warfarin, apixaban, enoxaparin.","Tachycardia first. Pale, cool skin and mucous membranes. Loss of urine output.","Fatigue, SOB, angina, headache, lightheadedness, syncope, tachypnea.","Timing: a sudden loss shows every symptom quickly. A slow loss may sit up and talk with a hemoglobin of 4.","H&H and CBC, trended: deceptive in an acute bleed, diluted by fluid. The full extent shows after 48 to 72 hours.","Reticulocyte count, raised within several days.","Bleeding time: PT, PTT, fibrinogen. A TEG.","Deficient fluid volume: blood lost from the vessels.","Activity intolerance: too few RBCs carry oxygen. Getting from the bed to the chair uses all their energy.","Active bleeding leads to hypovolemic shock.","A coagulopathy develops quickly when actively bleeding, even on no anticoagulation. Cold makes it worse.","Identify the source and treat the cause: clip the bleed, reverse the thinner, the OR.","Transfuse: PRBCs primarily. A TEG shows what else. Fluid resuscitation as well.","Anticoagulants off. Weigh dressings. Limit invasive procedures.","Massive transfusion protocol for a patient bleeding to death: 1:1:1:1, warmed.","Acute kidney injury (renal): selective vasoconstriction hits the kidneys hard.","Angina, then high-output cardiac failure (cardiovascular).","Transfusion reactions, and TACO (respiratory).","Thrombocytopenia and HIT — Low platelets, technically under 150,000. Usually a bone marrow issue: chemo, radiation, a bone marrow cancer. Also cardiac bypass, sepsis, cancers. HIT: platelets trending down on a heparin drip. Some antibiotics…","Low platelets, technically under 150,000.","Usually a bone marrow issue: chemo, radiation, a bone marrow cancer. Also cardiac bypass, sepsis, cancers.","HIT: platelets trending down on a heparin drip.","Some antibiotics, antidysrhythmics such as amiodarone, and NSAIDs.","Skin: petechiae, purpura, ecchymosis.","Oozing mucous membranes: the nose, mouth and lips.","Bleeding in wounds, stool, urine, the nose and pulmonary secretions.","Temperature, when chemo or the bone marrow is the cause: with a very low white count, any temp is very concerning.","Platelet count: under 30,000 is critically low.","Serotonin release assay (SRA): tells whether it is HIT. Hard to obtain.","Coags, H&H and CBC monitored.","Bleeding: too few platelets for the platelet plug.","Infection, when chemo or the bone marrow is the cause: white blood cells decreased or not working, so no normal response to an infection.","Under 30,000: a risk for a spontaneous bleed.","A medication that lowers platelets drops the count further.","Find the cause and address it. HIT: stop the heparin. Dropping platelets: usually off anticoagulation.","Thrombocytopenic precautions: toothettes, electric shaver, no aspirin, no needle sticks where avoidable, protection from falls.","Meds evaluated with pharmacy. Platelets only when very low.","A head injury and a subarachnoid hemorrhage after a fall (neurologic).","The immunocompromised patient — WBCs decreased or non-functioning, congenital or acquired. Organ transplant, bone marrow transplant, chemo, AIDS. — Usually asymptomatic until an infection. May not look very sick. Temperature: with a very lo…","WBCs decreased or non-functioning, congenital or acquired.","Organ transplant, bone marrow transplant, chemo, AIDS.","Usually asymptomatic until an infection. May not look very sick.","Temperature: with a very low white count, any temp is very concerning.","Opportunistic pneumonia: fever, chills, cough, dyspnea, fatigue.","CBC: the WBC count shows leukopenia.","CD4 counts, for a solid organ transplant or HIV and AIDS.","Infection: no normal white blood cell response.","Impaired gas exchange: an opportunistic pneumonia infects the lung parenchyma, and with no normal white cell response it can progress to respiratory distress, then respiratory failure.","Cannot mount a response to an infection.","Opportunistic infections. Infection and sepsis are the primary cause of death.","Protective isolation or neutropenic precautions: masks all the time, a HEPA filter room if needed.","No raw fruits and vegetables. Watch the temperature.","Teaching for home: wash fruits and vegetables, no raw fish or sushi, cook meat thoroughly.","Sepsis, which can set off DIC (hematologic).","The exemplars: DIC set off by sepsis from pneumonia, in the DIC tab. Anemia from blood loss after a hip replacement, in the Anemia from Blood Loss tab."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"dic-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Raymond Ellis is a 72-year-old Black man brought to the ED by his wife at 1000. For 4 days he has had a cough bringing up thick, rust-colored sputum, with fever, chills and aching muscles. Since yesterday he has been short of breath wa…","PMH: Peripheral arterial disease, 6 years, with intermittent claudication after walking about a block. Hypertension, 20 years. Hyperlipidemia, 12 years.","SH: Married, lives with his wife in a one-story house. Retired electrician. Former smoker, 40 pack-years, quit 6 years ago when his PAD was diagnosed. Two beers a week. No drug use.","FH: Father died of a stroke at 74. Mother had hypertension.","Admission VS: BP 96/58 (MAP 71), HR 114 (sinus tachycardia), RR 28, shallow, SpO2 90% on room air and 94% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 39.3 °C (102.7 °F), pain 5/10.","Home medications:","Aspirin, an antiplatelet, for peripheral arterial disease. Held since admission.","Lisinopril, for hypertension","Atorvastatin, for hyperlipidemia","Allergies: Sulfa (rash).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 72-year-old man sitting up in bed, flushed and shivering under two blankets. Restless, picking at his gown. Speaks in short phrases of 4 or 5 words, stopping to cough. Oriented to person only, not to place or time: GCS 14 (E…","Pain: 5/10 sharp pain in the right lower chest on deep breaths and coughing. A burning ache in his toes, 3/10, constant since last night.","Respiratory: RR 28, shallow, splinting his right side. Using his neck (sternocleidomastoid) muscles to breathe. Frequent cough, productive of thick, rust-colored sputum. Crackles in the right lower lobe, posterior and lateral. Breath sounds…","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and regular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 114. Neck veins flat at 45 degrees. No edema of the hands, sacrum, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial pulses 2+ and equal …","Integumentary: Skin hot, flushed and dry on the trunk. Petechiae across the chest and both forearms. A 6 cm ecchymosis at the left antecubital, where the ED drew blood. Oozing around both peripheral IV sites. Gums oozing blood along the low…","Labs: CBC: WBC 19,800 with a shift to the left, hemoglobin 12.4, hematocrit 37%, platelets 72,000 (238,000 at his checkup 2 months ago). Coags: PT 18 seconds and PTT 46 seconds, both prolonged. Fibrinogen 140, low.","Diagnostic: Chest x-ray: infiltrates in the right lower lobe."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"dic-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Bleeding — Ineffective peripheral tissue perfusion","Bleeding","Ineffective peripheral tissue perfusion","Supporting cues — Platelets 72,000, down from 238,000 two months ago: being used up. PT 18 and PTT 46, both prolonged, with fibrinogen 140, low: his clotting factors are being used up, and he takes longer to clot. Oozing around both IV site…","Platelets 72,000, down from 238,000 two months ago: being used up.","PT 18 and PTT 46, both prolonged, with fibrinogen 140, low: his clotting factors are being used up, and he takes longer to clot.","Oozing around both IV sites and from his gums: bleeding from insertion sites and mucous membranes.","Petechiae on his chest and arms, and an ecchymosis where blood was drawn: little bleeds under the skin.","The tips of 5 toes dusky purple, mottled and cool: little blood reaching them.","Capillary refill 4 seconds in the toes, against 2 in the fingers: the flow to his feet is slowest.","A new burning ache in his toes, 3/10, unlike his claudication: tissue short of blood.","Pedal pulses 1+ and feet cool to the ankles, with PAD: his feet started with less flow.","Relevance to the diagnosis — His pneumonia has become sepsis. Mediators from the bacteria and his white cells set off unregulated clotting: platelets clump, and microclots form throughout his small vessels. This exaggerated clotting uses up…","His pneumonia has become sepsis. Mediators from the bacteria and his white cells set off unregulated clotting: platelets clump, and microclots form throughout his small vessels. This exaggerated clotting uses up his platelets and clotting f…","The same microclots that use up his clotting factors lodge in his small vessels and cut off the blood flow behind them: ischemia, and then necrosis. In sepsis, clots in the peripheral tissues can cost a patient their hands and feet, and DIC…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. His platelets are falling fast and he is already oozing. At 72,000, above the 30,000 of a spontaneous bleed, with a hemoglobin of 12.4, he is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. Without perfusion, h…","2, urgent. His platelets are falling fast and he is already oozing. At 72,000, above the 30,000 of a spontaneous bleed, with a hemoglobin of 12.4, he is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. Without perfusion, his toes can die, and he could lose them. His fingertips are still pink and his MAP is 71: he is not expected to die within the hour."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"dic-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Night","By 2100 Mr. Ellis had been given 3 L of LR, but his BP fell to 82/46, a MAP of 58, and stayed there. Norepinephrine, a vasopressor, was started to raise his BP, and a central line and an arterial line were placed. At 0300, blood has soaked …","Updated VS: BP 88/50 (MAP 63) on norepinephrine, HR 128 (sinus tachycardia), RR 32, SpO2 92% on 4 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), pain 7/10. GCS 13.","Labs: CBC: hemoglobin 9.1, platelets 26,000. Coags: PT 24 seconds, PTT 62 seconds, fibrinogen 88."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"dic-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Morgan Reyes, RN in the MICU. I'm calling about Raymond Ellis in room 12, admitted yesterday at 1400 with DIC from sepsis and a right lower lobe pneumonia. I'm calling because he is now bleeding from every site and his platelets…","B — He is 72, with peripheral arterial disease, hypertension and hyperlipidemia. His home aspirin is held. He has had antibiotics since 1045 yesterday and 3 L of LR, and norepinephrine since 2100 for a MAP of 58. His central line dressing h…","A — I think his DIC is worsening: he has used up his platelets and clotting factors and is bleeding everywhere, while the clots cut off the blood to his toes and fingers.","R — I recommend platelets now, the highest priority, since they make the platelet plug that starts clotting, and FFP to replace his fibrinogen and other clotting factors. I'd like a TEG, to show which products he needs. And a repeat CBC and…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"dic-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: bleeding, from the first part. At 26,000 his platelets are under 30,000, critically low, with a risk for a spontaneous bleed, and he is already bleeding from every site. The bleeding drives his falling BP and hemoglobin, a…","Independent — Thrombocytopenic precautions: no needle sticks, with every lab drawn from the arterial line. Pressure dressings on the central and arterial line sites. Toothettes for oral care, gentle nose care, no rectal temperatures, an ele…","Dependent — Platelets, then FFP, per order. — Platelets are the highest priority: they make the platelet plug at the beginning of the clotting cascade. FFP replaces fibrinogen and the other clotting factors his clotting has used up. — Plate…","Interdisciplinary — The provider and pharmacy: treat the underlying cause and review his medications. His antibiotics checked against the sputum and blood cultures, and every drug he is given checked so that nothing lowers his platelets fur…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"dic-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — A stroke (neurologic). At 0600 on his third day, found on morning rounds.","Why it could occur — In DIC, microclots lodge in the small vessels and clog an organ's circulation, and a stroke is one of the ways DIC shows. His brain had little margin: hypertension and hyperlipidemia have built atherosclerosis in his ar…","Priority cues — His right arm drifts down and he cannot lift it off the bed, his right leg is weak, and the right side of his face droops: weakness on one side. His speech is slurred, and he cannot find his words, though he follows commands…","Actions — Independent: the provider called at once, since time is brain cells. When he was last seen normal, 0400, reported, since the symptoms' onset matters above all. The NIHSS, GCS and level of consciousness checked and trended every ho…","Evaluation — NIHSS: holding at 9 or trending down, which means his brain function is improving. A rising score means the stroke is extending."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"anemia-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Patricia Hale is a 68-year-old white woman, 70 kg, who had a right total hip replacement for osteoarthritis yesterday at 0800. She went to the orthopedic floor at 1300 with a drain in her hip and a Foley catheter. Her hemoglobin was 10…","PMH: Chronic kidney disease, stage 3, 7 years, with anemia from it. Hypertension, 15 years. Osteoarthritis of the right hip, 10 years.","SH: Married, lives with her husband in a two-story house. Retired school librarian. Never smoked. A glass of wine on weekends. No drug use. At home she gets winded walking to her mailbox, her normal for the past year.","FH: Mother had hypertension. Father died of a heart attack at 70.","Admission VS: BP 98/60 (MAP 73), HR 118 (sinus tachycardia), RR 24, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 36.1 °C (97.0 °F), pain 6/10.","Home medications:","Epoetin alfa, an erythropoietin, for anemia from chronic kidney disease","Amlodipine, for hypertension","Acetaminophen, for osteoarthritis pain","Allergies: Codeine (nausea and vomiting).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 68-year-old woman lying in bed, the head of the bed at 30 degrees, pale, eyes closed until spoken to. Lies still, guarding her right hip. Awake and oriented × 4: GCS 15. Speaks in full sentences, slowly: \"I feel so weak.\" An…","Pain: 6/10 deep ache in the right hip and thigh, worse with any movement. Says her thigh feels tight.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and regular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 118. Neck veins flat at 30 degrees. Radial, dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses 2+ and equal bilaterally. Capillary refil…","Respiratory: RR 24, regular and even at rest. No use of the neck or chest muscles to breathe at rest. Breath sounds clear in the anterior, posterior and lateral fields bilaterally. Occasional cough, nonproductive. SpO2 95% on 2 L/min by nas…","Integumentary: Skin pale, cool and dry on the arms and legs. Conjunctiva, nailbeds and palms pale. Oral mucous membranes pale and dry. Right lateral hip: a 15 cm incision, staples intact, the dressing changed at 1000 and half soaked again w…","Labs: CBC: hemoglobin 8.0 (10.9 before surgery, 9.6 last evening), hematocrit 24%, platelets 176,000, WBC 9,800. Coags: PT 13 seconds and PTT 32 seconds, normal. Fibrinogen 260.","Procedure: Right total hip replacement, yesterday at 0800."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"anemia-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Deficient fluid volume — Activity intolerance","Deficient fluid volume","Activity intolerance","Supporting cues — HR 118, sinus tachycardia, and 140 when she stood: her first compensation for lost volume. BP 84/52 this morning, and 98/60 only after a liter of LR: too little volume in her vessels. 220 mL of bright red blood in her drai…","HR 118, sinus tachycardia, and 140 when she stood: her first compensation for lost volume.","BP 84/52 this morning, and 98/60 only after a liter of LR: too little volume in her vessels.","220 mL of bright red blood in her drain since midnight, a dressing soaking again, and a swollen, firm thigh with a spreading ecchymosis: she is still bleeding, out of the wound and into her thigh.","Hemoglobin 8.0, down from 10.9 before surgery: blood lost. The full loss will not show for 48 to 72 hours.","Urine 25 mL/hr, under 0.5 mL/kg/hr for her 70 kg, with pale, cool skin: her vessels are sending blood away from her kidneys and skin.","Standing with physical therapy: lightheaded, short of breath, HR 140, nearly fainting. The effort asked for more oxygen than her blood could carry.","After the move from the stretcher to the bed: RR 30 and short phrases for 3 minutes. Even a small move uses all her energy.","\"I feel so weak\": weakness and fatigue, with less oxygen reaching her muscles.","Hemoglobin 8.0, from a usual 10.9 with CKD, and winded at home walking to her mailbox: a moderate anemia on top of a chronic one.","Relevance to the diagnosis — She is bleeding from her new hip into her drain, her dressing and the tissue of her thigh, and blood lost from her vessels leaves too little volume to fill them. Her heart compensates first, with a faster rate. …","She is bleeding from her new hip into her drain, her dressing and the tissue of her thigh, and blood lost from her vessels leaves too little volume to fill them. Her heart compensates first, with a faster rate. Her vessels clamp down to rai…","Her red cells carry oxygen on their hemoglobin, and with fewer of them, too little oxygen reaches her tissues, which survive on aerobic metabolism. At rest she makes up for it with a faster heart rate and breathing. Any effort asks for more…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. She is still bleeding, and her BP is holding only after a fluid bolus. With a MAP of 73, a hemoglobin of 8.0 and a GCS of 15, she is not expected to die within the hour. — 3, general. Effort is beyond her for now…","2, urgent. She is still bleeding, and her BP is holding only after a fluid bolus. With a MAP of 73, a hemoglobin of 8.0 and a GCS of 15, she is not expected to die within the hour.","3, general. Effort is beyond her for now, but resting in bed, with her heart rate and breathing compensating, she is not in danger from it."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"anemia-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Evening","By 1900 Mrs. Hale has been given a second liter of LR. At 2000 her drain has put out 380 mL of bright red blood in 4 hours, and her dressing has soaked through twice since 1500. Her right thigh is larger and tighter, and the bruise has spre…","Updated VS: BP 80/50 (MAP 60), HR 134 (sinus tachycardia), RR 30, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 35.6 °C (96.1 °F), pain 7/10. GCS 14.","Labs: CBC: hemoglobin 6.4, hematocrit 19%, platelets 128,000. Coags: PT 17 seconds and PTT 45 seconds, both prolonged. Fibrinogen 140, low."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"anemia-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Jordan Lee, RN in the SICU. I'm calling about Patricia Hale in room 6, admitted to the ICU today at 1100 with bleeding after a right total hip replacement yesterday. I'm calling because she is still bleeding, her BP is falling, …","B — She is 68, with chronic kidney disease and anemia from it, on epoetin alfa, and hypertension. Her hemoglobin was 10.9 before surgery and 8.0 this morning. She has had 2 L of LR today. Since 1600 her drain has put out 380 mL of bright re…","A — I think she is bleeding into her hip and thigh and going into hypovolemic shock, and her clotting is starting to fail.","R — I recommend PRBCs now, warmed, since red cells are mainly what she is losing, and FFP to replace her clotting factors. I'd like a TEG, to show what else she needs. I'd like to hold tonight's enoxaparin while she is actively bleeding. An…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"anemia-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: deficient fluid volume, from the first part. She is still bleeding, her MAP is 60, and her urine output and level of consciousness are falling. Perfusion to her vital organs is failing, and active bleeding leads to hypovol…","Independent — Warm her: warm blankets, and every fluid and blood product given warmed. Her temperature, dressing, drain, skin and urine output checked each hour, and every soaked dressing weighed. — Temperature plays a big role in clotting.…","Dependent — PRBCs, then FFP, warmed, and IV fluid, per order. — PRBCs are mainly what is replaced in a patient who is actively bleeding. FFP replaces the coagulation factors her bleeding has used up, which is why her PT and PTT are prolonge…","Interdisciplinary — The surgeon and pharmacy: tonight's enoxaparin questioned before it is given. — She is known to be bleeding after surgery, so an enoxaparin order is questioned. It is sometimes still given, because the risk of a DVT is s…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"anemia-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — An acute kidney injury (renal). At 0700 the next morning. In the OR at 0030, a bleeding artery in her hip was tied off, and she has been stable since, her hemoglobin 8.2.","Why it could occur — Her kidneys take about 20% of her cardiac output, so when she bled and her vessels clamped down to send blood to her brain, heart and lungs, her kidneys took a big hit. Hours of low BP last night cut their perfusion fur…","Priority cues — Urine 10 to 15 mL/hr for 6 hours, under 0.5 mL/kg/hr for her 70 kg, though her MAP is back to 72: oliguria. Creatinine 2.4, up from her usual 1.5, and BUN 46: azotemia, and the creatinine is the most reliable sign of it. Pot…","Actions — Independent: urine output measured every hour, and her telemetry watched for a peaked T wave until her potassium is back in range. Dependent: IV fluid replacement per order, normal saline or LR, to flush her kidneys, while she can…","Evaluation — Urine output: back above 0.5 mL/kg/hr, about 35 mL/hr for her, and her creatinine trending back toward 1.5 over the next days: her kidneys are recovering. A creatinine still rising, or a potassium climbing, means the AKI is wor…","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"signature-cardiovascular.html","t":"Cardiovascular","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 3"]},{"s":"The Cardiovascular Diagnoses","a":"compare-diagnoses","k":["The Cardiovascular Diagnoses","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS) — CAD: a plaque ruptures and a thrombus blocks a coronary artery. Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, smoking, diabetes. Male, older, family history. — Chest pressure not relieved by rest, position or nitrates, lasti…","CAD: a plaque ruptures and a thrombus blocks a coronary artery.","Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, smoking, diabetes. Male, older, family history.","Chest pressure not relieved by rest, position or nitrates, lasting 20 minutes or more.","Nausea, diaphoresis, dyspnea, anxiety, dysrhythmias.","Atypical in women, older patients and patients with diabetes.","Troponin elevated in an MI, drawn serially.","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes: ST elevation in a STEMI. ST depression or T wave inversion in an NSTEMI.","Potassium, magnesium, calcium.","Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion: the myocardium past the blockage gets no oxygen.","Impaired gas exchange when a second disease, such as COPD, lowers the oxygen supply.","Acute pain: a general concern.","More cells die: dysrhythmias, heart failure, cardiogenic shock.","Thromboembolism, rupture of part of the heart, pericarditis: more common after a true MI.","MONA-TAS: morphine, oxygen, nitroglycerin, aspirin, thrombolytics, anticoagulants, sedatives.","Reperfusion: a PCI within 90 minutes, thrombolytics within 30 for a STEMI, or a CABG.","Nitroglycerin: blood pressure first. Too low: morphine instead.","AKI after a cath: contrast dye is nephrotoxic. Watch the urine output, BUN and creatinine.","Lost cardiac output: the kidneys lose perfusion, and the work of breathing worsens.","Acute heart failure — New onset after a big MI or cardiac surgery. Exacerbation of chronic HF. Example: a few days of really salty food. Left-sided failure is related to hypertension and diabetes. — Both sides in the ICU. SOB the biggest, w…","New onset after a big MI or cardiac surgery.","Exacerbation of chronic HF. Example: a few days of really salty food.","Left-sided failure is related to hypertension and diabetes.","Both sides in the ICU. SOB the biggest, with fatigue. Crackles, blood-tinged sputum.","JVD, 3+ or 4+ pitting edema, ascites, hepatomegaly.","Low BP, dysrhythmias, extra heart sounds.","Echo: the ejection fraction, 40% or less in heart failure.","BUN and creatinine: low cardiac output starves the kidneys.","Potassium, watched with diuretics and digoxin.","Impaired gas exchange: the left ventricle cannot pump, and blood backs up into the lungs.","Excess fluid volume: the right side backs up into the venous circulation.","Decreased cardiac output: a floppy muscle cannot generate the blood pressure.","Cardiogenic shock: the BP falls and perfusion fails.","Dysrhythmias: less coronary flow, and the conducting cells die.","Keep the patient dry: IV furosemide or bumetanide, ultrafiltration.","Low BP: not fluid. Positive inotropes (milrinone, dobutamine) and vasopressors.","Sedation. An LVAD or VA ECMO. The four pillars, for life.","AKI: low cardiac output from heart failure is a pre-renal cause.","Ischemic bowel: heart failure and high-dose vasopressors cut the gut's blood flow.","Acute liver failure: heart failure exacerbating, an acute loss of perfusion.","Aortic aneurysm — Atherosclerosis weakens the aortic wall. Male, older, smoking: the three huge risk factors. In the ICU after a rupture or a repair. — Usually asymptomatic until it ruptures. Sometimes back or abdominal pain with a AAA. Rup…","Atherosclerosis weakens the aortic wall.","Male, older, smoking: the three huge risk factors.","In the ICU after a rupture or a repair.","Usually asymptomatic until it ruptures. Sometimes back or abdominal pain with a AAA.","Ruptured: very hypovolemic, chest pain, diaphoretic.","CT angiogram.","After a rupture or repair: BUN and creatinine, AST and ALT, bilirubin.","Deficient fluid volume: a rupture bleeds out.","Ineffective tissue perfusion: blood flow lost below the rupture.","Grows, then ruptures. A big rupture is usually fatal.","Avoid rupture: watched or repaired, by size. Smoking cessation.","After a repair: post-op care as after a CABG, with chest tubes.","Blood flow lost below the rupture: dead bowel, severe AKI, liver damage, pancreatic issues, poorly perfused legs.","Cardiac tamponade — Most common after a CABG or any open heart surgery. Also after a cath. Chest trauma, a ruptured aorta, a ruptured ventricle after an MI. Pericarditis not watched closely. — Beck's triad: low BP, JVD, muffled heart sounds…","Most common after a CABG or any open heart surgery. Also after a cath.","Chest trauma, a ruptured aorta, a ruptured ventricle after an MI. Pericarditis not watched closely.","Beck's triad: low BP, JVD, muffled heart sounds.","After surgery: chest tube output stops abruptly, with sudden hypotension and tachycardia.","H&H drop with no obvious bleeding, trended every 1 to 2 hours after cardiac surgery.","Decreased cardiac output: blood in the pericardial space keeps the heart from beating effectively.","Circulatory shock and cardiac arrest: a medical emergency.","Blocked chest tube: the surgeons may milk it.","After surgery: back to the OR. Otherwise: pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window.","None taught.","Pericarditis — Often after an MI or open heart surgery. Chest trauma, infection, radiation, cancer, lupus. — Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark. Very painful: mimics MI pain. Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety. — ST elevation on the EKG. WBC and…","Often after an MI or open heart surgery.","Chest trauma, infection, radiation, cancer, lupus.","Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark.","Very painful: mimics MI pain. Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety.","ST elevation on the EKG. WBC and sed rate up.","Troponin, to rule out an MI. An echo (TTE).","Decreased cardiac output: it can cause a tamponade.","A tamponade, if not monitored closely.","Anti-inflammatories: ibuprofen, colchicine, ketorolac for a big one.","Rule out an MI. Listen for the rub.","Valve disease and infective endocarditis — Aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation: the two most common. Can be congenital. Endocarditis: IV drug use, sepsis. In the ICU after a valve replacement. — Valve disease: heart failure signs, exertio…","Aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation: the two most common. Can be congenital.","Endocarditis: IV drug use, sepsis.","In the ICU after a valve replacement.","Valve disease: heart failure signs, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, a murmur.","Endocarditis: a really sick infection. High temp, chills, diaphoresis, low BP, dysrhythmias.","Echo: the valves, the chambers and the ejection fraction.","Labs: none taught.","Decreased cardiac output: the blood backs up behind the valve.","Hyperthermia in endocarditis: bacteria in the bloodstream lodge on the valve as vegetations.","Heart failure: from the left side into the lungs, from the right into the periphery.","Endocarditis: the valve too damaged to save.","Valve replacement: post-op care as after a CABG.","Endocarditis: continuous IV antibiotics for 2 to 6 weeks. Prophylactic antibiotics for life.","AV blocks — Age or ischemic disease, as in post-MI heart failure. — Bradycardia: the biggest concern. Compromised cardiac output, more symptomatic with each degree. — The rhythm on the monitor or a 12-lead EKG. Labs: none taught. — Decrease…","Age or ischemic disease, as in post-MI heart failure.","Bradycardia: the biggest concern.","Compromised cardiac output, more symptomatic with each degree.","The rhythm on the monitor or a 12-lead EKG.","Decreased cardiac output: the rate is too slow.","Activity intolerance: not enough cardiac output brings fatigue.","1st degree, 2nd degree type I, type II, then 3rd degree: complete heart block.","A pacemaker: no medication fixes a heart block.","Symptomatic bradycardia: a transvenous pacer."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"acs-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Daniel Brooks is a 63-year-old white man brought to the ED by his wife at 2130. At 2045, carrying groceries up the stairs, he felt a heavy pressure in the center of his chest. He sat down, but after 20 minutes of rest it had not eased,…","PMH: Hypertension, 12 years. Hyperlipidemia, 8 years. COPD (emphysema), diagnosed 6 years ago, no exacerbation in the past year. Stable angina, 2 years: chest tightness with exertion, relieved by rest.","SH: Married, lives with his wife in a two-story house. Retired machinist. Former smoker: a pack a day for 40 years, quit 3 years ago. Two or three beers on weekends. No drug use.","FH: Father died of an MI at 58. Mother has hypertension.","Admission VS: BP 158/94, HR 104 (sinus tachycardia with occasional PVCs), RR 24, SpO2 89% on room air, temperature 37.0 °C (98.6 °F), pain 8/10.","Home medications:","Amlodipine, for hypertension","Atorvastatin, for hyperlipidemia","Aspirin, an antiplatelet, for his CAD","Tiotropium, for COPD","Albuterol, for wheezing","Nitroglycerin, for chest pain","Allergies: Sulfa drugs (rash).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 63-year-old man sitting upright in bed, a hand pressed to his chest. Pale and diaphoretic. Restless and anxious: \"Am I having a heart attack?\" Speaks in full sentences, pausing for breath. Clean and well groomed, in a hospit…","Pain: 8/10 pressure in the center of his chest, \"like a heavy weight\", constant since 2045, not relieved by rest or his own nitroglycerin.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid, with occasional early beats. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 104 with occasional PVCs. No JVD at 45 degrees. No edema of the hands, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial…","Respiratory: RR 24, slightly labored. Barrel chest. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds decreased in the posterior bases bilaterally, with expiratory wheezes in the posterior upper lobes. Anterior and lateral fie…","Genitourinary: Foley placed in the ED, draining clear yellow urine at 50 mL/hr. No suprapubic tenderness or distention.","Labs: Troponin 0.84 ng/mL, elevated. BMP: sodium 139, potassium 3.6, chloride 102, CO2 27, BUN 16, creatinine 0.9, glucose 142.","Diagnostic: 12-lead EKG at 2135: sinus tachycardia at 104, ST depression and T wave inversion in two contiguous leads. No ST elevation."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"acs-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion — Impaired gas exchange","Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion","Impaired gas exchange","Supporting cues — Chest pressure 8/10 for over 45 minutes, not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin: ischemic pain, the hallmark of an MI. Troponin 0.84 ng/mL, elevated: heart cells are dying. ST depression and T wave inversion with no ST elev…","Chest pressure 8/10 for over 45 minutes, not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin: ischemic pain, the hallmark of an MI.","Troponin 0.84 ng/mL, elevated: heart cells are dying.","ST depression and T wave inversion with no ST elevation: ischemia, read with the troponin as an NSTEMI.","HR 104 with occasional PVCs: a faster heart needs more oxygen, and ischemic conducting cells misfire.","SpO2 89% on room air: hypoxemia.","RR 24, slightly labored: tachypnea.","Breath sounds decreased in the posterior bases, expiratory wheezes, prolonged expiration, barrel chest: his COPD.","Relevance to the diagnosis — Years of hypertension, hyperlipidemia and 40 pack-years of smoking built atherosclerotic plaque in his coronary arteries: his stable angina. Tonight a plaque ruptured and a thrombus formed, partly blocking a cor…","Years of hypertension, hyperlipidemia and 40 pack-years of smoking built atherosclerotic plaque in his coronary arteries: his stable angina. Tonight a plaque ruptured and a thrombus formed, partly blocking a coronary artery. The myocardium …","Emphysema has damaged his alveoli, so less oxygen crosses into his blood, and that blood is all his ischemic myocardium gets. Hypoxemia severely impacts cardiac tissue and can set off rhythm changes, on top of the PVCs already seen. Oxygen …","Concern rating — 2, urgent. Every minute of ischemia costs heart muscle, so it must be treated quickly. His BP is holding, he is alert, and the rhythm is sinus: he is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. Low oxygen worsens the …","2, urgent. Every minute of ischemia costs heart muscle, so it must be treated quickly. His BP is holding, he is alert, and the rhythm is sinus: he is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. Low oxygen worsens the ischemia and can trigger dysrhythmias. He speaks in full sentences with no accessory muscles in use: not life-threatening within the hour."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"acs-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","Early the Next Morning","In the ED Mr. Brooks was given aspirin, to keep platelets from clumping on the clot, nitroglycerin, to open his coronary arteries, oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal cannula, and heparin, to keep the clot from growing. His pain eased to 2/10. He ca…","Updated VS: BP 96/60, HR 112 (sinus tachycardia with frequent PVCs), RR 26, SpO2 90% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 37.1 °C (98.8 °F), pain 7/10.","Lab: Troponin 3.6 ng/mL, up from 0.84."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"acs-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Jordan Lee, RN in the CVICU. I'm calling about Daniel Brooks in room 6, admitted last night at 2200 with acute coronary syndrome, an NSTEMI. I'm calling because his chest pain is back and not relieved by nitroglycerin.","B — He is 63, with hypertension, hyperlipidemia, COPD, stable angina and 40 pack-years of smoking. He is on heparin, to keep the clot from growing, and 2 L of oxygen, and had aspirin, an antiplatelet, on admission. At 0400 his chest pressur…","A — I think the blocked artery is still starving his heart muscle, and his MI is getting bigger.","R — I recommend morphine now for the pain, since his BP is too low for nitroglycerin: it eases the heart's workload and his anxiety. I'd like a potassium and magnesium level, because electrolytes are often behind PVCs and runs of VTach. I'm…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"acs-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: decreased cardiac tissue perfusion. His heart muscle is still dying. The troponin is rising, and the dying cells are driving the PVCs, the VTach and the falling BP. Restoring blood flow limits the infarct and every problem…","Independent — Bed rest with the head of the bed up. Cluster care, a quiet room, and stay with him, explaining each step. — Rest decreases the heart's oxygen demand, the reason stable angina eases with rest. Anxiety and a fast heart rate rai…","Dependent — Morphine per order, for the pain, with the BP checked first. — Reduces pain and anxiety. A vasodilator: it lowers the preload and afterload, so the heart's oxygen demand falls. Better tolerated than nitroglycerin when the BP is …","Interdisciplinary — Cardiology and the cath lab: an urgent cardiac cath with a stent (PCI). — Reperfusion. The stent pushes the plaque against the vessel wall and restores blood flow, reducing the size of the infarct. — The artery open with…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"acs-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Acute kidney injury (renal). The next morning, 26 hours after the cath.","Why it could occur — Two hits, the usual pattern. Pre-renal: for hours before the cath his BP was low, and the kidneys take 20 to 25% of cardiac output. Intrarenal: the contrast dye from the cath is nephrotoxic.","Priority cues — Urine output 15 mL/hr for 6 hours: oliguria, under the 30 mL/hr of an average adult. Creatinine 2.2, up from 0.9, and BUN 44, up from 16: azotemia. Potassium 5.4: the kidneys are not excreting it. Tired and drowsy, a vague s…","Actions — Dependent: normal saline per order, to flush the dye from the kidneys and restore their perfusion. His lungs are checked first: still no crackles or JVD, so he can tolerate it. Independent: strict intake and output, the urine outp…","Evaluation — Urine output: back to 30 mL/hr or more. Then the BUN and creatinine, trending down."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"ahf-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Loretta Hayes is a 74-year-old Black woman brought to the ED by her daughter at 1900. Over 4 days she has grown more and more short of breath, and today she is breathless at rest. She has slept sitting up in her recliner for 3 nights, …","PMH: Chronic heart failure since a large MI 4 years ago, with an ejection fraction of 30% a year ago. Hypertension, 20 years. Type 2 diabetes, 15 years. Chronic kidney disease, stage 3, baseline creatinine 1.6.","SH: Widowed, lives alone in a one-story house. Retired school cafeteria cook. Her daughter lives 10 minutes away. Never smoked. No alcohol or drug use.","FH: Mother had hypertension and died of a stroke at 70. Brother has type 2 diabetes.","Admission VS: BP 152/90, HR 112 (sinus tachycardia with frequent PVCs), RR 30, SpO2 85% on room air, temperature 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), pain 3/10. Weight 84 kg, up from 81 kg.","Home medications:","Furosemide, a diuretic, for heart failure","Carvedilol, a beta blocker, for heart failure","Lisinopril, for heart failure and hypertension","Spironolactone, for heart failure","Empagliflozin, for heart failure","Insulin glargine, for diabetes","Allergies: Penicillin (hives).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 74-year-old woman sitting bolt upright, leaning forward on the over-bed table in the tripod position. Speaks in 3- to 4-word phrases. Anxious: \"I can't catch my breath.\" Tired, resting after each answer. Hair combed, in her …","Pain: 3/10 dull ache in the right upper abdomen, constant for 2 days. Denies chest pain.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid, with an extra heart sound and occasional early beats. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 112 with frequent PVCs, about 6 a minute. JVD to the angle of the jaw at 45 degrees. 3+ pitting edema of both le…","Respiratory: RR 30, labored, using the neck and thoracic (intercostal) muscles. Crackles in the posterior bases up to the mid lungs, and in the lateral bases, bilaterally. Anterior upper fields clear. Frequent cough, productive of thin, pin…","Gastrointestinal: Abdomen distended with ascites. Hepatomegaly: the liver enlarged and tender in the right upper quadrant. Bowel sounds active in all 4 quadrants. Nauseated, no vomiting. Last bowel movement yesterday: formed, brown, no bloo…","Labs: BMP: sodium 131, potassium 5.6 (high), chloride 98, CO2 24, BUN 46, creatinine 2.0 (baseline 1.6), glucose 188. ABG on room air: pH 7.48, PaCO2 31, HCO3 24, PaO2 55.","Diagnostic: Echo: ejection fraction 25%, down from 30% a year ago."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"ahf-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Impaired gas exchange — Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium","Impaired gas exchange","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium","Supporting cues — SpO2 85% on room air and PaO2 55: hypoxemia, well under the normal 80 to 100. RR 30 with a PaCO2 of 31, pH 7.48 and HCO3 24: tachypnea blowing off CO2, an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. Crackles up to the mid lungs, …","SpO2 85% on room air and PaO2 55: hypoxemia, well under the normal 80 to 100.","RR 30 with a PaCO2 of 31, pH 7.48 and HCO3 24: tachypnea blowing off CO2, an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis.","Crackles up to the mid lungs, with pink, blood-tinged sputum: pulmonary edema.","Sleeping upright, the tripod position, neck and thoracic muscles in use.","Potassium 5.6: high.","Frequent PVCs, about 6 a minute: an irritable heart.","Creatinine 2.0, up from her baseline 1.6, with a BUN of 46: her kidneys are excreting less.","Spironolactone at home: a potassium-sparing diuretic.","Relevance to the diagnosis — Her MI 4 years ago left a damaged myocardium that cannot contract well: an ejection fraction now 25%. A weekend of salty food raised her blood volume and her preload. Her weak left ventricle cannot pump it forwa…","Her MI 4 years ago left a damaged myocardium that cannot contract well: an ejection fraction now 25%. A weekend of salty food raised her blood volume and her preload. Her weak left ventricle cannot pump it forward, so blood backs up into th…","The kidneys are primarily responsible for excreting potassium. Years of diabetes and hypertension left her with chronic kidney disease, and now her failing heart sends them less blood: they take 20 to 25% of cardiac output. Her spironolacto…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. Her oxygen is low and her breathing labored, but she is alert and talking, and her SpO2 rises to 91% on 4 L by nasal cannula. She is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. The PVCs show the potassium a…","2, urgent. Her oxygen is low and her breathing labored, but she is alert and talking, and her SpO2 rises to 91% on 4 L by nasal cannula. She is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. The PVCs show the potassium already affecting her heart, and it could lead to a lethal rhythm. Her rhythm is still sinus, and her BP is holding."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"ahf-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","The Next Morning","Overnight in the CVICU Mrs. Hayes was given furosemide, to pull the fluid off, and kept on 4 L of oxygen by nasal cannula. She put out only 400 mL. At 0700 she is newly confused: she knows her name, but not where she is or the day, and she …","Updated VS: BP 84/52, HR 118 (sinus tachycardia with frequent PVCs), RR 32, SpO2 88% on 4 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 36.4 °C (97.5 °F), pain 3/10. GCS 14.","Lab: Creatinine 2.7, up from 2.0."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"ahf-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Jordan Lee, RN in the CVICU. I'm calling about Loretta Hayes in room 3, admitted last night at 2000 with acute decompensated heart failure. I'm calling because her blood pressure has dropped and she is newly confused.","B — She is 74, with chronic heart failure since an MI 4 years ago, an ejection fraction now 25%, hypertension, type 2 diabetes and stage 3 chronic kidney disease. She had furosemide overnight, to pull the fluid off, and put out only 400 mL.…","A — I'm worried she is going into cardiogenic shock: her heart can no longer generate a blood pressure.","R — I recommend a positive inotrope, such as milrinone, to strengthen her contractions and raise her cardiac output and blood pressure. I'd recommend against a fluid bolus for the BP: it would worsen her pulmonary edema and her heart's work…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"ahf-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: decreased cardiac output, new this morning. Her heart can no longer pump enough blood, and the low BP is starving her brain (the confusion), her kidneys (10 mL/hr) and her limbs. Her gas exchange will not improve, and flui…","Independent — Bed rest. Cluster care, keep her calm, and reorient her each time she wakes. — Rest lowers the oxygen demand and the workload of a failing heart. — HR 110 or less and RR 28 or less within 1 hour. — Met. At 0815, HR 108, RR 26,…","Dependent — Milrinone per order, to strengthen her heart's contractions. — A phosphodiesterase inhibitor and a positive inotrope. Stronger contraction raises the stroke volume, which raises the cardiac output. Helps the low BP and the fatig…","Interdisciplinary — Nephrology and the ICU team: ultrafiltration through CRRT, pulling fluid off slowly. — Keep the patient dry: less fluid means less cardiac workload. CRRT suits a fragile BP: gentle, its rate slowed or sped up hour to hou…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"ahf-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Ischemic bowel (GI). The afternoon of her third day, after hours of low BP and 30 hours on norepinephrine.","Why it could occur — With a low blood pressure, the gut and the skin take the hit first. Norepinephrine, high and prolonged, clamps off the gut's arterial supply to shift the blood to vital organs. Heart failure is a risk factor in itself, …","Priority cues — New abdominal pain, 7/10, constant and all over, beyond her old right upper ache. Two loose stools with dark red clots. Lactate 4.2, up from 1.8 this morning and still rising: cells in the gut are dying. WBC 16,000: leukocyt…","Actions — Dependent: bowel rest, NPO per order, and the lactate trended every 2 hours. The provider is told at once: the diagnosis needs a colonoscopy or a CT, and dead bowel needs surgery. Independent: the abdomen, the pain and every stool…","Evaluation — Lactate: stabilizing, then coming down. A lactate that keeps rising means the gut is still dying."]}]},{"f":"signature-renal.html","t":"Renal","secs":[{"s":"Renal","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 4"]},{"s":"The Three Kinds of AKI","a":"compare-diagnoses","k":["The Three Kinds of AKI","Pre-renal — Hypoperfusion: a sudden, severe drop in BP (shock), or interrupted blood flow to the kidneys. Very dehydrated, a lot of blood lost, low cardiac output from heart failure. Very common in septic shock. The most common cause of AKI…","Hypoperfusion: a sudden, severe drop in BP (shock), or interrupted blood flow to the kidneys.","Very dehydrated, a lot of blood lost, low cardiac output from heart failure. Very common in septic shock.","The most common cause of AKI.","Hypotension, with tachycardia.","Hypotensive or hypovolemic for a while: the AKI may develop within the next day or two.","BUN: dehydrated, a false high. Read it with the creatinine.","Deficient fluid volume: too little volume to perfuse the kidneys.","Decreased cardiac output when heart failure is the cause.","Prolonged hypotension cuts kidney perfusion further, and can damage the kidneys more.","The oliguric phase begins about 1 day after a hypotensive event.","IV fluid replacement: crystalloid, normal saline or LR, to flush the kidneys, if the patient can tolerate it.","Vasopressors for hypoperfusion: tighter vessels, better perfusion to the kidneys.","Hypotension on IHD: fluid pulled off a patient already a little too dry, so the preload falls too low.","Intrarenal — Direct nephron injury: inflammation, toxins, drugs, infection or reduced blood supply. Toxins and drugs are the big ones: the nephrotoxic antibiotics and contrast dye, high doses. Reduced blood supply over time, from prolonged …","Direct nephron injury: inflammation, toxins, drugs, infection or reduced blood supply.","Toxins and drugs are the big ones: the nephrotoxic antibiotics and contrast dye, high doses.","Reduced blood supply over time, from prolonged hypertension.","Most common: acute tubular necrosis (ATN).","Examples: glomerulonephritis from lupus or a virus, pyelonephritis, hypertensive glomerulonephropathy.","Hypertension for a short time, as in ATN, from RAAS malfunction.","A nephrotoxic drug or contrast dye in the history.","Peak and trough levels of a nephrotoxic drug.","Excess fluid volume: injured nephrons cannot excrete the fluid.","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium, no longer excreted.","More nephrotoxic exposure: poorly perfused kidneys asked to filter the ICU's antibiotics and contrast dye.","A nephrotoxic drug given in a fluid volume deficit: a double punch to the kidneys.","Address the drug: a team talk. Is it appropriate? Switch it, lower the dose, monitor peak and trough levels.","IV fluids flush out the nephrotoxic meds.","None taught.","Post-renal — Tubular obstruction: sudden obstruction of urine flow. Urine backs up from a problem past the kidneys. An enlarged prostate, kidney stones, a bladder tumor, injury. Not usually the cause in the critically ill, but can happen. —…","Tubular obstruction: sudden obstruction of urine flow. Urine backs up from a problem past the kidneys.","An enlarged prostate, kidney stones, a bladder tumor, injury.","Not usually the cause in the critically ill, but can happen.","Impaired urinary elimination: urine cannot get past the blockage.","Every AKI, Whatever the Cause","Usually multifactorial: most often pre-renal plus intrarenal. Sepsis is the most common cause in the critically ill.","Oliguria: under about 0.5 mL/kg/hr, typically 30 mL/hr for an average adult.","Azotemia: the BUN and creatinine rise. Both up: the kidneys. The creatinine is the most reliable indicator.","Potassium builds up: the kidneys excrete it. A peaked T wave may be a potassium imbalance, so telemetry until it is back in range.","Metabolic acidosis from a lack of bicarb: the respirations change to compensate, and hyperventilation may be seen.","Uremia or azotemia: malaise, fatigue, disorientation, drowsiness. Super vague, but always look for them.","Every AKI: How It Worsens and What Is Done","The oliguric phase: fluid, potassium and wastes are retained, for 1 to 3 weeks.","Dialysis indications: acidosis, hyperkalemia, volume overload, uremia. A combination: low urine output alone is not one.","No medication fixes the kidneys: diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide) raise the urine output and flush potassium out. Monitor the electrolytes.","Renal replacement therapy when the rest is not working: IHD if hemodynamically stable, CRRT if fragile.","Other systems: volume overload brings crackles and pulmonary edema. Hyperkalemia brings dysrhythmias. Uremia changes the level of consciousness.","On IHD: hypotension, dysrhythmias from too much potassium pulled off, disequilibrium syndrome.","Afterward: the kidneys can recover. Some develop chronic renal disease.","The exemplar: a pre-renal plus intrarenal AKI, in the Acute Kidney Injury tab."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"aki-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Rosa Delgado is a 69-year-old Hispanic woman brought to the ED by her son at 1400. For 4 days she has had vomiting and watery diarrhea, after a stomach bug went through her grandchildren, and she has kept almost nothing down. Two days …","PMH: Hypertension, 18 years. Chronic kidney disease, stage 3A, from her hypertension, diagnosed 3 years ago: baseline creatinine 1.3, eGFR 52. Hyperlipidemia, 10 years. Osteoarthritis of both knees.","SH: Widowed, lives with her son, his wife and their two young children. Retired seamstress. Never smoked. A glass of wine on holidays. No drug use.","FH: Mother had hypertension and kidney failure, on dialysis in her 70s. Father had type 2 diabetes.","Admission VS: BP 90/54 (MAP 66), HR 118 (sinus tachycardia), RR 24, deep and regular, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pain 4/10. Weight 62 kg, down from her usual 65 kg.","Home medications:","Amlodipine, for hypertension","Atorvastatin, for hyperlipidemia","Acetaminophen, for knee pain","Ondansetron, for nausea, since 2 days ago","Allergies: Codeine (vomiting).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 69-year-old woman lying curled on her side on the stretcher, knees drawn up. Eyes open, but drifts off between questions. Speech slow and soft. Oriented to person and place, not to the date: GCS 14 (E4, V4, M6). Flat and tir…","Pain: 4/10 cramping across the lower abdomen, coming in waves before each loose stool. Last stool 1 hour ago, watery and brown, no blood.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and regular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 118. Neck veins flat at 45 degrees. No edema of the hands, sacrum, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial and pedal pulses 1+,…","Respiratory: RR 24, deep and regular. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds clear in the anterior, posterior and lateral fields bilaterally, with no crackles or wheezes. No cough. SpO2 96% on room air.","Genitourinary: Foley placed in the ED: 40 mL of dark, concentrated amber urine, then 10 mL/hr. No blood in the urine. Bladder not distended, no suprapubic tenderness, no flank tenderness bilaterally.","Labs: BMP: sodium 133, potassium 5.8 (high), chloride 104, CO2 16, BUN 74, creatinine 3.6 (baseline 1.3), glucose 98. ABG on room air: pH 7.30, PaCO2 30, HCO3 16, PaO2 88.","Diagnostic: 12-lead EKG at 1415: sinus tachycardia at 118, with tall, peaked T waves."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"aki-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Deficient fluid volume — Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium","Deficient fluid volume","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium","Supporting cues — BP 90/54 with HR 118: hypotension with tachycardia, what a pre-renal AKI looks like. Urine 10 mL/hr, dark and concentrated: oliguria, under her 0.5 mL/kg/hr, which at 62 kg is 31 mL/hr. Radial and pedal pulses weak and thr…","BP 90/54 with HR 118: hypotension with tachycardia, what a pre-renal AKI looks like.","Urine 10 mL/hr, dark and concentrated: oliguria, under her 0.5 mL/kg/hr, which at 62 kg is 31 mL/hr.","Radial and pedal pulses weak and thready, capillary refill 4 seconds, cool and clammy skin, flat neck veins, dry mucous membranes, 3 kg lost: little volume in her vessels.","Creatinine 3.6, up from her baseline of 1.3, with BUN 74: both up, so the kidneys. The BUN is higher still because dehydration gives it a false high.","Potassium 5.8: high.","Tall, peaked T waves on the 12-lead: a potassium imbalance, showing on the EKG.","pH 7.30, PaCO2 30, HCO3 16: a partially compensated metabolic acidosis. The low HCO3 is her kidneys failing to keep bicarb. The low PaCO2 is her lungs compensating: RR 24, deep, hyperventilation.","Stage 3A CKD: her kidneys already excrete less.","Relevance to the diagnosis — Four days of vomiting and diarrhea emptied her vessels, and her BP fell. The kidneys take 20 to 25% of cardiac output and are highly affected by changes in blood pressure: hypoperfusion, a pre-renal cause. Two d…","Four days of vomiting and diarrhea emptied her vessels, and her BP fell. The kidneys take 20 to 25% of cardiac output and are highly affected by changes in blood pressure: hypoperfusion, a pre-renal cause. Two days ago, while very dehydrate…","The kidneys are primarily responsible for excreting potassium, and in CKD it is one of the first electrolytes to become abnormal. Making almost no urine, she cannot get rid of it, so it builds up. Her failing kidneys also cannot keep bicarb…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. Every hour of low perfusion injures her kidneys further, and the volume must be replaced quickly. Her MAP of 66 is holding above 60, and she answers questions: she is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urg…","2, urgent. Every hour of low perfusion injures her kidneys further, and the volume must be replaced quickly. Her MAP of 66 is holding above 60, and she answers questions: she is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. The peaked T waves say the potassium must come down before a dysrhythmia comes. At 5.8, under 6.0, with a sinus rhythm, it is not life-threatening within the hour."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"aki-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Night","Mrs. Delgado came to the MICU at 1700. Her amlodipine was held, and by 2300 she had been given 3 L of normal saline, to rehydrate her and flush her kidneys. Her BP came up to 118/70 by 2100, but her urine output never rose. At 0300 she has …","Updated VS: BP 164/92, HR 64 (sinus rhythm with occasional PVCs), RR 30, deep, SpO2 94% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pain 2/10. GCS 13.","Lab: BMP: sodium 130, potassium 6.6, CO2 12, BUN 96, creatinine 5.0."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"aki-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Jordan Lee, RN in the MICU. I'm calling about Rosa Delgado in room 8, admitted yesterday at 1700 with an acute kidney injury. I'm calling because her potassium is 6.6 and she is fluid overloaded, with almost no urine.","B — She is 69, with stage 3A CKD from hypertension, baseline creatinine 1.3, after 4 days of vomiting and diarrhea and contrast dye 2 days ago. She has had 3 L of normal saline since admission and made 6 mL/hr of urine for 6 hours. Her BP i…","A — I think her AKI is getting more severe: her kidneys have stopped making urine, and she now has every indication for dialysis.","R — I recommend stopping the IV fluids, because her lungs show she can no longer tolerate them. I'd like furosemide now, to try to raise her urine output and flush potassium out. I'd ask nephrology to see her now for urgent dialysis: she ha…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"aki-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: electrolyte imbalance, a high potassium, from the first part. At 6.6 she is above the 6.0 where dysrhythmias come, with peaked T waves and new PVCs, and a dysrhythmia can kill faster than the fluid in her lungs, which is h…","Independent — Continuous telemetry with the alarms on, the rhythm watched for runs of PVCs and slowing, and the provider called at any change. No potassium in anything she is given: every IV bag and her tray checked. — A potassium above 6.0…","Dependent — Furosemide per order, to raise her urine output. — A diuretic: it increases the urine output and helps with the buildup of electrolytes, flushing potassium out. — Urine output 30 mL/hr or more within 2 hours, and the potassium s…","Interdisciplinary — Nephrology: a temporary trialysis catheter in the right internal jugular, then urgent intermittent hemodialysis (IHD) at the bedside. Labs drawn before and after, and her amlodipine held before the run. — The dialysate h…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"aki-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Disequilibrium syndrome (neurologic). The next morning, her third day, 2 hours into her second IHD run.","Why it could occur — Disequilibrium syndrome is unique to hemodialysis, and most often comes in the first few treatments: the body is not used to so much fluid pulled off and put on so quickly. Yesterday's run pulled 2.4 L off and her BUN f…","Priority cues — Disoriented again, to place and time, after being oriented × 3 since yesterday's run: GCS 14. Restless and agitated, pulling at her blankets and reaching for the catheter in her neck. A new pounding headache, 7/10. Nauseated…","Actions — Dependent: nephrology told at once, and per order the run is slowed, pulling less fluid off, or stopped for today. Independent: seizure precautions, since disequilibrium syndrome can bring seizures: suction set up and working, oxy…","Evaluation — Orientation: back to oriented × 3 within an hour of slowing the run, with the headache easing. Worsening confusion, or a seizure, means the shift is still too fast.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"signature-gi-hepatic.html","t":"GI and Hepatic","secs":[{"s":"GI and Hepatic","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 5"]},{"s":"The GI and Hepatic Diagnoses","a":"compare-diagnoses","k":["The GI and Hepatic Diagnoses","Acute pancreatitis — Alcohol or biliary disease: the most common causes. I GET SMASHED: also trauma, steroids, hypercalcemia, hypertriglyceridemia, ERCP, drugs. Severe pancreatitis is what leads to critical care. — Epigastric pain radiating…","Alcohol or biliary disease: the most common causes.","I GET SMASHED: also trauma, steroids, hypercalcemia, hypertriglyceridemia, ERCP, drugs.","Severe pancreatitis is what leads to critical care.","Epigastric pain radiating to the flank or back, constant and dull, worse lying supine.","Guarding, distension, heavy nausea and vomiting, quick dehydration.","Fever, tachycardia, jaundice, restlessness. Cullen's sign and Grey Turner's sign.","Lipase: the most diagnostic, trended, with amylase.","A low calcium within 48 hours: very high risk for severe problems.","Albumin, for fluid status. Glucose, potassium, magnesium, CRP.","Deficient fluid volume: fluid collects in the retroperitoneal space, up to a third of the plasma volume.","Unstable blood glucose: hyperglycemia, or hypoglycemia when severe.","Acute pain: a general concern.","Starts localized, then can accelerate to a systemic inflammatory state.","Hypovolemic shock: the primary cause of death.","Necrotizing or hemorrhagic pancreatitis, a pseudocyst, abdominal compartment syndrome.","Rest the GI tract: NPO. An NG tube. TPN when severe.","Fluid replacement: usually colloids, such as albumin. LR. Potassium, calcium and magnesium replaced.","Glucose closely: an insulin or a dextrose drip. Pain management, quite a bit.","ARDS: pancreatitis is one of the biggest risk factors. Hypoxemia, pleural effusions.","AKI, from the fluid shifting. Pericardial effusion, DIC, sepsis, decreased LOC.","GI bleed, including variceal — A peptic ulcer before: very high risk of another, especially on an anticoagulant. Critically ill: stress ulcers. A Mallory-Weiss tear after aggressive vomiting. Varices: end-stage liver disease or acute liver …","A peptic ulcer before: very high risk of another, especially on an anticoagulant.","Critically ill: stress ulcers. A Mallory-Weiss tear after aggressive vomiting.","Varices: end-stage liver disease or acute liver failure, from portal hypertension.","Tachycardia first, then a falling BP, a rising RR and a falling urine output.","Getting paler. Bright red stool or emesis: active. Dark or melena: older.","Looks like anemia: weakness, shortness of breath, angina, syncope.","H&H every 1 to 2 hours. The hematocrit may not change for several hours.","BUN up with no kidney cause: blood metabolized in the GI tract. Lactate, clotting profiles, LFTs.","Endoscopy finds the bleed. Gastric lavage: active, old or stopped?","Deficient fluid volume: blood lost from the vessels.","Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion: too little hemoglobin to carry oxygen, with angina.","Aspiration in a variceal bleed: the airway comes first.","Hypovolemic shock, from the fluid volume deficit.","Massive rebleeding, such as the day after an endoclip.","Variceal: rapidly hemodynamically unstable, and quickly in hypovolemic shock.","Stabilize the vital signs: isotonic fluids, transfusions guided by the hemoglobin. Vasopressors only once the tank is full.","Endoscopy for an upper bleed. Surgery for massive rebleeding. A continuous pantoprazole drip.","Variceal: the airway first. Octreotide or vasopressin, banding, TIPS, a tamponade tube: never clamp it.","Kidneys and liver lose perfusion: BUN, creatinine, LFTs.","EKG changes from lack of blood flow, in a very severe bleed.","Clotting factors used up: FFP may be needed. In liver disease, blood in the gut drives up the ammonia.","Hepatic failure — Acetaminophen overdose: the biggest cause, especially with binge drinking. Binge drinking, hepatotoxic drugs, a virus, a loss of perfusion, HELLP syndrome. Chronic liver disease exacerbating, or damage to a healthy liver. …","Acetaminophen overdose: the biggest cause, especially with binge drinking.","Binge drinking, hepatotoxic drugs, a virus, a loss of perfusion, HELLP syndrome.","Chronic liver disease exacerbating, or damage to a healthy liver.","Neurologic, from lost detoxification: decreased LOC, irritability, convulsions, coma. Asterixis.","Jaundice, a sudden high fever, nausea and vomiting, lethargy.","Bleeding: from falls, GI bleeds, varices. Ascites.","Ammonia: the level of hepatic encephalopathy. Above 150, a risk of cerebral edema.","INR above 1.5, platelets below 150,000, low fibrinogen.","Albumin, bilirubin, liver enzymes. A liver biopsy when the cause is unknown: very dangerous.","Acute confusion: toxins such as ammonia build up and depress the brain.","Bleeding: the failing liver makes too few clotting factors.","Excess fluid volume: albumin production falls, and fluid third spaces.","Hepatic encephalopathy, grades 1 to 4. Grades 3 and 4: worse outcomes.","Cerebral edema: life-threatening. Ammonia above 200: brain herniation.","Bleeding, GI and variceal. Hypoglycemia.","Lactulose, titrated to 2 to 4 bowel movements a day and the neurologic status.","Coagulopathy: vitamin K, platelets when very low, FFP when bleeding with an INR above 1.5.","Focused neurologic assessments: seizures and the airway. Fluid status.","AKI. Infection, a higher risk.","Cardiopulmonary: the hyperdynamic state, then acute, severe heart failure (cardiohepatic syndrome).","Ischemic bowel — Low blood pressure: the gut takes the hit first. A clot or blockage in an artery. Atherosclerosis, age over 50, smoking, heart failure and AFib, clotting disorders. High-dose vasopressors, especially norepinephrine. — Abdom…","Low blood pressure: the gut takes the hit first. A clot or blockage in an artery.","Atherosclerosis, age over 50, smoking, heart failure and AFib, clotting disorders.","High-dose vasopressors, especially norepinephrine.","Abdominal pain, especially after eating, then at baseline.","Urgent, loose stools. Bloody stool with dark red clots. Nausea, vomiting.","Fever, from dead gut.","Lactate increasing: trended very frequently. WBC usually elevated.","Colonoscopy, or a barium study or CT with contrast. Not seen on an X-ray.","Ineffective GI tissue perfusion: the bowel wall gets no oxygenated blood, and dies.","Deficient fluid volume: fluid shifting, often intravascularly depleted.","The infarction can spread: a lactate that keeps rising means the gut is still dying.","A paralytic ileus, abdominal compartment syndrome.","Bowel rest. Fluid resuscitation.","Surgery into healthy pink bowel, often ending in a colostomy.","Treat the cause. Trend the lactate.","None taught.","Bowel obstruction and paralytic ileus — Mechanical SBO: a tumor, adhesion or hernia. Large bowel: usually a neoplasm. Paralytic ileus, common in the ICU: opioids and anesthesia, GI surgery, ischemia or shock, peritonitis. — Distension: the …","Mechanical SBO: a tumor, adhesion or hernia. Large bowel: usually a neoplasm.","Paralytic ileus, common in the ICU: opioids and anesthesia, GI surgery, ischemia or shock, peritonitis.","Distension: the hallmark. Cramping periumbilical pain in waves.","Large-volume bilious vomiting. No flatus.","Bowel sounds: high-pitched and tinkling in a mechanical SBO, absent to hypoactive in an ileus.","X-ray (KUB) or CT: dilated loops of small bowel, a collapsed colon.","Electrolytes: the small bowel absorbs nutrients and vitamins.","Dysfunctional GI motility: peristalsis is lost or blocked.","Deficient fluid volume: vomiting, and no PO intake.","Partial to complete: complete is more severe.","A severe ileus or obstruction can cause abdominal compartment syndrome.","NPO, fluid resuscitation, an NG tube, electrolytes, metoclopramide. Usually surgery for a mechanical SBO.","Avoid opioids and anesthesia when possible.","Ileus: moving, an aggressive bowel regimen, acetaminophen or NSAIDs for pain.","Abdominal compartment syndrome — Massive fluid resuscitation: trauma, shock. Third spacing: liver disease, heart failure, pancreatitis. Abdominal surgery, a severe ileus, ischemic bowel, a ruptured aortic aneurysm. — Hugely distended, possi…","Massive fluid resuscitation: trauma, shock.","Third spacing: liver disease, heart failure, pancreatitis.","Abdominal surgery, a severe ileus, ischemic bowel, a ruptured aortic aneurysm.","Hugely distended, possibly rigid: hard from the fluid.","Organs lose their blood flow as the pressure rises.","Bladder pressure gives the IAP: 16 to 20 mmHg organ ischemia, above 20 multiple organ dysfunction.","Labs: none taught.","Ineffective tissue perfusion: fluid presses on the abdominal organs.","Acute pain: very painful, and pain raises the abdominal pressure.","IAP 16 to 20 mmHg: organ ischemia. Above 20: multiple organ dysfunction.","HOB completely flat: even reverse Trendelenburg raises the pressure.","Sedation and paralytics: pain and fighting the ventilator raise the pressure.","Surgical decompression: the abdomen left open. Antibiotics.","Multiple organ dysfunction: impaired blood flow to multiple organs.","An open abdomen: peritonitis, sepsis, septic shock, as bacteria translocate out of the gut."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"pan-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Linda Morales is a 56-year-old Hispanic woman brought to the ED by her husband at 0300. Yesterday at 1600 she felt pain in her upper middle abdomen. It grew severe over the evening and now bores through to her back. It is constant, dul…","PMH: Gallstones with biliary colic, 2 years, waiting for elective gallbladder surgery. Type 2 diabetes, 9 years. Hypertriglyceridemia, 6 years. Hypertension, 12 years: her BP usually runs in the 140s over 80s.","SH: Married, lives with her husband in a one-story house. School bus driver. Never smoked. A glass of wine on holidays only. No drug use.","FH: Mother had gallstones and type 2 diabetes. Father died of an MI at 70.","Admission VS: BP 100/62, HR 124 (sinus tachycardia), RR 24, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 38.2 °C (100.8 °F), pain 9/10.","Home medications:","Metformin, for diabetes","Lisinopril, for hypertension","Fenofibrate, for high triglycerides","Acetaminophen, for gallstone pain","Allergies: Latex (hives).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 56-year-old woman lying on her left side with her knees drawn up, arms guarding her abdomen. Grimacing and restless, unable to get comfortable. Speaks in full sentences between waves of nausea. Face flushed, lips dry and cra…","Pain: 9/10 constant, dull, aching pain in the epigastrium, radiating to her back, worse lying supine, since 1600 yesterday.","Gastrointestinal: Abdomen distended, with guarding. Tender to light palpation across the upper abdomen. Bowel sounds hypoactive in all 4 quadrants. No bruising around the umbilicus or on the flanks. Sclera white, skin not jaundiced. Vomited…","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and regular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 124. Neck veins flat at 45 degrees. No edema of the hands, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial and pedal pulses 1+ and weak…","Respiratory: RR 24, shallow: she splints her breathing against the pain. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds clear in the anterior and lateral fields, diminished in the posterior bases bilaterally. No cough.","Labs: Lipase 2,850 U/L and amylase 1,120 U/L, both elevated. BMP: sodium 135, potassium 3.4, chloride 97, CO2 21, BUN 30, creatinine 1.2, glucose 338, calcium 8.6.","Procedure: NG tube placed in the ED at 0400, to low wall suction: 250 mL of green fluid in the first hour."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"pan-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Deficient fluid volume — Unstable blood glucose","Deficient fluid volume","Unstable blood glucose","Supporting cues — BP 100/62, down from her usual 140s, with HR 124: tachycardia compensating for too little volume in her vessels. Vomited 8 times, nothing kept down since 1600: fluid lost, and none taken in. Radial pulses 1+ and weak, capi…","BP 100/62, down from her usual 140s, with HR 124: tachycardia compensating for too little volume in her vessels.","Vomited 8 times, nothing kept down since 1600: fluid lost, and none taken in.","Radial pulses 1+ and weak, capillary refill 4 seconds, dry and sticky mucous membranes.","BUN 30 and creatinine 1.2: her kidneys are getting less blood.","Glucose 338: hyperglycemia.","Type 2 diabetes for 9 years: her glucose was already hard to control.","Lipase 2,850 with fever and tachycardia: a severe case, which can swing her glucose low instead.","Relevance to the diagnosis — Biliary disease is one of the two most common causes of acute pancreatitis. Her pancreatic enzymes leaked into the pancreas's own tissue, and it began digesting itself. The dying cells set off the inflammatory r…","Biliary disease is one of the two most common causes of acute pancreatitis. Her pancreatic enzymes leaked into the pancreas's own tissue, and it began digesting itself. The dying cells set off the inflammatory response, and fluid is collect…","Pancreatitis can cause hyperglycemia, and her diabetes already makes her glucose hard to control. The pancreas makes insulin, and its inflamed, dying cells release less of it. Severe enough, pancreatitis can cause hypoglycemia instead, so h…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. The fluid keeps shifting, and hypovolemic shock is the primary cause of death. She is alert, compensating, and her systolic BP is above 90: she is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. A glucose this …","2, urgent. The fluid keeps shifting, and hypovolemic shock is the primary cause of death. She is alert, compensating, and her systolic BP is above 90: she is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. A glucose this high, or a swing low, must be caught quickly. She is alert and talking, with no sign of a low glucose yet."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"pan-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","Early the Next Morning","Mrs. Morales came to the ICU at 0600 for close hemodynamic monitoring. She was kept NPO, her NG tube to low wall suction, and given LR, to replace her volume, an opioid, for her pain, and an insulin drip, for her glucose. A Foley was placed…","Updated VS: BP 82/50, HR 138 (sinus tachycardia), RR 28, SpO2 93% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), pain 7/10.","Lab: Calcium 7.1, down from 8.6 on admission."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"pan-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Casey Nguyen, RN in the ICU. I'm calling about Linda Morales in room 12, admitted yesterday at 0600 with severe acute pancreatitis from gallstones. I'm calling because her blood pressure is dropping and her urine output has fall…","B — She is 56, with gallstones, type 2 diabetes, hypertriglyceridemia and hypertension, and her BP usually runs in the 140s. She is NPO with her NG tube to suction, on LR and an insulin drip. Since 0400 she has been restless. Her BP is 82/5…","A — I'm worried she is going into hypovolemic shock as fluid keeps shifting out of her vessels.","R — I recommend albumin, a colloid, to pull volume back into her vessels, since the LR is not holding her pressure. I'd like her calcium replaced now: it is falling, a sign of severe pancreatitis. And I'd like to measure her bladder pressur…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"pan-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: deficient fluid volume. She is going into hypovolemic shock, the primary cause of death in acute pancreatitis. The fluid leaving her vessels has left her kidneys (15 mL/hr) and her brain (the restlessness) short of blood. …","Independent — Bed rest. Cluster care, a quiet room, and stay with her, reorienting her each time she tries to get up. — Movement, pain and anxiety all raise the body's oxygen consumption, which a body short of volume cannot deliver. — Resti…","Dependent — Albumin per order, a colloid, to restore her volume. — Pulls volume back into the vascular system from the tissues, where the LR she was given leaks out. — Systolic BP 90 or more, and urine output 30 mL/hr or more, within 2 hour…","Interdisciplinary — The surgical team: her bladder pressure measured every 4 hours, with surgical decompression if it climbs. — Fluid shifting into the abdominal compartment raises the pressure on her organs. At 16 to 20 mmHg, their blood f…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"pan-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Acute respiratory distress syndrome, ARDS (respiratory). The morning of her third day in the ICU.","Why it could occur — Pancreatitis is one of the biggest risk factors for ARDS, through its systemic inflammatory response. Inflammatory mediators in her bloodstream injure the vessels of other organs, and in her lungs they damage the alveol…","Priority cues — SpO2 86% on 6 L/min by nasal cannula, up from 2 L yesterday, not rising as the oxygen goes up: refractory hypoxemia. PaO2 52 on the ABG: under 60, hypoxemia. RR 34 and dyspneic: tachypnea. Fine crackles in the posterior and …","Actions — Dependent: a high-flow nasal cannula per order, set up by respiratory therapy, its oxygen turned up toward an SpO2 above 90% and a PaO2 above 60. A repeat ABG in 1 hour. Independent: sit her upright with the airway open, and minim…","Evaluation — SpO2: above 90% on the high-flow cannula, with a PaO2 above 60. An SpO2 that will not rise means she will likely need intubation."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"gib-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Harold Jensen is a 74-year-old white man brought to the ED by his wife at 1100, after he fainted in the bathroom at 1030. For 2 days his stools have been black, tarry and foul-smelling. This morning he vomited bright red blood twice, a…","PMH: Atrial fibrillation, 5 years, on an anticoagulant. Heart failure, 3 years, ejection fraction 40% a year ago. Duodenal ulcer, 6 years ago, with H. pylori, treated. Osteoarthritis of both knees, 10 years. Hypertension, 15 years.","SH: Married, lives with his wife in a two-story house. Retired mail carrier. Former smoker, quit 20 years ago. One or two beers on weekends. No drug use.","FH: Brother had stomach ulcers. Father died of heart failure at 79.","Admission VS: BP 96/58, HR 118 (atrial fibrillation), RR 24, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 36.6 °C (97.9 °F), pain 4/10.","Home medications:","Apixaban, an anticoagulant, for atrial fibrillation","Metoprolol, for atrial fibrillation and heart failure","Furosemide, a diuretic, for heart failure","Lisinopril, for heart failure and hypertension","Ibuprofen, an NSAID, over the counter, for knee pain","Allergies: Penicillin (rash).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 74-year-old man lying flat on the stretcher, pale, dried blood at the corners of his mouth. Restless, picking at his sheets. Anxious: \"Am I bleeding inside?\" Speaks in full sentences, slowly. Clean-shaven, in a hospital gown…","Pain: 4/10 tightness in the center of his chest since he fainted, worse when he sits up. No abdominal pain now.","Gastrointestinal: Abdomen soft and not distended, mildly tender in the epigastrium, no guarding or rigidity. Bowel sounds hyperactive in all 4 quadrants. Passed 300 mL of black, tarry, foul-smelling stool in the ED.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and irregularly irregular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: atrial fibrillation at 118. Neck veins flat at 45 degrees. Trace edema of both ankles, none in the hands, legs or feet. Radial and peda…","Respiratory: RR 24, unlabored at rest. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds clear in the anterior, posterior and lateral fields bilaterally. No cough.","Labs: CBC: hemoglobin 7.8 (13.5 at his last checkup), hematocrit 25%, platelets 210,000. BMP: sodium 138, potassium 4.6, chloride 102, CO2 22, BUN 52, creatinine 1.0, glucose 128.","Procedure: NG tube to low wall suction, with gastric lavage: bright red blood that clears to pink after 1 L."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"gib-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Deficient fluid volume — Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion","Deficient fluid volume","Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion","Supporting cues — Bright red hematemesis and a bright red lavage: actively bleeding. Melena for 2 days: older blood too. HR 118, the first sign, compensating. Then BP 96/58. Pale, cool and clammy, capillary refill 4 seconds, weak pulses: ge…","Bright red hematemesis and a bright red lavage: actively bleeding. Melena for 2 days: older blood too.","HR 118, the first sign, compensating. Then BP 96/58.","Pale, cool and clammy, capillary refill 4 seconds, weak pulses: getting paler.","Hemoglobin 7.8, down from 13.5. BUN 52 with creatinine 1.0: blood metabolized in his GI tract, not a kidney problem.","Chest tightness 4/10 since he fainted, worse when he sits up: angina.","Hemoglobin 7.8: too few red cells to carry oxygen.","HR 118 in atrial fibrillation: a faster heart needs more oxygen.","Short of breath walking to the bathroom, SpO2 93%, fainted at home.","Relevance to the diagnosis — A past duodenal ulcer puts him at very high risk of another, and 3 weeks of ibuprofen, an NSAID, is the most common drug cause. His apixaban vastly increases the risk of bleeding. A new ulcer is bleeding into hi…","A past duodenal ulcer puts him at very high risk of another, and 3 weeks of ibuprofen, an NSAID, is the most common drug cause. His apixaban vastly increases the risk of bleeding. A new ulcer is bleeding into his duodenum and stomach: he vo…","An acute GI bleed looks like anemia: weakness, shortness of breath, angina, syncope. With so little hemoglobin, his blood carries too little oxygen to his tissues, his own myocardium included. His heart, already weakened by heart failure, b…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. He is actively bleeding and could go into hypovolemic shock. He is alert and his systolic BP is in the 90s: he is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. His heart muscle is short of oxygen, and it will…","2, urgent. He is actively bleeding and could go into hypovolemic shock. He is alert and his systolic BP is in the 90s: he is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. His heart muscle is short of oxygen, and it will worsen if the bleeding continues. The pain is moderate and his rhythm is his usual one."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"gib-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","Early the Next Morning","In the ED Mr. Jensen was given 2 units of packed red blood cells, normal saline, and a continuous pantoprazole drip, a PPI, for his ulcer. He came to the ICU at 1300. At 1600, once he was hemodynamically stable, an endoscopy at the bedside …","Updated VS: BP 84/50, HR 132 (atrial fibrillation), RR 28, SpO2 91% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 36.4 °C (97.5 °F), pain 5/10.","Lab: Hemoglobin 6.6, down from 9.1 at 2200."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"gib-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Casey Nguyen, RN in the ICU. I'm calling about Harold Jensen in room 8, admitted yesterday at 1300 with an upper GI bleed from a duodenal ulcer, clipped at 1600. I'm calling because he is bleeding again.","B — He is 74, with atrial fibrillation on apixaban at home, heart failure with an ejection fraction of 40%, hypertension, a duodenal ulcer 6 years ago, and 3 weeks of ibuprofen. He had 2 units of packed cells yesterday and is on a pantopraz…","A — I think his ulcer is rebleeding massively, despite the clip.","R — I recommend 2 units of packed red cells now, guided by his hemoglobin, to replace the blood and its oxygen-carrying capacity. I'd like a clotting profile, since a bleed this big uses up clotting factors and he may need FFP. And I'd ask …"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"gib-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: deficient fluid volume. He is bleeding faster than he is being replaced. The lost volume and hemoglobin are behind his low BP, his chest tightness and his restlessness, and none of them improves until the bleeding stops.","Independent — Bed rest, no getting up. Cluster care, keep him calm, and stay with him. — Movement and anxiety raise the body's oxygen consumption, while his blood carries too little oxygen to meet it. — Chest tightness 3/10 or less, and res…","Dependent — 2 units of packed red blood cells per order. — Replaces the lost blood and its oxygen-carrying capacity. Guided by the hemoglobin, since the hematocrit can lag for hours. — Hemoglobin 7.5 or more and systolic BP 90 or more withi…","Interdisciplinary — GI and surgery: surgery to remove the bleeding ulcer. — A massive rebleed after an endoclip usually needs surgery to stop it. Nothing else holds his volume until it stops. — No new blood, and a hemoglobin stable over 6 h…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"gib-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Transfusion-associated circulatory overload, TACO (cardiovascular and respiratory). At 0600 on his third day, an hour into a unit of packed cells ordered for a hemoglobin of 7.0.","Why it could occur — In 2 days he has had 7 units of packed cells, FFP and liters of saline. TACO is too much blood volume for the patient to handle. His heart, weakened by heart failure, cannot pump the excess forward, and it backs up into…","Priority cues — A new cough, and short of breath at rest. RR 30, SpO2 89% on 2 L. Crackles in the posterior bases bilaterally, clear yesterday. Neck veins distended at 45 degrees. BP 166/90, up from 118/70, HR 112. A new headache.","Actions — Independent: stop the transfusion, keep the line open, and sit him upright. Report it to the provider at once. Dependent: furosemide per order, to pull off the excess fluid and ease his heart's workload, oxygen turned up to an SpO…","Evaluation — Crackles: clearing from the bases, with the SpO2 back above 92% on 2 L."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"hep-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Andre Thompson is a 52-year-old Black man brought to the ED by his sister at 1500. Over 2 days he has grown confused and sleepy: he forgets where he is, mixes up the days, and snaps at her, and this morning he could not finish a senten…","PMH: Cirrhosis (chronic liver disease), 6 years, with ascites. Esophageal varices, banded 2 years ago. Hepatic encephalopathy, one admission a year ago, on lactulose since. Type 2 diabetes, 8 years.","SH: Divorced, lives with his sister. Warehouse supervisor. Sober 3 years until Saturday. Never smoked. No drug use.","FH: Mother had type 2 diabetes. Father died of colon cancer at 64.","Admission VS: BP 104/62, HR 108 (sinus tachycardia), RR 20, SpO2 96% on room air, temperature 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), pain: denies.","Home medications:","Lactulose, for hepatic encephalopathy","Spironolactone, a diuretic, for ascites","Furosemide, a diuretic, for ascites","Insulin glargine, for diabetes","Allergies: Ciprofloxacin (hives).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 52-year-old man lying in bed, drowsy, opening his eyes to voice and drifting off mid-answer. Irritable, pulling at his IV. Speech slow. Skin and sclera yellow. Scattered bruises on both forearms, and his gums ooze. Unshaven,…","Pain: denies pain when asked. No grimacing or guarding.","Neurologic: GCS 13: opens eyes to voice (3), confused (4), obeys commands (6). Oriented to himself only. Pupils 3 mm, equal and briskly reactive to light. Moves all 4 extremities, grips equal and strong. Asterixis: his hands flap when held …","Gastrointestinal: Abdomen distended with ascites, girth 112 cm, not tender. Spider angiomas on his chest, and dilated veins on his abdomen (caput medusae). Bowel sounds hypoactive in all 4 quadrants. No bowel movement for 3 days. Nauseated.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, rapid and regular. No murmur, gallop or rub. Telemetry: sinus tachycardia at 108. No JVD at 45 degrees. 2+ pitting edema of both ankles and feet, none in the hands. Radial and pedal pulses 2+ bilaterally. …","Labs: Ammonia 168 mcg/dL, high. Coagulation: INR 2.6, platelets 58,000.","Diagnostic: CT of the head: no bleeding, and no cerebral edema."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"hep-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Acute confusion — Bleeding","Acute confusion","Bleeding","Supporting cues — GCS 13, oriented to himself only, drowsy and irritable: a decreased level of consciousness. Asterixis: his hands flap when held out. Ammonia 168: hepatic encephalopathy, and above 150, a raised risk of cerebral edema. No l…","GCS 13, oriented to himself only, drowsy and irritable: a decreased level of consciousness.","Asterixis: his hands flap when held out.","Ammonia 168: hepatic encephalopathy, and above 150, a raised risk of cerebral edema.","No lactulose and no bowel movement for 3 days: the ammonia stays in his gut.","INR 2.6: above 1.5, his blood is slow to clot.","Platelets 58,000: thrombocytopenia, under 150,000.","New bruises on his arms, and his gums ooze.","Esophageal varices, banded 2 years ago.","Relevance to the diagnosis — His liver, already scarred by cirrhosis, was hit by a binge and by days of acetaminophen, the biggest cause of acute liver failure, especially with binge drinking. A failing liver cannot detoxify. Toxins such as…","His liver, already scarred by cirrhosis, was hit by a binge and by days of acetaminophen, the biggest cause of acute liver failure, especially with binge drinking. A failing liver cannot detoxify. Toxins such as ammonia build up, cross the …","The liver makes clotting factors II, V, VII, IX and X. His failing liver makes too few, so his bleeding times are prolonged, and his platelets are low. His body cannot correct a bleed on its own. His portal hypertension has engorged the vei…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. His ammonia is above 150 and his level of consciousness is falling, so it must be brought down quickly. At GCS 13 he still protects his airway: he is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. He is oozing…","2, urgent. His ammonia is above 150 and his level of consciousness is falling, so it must be brought down quickly. At GCS 13 he still protects his airway: he is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. He is oozing, not bleeding briskly, and his vital signs are holding. A variceal bleed could come at any time."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"hep-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Night","Mr. Thompson came to the ICU at 1700. He was given lactulose, to clear the ammonia through his bowels, and vitamin K, so his liver can make clotting factors. He had one loose bowel movement. At 0200 he retches and vomits about 500 mL of bri…","Updated VS: BP 82/48, HR 128 (sinus tachycardia), RR 26, SpO2 92% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 38.2 °C (100.8 °F). Pain: unable to rate, grimaces when his abdomen is touched. GCS 10: opens eyes to pain (2), inappropriate words (…","Lab: Hemoglobin 7.2, down from 10.4 on admission."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"hep-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Casey Nguyen, RN in the ICU. I'm calling about Andre Thompson in room 4, admitted yesterday at 1700 with acute liver failure on cirrhosis, with hepatic encephalopathy. I'm calling because he is vomiting bright red blood.","B — He is 52, with cirrhosis and ascites, esophageal varices banded 2 years ago, past hepatic encephalopathy and type 2 diabetes. He binge drank on Saturday and took acetaminophen for days after. On admission his INR was 2.6 and his platele…","A — I think his varices are bleeding, and with a GCS of 10 I'm worried he cannot protect his airway.","R — I recommend intubating him now to protect his airway, before he aspirates blood. I'd like an octreotide drip, to reduce his portal pressure and slow the bleeding. I'd like packed red cells and FFP, since he is actively bleeding with an …"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"hep-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: deficient fluid volume. His airway, the first priority in a variceal bleed, was secured when he was intubated at 0230. Hemodynamic stability comes next. A variceal bleed quickly brings hypovolemic shock, and with too few c…","Independent — A second large-bore IV, 18-gauge, so blood products and fluid can run at the same time. — A variceal bleed usually needs multiple transfusions and fluid resuscitation, given fast. — Two 18-gauge IVs running, and packed cells a…","Dependent — An octreotide drip per order, to slow the bleeding. — Decreases splanchnic blood flow and reduces the portal pressure that swells the varices, so they bleed less. Very safe, given to stabilize before definitive treatment. — No n…","Interdisciplinary — GI: an endoscopy at the bedside with band ligation. — Endoscopy shows what the varices are doing, and the bands close off the bleeding veins. — Bleeding stopped, with a stable hemoglobin, within 6 hours. — Met. At 0600, …"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"hep-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Acute kidney injury (renal). The next morning, a day after the bleed.","Why it could occur — Pre-renal: for hours during the bleed his BP was low, and the kidneys take 20 to 25% of cardiac output. AKI is also a complication of acute liver failure itself. His low albumin lets fluid third space into his abdomen i…","Priority cues — Urine output 12 mL/hr for 6 hours: oliguria, under 30 mL/hr. Creatinine 2.4, up from 0.9 on admission: the cue to trust, since blood in his gut also raises his BUN. Potassium 5.5: his kidneys are not excreting it.","Actions — Dependent: IV albumin per order, a colloid, to raise his oncotic pressure and pull fluid back into his vessels, where it can perfuse his kidneys. Independent: strict intake and output, the urine output every hour, his weight daily…","Evaluation — Urine output: back to 30 mL/hr or more. Then the creatinine, trending down.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"signature-neuro.html","t":"Neuro","secs":[{"s":"Neuro","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 6"]},{"s":"The Neuro Diagnoses","a":"compare-diagnoses","k":["The Neuro Diagnoses","TBI with increased ICP — An external impact to the head. Falls the leading cause, then struck by or against, motor vehicle crashes, assaults. Highest risk: males, children under 4, young adults 15 to 19. — If awake: a significant headache, …","An external impact to the head. Falls the leading cause, then struck by or against, motor vehicle crashes, assaults.","Highest risk: males, children under 4, young adults 15 to 19.","If awake: a significant headache, vomiting, blurred vision, lethargy, changes in behavior.","A falling GCS. One pupil a different size: pressure on one side of the brain.","Late: Cushing's triad, bradycardia, irregular respirations and a widened pulse pressure.","ICP above 20 on an ICP monitor, usually placed for a GCS of 3 to 8. CPP = MAP − ICP, the goal above 70.","Urine output and sodium, for DI, SIADH and cerebral salt wasting.","PaO2: below 50 increases ICP.","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion: swelling in a skull that cannot give raises the ICP and lowers the CPP.","Ineffective airway clearance: a falling GCS. Can the patient maintain the airway?","Seizures: a higher risk with any TBI.","Secondary injury: ischemia, inflammation, swelling, hematomas. Decreased cerebral perfusion is the primary driver.","Herniation: downward the most damaging. Recovery very unlikely. Then brain death.","Airway and oxygenation: the priority.","ICP below 20, CPP above 70: head of the bed at least 30°, head midline, no fever, mannitol, sedation.","Surgery: burr holes for a subdural hematoma, a craniectomy.","Endocrine: DI, SIADH, cerebral salt wasting. The pituitary is damaged. Monitor urine output.","Hypermetabolism: higher nutritional needs.","Stroke — Ischemic, 87%: large artery atherosclerosis, the biggest risk factor. Cardioembolic, as from AFib. Lacunar. Hemorrhagic: uncontrolled hypertension the most common cause. A ruptured aneurysm, an AV malformation. A TIA before: a warn…","Ischemic, 87%: large artery atherosclerosis, the biggest risk factor. Cardioembolic, as from AFib. Lacunar.","Hemorrhagic: uncontrolled hypertension the most common cause. A ruptured aneurysm, an AV malformation.","A TIA before: a warning.","Sudden weakness or numbness of one side, on the side opposite the stroke. Slurred speech, a visual disturbance, dizziness.","A sudden-onset severe headache: \"worst headache of my life\".","The time of onset: critical information.","A stat CT without contrast: hemorrhagic or ischemic, which decides what is done next.","The NIHSS: the higher the score, the more severe the stroke. Trended with the GCS.","Blood glucose: hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion: brain cells survive 3 to 4 minutes without oxygen. The penumbra can still be saved.","Aspiration: swallowing and the gag reflex impaired. A suspected stroke is NPO.","Bleeding after tPA.","Many worsen in the first 24 to 48 hours.","The dying cells, and secondary cerebral edema. A severe stroke raises the ICP.","Hemorrhagic: a very poor prognosis.","Ischemic: bust the clot. tPA first-line, within 4.5 hours of onset. A thrombectomy through interventional radiology.","Hemorrhagic: BP control and surgery.","Once stable: anticoagulants, antiplatelets, statins.","Bleeding after tPA: anyone who gets it is at high risk.","Aspiration pneumonia: impaired swallowing and a decreased level of consciousness.","Airway, breathing and circulation, in a severe stroke.","Spinal cord injury — Most common at ages 16 to 30. Motor vehicle accidents, falls, violence, sports, diving. Often with a TBI. — Spinal shock: complete loss of motor, sensory and reflex activity below the injury. Neurogenic shock: hypotensi…","Most common at ages 16 to 30. Motor vehicle accidents, falls, violence, sports, diving.","Often with a TBI.","Spinal shock: complete loss of motor, sensory and reflex activity below the injury.","Neurogenic shock: hypotension, bradycardia, hypothermia. More likely at T6 or higher.","The level decides the loss: C4 and C6 tetraplegia, T6 and L1 paraplegia.","None taught.","Ineffective breathing pattern: a cervical or thoracic injury can affect the diaphragm.","Ineffective tissue perfusion: neurogenic shock lowers the BP, and the cord needs a MAP of 85 to 90.","Pressure ulcers: no movement or sensation, impaired circulation.","Cord edema can ascend or descend, then microscopic hemorrhages, ischemia and axonal degeneration: a permanent loss.","Neurogenic shock. The permanence is not known until spinal shock resolves.","Airway first: a paralyzed diaphragm needs intubation.","MAP 85 to 90 for about 7 days: no systolic hypotension.","Immobilized until cleared. Unstable fracture: surgery within 24 hours.","GI: paralytic ileus. GU: urinary retention. Spinal shock leaves the bowel and bladder with atony.","DVT and PE: venous stasis. Pressure ulcers.","Later, at T6 or above: autonomic dysreflexia, an elevated, uncontrolled BP.","Meningitis — A nearby local infection: an open skull fracture, an abscess on the skull or in the brain, an eye abscess or infection. A basilar skull fracture leads to it more quickly. — Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days. F…","A nearby local infection: an open skull fracture, an abscess on the skull or in the brain, an eye abscess or infection.","A basilar skull fracture leads to it more quickly.","Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days.","Fever, a throbbing headache, nuchal rigidity.","Spinal tap: WBCs in the CSF indicate bacterial meningitis. There should be none.","Hyperthermia: infection of the meninges and the CSF.","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion: the inflammation can cause cerebral edema and increased ICP.","Cerebral edema and increased ICP from the inflammation.","Bacterial: a very high risk of neurologic problems.","Bacterial: IV antibiotics STAT, a neurological emergency.","Droplet precautions, seizure precautions, temperature management, ICP monitoring.","Seizures — A complication of TBI, stroke, really any brain damage. After a TBI, blood on the brain irritates the tissue. — A decreased neurologic status with no clear cause. Status epilepticus: a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or repeated s…","A complication of TBI, stroke, really any brain damage. After a TBI, blood on the brain irritates the tissue.","A decreased neurologic status with no clear cause.","Status epilepticus: a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures over 30 minutes.","EEG monitoring, continuous or a snapshot: brain activity and seizure activity.","Labs: none taught.","Injury: seizure precautions, nothing forced into the mouth, no restraint.","Ineffective airway clearance: maintain the airway, during and after the seizure.","Seizures greatly increase ICP.","Status epilepticus: a medical emergency. Over 10 minutes can cause death.","Seizure precautions: suction, oxygen, padded rails, an Ambu bag.","IV benzodiazepines for status epilepticus: lorazepam or diazepam. IV access kept.","Levetiracetam: continuously, preventatively, or as treatment."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"tbi-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Luis Ortega is a 44-year-old Hispanic man brought to the ED by ambulance at 1640. At 1600 he was on a ladder cleaning his gutters when his wife saw him stiffen and jerk for about a minute, then fall about 10 feet onto the concrete driv…","PMH: Epilepsy (a seizure disorder) since age 24, controlled on medication. Hypertension, 5 years. GERD, 3 years.","SH: Married, two teenage children, lives in a two-story house. Electrician. Never smoked. One or two beers on weekends. No drug use.","FH: Father has hypertension. Mother has type 2 diabetes.","Admission VS: BP 156/88, HR 66 (normal sinus rhythm), RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 37.2 °C (99.0 °F), pain 6/10.","Home medications:","Levetiracetam, for seizures","Lisinopril, for hypertension","Pantoprazole, for GERD","Allergies: Codeine (nausea and vomiting).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 44-year-old man lying on the stretcher with his eyes closed. Opens his eyes when his name is said, then drifts off. Confused: knows his name, not where he is or the date. Restless, pulling at his blanket. A 4 cm laceration a…","Pain: 6/10 pounding headache at the back of his head and behind his eyes, constant since the fall.","Neurologic: GCS 12: eyes open to speech (3), confused (4), localizes pain (5). Pushes the examiner's hand away from a nail bed squeeze, but does not follow commands. Pupils equal, 3 mm, round and brisk to light. Moves all four limbs, streng…","Respiratory: RR 18, regular and unlabored. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds clear in the anterior, posterior and lateral fields bilaterally. Infrequent, nonproductive cough.","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present and regular, no murmur. Monitor: normal sinus rhythm at 66. No JVD at 30 degrees. No edema of the hands, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial and pedal pulses 2+ and equal bilaterally. Capillary refill …","Labs: BMP: sodium 139, potassium 4.0, chloride 103, CO2 25, BUN 14, creatinine 0.9, glucose 108. Serum osmolality 286, normal (270 to 290).","Diagnostic: CT of the head: a small subdural hematoma over the right side of the brain, and a contusion at the front, opposite the impact. Mild cerebral edema. No skull fracture."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"tbi-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion — Seizures","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion","Seizures","Supporting cues — GCS 12, down from his normal 15: opens his eyes to speech, confused, localizes but does not obey. A pounding headache, 6/10, and vomiting twice: signs of increased ICP in an awake patient. CT: a subdural hematoma, a contus…","GCS 12, down from his normal 15: opens his eyes to speech, confused, localizes but does not obey.","A pounding headache, 6/10, and vomiting twice: signs of increased ICP in an awake patient.","CT: a subdural hematoma, a contusion and mild cerebral edema, more volume inside the skull.","BP 156/88: the systolic still below 160.","Epilepsy for 20 years, and a seizure today despite his levetiracetam.","The seizure his wife saw: a minute of stiffening and jerking, a bitten tongue, incontinence.","Blood on his brain from the subdural hematoma.","Relevance to the diagnosis — His fall was an acceleration and deceleration injury. The back of his head struck the concrete, and his brain rebounded into the front of his skull: two impacts, two areas of the brain. The contusion is the prim…","His fall was an acceleration and deceleration injury. The back of his head struck the concrete, and his brain rebounded into the front of his skull: two impacts, two areas of the brain. The contusion is the primary injury. The hematoma and …","His seizure disorder already makes him prone to seizures, and a seizure caused today's fall. Any TBI raises the risk further: blood on the brain irritates the tissue. A seizure now would greatly increase his ICP and his brain's metabolic de…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. His ICP is rising and needs neuro checks every hour. His pupils are equal and brisk, he localizes, and his vital signs show no Cushing's triad: he is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. A seizure wo…","2, urgent. His ICP is rising and needs neuro checks every hour. His pupils are equal and brisk, he localizes, and his vital signs show no Cushing's triad: he is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. A seizure would raise his ICP and threaten his airway, so the precautions must be in place quickly. He has not seized since the fall."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"tbi-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","Early the Next Morning","Mr. Ortega came to the neuro ICU at 1900. He was kept with the head of the bed at 30 degrees and his head midline, with neuro checks every hour. He was placed on seizure precautions, with suction, oxygen, padded rails and an Ambu bag in the…","Updated VS: BP 184/74, HR 52 (sinus bradycardia), RR 10 and irregular, SpO2 92% on room air, temperature 37.4 °C (99.3 °F), pain unable to rate: grimaces to a nail bed squeeze. GCS 7.","Lab: ABG on room air: pH 7.31, PaCO2 52, HCO3 25, PaO2 64."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"tbi-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Riley Morgan, RN in the neuro ICU. I'm calling about Luis Ortega in room 4, admitted last night at 1900 with a traumatic brain injury. I'm calling because his neuro status has suddenly worsened and his right pupil is dilated.","B — He is 44, with epilepsy, hypertension and GERD. Yesterday at 1600 he had a seizure on a ladder and fell 10 feet onto the back of his head. He has a small right subdural hematoma, a frontal contusion and mild cerebral edema. At 1900 his …","A — I think his hematoma is growing and his ICP is rising fast, and I'm worried he is starting to herniate.","R — I recommend intubation now: with a GCS of 7 he cannot protect his airway, and his low oxygen and rising CO2 are raising his ICP further. I recommend mannitol now, an osmotic diuretic that works in minutes to lower the ICP. And I'd ask n…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"tbi-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion. The blown pupil, the posturing and Cushing's triad say his ICP is pressing on his brain, and herniation may be near. Once the ICP rises above the MAP, his brain receives no oxygen. He…","Independent — After surgery: the head of the bed at 30 degrees, his head neutral and midline, propped so it does not sink forward. Care clustered, the room quiet, and each turn slow and gentle. — The head of the bed at 30 degrees promotes c…","Dependent — Mannitol per order at 0325, to lower the ICP, with the BP watched for hypotension and the electrolytes checked. — An osmotic diuretic, working in minutes: it decreases the cerebral blood volume, and the ICP falls. — Within 30 mi…","Interdisciplinary — Neurosurgery: burr holes to drain the subdural hematoma, and a ventricular drain. — Burr holes for a subdural hematoma decrease the ICP. The ventricular drain drains CSF in a controlled manner and shows the ICP, so his r…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"tbi-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Diabetes insipidus (endocrine). The afternoon of his second day, about 36 hours after the fall.","Why it could occur — ADH is released from the posterior pituitary, in the brain, and head trauma can damage it: the cause of neurogenic DI. With too little ADH, his kidneys reabsorb less water, and he loses pure water. He becomes dehydrated…","Priority cues — Urine output 450, 520 and 480 mL in the last 3 hours, as pale as water. Urine specific gravity 1.002: very dilute. Serum sodium 151, up from 139: hypernatremia. Serum osmolality 309, up from 286. BP 108/62 and HR 106 as his …","Actions — Dependent: desmopressin per order, a synthetic ADH, so his kidneys hold water again, with IV fluids per order to replace what he has lost. Independent: strict intake and output, the urine output and specific gravity every hour, an…","Evaluation — Urine output: falling within hours of the desmopressin, with the specific gravity rising toward 1.010 to 1.030."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"stroke-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Gloria Mitchell is a 66-year-old Black woman brought to the ED by ambulance at 1045. At 1010, at a church luncheon, her friend saw the right side of her face droop. Her speech became slurred, she struggled to find her words, and she dr…","PMH: Hypertension, 18 years: she often skips her medication. Hyperlipidemia, 10 years. Type 2 diabetes, 12 years. A small stroke 3 years ago: she recovered, except that she sometimes coughs when she drinks thin liquids.","SH: Widowed, lives with her sister in a one-story house. Retired postal worker. Smokes half a pack a day, for 45 years. No alcohol or drug use.","FH: Mother died of a stroke at 72. Brother had an MI at 60.","Admission VS: BP 178/96, HR 88 (normal sinus rhythm), RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, temperature 36.9 °C (98.4 °F), pain 0/10.","Home medications:","Amlodipine, for hypertension","Atorvastatin, for hyperlipidemia","Metformin, for diabetes","Aspirin, an antiplatelet, since her first stroke","Allergies: Latex (rash).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 66-year-old woman lying on the stretcher, the right side of her face drooping, saliva running from the right corner of her mouth. Speech slurred, and she struggles to find words, but she understands and follows commands. Anx…","Pain: Denies pain, 0/10. No headache.","Neurologic: GCS 14: eyes open spontaneously (4), confused words (4), obeys commands (6). Right facial droop. Right arm drifts down and cannot be raised against gravity. Right leg weak, right hand grasp weak. Left arm and leg strong. Sensati…","Respiratory: RR 18, regular and unlabored. No use of the neck or thoracic accessory muscles. Breath sounds clear in the anterior, posterior and lateral fields bilaterally. Infrequent, weak, nonproductive cough. Voice wet and gurgling after …","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present and regular, no murmur. Monitor: normal sinus rhythm at 88. No JVD at 45 degrees. No edema of the hands, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial and pedal pulses 2+ and equal bilaterally. Capillary refill …","Labs: Blood glucose 186: high, and not hypoglycemia, which can mimic a stroke. BMP: sodium 140, potassium 4.1, chloride 102, CO2 24, BUN 16, creatinine 0.9.","Diagnostic: CT of the head without contrast at 1055: no bleeding."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"stroke-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion — Aspiration","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion","Aspiration","Supporting cues — Sudden right facial droop, right arm and leg weakness, slurred speech and trouble finding words: one side of the body, from the left side of the brain. NIHSS 9: a moderate stroke. CT without contrast: no bleeding, so ische…","Sudden right facial droop, right arm and leg weakness, slurred speech and trouble finding words: one side of the body, from the left side of the brain.","NIHSS 9: a moderate stroke.","CT without contrast: no bleeding, so ischemic.","Onset at 1010, a clear time: inside the window for tPA.","Her right hand numb for 20 minutes 2 weeks ago: a TIA, the warning.","Saliva running from the right corner of her mouth, and a weak gag reflex.","A wet, gurgling voice after swallowing her saliva, with a weak cough.","Slurred speech: the muscles of her mouth and throat are weak.","Coughing on thin liquids since her first stroke.","Relevance to the diagnosis — Eighteen years of poorly controlled hypertension, with hyperlipidemia, diabetes and 45 years of smoking, built atherosclerotic plaque in the large arteries of her head and neck: the biggest risk factor for an is…","Eighteen years of poorly controlled hypertension, with hyperlipidemia, diabetes and 45 years of smoking, built atherosclerotic plaque in the large arteries of her head and neck: the biggest risk factor for an ischemic stroke. Today a clot f…","A stroke impairs motor function, swallowing and the gag reflex included, and her first stroke had already left her swallow weak. Saliva or fluid can slip past it into her trachea and lungs, and aspiration may be silent, with no cough at all…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. Time is brain cells: every minute without blood flow loses more of the penumbra, so she needs tPA quickly. Her airway, breathing and circulation are stable, and she is not expected to die within the hour. — 2, ur…","2, urgent. Time is brain cells: every minute without blood flow loses more of the penumbra, so she needs tPA quickly. Her airway, breathing and circulation are stable, and she is not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. One unsafe sip could become an aspiration pneumonia, so she must stay NPO. She is alert and her lungs are clear: no harm yet, and nothing life-threatening within the hour."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"stroke-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Night","In the ED Mrs. Mitchell was given tPA (alteplase) at 1125, to bust the clot. She came to the neuro ICU at 1300, NPO, with IV fluid at a slow rate and the head of the bed up. By 2000 she could lift her right arm a little, and her NIHSS was 7…","Updated VS: BP 168/90, HR 94 (normal sinus rhythm), RR 20, SpO2 94% on room air, temperature 37.3 °C (99.1 °F), pain: no grimacing or guarding. GCS 12.","Lab: BMP: sodium 147, up from 140. BUN 30, up from 16. Creatinine 1.1."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"stroke-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Riley Morgan, RN in the neuro ICU. I'm calling about Gloria Mitchell in room 12, admitted yesterday at 1300 with an acute ischemic stroke. I'm calling because her neuro status has worsened: her NIHSS has risen from 7 to 17.","B — She is 66, with hypertension, hyperlipidemia, type 2 diabetes and a small stroke 3 years ago, and she smokes. Her symptoms began at 1010 yesterday, and she was given tPA at 1125. By 2000 her NIHSS had improved to 7. At 0200 her GCS is 1…","A — I'm worried her stroke is extending: the blocked artery is cutting off blood to more of her brain.","R — I recommend a stat CT without contrast now, to see whether she is bleeding after the tPA, because that decides what we do next. If there is no bleed, I'd ask neurology about a thrombectomy through interventional radiology, to pull out t…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"stroke-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion. The penumbra the tPA saved is dying, and every minute without blood flow adds to a permanent deficit. Her airway is a growing concern at a GCS of 12, so she stays NPO with suction at …","Independent — The head of the bed at 30 degrees, her head neutral and midline. The room quiet, care clustered. The GCS and NIHSS every hour, trended. — Improves venous drainage and cerebral blood flow. Trending the NIHSS and GCS shows at on…","Dependent — A normal saline bolus per order at 0240, to hydrate her. — Adequate hydration promotes perfusion and decreases further brain injury. Her rising sodium and BUN and her dark, scant urine show she is dry. — Urine output 30 mL/hr or…","Interdisciplinary — Neurology and interventional radiology: a mechanical thrombectomy with a stent retriever, a brain cath like a heart cath. — Pulls the clot from the artery, restoring blood flow to the penumbra before it dies. — NIHSS fal…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"stroke-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Aspiration pneumonia (respiratory). The afternoon of her third day, 52 hours after admission.","Why it could occur — Her stroke left her swallow and gag weak, and her level of consciousness was low for hours. An NG tube was placed for feeding on her second day. Saliva or feeding slipped silently into her lungs. Secretions sitting in t…","Priority cues — Temperature 38.7 °C (101.7 °F). RR 28 and SpO2 89% on room air, down from 96%: tachypnea and hypoxemia. Coarse crackles in the right posterior and lateral base. A wet cough too weak to bring up her thick, yellow-green sputum…","Actions — Dependent: oxygen per order, to keep her SpO2 above 95%. A sputum culture, then an antibiotic per order, for the bacterial pneumonia: the culture names the bacteria and the antibiotics that will kill it. Independent: pulmonary toi…","Evaluation — SpO2: 95% or more on oxygen. Then her temperature and WBC, trending down."]},{"s":"The Patient","a":"sci-the-patient","k":["The Patient","Histories, Vital Signs and Medications","HPI: Ethan Tran is a 20-year-old Asian American man flown to the ED at 1530. At 1450, at a lake, he dove headfirst from a dock into shallow water and struck his head on the bottom. His friends pulled him out at once, his face above the wate…","PMH: Asthma since childhood, well controlled, last hospitalized at 14. Seasonal allergic rhinitis. Generalized anxiety disorder, 2 years.","SH: Single, a college junior, lives in an apartment with two roommates. Never smoked. Four or five beers at weekend parties, two today. No drug use.","FH: Father has type 2 diabetes. Mother has asthma.","Admission VS: BP 94/56 (MAP 69), HR 54 (sinus bradycardia), RR 24 and shallow, SpO2 93% on room air, temperature 36.0 °C (96.8 °F), pain 7/10.","Home medications:","Fluticasone, a controller, for asthma","Albuterol, a reliever, for wheezing","Cetirizine, for allergies","Sertraline, for anxiety","Allergies: No known drug allergies. Peanuts (hives).","General Survey, Physical Exam, Labs and Diagnostics","General survey: 20-year-old man lying flat in a cervical collar. Awake, alert and oriented × 4, GCS 15. Frightened: \"Am I going to walk again?\" Speaks in short sentences, pausing for breath. Wet swim trunks cut off, now in a hospital gown. …","Pain: 7/10 sharp pain at the back of his neck, constant since the dive.","Neurologic: GCS 15. Shrugs both shoulders and bends both elbows weakly. No movement of his hands, trunk or legs. No sensation from the upper chest down, or in his hands. No deep tendon reflexes in his legs. Pupils equal, 3 mm and brisk.","Respiratory: RR 24 and shallow. His abdomen rises with each breath while his chest wall barely moves: breathing with the diaphragm. Using his neck muscles with each breath. Breath sounds decreased in the posterior bases bilaterally, with fa…","Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, slow and regular, no murmur. Monitor: sinus bradycardia at 54. No JVD. No edema of the hands, legs, ankles or feet bilaterally. Radial and pedal pulses 2+ and equal bilaterally. Capillary refill 3 seconds …","Labs: ABG on room air: pH 7.34, PaCO2 48, HCO3 25, PaO2 68. Lactate 1.8.","Procedure: Foley catheter placed in the ED: 1,100 mL of clear yellow urine drained at once, urinary retention."]},{"s":"Priority Problems","a":"sci-priority-problems","k":["Priority Problems","Priority nursing problem — Ineffective tissue perfusion: the spinal cord — Ineffective breathing pattern","Ineffective tissue perfusion: the spinal cord","Ineffective breathing pattern","Supporting cues — BP 94/56, a MAP of 69: well below the goal of 85 to 90 for spinal cord perfusion. HR 54, sinus bradycardia. Temperature 36.0 °C: an injury at T6 or higher cannot regulate body temperature. An injury at C6, with no movement…","BP 94/56, a MAP of 69: well below the goal of 85 to 90 for spinal cord perfusion.","HR 54, sinus bradycardia.","Temperature 36.0 °C: an injury at T6 or higher cannot regulate body temperature.","An injury at C6, with no movement, sensation or reflexes below it: neurogenic shock is more likely at T6 or higher.","RR 24 and shallow, breathing with his diaphragm, his chest wall barely moving, his neck muscles in use.","ABG: pH 7.34, PaCO2 48, HCO3 25: an uncompensated respiratory acidosis, the shallow breaths not blowing off CO2.","PaO2 68 and SpO2 93% on room air: hypoxemia.","A weak cough, and expiratory wheezes from his asthma.","Relevance to the diagnosis — The injury disrupted his autonomic pathways below C6: neurogenic shock. With the sympathetic input lost, his vessels dilate, a distributive shock, so his BP falls, his heart rate slows and he loses heat. His cor…","The injury disrupted his autonomic pathways below C6: neurogenic shock. With the sympathetic input lost, his vessels dilate, a distributive shock, so his BP falls, his heart rate slows and he loses heat. His cord is already swelling from th…","At C5 to C6 the diaphragm usually still works, but the intercostal and abdominal muscles are weakened, so each breath is shallow and his cough cannot clear secretions. Any injury at T6 or above is a very high risk for breathing problems. Hi…","Concern rating — 2, urgent. Every hour of low pressure risks more cord damage, so his MAP must come up quickly. He is awake and oriented, perfusing his brain, and not expected to die within the hour. — 2, urgent. His breathing is shallow an…","2, urgent. Every hour of low pressure risks more cord damage, so his MAP must come up quickly. He is awake and oriented, perfusing his brain, and not expected to die within the hour.","2, urgent. His breathing is shallow and his CO2 rising, and he must be watched very closely. He speaks in short sentences, and his SpO2 rises to 96% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula: not life-threatening within the hour."]},{"s":"The Story Goes On","a":"sci-story-goes-on","k":["The Story Goes On","That Night","Mr. Tran came to the neuro ICU at 1730, in his collar, with surgery to stabilize the fracture planned for 0800. He had 1 L of normal saline in the ED and oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal cannula, with a continuous bladder temperature. At 0100 his…","Updated VS: BP 76/42 (MAP 53), HR 42 (sinus bradycardia), RR 22, SpO2 95% on 2 L/min by nasal cannula, temperature 35.2 °C (95.4 °F), pain 5/10.","Lab: Lactate 3.2, up from 1.8."]},{"s":"SBAR","a":"sci-sbar-heading","k":["SBAR","S — This is Riley Morgan, RN in the neuro ICU. I'm calling about Ethan Tran in room 8, admitted last night at 1730 with a C6 spinal cord injury. I'm calling because his blood pressure and heart rate have dropped.","B — He is 20, with asthma, allergic rhinitis and anxiety. Yesterday at 1450 he dove into shallow water and fractured C6, with no movement or sensation below the injury. Surgery is planned for 0800. He had 1 L of normal saline in the ED. At …","A — I think his neurogenic shock is worsening, and his spinal cord is not being perfused.","R — I recommend a fluid bolus now, and if his MAP stays below 85, a vasopressor such as norepinephrine, to raise his blood pressure. I recommend atropine for his heart rate of 42 with this low a BP. And I'd like a repeat lactate in an hour,…"]},{"s":"Take Action and Evaluation","a":"sci-take-action","k":["Take Action and Evaluation","Priority problem: ineffective tissue perfusion of the spinal cord. A MAP of 53 cannot perfuse a swollen cord. Systolic hypotension now causes further damage and secondary injury, and can make his loss of function permanent. His breathing is…","Independent — Warm blankets and a warm room, with the bladder temperature watched continuously. — An injury at T6 or higher cannot regulate body temperature, and hypothermia is one cause of his bradycardia. — Bladder temperature 36.5 °C or …","Dependent — A 1 L normal saline bolus per order. — His dilated vessels leave him relatively hypovolemic: the fluid refills the tank. — MAP 85 or more within 1 hour. — Not met. At 0215, BP 82/46, a MAP of 58. In distributive shock fluid refi…","Interdisciplinary — Neurosurgery: surgery to stabilize the unstable C6 fracture. — An unstable vertebral fracture is repaired, usually within 24 hours, so that movement does not further damage the cord. — Spine stabilized within 24 hours of…"]},{"s":"A Complication in Another System","a":"sci-complication-heading","k":["A Complication in Another System","Complication — Paralytic ileus (GI). The morning of his third day.","Why it could occur — Spinal shock leaves his bowel with atony: no peristalsis, a high risk for paralytic ileus. Morphine for his neck pain since surgery slows his gut further, and so did the hours of shock.","Priority cues — Abdomen distended, firm and tympanic. Bowel sounds absent in all 4 quadrants. Vomited 400 mL of green, bilious fluid at 0700. No stool or flatus since the injury. He cannot feel abdominal pain, but feels full and nauseated.","Actions — Dependent: an NG tube per order for decompression, with bowel rest, IV fluids and electrolytes per order. Acetaminophen per order for pain in place of morphine where it is enough: opioids slow the gut. Independent: his abdomen, bo…","Evaluation — Bowel sounds: returning, with flatus and a falling NG output.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"signature-priority-problems.html","t":"Priority Problem Bank","secs":[{"s":"Priority Problem Bank","a":"","k":["Signature Assessment · Topic 7"]},{"s":"Hematologic","a":"bank-hematologic-heading","k":["Hematologic","DIC — Bleeding — Platelets falling fast: being used up. PT and PTT prolonged, fibrinogen low: the clotting factors used up. Oozing at IV sites and gums: bleeding from insertion sites and mucous membranes. Petechiae, an ecchymosis: little bl…","Platelets falling fast: being used up.","PT and PTT prolonged, fibrinogen low: the clotting factors used up.","Oozing at IV sites and gums: bleeding from insertion sites and mucous membranes.","Petechiae, an ecchymosis: little bleeds under the skin.","Sepsis: mediators from the bacteria and white cells set off unregulated clotting. Platelets clump, and microclots form in the small vessels.","The clotting uses up the platelets and clotting factors, fibrinogen among them, and the body tries to thin the blood.","Unable to clot: any area that can bleed will bleed. Aspirin, an antiplatelet, adds to it, so it is held.","Below 30,000, the platelets put the patient at risk for a spontaneous bleed.","Ineffective peripheral tissue perfusion, with PAD — The tips of the toes dusky purple, mottled and cool. Capillary refill slower in the toes than the fingers. A new burning ache in the toes, unlike the claudication. Weak pedal pulses and co…","The tips of the toes dusky purple, mottled and cool.","Capillary refill slower in the toes than the fingers.","A new burning ache in the toes, unlike the claudication.","Weak pedal pulses and cool feet, with PAD: less flow to start with.","The same microclots lodge in the small vessels and cut off the flow behind them: ischemia, then necrosis.","DIC's skin signs often show first on the tips of the toes.","PAD: atherosclerosis in the leg arteries, and perfusion cannot get past it.","Sepsis opens the vessels wide, lowering the BP and the perfusion to the tissues. Dying tissue is very painful.","The bleeding patient and anemia — Deficient fluid volume — Tachycardia, the first compensation for lost volume. A low BP that holds only after fluid. Bright red blood in the drain and dressing, and a spreading ecchymosis: still bleeding. He…","Tachycardia, the first compensation for lost volume.","A low BP that holds only after fluid.","Bright red blood in the drain and dressing, and a spreading ecchymosis: still bleeding.","Hemoglobin falling. The full loss does not show for 48 to 72 hours.","Urine output under 0.5 mL/kg/hr, with pale, cool skin.","Blood lost from the vessels leaves too little volume to fill them.","Tachycardia first, then the vessels clamp down to send blood to the brain, heart and lungs. The skin is restricted first, the kidneys next.","Stroke volume cannot rise with the volume gone.","The hemoglobin may not show the whole loss: plasma is lost with the cells, and IV fluid dilutes what is left.","Active bleeding can lead to hypovolemic shock.","Activity intolerance, with CKD — Standing: lightheaded, short of breath, tachycardic, nearly fainting. Short of breath for minutes after a small move. Weakness and fatigue. A moderate anemia on top of a chronic one. — Fewer red cells: too l…","Standing: lightheaded, short of breath, tachycardic, nearly fainting.","Short of breath for minutes after a small move.","Weakness and fatigue.","A moderate anemia on top of a chronic one.","Fewer red cells: too little oxygen reaches the tissues.","At rest, a faster heart rate and breathing make up for it. Any effort asks for more oxygen than the blood can carry: getting from the bed to the chair uses all their energy.","CKD: the kidneys make less erythropoietin, so the marrow makes fewer red cells, and the patient was anemic before the bleed.","Thrombocytopenia and HIT — Bleeding — Skin: petechiae, purpura, ecchymosis. Oozing mucous membranes: the nose, mouth and lips. Bleeding in wounds, stool, urine, the nose and pulmonary secretions. Temperature, when chemo or the bone marrow i…","Skin: petechiae, purpura, ecchymosis.","Oozing mucous membranes: the nose, mouth and lips.","Bleeding in wounds, stool, urine, the nose and pulmonary secretions.","Temperature, when chemo or the bone marrow is the cause: with a very low white count, any temp is very concerning.","Too few platelets for the platelet plug.","Infection, when chemo or the bone marrow is the cause — White blood cells decreased or not working: no normal response to an infection.","White blood cells decreased or not working: no normal response to an infection.","The immunocompromised patient — Infection — Usually asymptomatic until an infection. May not look very sick. Temperature: with a very low white count, any temp is very concerning. Opportunistic pneumonia: fever, chills, cough, dyspnea, fati…","Usually asymptomatic until an infection. May not look very sick.","Temperature: with a very low white count, any temp is very concerning.","Opportunistic pneumonia: fever, chills, cough, dyspnea, fatigue.","No normal white blood cell response.","Impaired gas exchange — An opportunistic pneumonia infects the lung parenchyma. With no normal white cell response, it can progress to respiratory distress, then respiratory failure.","An opportunistic pneumonia infects the lung parenchyma. With no normal white cell response, it can progress to respiratory distress, then respiratory failure."]},{"s":"Cardiovascular","a":"bank-cardiovascular-heading","k":["Cardiovascular","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS) — Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion — Chest pressure not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin: ischemic pain, the hallmark of an MI. Troponin elevated: heart cells are dying. ST depression and T wave inversion, n…","Chest pressure not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin: ischemic pain, the hallmark of an MI.","Troponin elevated: heart cells are dying.","ST depression and T wave inversion, no ST elevation: read with the troponin as an NSTEMI.","Tachycardia with PVCs: more oxygen needed, and ischemic conducting cells misfire.","Hypertension, hyperlipidemia and smoking build atherosclerotic plaque in the coronary arteries.","A plaque ruptures and a thrombus partly blocks a coronary artery. The myocardium past it gets no oxygen and starts to die.","A fast heart rate raises the demand while the supply is cut. Without reperfusion, the infarct grows.","Impaired gas exchange, with COPD — Low SpO2 on room air: hypoxemia. Tachypnea, slightly labored. Decreased breath sounds in the bases, expiratory wheezes, prolonged expiration, barrel chest: the COPD. — Emphysema has damaged the alveoli: le…","Low SpO2 on room air: hypoxemia.","Tachypnea, slightly labored.","Decreased breath sounds in the bases, expiratory wheezes, prolonged expiration, barrel chest: the COPD.","Emphysema has damaged the alveoli: less oxygen crosses into the blood, all the ischemic myocardium gets.","Hypoxemia severely impacts cardiac tissue and can set off rhythm changes.","Oxygen improves the heart's supply. The SpO2 target is 88 to 92%, as for any patient with COPD.","Acute heart failure — Impaired gas exchange — Low SpO2 and PaO2: hypoxemia. Tachypnea blowing off CO2: an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. Crackles with pink, blood-tinged sputum: pulmonary edema. Sleeping upright, the tripod position, …","Low SpO2 and PaO2: hypoxemia.","Tachypnea blowing off CO2: an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis.","Crackles with pink, blood-tinged sputum: pulmonary edema.","Sleeping upright, the tripod position, neck and thoracic muscles in use.","An old MI left a damaged myocardium that cannot contract well: a low ejection fraction.","Salty food raises the blood volume and the preload.","The weak left ventricle cannot pump it forward: blood backs up into the left atrium and the pulmonary veins, pulmonary congestion and edema.","Too little oxygen reaches the blood, and breathing speeds up to make up for it.","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium, with CKD — Potassium high. Frequent PVCs: an irritable heart. Creatinine up from baseline, with the BUN: the kidneys are excreting less. Spironolactone at home: a potassium-sparing diuretic. — The ki…","Potassium high.","Frequent PVCs: an irritable heart.","Creatinine up from baseline, with the BUN: the kidneys are excreting less.","Spironolactone at home: a potassium-sparing diuretic.","The kidneys are primarily responsible for excreting potassium.","CKD from diabetes and hypertension, and the failing heart sends the kidneys less blood: they take 20 to 25% of cardiac output.","Spironolactone spares potassium too.","A high potassium brings EKG changes and ventricular dysrhythmias that can be life-threatening, and each lowers the cardiac output further.","Aortic aneurysm — Deficient fluid volume — Usually asymptomatic until it ruptures. Sometimes back or abdominal pain with a AAA. Ruptured: very hypovolemic, chest pain, diaphoretic. — A rupture bleeds out.","Usually asymptomatic until it ruptures. Sometimes back or abdominal pain with a AAA.","Ruptured: very hypovolemic, chest pain, diaphoretic.","A rupture bleeds out.","Ineffective tissue perfusion — Blood flow lost below the rupture.","Blood flow lost below the rupture.","Cardiac tamponade — Decreased cardiac output — Beck's triad: low BP, JVD, muffled heart sounds. After surgery: chest tube output stops abruptly, with sudden hypotension and tachycardia. — Blood in the pericardial space keeps the heart from …","Beck's triad: low BP, JVD, muffled heart sounds.","After surgery: chest tube output stops abruptly, with sudden hypotension and tachycardia.","Blood in the pericardial space keeps the heart from beating effectively.","Pericarditis — Acute pain — Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark. Very painful: mimics MI pain. Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety.","Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark.","Very painful: mimics MI pain. Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety.","Decreased cardiac output — It can cause a tamponade.","It can cause a tamponade.","Valve disease and infective endocarditis — Decreased cardiac output — Valve disease: heart failure signs, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, a murmur. Endocarditis: a really sick infection. High temp, chills, diaphoresis, low BP, dysr…","Valve disease: heart failure signs, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, chest pain, a murmur.","Endocarditis: a really sick infection. High temp, chills, diaphoresis, low BP, dysrhythmias.","The blood backs up behind the valve.","Hyperthermia, in endocarditis — Bacteria in the bloodstream lodge on the valve as vegetations.","Bacteria in the bloodstream lodge on the valve as vegetations.","AV blocks — Decreased cardiac output — Bradycardia: the biggest concern. Compromised cardiac output, more symptomatic with each degree. — The rate is too slow.","Bradycardia: the biggest concern.","Compromised cardiac output, more symptomatic with each degree.","The rate is too slow.","Activity intolerance — Not enough cardiac output brings fatigue.","Not enough cardiac output brings fatigue."]},{"s":"Renal","a":"bank-renal-heading","k":["Renal","Acute kidney injury — Deficient fluid volume — Hypotension with tachycardia: what a pre-renal AKI looks like. Oliguria: under 0.5 mL/kg/hr, dark and concentrated. Weak, thready pulses, slow capillary refill, cool and clammy skin, flat neck …","Hypotension with tachycardia: what a pre-renal AKI looks like.","Oliguria: under 0.5 mL/kg/hr, dark and concentrated.","Weak, thready pulses, slow capillary refill, cool and clammy skin, flat neck veins, dry mucous membranes, weight lost.","Creatinine up from baseline with the BUN: both up, so the kidneys. Creatinine is the most reliable indicator. Dehydration gives the BUN a false high.","Usually multifactorial: most often pre-renal plus intrarenal, as here. Sepsis is the most common cause in the critically ill.","Vomiting and diarrhea empty the vessels, and the BP falls. The kidneys take 20 to 25% of cardiac output: hypoperfusion, a pre-renal cause.","Contrast dye while very dehydrated: a nephrotoxic drug in a fluid volume deficit, a double punch. Direct nephron injury, an intrarenal cause, on top.","CKD carries a much higher risk for AKI.","Rehydrated early, with the cause solved, an AKI can reverse.","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium, with CKD — Potassium high. Tall, peaked T waves: a potassium imbalance, showing on the EKG. A partially compensated metabolic acidosis: the kidneys failing to keep bicarb, the lungs compensating with…","Potassium high.","Tall, peaked T waves: a potassium imbalance, showing on the EKG.","A partially compensated metabolic acidosis: the kidneys failing to keep bicarb, the lungs compensating with hyperventilation.","Stage 3A CKD: the kidneys already excrete less.","The kidneys are primarily responsible for excreting potassium. In CKD it is one of the first electrolytes to become abnormal.","Almost no urine: the potassium builds up.","Failing kidneys cannot keep bicarb or excrete acid. In a metabolic acidosis, H+ moves into the cells and K+ comes out.","Hyperkalemia slows depolarization. Above 6.0 come dysrhythmias that can be life-threatening.","Pre-renal AKI — Deficient fluid volume — Hypotension, with tachycardia. Hypotensive or hypovolemic for a while: the AKI may develop within the next day or two. — Too little volume to perfuse the kidneys.","Hypotension, with tachycardia.","Hypotensive or hypovolemic for a while: the AKI may develop within the next day or two.","Too little volume to perfuse the kidneys.","Decreased cardiac output, when heart failure is the cause — Low cardiac output from heart failure: hypoperfusion of the kidneys.","Low cardiac output from heart failure: hypoperfusion of the kidneys.","Intrarenal AKI — Excess fluid volume — Hypertension for a short time, as in ATN, from RAAS malfunction. A nephrotoxic drug or contrast dye in the history. — Injured nephrons cannot excrete the fluid.","Hypertension for a short time, as in ATN, from RAAS malfunction.","A nephrotoxic drug or contrast dye in the history.","Injured nephrons cannot excrete the fluid.","Electrolyte imbalance: a high potassium — No longer excreted.","No longer excreted.","Post-renal AKI — Impaired urinary elimination — None taught. — Urine cannot get past the blockage.","None taught.","Urine cannot get past the blockage."]},{"s":"GI and Hepatic","a":"bank-gi-hepatic-heading","k":["GI and Hepatic","Acute pancreatitis — Deficient fluid volume — Hypotension with tachycardia: compensating for too little volume in the vessels. Repeated vomiting, nothing kept down: fluid lost, and none taken in. Weak pulses, slow capillary refill, dry and …","Hypotension with tachycardia: compensating for too little volume in the vessels.","Repeated vomiting, nothing kept down: fluid lost, and none taken in.","Weak pulses, slow capillary refill, dry and sticky mucous membranes.","BUN and creatinine up: the kidneys are getting less blood.","Biliary disease: one of the two most common causes.","Enzymes leak into the pancreas's own tissue, and it digests itself. The dying cells set off the inflammatory response.","Fluid collects in the retroperitoneal space: up to a third of the plasma volume. Vomiting and a lost appetite add to it.","Shock and hypovolemia: the primary cause of death in acute pancreatitis.","Unstable blood glucose, with type 2 diabetes — Hyperglycemia. Type 2 diabetes: the glucose already hard to control. Lipase very high, with fever and tachycardia: a severe case, which can swing the glucose low. — Pancreatitis can cause hyper…","Hyperglycemia.","Type 2 diabetes: the glucose already hard to control.","Lipase very high, with fever and tachycardia: a severe case, which can swing the glucose low.","Pancreatitis can cause hyperglycemia, and diabetes already makes the glucose hard to control.","The pancreas makes insulin, and its inflamed, dying cells release less of it.","Severe enough, pancreatitis can cause hypoglycemia instead.","Poor glycemic control varies oxygen consumption in a body under stress.","GI bleed, including variceal — Deficient fluid volume — Bright red hematemesis and lavage: actively bleeding. Melena: older blood. Tachycardia first, compensating. Then the BP falls. Pale, cool and clammy, slow capillary refill, weak pulses…","Bright red hematemesis and lavage: actively bleeding. Melena: older blood.","Tachycardia first, compensating. Then the BP falls.","Pale, cool and clammy, slow capillary refill, weak pulses: getting paler.","Hemoglobin falling. BUN up with a normal creatinine: blood metabolized in the GI tract.","A past ulcer: a very high risk of another. NSAIDs are the most common drug cause, and an anticoagulant vastly increases the bleeding risk.","Blood lost from the vessels: a fluid volume deficit. The heart rate rises first, then the BP falls.","The hematocrit can lag for hours behind the loss.","Decreased cardiac tissue perfusion, with heart failure — Chest tightness: angina. Low hemoglobin: too few red cells to carry oxygen. Tachycardia: a faster heart needs more oxygen. Short of breath walking, fainted at home. — An acute GI blee…","Chest tightness: angina.","Low hemoglobin: too few red cells to carry oxygen.","Tachycardia: a faster heart needs more oxygen.","Short of breath walking, fainted at home.","An acute GI bleed looks like anemia: weakness, shortness of breath, angina, syncope.","Too little hemoglobin carries too little oxygen to the tissues, the myocardium included.","A heart weakened by heart failure beats faster for the lost volume: it needs more oxygen while it gets less.","A very severe bleed brings EKG changes.","Hepatic failure — Acute confusion — Decreased level of consciousness: oriented to self only, drowsy and irritable. Asterixis: the hands flap when held out. Ammonia high: hepatic encephalopathy. Above 150, a raised risk of cerebral edema. No…","Decreased level of consciousness: oriented to self only, drowsy and irritable.","Asterixis: the hands flap when held out.","Ammonia high: hepatic encephalopathy. Above 150, a raised risk of cerebral edema.","No lactulose and no bowel movement: the ammonia stays in the gut.","Cirrhosis, then a binge and days of acetaminophen: the biggest cause of acute liver failure, especially with binge drinking.","A failing liver cannot detoxify. Ammonia builds up, crosses the blood-brain barrier and depresses the brain.","Hepatic encephalopathy raises the risk of seizures. A falling level of consciousness puts the airway at risk.","Bleeding — INR above 1.5: slow to clot. Platelets low: thrombocytopenia, under 150,000. New bruises, and the gums ooze. Esophageal varices. — The liver makes clotting factors II, V, VII, IX and X. A failing liver makes too few: prolonged bl…","INR above 1.5: slow to clot.","Platelets low: thrombocytopenia, under 150,000.","New bruises, and the gums ooze.","Esophageal varices.","The liver makes clotting factors II, V, VII, IX and X. A failing liver makes too few: prolonged bleeding times.","With low platelets too, the body cannot correct a bleed on its own.","Portal hypertension engorges the esophageal veins into varices. A bleeding varix without clotting factors: a critical care emergency.","Ischemic bowel — Ineffective GI tissue perfusion — Abdominal pain, especially after eating, then at baseline. Urgent, loose stools. Bloody stool with dark red clots. Nausea, vomiting. Fever, from dead gut. — The bowel wall gets no oxygenate…","Abdominal pain, especially after eating, then at baseline.","Urgent, loose stools. Bloody stool with dark red clots. Nausea, vomiting.","Fever, from dead gut.","The bowel wall gets no oxygenated blood, and dies.","Deficient fluid volume — Fluid shifting: often intravascularly depleted.","Fluid shifting: often intravascularly depleted.","Bowel obstruction and paralytic ileus — Dysfunctional GI motility — Distension: the hallmark. Cramping periumbilical pain in waves. Large-volume bilious vomiting. No flatus. Bowel sounds: high-pitched and tinkling in a mechanical SBO, absen…","Distension: the hallmark. Cramping periumbilical pain in waves.","Large-volume bilious vomiting. No flatus.","Bowel sounds: high-pitched and tinkling in a mechanical SBO, absent to hypoactive in an ileus.","Peristalsis is lost or blocked.","Deficient fluid volume — Vomiting, and no PO intake.","Vomiting, and no PO intake.","Abdominal compartment syndrome — Ineffective tissue perfusion — Hugely distended, possibly rigid: hard from the fluid. Organs lose their blood flow as the pressure rises. — Fluid presses on the abdominal organs.","Hugely distended, possibly rigid: hard from the fluid.","Organs lose their blood flow as the pressure rises.","Fluid presses on the abdominal organs.","Acute pain — Very painful, and pain raises the abdominal pressure.","Very painful, and pain raises the abdominal pressure."]},{"s":"Neuro","a":"bank-neuro-heading","k":["Neuro","TBI with increased ICP — Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion — GCS falling from the normal 15. A pounding headache and vomiting: signs of increased ICP in an awake patient. CT: a subdural hematoma, a contusion and cerebral edema, more vol…","GCS falling from the normal 15.","A pounding headache and vomiting: signs of increased ICP in an awake patient.","CT: a subdural hematoma, a contusion and cerebral edema, more volume inside the skull.","An acceleration and deceleration injury: two impacts, two areas of the brain.","The contusion is the primary injury. The hematoma and the swelling are the secondary injury, and they keep growing.","Brain, blood and CSF share a skull that cannot give: as they expand, the ICP rises.","CPP = MAP − ICP: a rising ICP leaves less blood reaching the brain, the primary driver of more secondary injury.","Seizures, with epilepsy — Epilepsy, and a seizure today despite the anti-seizure medication. A seizure seen: stiffening and jerking, a bitten tongue, incontinence. Blood on the brain from the subdural hematoma. — A seizure disorder already …","Epilepsy, and a seizure today despite the anti-seizure medication.","A seizure seen: stiffening and jerking, a bitten tongue, incontinence.","Blood on the brain from the subdural hematoma.","A seizure disorder already makes seizures likely. Any TBI raises the risk: blood on the brain irritates the tissue.","A seizure greatly increases the ICP and the brain's metabolic demand, and could cost the airway.","Stroke — Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion — Sudden one-sided weakness: facial droop, arm and leg weakness, slurred speech, from the opposite side of the brain. NIHSS: a moderate stroke. CT without contrast: no bleeding, so ischemic. A …","Sudden one-sided weakness: facial droop, arm and leg weakness, slurred speech, from the opposite side of the brain.","NIHSS: a moderate stroke.","CT without contrast: no bleeding, so ischemic.","A clear time of onset, inside the window for tPA. A TIA 2 weeks before: the warning.","Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes and smoking build plaque in the large arteries: the biggest risk factor for an ischemic stroke.","A clot on a ruptured plaque blocks an artery, and the brain it feeds loses its blood supply. Brain cells survive about 3 to 4 minutes without oxygen.","The penumbra around the dying core can still be saved if blood flow returns soon.","Dying neurons release glutamate, lactic acid builds, and cytotoxic edema injures more cells.","Aspiration, with an earlier stroke — Drooling from the corner of the mouth, a weak gag reflex. A wet, gurgling voice after swallowing, with a weak cough. Slurred speech: weak muscles of the mouth and throat. Coughing on thin liquids since t…","Drooling from the corner of the mouth, a weak gag reflex.","A wet, gurgling voice after swallowing, with a weak cough.","Slurred speech: weak muscles of the mouth and throat.","Coughing on thin liquids since the first stroke.","A stroke impairs motor function, swallowing and the gag reflex included. The first stroke had already weakened the swallow.","Saliva or fluid slips into the trachea and lungs, and aspiration may be silent.","Dysphagia and a decreased level of consciousness: both risk factors for aspiration pneumonia. A suspected stroke is NPO.","Spinal cord injury — Ineffective tissue perfusion: the spinal cord — MAP well below the goal of 85 to 90 for spinal cord perfusion. Sinus bradycardia. Temperature low: an injury at T6 or higher cannot regulate body temperature. A C6 injury,…","MAP well below the goal of 85 to 90 for spinal cord perfusion.","Sinus bradycardia.","Temperature low: an injury at T6 or higher cannot regulate body temperature.","A C6 injury, no movement, sensation or reflexes below it: neurogenic shock is more likely at T6 or higher.","Autonomic pathways disrupted below the injury: neurogenic shock. Without sympathetic input the vessels dilate, a distributive shock.","The BP falls, the heart rate slows, and heat is lost.","A swelling cord with a low BP: ischemia, more secondary injury, a permanent loss of function. So the MAP is kept at 85 to 90 for about 7 days.","Ineffective breathing pattern, with asthma — Fast, shallow breaths with the diaphragm, the chest wall barely moving, neck muscles in use. An uncompensated respiratory acidosis: shallow breaths not blowing off CO2. Hypoxemia. A weak cough, a…","Fast, shallow breaths with the diaphragm, the chest wall barely moving, neck muscles in use.","An uncompensated respiratory acidosis: shallow breaths not blowing off CO2.","Hypoxemia.","A weak cough, and expiratory wheezes from the asthma.","At C5 to C6 the diaphragm usually still works, but the intercostal and abdominal muscles are weak: shallow breaths, a cough that cannot clear secretions.","Any injury at T6 or above: a very high risk for breathing problems.","Asthma narrows the airways, adding work the weak muscles cannot meet.","Swelling that climbs toward the diaphragm's nerves could stop the breathing altogether.","Meningitis — Hyperthermia — Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days. Fever, a throbbing headache, nuchal rigidity. — Infection of the meninges and the CSF.","Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days.","Fever, a throbbing headache, nuchal rigidity.","Infection of the meninges and the CSF.","Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion — The inflammation can cause cerebral edema and increased ICP.","The inflammation can cause cerebral edema and increased ICP.","Seizures — Injury — A decreased neurologic status with no clear cause. Status epilepticus: a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures over 30 minutes.","A decreased neurologic status with no clear cause.","Status epilepticus: a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures over 30 minutes.","Ineffective airway clearance","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day1-respiratory.html","t":"Respiratory and Progressive Issues in Critical Care","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory and Progressive Issues in Critical Care","a":"","k":["Day 1 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Oxygen delivery devices: when to use a nasal cannula or a non-rebreather, what CPAP does, why positive airway pressure is needed.","Respiratory assessment: every aspect, not only breath sounds, O2 sat and respiratory rate. Know the treatments for each abnormal finding, such as the interventions for rhonchi.","Respiratory diagnostics: chest x-ray, CT scan, thoracentesis. What each one tells you, when it is used, the nurse's responsibilities.","Chest tubes: care, normal and abnormal findings, expected drainage.","COPD (emphysema, chronic bronchitis), asthma, and pneumonia: the basics, and why this patient is in the ICU. A COPD patient is usually there for pneumonia or an exacerbation.","ABG interpretation: simple interpretation of PaO2, PaCO2, bicarb and pH.","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS): the scale and its scores.","Venous thromboembolism: who is at high risk for a PE, and preventing DVTs and PEs."]},{"s":"Advanced Respiratory Assessment","a":"advanced-assessment","k":["Advanced Respiratory Assessment","Neurological status — Altered level of consciousness or sedation: can the patient maintain a patent airway? Effective cough and a gag reflex? Aware enough to follow instructions to deep breathe and cough?","Altered level of consciousness or sedation: can the patient maintain a patent airway?","Effective cough and a gag reflex?","Aware enough to follow instructions to deep breathe and cough?","Pain, procedures, conditions — Where the pain is matters. Chest surgery (bypass) and big abdominal surgery make deep breaths hard: watch for, treat and prevent atelectasis and pneumonia. Anxiety and panic: breathing 40 times a minute blows …","Where the pain is matters. Chest surgery (bypass) and big abdominal surgery make deep breaths hard: watch for, treat and prevent atelectasis and pneumonia.","Anxiety and panic: breathing 40 times a minute blows off CO2 and prevents the deep breaths needed for effective gas exchange.","Weakness and coughing — Weakness follows an ICU stay, big surgery, big trauma or a long time in bed, even with mobilization. A weak patient cannot protect the airway as effectively. Cough is a major indicator of respiratory function and ris…","Weakness follows an ICU stay, big surgery, big trauma or a long time in bed, even with mobilization.","A weak patient cannot protect the airway as effectively. Cough is a major indicator of respiratory function and risk.","Is the cough effective? How frequent? What does the sputum look like?","Ineffective cough: pre-medicate for pain or change position.","Oral or nasal suction may be needed.","Concerning: coughing that is not productive and is blocking the airway.","Medications — Some help respiratory function and some inhibit it. Watch pain medication and sedation: heavy opioid use, a sleepy patient, recent anesthesia.","Some help respiratory function and some inhibit it.","Watch pain medication and sedation: heavy opioid use, a sleepy patient, recent anesthesia.","Glasgow Coma Scale and Airway Protection","3","Minimum score.","Less Than 8","Usually indicates coma.","9 to 12","Airway protection becomes the concern.","15","Highest score."]},{"s":"Acute Respiratory Failure","a":"acute-respiratory-failure","k":["Acute Respiratory Failure","Inability of the respiratory system to provide oxygen and/or remove carbon dioxide from the body. Most common admitting diagnosis in critical care units, as the primary problem (severe pneumonia) or the result of another condition (major su…","Oxygenation Failure: Hypoxemia","PaO2 less than 60 (partial pressure of arterial oxygen), found on an ABG.","PaO2 is the most accurate oxygen level. An O2 sat is affected by perfusion to the fingers, warmth and arterial disease.","See below for causes.","Ventilatory Failure: Hypercapnia","PaCO2 greater than 50 on an ABG. A venous CO2 is not as diagnostic as an arterial one.","Can be related to chronic respiratory failure, but not mutually exclusive. A stressor such as a respiratory infection overwhelms a system already compromised by COPD.","Also related to hypoventilation and/or V/Q mismatch.","Hypercapnia significantly increases cerebral blood flow, so CNS changes occur: anxiety, restlessness, rising intracranial pressure. LOC changes come more quickly than in oxygenation failure.","Hypoventilation leaves CO2 sitting in the alveoli: respiratory acidosis develops quickly. The kidneys cannot compensate the way the lungs can.","A patient is not usually labelled as one type. Look at the clinical picture and decide whether it is both, or primarily an oxygen problem.","Mechanisms of Hypoxemia (Oxygenation Failure)","Hypoventilation — Alveoli do not get the oxygen they need from outside. Rate or depth of respirations severely decreased (slow and deep at 4 to 6 breaths a minute, or a normal rate of 16 but very shallow). CO2 problems often come with it. E…","Alveoli do not get the oxygen they need from outside. Rate or depth of respirations severely decreased (slow and deep at 4 to 6 breaths a minute, or a normal rate of 16 but very shallow).","CO2 problems often come with it.","Examples: CNS depression (overdose, brain injury), after anesthesia, pain.","Intrapulmonary shunting — Blood flow reaches the alveoli, but the alveoli cannot exchange gas. Examples: alveoli that are not compliant (as in emphysema) or are filled with something (as in chronic bronchitis or pneumonia).","Blood flow reaches the alveoli, but the alveoli cannot exchange gas.","Examples: alveoli that are not compliant (as in emphysema) or are filled with something (as in chronic bronchitis or pneumonia).","V/Q mismatch — Ventilation-perfusion mismatching. Gas exchange depends on the balance between the ventilated (V) and the perfused (Q) areas of the lungs. Example: ventilation without perfusion (as in pulmonary embolism).","Ventilation-perfusion mismatching. Gas exchange depends on the balance between the ventilated (V) and the perfused (Q) areas of the lungs.","Example: ventilation without perfusion (as in pulmonary embolism).","Diffusion defects — Defect in the alveolar-capillary membrane, where the alveoli meet the pulmonary capillaries. Examples: fluid accumulation (as in pneumonia) or fibrotic changes (as in COPD or pulmonary fibrosis).","Defect in the alveolar-capillary membrane, where the alveoli meet the pulmonary capillaries.","Examples: fluid accumulation (as in pneumonia) or fibrotic changes (as in COPD or pulmonary fibrosis).","Decreased barometric pressure — Example: high altitude.","Example: high altitude.","Low cardiac output — Heart is not pumping oxygenated blood where it needs to go.","Heart is not pumping oxygenated blood where it needs to go.","Low hemoglobin — Too anemic to carry oxygen to the tissues.","Too anemic to carry oxygen to the tissues.","How Respiratory Failure Is Recognized","Signs That Point to Respiratory Failure","Increased work of breathing.","Increasing O2 needs. Turned up from 2 L to 6 L with no improvement: not good.","Chest x-ray.","Labs: H&H and ABG (PaO2, PaCO2).","Interventions for Respiratory Failure","Maintain a patent airway — Head tilt, chin lift. Get the patient into the best position first: sitting up, airway open. Oral airway, bag mask, or intubation if needed.","Head tilt, chin lift. Get the patient into the best position first: sitting up, airway open.","Oral airway, bag mask, or intubation if needed.","Optimize O2 delivery — Goal: PaO2 above 60, SpO2 above 90%. Start with supplemental O2, turn it up, then advance to higher levels.","Goal: PaO2 above 60, SpO2 above 90%.","Start with supplemental O2, turn it up, then advance to higher levels.","Minimize O2 demand — Avoid physical activity: no bed bath, no turning, no walking. Even a breathing treatment may have to wait. Involve respiratory therapy. May ultimately need to sedate and paralyze.","Avoid physical activity: no bed bath, no turning, no walking.","Even a breathing treatment may have to wait. Involve respiratory therapy.","May ultimately need to sedate and paralyze.","Treat the cause — Provide rest, reduce fever, treat pain.","Provide rest, reduce fever, treat pain.","Declining quickly: give the oxygen the patient needs, even in chronic COPD. CO2 and oxygen toxicity are dealt with as they come."]},{"s":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","a":"ards","k":["Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","Most severe version of acute respiratory failure, and a disease process of its own. Anyone in ARDS is in acute respiratory failure.","Acute onset — Within 1 week of a clinical insult. Abrupt. Not someone who became very sick from a chronic respiratory issue.","Within 1 week of a clinical insult.","Abrupt. Not someone who became very sick from a chronic respiratory issue.","Bilateral pulmonary opacities — Both lungs \"whited out\" on chest x-ray. Dark lung means airflow and gas exchange. Not explained by another condition such as pneumonia or pulmonary fibrosis.","Both lungs \"whited out\" on chest x-ray. Dark lung means airflow and gas exchange.","Not explained by another condition such as pneumonia or pulmonary fibrosis.","Altered PaO2/FiO2 ratio — Arterial oxygen (PaO2) not responding to the oxygen being given (FiO2). Measured while on positive airway pressure: PEEP or CPAP. The number determines diagnosis and severity.","Arterial oxygen (PaO2) not responding to the oxygen being given (FiO2).","Measured while on positive airway pressure: PEEP or CPAP.","The number determines diagnosis and severity.","Causes of ARDS","Direct Causes","Aspiration of gastric contents","Fat embolism","Inhalation of toxic gases","Multisystem trauma (chest and/or lung injury)","Near-drowning","Pneumonia","Indirect Causes","Burns","Cardiopulmonary bypass","Drug overdose","Fractures, especially of the pelvis or long bones (through fat embolism)","Multiple transfusions","Multisystem trauma (without chest and/or lung injury)","Pancreatitis","Sepsis","Highest-frequency risk factors: new-onset pneumonia, sepsis, aspiration and trauma. Two or more together, such as pneumonia followed by sepsis, is very high risk.","Pathophysiology of ARDS","From Lung Injury to Hypoxemia","Acute and diffuse injury to the lungs.","Inflammatory response: a cell-mediated, overly aggressive immune response damages the alveolar-capillary membrane.","Massive platelet aggregation and intravascular thrombi. Sputum is often blood-tinged or pink.","The membrane becomes very permeable. Fluid, protein and blood cells leak into the alveoli, and massive fluid leakage causes pulmonary edema.","Alveolar flooding: noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, shunting, V/Q mismatch, decreased compliance, hypoxemia. The edema sitting in the lungs puts the patient at very high risk of pneumonia.","Pulmonary hypertension causes further shunting. Dead space grows and surfactant is not produced.","Assessment Findings in ARDS","Acute Onset, Not Yet Intubated","Dyspnea, tachypnea.","Refractory hypoxemia: FiO2 goes up through high flow, CPAP and intubation, and PaO2 and SpO2 still do not rise.","Fine crackles from fluid in the alveoli. Often no breath sounds at all.","Restlessness, disorientation, decreasing LOC.","Pulse and temperature may increase.","PaO2 continues to decrease and dyspnea becomes severe. Central cyanosis when severe enough.","Intubated Patient","Usually sedated, paralyzed and ventilated.","Peak pressures increasing: how the ventilator shows decreased compliance.","Increasing O2 needs, with no rise in SpO2 or PaO2.","ARDS Interventions","Primary goal: adequate oxygenation.","Mechanical ventilation — Usually needed. Not many patients manage on CPAP or BiPAP. Settings are protective: as minimal as possible. Max settings cause permanent lung damage. The PaO2 goal may be 50 to 55 instead of above 60.","Usually needed. Not many patients manage on CPAP or BiPAP.","Settings are protective: as minimal as possible. Max settings cause permanent lung damage.","The PaO2 goal may be 50 to 55 instead of above 60.","ECMO — Bypasses the lungs and oxygenates for them, giving the lungs a chance to rest.","Bypasses the lungs and oxygenates for them, giving the lungs a chance to rest.","Corticosteroids — Dampen the inflammatory response that is causing ARDS.","Dampen the inflammatory response that is causing ARDS.","Prone positioning — Patient is turned onto the stomach. Brings in alveoli that are not used as often and are less likely to be damaged.","Patient is turned onto the stomach.","Brings in alveoli that are not used as often and are less likely to be damaged.","Therapeutic hypothermia — Turns the body's metabolic demands down very low for a period of time. Preserves function, especially in the brain, when PaO2 is very low.","Turns the body's metabolic demands down very low for a period of time.","Preserves function, especially in the brain, when PaO2 is very low.","ARDS Consequences","Problems Common After ARDS","PTSD, anxiety and depression: more than half of survivors, within two years of recovery.","Weakness, mobility issues, breathing issues.","Nausea, swelling, memory issues, fatigue.","Multidisciplinary Care","PT/OT: mobility, to prevent weakness.","RT and MD: ventilator management and sedation needs.","Nutrition: usually a Dobhoff tube and high levels of supplemental nutrition.","Psychosocial and mental health: early treatment and awareness, beginning in the ICU and continuing through discharge and beyond.","Outpatient critical care follow-up for long-term needs."]},{"s":"Pulmonary Embolism","a":"pulmonary-embolism","k":["Pulmonary Embolism","What Can Lodge in the Pulmonary Circulation","Clot from a DVT: most common, from a lower extremity.","Fat emboli.","Septic vegetation, from a heart valve in sepsis.","Catheter fragment, from a PICC or central line.","Amniotic fluid emboli in pregnant women.","Most PEs and DVTs are never identified, because the lungs have an enormous capacity to compensate. One that does present is usually very dangerous.","Massive PE: systolic hypotension below 90 related to the PE. Mortality rate of 30 to 80%.","Shunting, V/Q Mismatch and the Death Spiral","Shunting and V/Q Mismatch in a PE","Airflow is there. The patient can take breaths in.","Blood cannot get to the alveoli to perform gas exchange.","Blood flow beyond the occlusion stops, so there is no gas exchange beyond it.","Blood cannot flow freely through the pulmonary vasculature. Pulmonary pressures rise.","Increased Pulmonary Vascular Resistance","The lungs back up against the blockage.","RV Dilation","Increased RV wall stress and ineffective contraction. The septum bows into the LV and decreases LV filling.","Hypotension","RV hypoperfusion and ischemia, and more fluid given by the physician, dilate the RV further. Can end in right heart failure and death.","Two worries in a PE: hypotension, and no ability to perform gas exchange because a huge embolism is blocking blood flow.","PE Identification and Assessment Findings","Assessment Findings","Symptoms are usually vague and nondescript, with no hallmark sign.","Classic: pleuritic chest pain, shortness of breath, hypoxia.","Less common: tachypnea, crackles, accentuated S2 heart sound, tachycardia, fever.","Suspect a PE in any patient with respiratory distress that cannot be easily explained.","Pregnancy is a very high risk for PE.","Who is at high risk for a PE is taught in the Respiratory Diseases review","Chest x-ray — Does not show a PE. It shows ventilation, and ventilation is not the problem. Lungs look clear while the patient cannot breathe.","Does not show a PE. It shows ventilation, and ventilation is not the problem.","Lungs look clear while the patient cannot breathe.","D-dimer — Lab: a product of fibrin (clot) breakdown. Positive can indicate thrombus formation. Quick and easy. Points toward a PE but is not diagnostic alone.","Lab: a product of fibrin (clot) breakdown.","Positive can indicate thrombus formation.","Quick and easy. Points toward a PE but is not diagnostic alone.","V/Q scan — Noninvasive nuclear medicine scan of airflow and blood flow in the lungs. Can show dead space. Once the hallmark test. Little used now that CT is so available.","Noninvasive nuclear medicine scan of airflow and blood flow in the lungs. Can show dead space.","Once the hallmark test. Little used now that CT is so available.","Spiral CT scan — Preferred: the most accurate. Pregnant patients cannot have a CT scan.","Preferred: the most accurate.","Pregnant patients cannot have a CT scan.","Pulmonary angiography — Direct anatomical visualization. Invasive and difficult. Done in the cath lab, and not often.","Direct anatomical visualization.","Invasive and difficult. Done in the cath lab, and not often.","Usual order: chest x-ray shows nothing, D-dimer comes back positive, then a CT scan confirms the PE.","PE Treatment","Less Severe","At home on blood thinners: enoxaparin, or Xa inhibitors such as apixaban long term. Follow-up, and risk factors addressed.","Severe","IV heparin and a bolus immediately, then transition to enoxaparin. Warfarin started immediately as well, for long-term prevention and management.","Massive","Surgical or catheter embolectomy. EKOS catheter: ultrasound with heparin, placed right in the pulmonary circulation. ECMO when very severe.","IVC Filter, Oxygenation and Prevention","IVC filter: sits in the inferior vena cava to catch clots that dislodge from the lower extremities.","Optimize oxygenation acutely, as well as you can. Intubation may be needed but does not solve the problem, which is blood flow to the lungs.","Prevention: heparin shots or enoxaparin, SCDs, mobilizing.","Preventing DVTs and PEs is taught in the Respiratory Diseases review"]},{"s":"Other Respiratory Topics and Diagnostics","a":"other-topics","k":["Other Respiratory Topics and Diagnostics","Hemodynamic Monitoring in Respiratory Problems","Capnography — Monitors CO2 as end-tidal CO2, shown as a waveform. Read through a nasal cannula that measures CO2, or through the ventilator. Used to measure effective CPR.","Monitors CO2 as end-tidal CO2, shown as a waveform.","Read through a nasal cannula that measures CO2, or through the ventilator.","Used to measure effective CPR.","Arterial line — Usually in the radial artery. Gives frequent ABG access: PaO2, PaCO2 and pH as often as every hour or every 30 minutes.","Usually in the radial artery.","Gives frequent ABG access: PaO2, PaCO2 and pH as often as every hour or every 30 minutes.","PA pressure — Pulmonary artery pressure. Looks at pulmonary vascular resistance.","Pulmonary artery pressure. Looks at pulmonary vascular resistance.","SvO2 — Venous oxygen saturation, also called mixed venous: the oxygen content of blood returning to the right side of the heart after perfusing the body. Normal: 60 to 80%. The tissues take only about 20 to 40%. Low (30 to 50%): the body is…","Venous oxygen saturation, also called mixed venous: the oxygen content of blood returning to the right side of the heart after perfusing the body.","Normal: 60 to 80%. The tissues take only about 20 to 40%.","Low (30 to 50%): the body is extracting more. Not enough oxygen for a very high demand.","Medications Frequently Used in Respiratory Issues","Corticosteroids(inhaled, IV, PO) — Reduce airway inflammation. Also part of ARDS treatment. Inhaled treatment for bronchospasm. Inhaled epinephrine is used a lot.","Reduce airway inflammation. Also part of ARDS treatment.","Inhaled treatment for bronchospasm. Inhaled epinephrine is used a lot.","IV diuretics — Pulmonary congestion and edema. Furosemide, bumetanide.","Pulmonary congestion and edema.","Furosemide, bumetanide.","Antibiotics — Treatment of pneumonia. Prevention: preventative IV antibiotics for a very high-risk patient.","Treatment of pneumonia.","Prevention: preventative IV antibiotics for a very high-risk patient.","Benzodiazepines,opioids, paralytics — Sedation and pain management. Consider how each one affects the respiratory system.","Sedation and pain management.","Consider how each one affects the respiratory system.","Breathing treatments(nebulizer) — Albuterol, acetylcysteine, bronchodilators: open constricted airways. Given by respiratory therapy. The nurse knows when they are due, whether they were given, and how the patient responded.","Albuterol, acetylcysteine, bronchodilators: open constricted airways.","Given by respiratory therapy. The nurse knows when they are due, whether they were given, and how the patient responded."]}]},{"f":"day1-oxygen-therapy.html","t":"Oxygen Therapy and Mechanical Ventilation","secs":[{"s":"Oxygen Therapy and Mechanical Ventilation","a":"","k":["Day 1 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Oxygen delivery devices: which device is in use.","Monitoring oxygen therapy: how to tell whether it is effective.","Tracheostomy: how to care for a patient with a trach, the nurse's responsibility, how to suction and what indicates suctioning.","CPAP and BiPAP: noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation."]},{"s":"Oxygen Therapy Principles","a":"oxygen-therapy-principles","k":["Oxygen Therapy Principles","Principles of Oxygen Therapy","Needs an order, usually an admission order set to maintain SpO2. An emergency is the exception.","Start low and progress up as needed. A nasal cannula starts at 2 L, not 6. Use the lowest flow that maintains mental status and an acceptable SpO2.","Reported in percentages once past a nasal cannula at 6 L. Lowest 35%, highest 100%.","Humidification: used in all methods of O2 therapy, and required above 4 L/min. Monitor secretions to make sure it is adequate.","Inspired gas: about 37 degrees and 100% humidity, managed by respiratory therapy. Dry mucous membranes exchange gas less effectively.","Low Flow","Patient does the work to breathe. No pressure goes in.","Patient still pulls in some room air, so FiO2 is not as precisely controlled.","What most patients are on. An oxygen tent is low flow too.","More helpful for oxygenation.","High Flow","More precise and more controlled.","Better to supplement ventilation: more effective CO2 removal.","Stays constant when the breathing pattern is irregular, shallow or deep.","Typically needed when level of consciousness is significantly altered.","Oxygen Therapy Devices","High-Flow Nasal Cannula","Still a nasal cannula, but it gives positive pressure: it forces air into the airway.","14 to 40 L/min, possibly higher, at about 60 to 100% O2.","Helps CO2 expulsion, so a great device for irregular breathing patterns.","Better than CPAP or BiPAP for most patients who are hypoxic or hypercapnic.","Patient has to be able to maintain head and chin position.","Respiratory therapy sets it up and manages its temperature and humidification.","Nurse: check it is plugged in and working, and report the liters and the percentage shown on the machine.","Other oxygen therapy devices are taught in the Respiratory review","Charted oxygen has to be correct. Check it at the wall at every assessment, usually every 4 hours. Oxygen comes unplugged."]},{"s":"Noninvasive Positive-Pressure Ventilation","a":"nippv","k":["Noninvasive Positive-Pressure Ventilation","Mode of ventilation to support gas exchange in a patient without an endotracheal tube (not intubated). Mask can be whole face or just nose.","CPAP","Continuous positive airway pressure.","Same air pressure on inhale and exhale.","BiPAP","An inspiratory positive airway pressure (IPAP) and an expiratory positive airway pressure (EPAP).","Different pressures for inhalation and exhalation.","When It Is Used, and Its Limits","For many respiratory conditions, usually severe. First line to prevent intubation, or used right at extubation.","Very helpful for getting CO2 off. Not as helpful in oxygenation respiratory failure.","Patient has to be able to maintain their own airway.","Not a long-term solution, though it can run continuously in the hospital: no eating or drinking with the mask on, and a lot of aspiration risk.","Respiratory therapy coordinates and controls the modes, usually based on pressure control or PEEP.","Examples: after a procedure, or while too much fentanyl wears off.","CPAP and BiPAP are taught in the Respiratory review"]},{"s":"Airway Management","a":"airway-management","k":["Airway Management","Airway is the most important aspect of any oxygenation or ventilation management. The patient has to be able to maintain it, and it starts with positioning.","Positioning — Head tilt-chin lift or jaw thrust. Comes first: airway open and able to take in air. Not always enough with an altered level of consciousness or weakness.","Head tilt-chin lift or jaw thrust. Comes first: airway open and able to take in air.","Not always enough with an altered level of consciousness or weakness.","Oral airway(oropharyngeal airway) — Keeps the tongue from falling back and obstructing the pharynx. For acute depression of level of consciousness. Short term only. Often used in CPR until the patient can be intubated, with bag-mask ventila…","Keeps the tongue from falling back and obstructing the pharynx.","For acute depression of level of consciousness. Short term only.","Often used in CPR until the patient can be intubated, with bag-mask ventilation over the top of it.","Correct size needed. Too short does not work: it has to get up and around the back of the tongue.","Also helps with oral suctioning.","Nasopharyngeal airway(nasal trumpet) — Used to suction through: a red rubber or suction catheter goes down it when the cough is weak or secretions are hard to expel. Second choice when an oral airway will not work: the patient is seizing or…","Used to suction through: a red rubber or suction catheter goes down it when the cough is weak or secretions are hard to expel.","Second choice when an oral airway will not work: the patient is seizing or the jaw is too tight.","Better tolerated than an oral airway."]},{"s":"Artificial Airways","a":"artificial-airways","k":["Artificial Airways","Endotracheal Tube","Endotracheal Tube (ETT or ET Tube)","The most common way to secure an airway. Inserted through the mouth into the trachea. Nose to trachea is possible with oral trauma, but not common.","Time to intubate when CPAP, BiPAP or an oral airway does not give the support needed. A big indication: a lot of secretions that are hard to contain.","Sized #6 to #9. Most common: 7 to 7.5 for a female, 7.5 to 8 for a male.","Can usually be suctioned through if placed in the hospital, with a red rubber catheter or inline suctioning.","Preparing to Intubate: The Nurse's Responsibilities","A good working IV.","Anticipate IV sedation and analgesia, short term or long term. Being intubated is not comfortable.","Patient is typically paralyzed momentarily with rapid sequence intubation (RSI) drugs.","Radiopaque line — Shows on the chest x-ray that verifies where the tube is.","Shows on the chest x-ray that verifies where the tube is.","Cuff — At the end of the tube. Inflated once the tube is known to be in the trachea, not the esophagus. Seals the space between the tube and the trachea, and holds the tube in place. Cuff inflated: the patient cannot speak. Pressure too low…","At the end of the tube. Inflated once the tube is known to be in the trachea, not the esophagus.","Seals the space between the tube and the trachea, and holds the tube in place.","Cuff inflated: the patient cannot speak.","Pressure too low: the tube may move. Too great: damage to the surrounding tissue.","Pilot balloon — How the cuff is inflated and deflated. Air in it means the cuff is inflated. Nurse checks it at every assessment. Inflating, deflating and moving the tube belong to respiratory therapy.","How the cuff is inflated and deflated. Air in it means the cuff is inflated.","Nurse checks it at every assessment. Inflating, deflating and moving the tube belong to respiratory therapy.","Marking at the lip — Where the tube sits, in centimeters: \"26 at the lip\". Check it all the time.","Where the tube sits, in centimeters: \"26 at the lip\".","Check it all the time.","Confirming Placement After Intubation","Bilateral breath sounds, listened for by whoever intubated, often anesthesia or the ED.","CO2 sensed by a marker hooked up to the ET tube.","Chest x-ray ASAP.","Care of the Intubated Patient","Verify placement,every assessment — Same side of the mouth, same size, same centimeter marking at the lip. Chest x-rays are repeated routinely. The nurse does not interpret them, but reads the report that the tube is where it should be.","Same side of the mouth, same size, same centimeter marking at the lip.","Chest x-rays are repeated routinely. The nurse does not interpret them, but reads the report that the tube is where it should be.","Oral care — Patient cannot do it. Every 2 hours with an oral care kit: wash out the mouth and clean the tube. Prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP). Patients on a ventilator have a much higher risk of pneumonia.","Patient cannot do it. Every 2 hours with an oral care kit: wash out the mouth and clean the tube.","Prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP). Patients on a ventilator have a much higher risk of pneumonia.","Verify secure — Same place, held by a securement device, not tape on the face: fewer ulcerations. A patient fighting the tube can tongue it out, even unconsciously.","Same place, held by a securement device, not tape on the face: fewer ulcerations.","A patient fighting the tube can tongue it out, even unconsciously.","Cuff inflated — Pilot balloon is inflated.","Pilot balloon is inflated.","Tube clear — Nothing obstructing it. Patients still cough through the tube, and secretions sit at mouth level: suction them out. Humidity inside the tube is not a concern.","Nothing obstructing it. Patients still cough through the tube, and secretions sit at mouth level: suction them out.","Humidity inside the tube is not a concern.","Not bitten — Biting the tube is a big deal. Devices can be put in to prevent it. Often why sedation is needed, when the patient cannot follow directions.","Biting the tube is a big deal. Devices can be put in to prevent it.","Often why sedation is needed, when the patient cannot follow directions.","Sides of the mouth switched — Usually done by respiratory therapy. The nurse monitors that it is done. A tube left sitting on the tongue or in the mouth causes ulcerations.","Usually done by respiratory therapy. The nurse monitors that it is done.","A tube left sitting on the tongue or in the mouth causes ulcerations.","Secure to the ventilator — All connectors secure. They pop apart, and patients and family members pull them apart.","All connectors secure. They pop apart, and patients and family members pull them apart.","Skin intact — In the mouth and at the tape or securement device. Pressure ulcers are common here.","In the mouth and at the tape or securement device. Pressure ulcers are common here.","Endotracheal intubation is not a long-term solution: it invites pressure ulcers, VAP and injury to the trachea. A longer-term need means a transition to a tracheostomy.","Tracheostomy","Why it is placed — Preferred airway for a patient requiring a long-term airway. Vent dependence: the patient cannot get off the ventilator. A patient with a trach may or may not be ventilated. Weakness and a long-term inability to clear sec…","Preferred airway for a patient requiring a long-term airway.","Vent dependence: the patient cannot get off the ventilator. A patient with a trach may or may not be ventilated.","Weakness and a long-term inability to clear secretions, such as needing suction every 2 hours.","Cuff — Inflated when the patient is on the ventilator, as the ET tube cuff is. All the air given goes in, and exhaled CO2 does not go around the trach.","Inflated when the patient is on the ventilator, as the ET tube cuff is. All the air given goes in, and exhaled CO2 does not go around the trach.","Care — Oral care and trach care. Skin integrity around the trach is crucial.","Oral care and trach care. Skin integrity around the trach is crucial.","Suctioning — The connection to the ventilator can have its own suction port, as with an ET tube. Otherwise a red rubber catheter, which is a little gentler on the airway, or a clear plastic one.","The connection to the ventilator can have its own suction port, as with an ET tube.","Otherwise a red rubber catheter, which is a little gentler on the airway, or a clear plastic one.","The two types of trach, the nurse's job, the cuff, suctioning and what to keep at the bedside are taught in the Respiratory review"]},{"s":"Mechanical Ventilation","a":"mechanical-ventilation","k":["Mechanical Ventilation","Purpose of Mechanical Ventilation","Support the respiratory system.","Respiratory failure: the patient cannot clear their own secretions or cannot perform adequate gas exchange.","Minimizing oxygen demand: the patient is sedated and paralyzed, as in sepsis and trauma, even without a major respiratory problem. Sedation and paralysis lower level of consciousness, so the respiratory system has to be supported.","Positive pressure ventilation: air is forced into the lungs, not passively accepted, even when the patient fights it.","Six Things the Nurse Is Responsible for Knowing","Mode — Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV). Pressure regulated volume control (PRVC). Pressure controlled (PC). Volume controlled (VC). Pressure support (PS): the weaning mode. Airway pressure release ventilation (APRV).","Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV).","Pressure regulated volume control (PRVC).","Pressure controlled (PC).","Volume controlled (VC).","Pressure support (PS): the weaning mode.","Airway pressure release ventilation (APRV).","FiO2 — Percent of oxygen the patient gets. 35 to 100%. Usually 40% at the lowest, and changed in increments of 10.","Percent of oxygen the patient gets.","35 to 100%. Usually 40% at the lowest, and changed in increments of 10.","Tidal volume — How much air goes into the lungs with each breath. Set by the ventilator in some modes and controlled by the patient in others. Monitored closely. Common setting: 6 to 10 mL/kg.","How much air goes into the lungs with each breath.","Set by the ventilator in some modes and controlled by the patient in others. Monitored closely.","Common setting: 6 to 10 mL/kg.","Inspiratory pressure — How much force the ventilator needs to push air in. Tells about lung compliance and how sick the lungs are. Can be set, depending on the mode, but usually is not. Peak inspiratory pressure is watched, not set. 25 is p…","How much force the ventilator needs to push air in. Tells about lung compliance and how sick the lungs are.","Can be set, depending on the mode, but usually is not.","Peak inspiratory pressure is watched, not set. 25 is pretty normal.","Common pressure control setting: 5 to 25.","Rate — Respiratory rate. Controls CO2 levels. Set by the ventilator or by the patient, depending on the mode. A set rate is the minimum: the patient can breathe more often. Common setting: 10 to 25, raised for acidosis.","Respiratory rate. Controls CO2 levels.","Set by the ventilator or by the patient, depending on the mode. A set rate is the minimum: the patient can breathe more often.","Common setting: 10 to 25, raised for acidosis.","PEEP — Positive end-expiratory pressure: a little extra pressure at the end of expiration that pops the alveoli open, for alveoli that are collapsed or not working well. Promotes oxygenation and gas exchange, and helps more than tidal volum…","Positive end-expiratory pressure: a little extra pressure at the end of expiration that pops the alveoli open, for alveoli that are collapsed or not working well.","Promotes oxygenation and gas exchange, and helps more than tidal volume does.","Can be very dangerous: it forces the alveoli open and can cause a lot of trauma to the lungs. Tidal volume can be damaging too.","Kept at the lowest level possible.","Common setting: 5 to 20."]},{"s":"Complications of Mechanical Ventilation","a":"complications","k":["Complications of Mechanical Ventilation","ET tube out of position — Securement issue: the patient tongues the tube out, or it moves during transport. Monitored with chest x-rays and the ventilator settings. Tidal volumes and pressures changing for no clear reason: check whether the…","Securement issue: the patient tongues the tube out, or it moves during transport.","Monitored with chest x-rays and the ventilator settings.","Tidal volumes and pressures changing for no clear reason: check whether the tube has moved and whether the cuff is inflated.","Unplanned extubation — Most of the time an emergency. Sometimes it means the patient no longer needs to be intubated. Monitor oxygenation and think about positioning. Bag-mask ventilation at 100% is probably needed, depending on level of co…","Most of the time an emergency. Sometimes it means the patient no longer needs to be intubated.","Monitor oxygenation and think about positioning. Bag-mask ventilation at 100% is probably needed, depending on level of consciousness. A code may need to be called.","The nurse makes that decision. A patient who was on pressure support is probably doing okay.","Laryngeal and tracheal injury — Tube sits along the lining of the trachea. Tube movement (a thrashing head, the tube sliding up and down) and excess cuff pressure cause abrasions. Respiratory team monitors cuff pressures.","Tube sits along the lining of the trachea. Tube movement (a thrashing head, the tube sliding up and down) and excess cuff pressure cause abrasions.","Respiratory team monitors cuff pressures.","Oral mucosa damage — Oral care, rotating the tube, monitoring the skin. Moisturizer for the mouth helps.","Oral care, rotating the tube, monitoring the skin. Moisturizer for the mouth helps.","Pulmonary Issues Related to Mechanical Ventilation","Barotrauma — Pressure trauma: injury to the lungs from ventilation. Alveoli are injured or ruptured by excessive pressure and excessive inflation. Seen a lot in ARDS, where damaged alveoli need a lot of PEEP. Too high, too hard or too long …","Pressure trauma: injury to the lungs from ventilation. Alveoli are injured or ruptured by excessive pressure and excessive inflation.","Seen a lot in ARDS, where damaged alveoli need a lot of PEEP. Too high, too hard or too long causes it.","Air escapes: subcutaneous emphysema, pneumothorax.","Why PEEP is used carefully and tidal volumes are kept appropriate for the patient.","Oxygen toxicity — Too much oxygen leads to pathological changes. How bad depends on how long. Titrate the O2 down as quickly as possible, within safe margins, monitored by PaO2 on an ABG or by SpO2. Can mimic ARDS. Not seen much.","Too much oxygen leads to pathological changes. How bad depends on how long.","Titrate the O2 down as quickly as possible, within safe margins, monitored by PaO2 on an ABG or by SpO2.","Can mimic ARDS. Not seen much.","Respiratory acidosis or alkalosis — Ventilator controls gas exchange, so it can blow off too much CO2 or retain too much. ABGs, usually daily, for a ventilated patient, whether by ET tube or trach.","Ventilator controls gas exchange, so it can blow off too much CO2 or retain too much.","ABGs, usually daily, for a ventilated patient, whether by ET tube or trach.","Infection — VAP is the biggest concern, and the focus is prevention. VAP bundle: managing oral secretions, good oral care, a cuff inflated appropriately, effective suctioning, a quicker transition to a trach.","VAP is the biggest concern, and the focus is prevention.","VAP bundle: managing oral secretions, good oral care, a cuff inflated appropriately, effective suctioning, a quicker transition to a trach.","Dysphagia or aspiration — Intubated more than 24 hours: high risk of swallowing difficulty and aspiration after extubation. The throat and the gag take a while to readjust.","Intubated more than 24 hours: high risk of swallowing difficulty and aspiration after extubation.","The throat and the gag take a while to readjust."]},{"s":"Weaning Considerations","a":"weaning","k":["Weaning Considerations","Readiness and Weaning Trials","Approached in a systematic fashion, always thinking about readiness: can the patient breathe on their own?","Weaning trial: a pressure support trial, which makes the patient do the work. Done every day.","Not appropriate for a patient who is sedated and paralyzed.","Tried for a few minutes first, then for longer. Sometimes 15 to 30 minutes is enough.","Ready: awake, following instructions, squeezing hands, understanding what is going on. Assess the cough and how weak the patient is too.","Extubation","Emergency supplies ready.","Positioning: sitting as high as possible, head of bed at 90 degrees.","Calm environment, usually without family at the bedside.","Lights on.","Pain managed, but level of consciousness not depressed.","Nothing to drink until a bedside swallow is done, even after intubation for a procedure only. The patient will be thirsty. Start with ice chips and advance slowly to water as tolerated. Coughing: go back a step, and a speech evaluation may …","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day1-hematologic.html","t":"Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","secs":[{"s":"Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","a":"","k":["Day 1 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Blood components: how to interpret an H&H and what a CBC shows, as general principles.","Coagulation and medications: for a patient on anticoagulation such as a heparin drip, the nurse's responsibilities, the labs to draw, and the signs and symptoms of an adverse reaction.","Transfusions and blood products: the nurse's responsibility in transfusing, what to look for and how to do it, and the different blood products.","Blood transfusion reactions: what to look for, and telling a reaction that needs little from one that is a full stop.","Anemia signs and symptoms: recognizing them and pointing them out.","Bleeding precautions: what a patient on them needs."]},{"s":"Anemia","a":"anemia","k":["Anemia","Why Anemia Is a Big Deal","Low hemoglobin or decreased RBCs. RBCs (erythrocytes) carry oxygen on their hemoglobin.","Either the RBCs are lacking, after a large loss of blood volume, or the hemoglobin is.","Not enough oxygen circulates to feed the tissues, which survive on aerobic metabolism.","General Signs and Symptoms of All Types of Anemia","Tissue hypoxia — Hypoxia-related symptoms develop if the anemia is severe. Whole body: each tissue shows its own lack of oxygen. The GI tract cramps, the brain aches or goes lightheaded, the muscles cramp. — Fatigue, SOB, angina, intermitte…","Hypoxia-related symptoms develop if the anemia is severe.","Whole body: each tissue shows its own lack of oxygen. The GI tract cramps, the brain aches or goes lightheaded, the muscles cramp.","Selective increase in tissue perfusion — Selective vasoconstriction shunts blood to the priority organs: brain, heart, lungs. Skin is restricted first. Kidneys next. They take about 20% of cardiac output, so they take a big hit: acute kidne…","Selective vasoconstriction shunts blood to the priority organs: brain, heart, lungs.","Skin is restricted first.","Kidneys next. They take about 20% of cardiac output, so they take a big hit: acute kidney injury.","GI system after that: blood flow to the gut is cut.","Increased cardiac output — Heart works harder to deliver oxygen. Tachycardia first: the first compensatory mechanism of fluid volume deficit. Vessels clamp down to raise the blood pressure. Stroke volume rises if it can, which is unlikely w…","Heart works harder to deliver oxygen.","Tachycardia first: the first compensatory mechanism of fluid volume deficit.","Vessels clamp down to raise the blood pressure. Stroke volume rises if it can, which is unlikely when volume has been lost.","Angina: the heart is working harder and may not be getting the oxygen it needs.","Increased pulmonary function — Due to hypoxia: the respiratory rate rises to compensate. Often what patients talk about most: getting from the bed to the chair uses all their energy. — Tachypnea. SOB, exertional or at rest.","Due to hypoxia: the respiratory rate rises to compensate.","Often what patients talk about most: getting from the bed to the chair uses all their energy.","Increased RBC function — Longer-term compensatory mechanism. More erythropoietin is produced, so more RBCs are made. Reticulocytes are not as effective as fully mature RBCs. — Stress reticulocytosis: an increased reticulocyte count.","Longer-term compensatory mechanism.","More erythropoietin is produced, so more RBCs are made.","Reticulocytes are not as effective as fully mature RBCs.","Timing","All the symptoms of anemia are based on timing: how fast it came on, and whether the compensatory responses had time to kick in.","Sudden Loss","All of the symptoms, very quickly: short of breath immediately, headache, lightheadedness, altered level of consciousness.","Compensatory responses have not had time to kick in.","Example: trauma with liters of blood lost.","Slow Loss","May be sitting up and talking with the same H&H as the trauma patient, even a hemoglobin of 4 and a hematocrit of 12.","The body has had time to compensate.","Example: a slow GI bleed.","Mechanisms of Anemia","RBC production — Chronic kidney disease. Radiation that has irradiated the bone marrow.","RBC destruction — Hemolytic anemia: an autoimmune problem, or the wrong blood transfusion.","Blood loss — The most common: post-op, trauma, GI bleed.","The signs and symptoms are essentially the same whatever the mechanism."]},{"s":"The Bleeding Patient","a":"bleeding-patient","k":["The Bleeding Patient","Identify the source and treat the cause.","Chronic GI bleed — Surgery to clip the bleed. Or, on a blood thinner, reverse it and give product.","Trauma — The OR, to clamp and sew off the bleeding.","Chronic kidney disease — Erythropoietin supplementation.","Bone marrow not working — Medications to replace what is lost. A change to the radiation, or bone marrow replacement.","Coagulopathies","Abnormalities in one of the stages of clotting: the clotting cascade is altered.","Bleeding patients often develop one, and very quickly when actively bleeding, even on no anticoagulation. Seen in surgery and trauma.","Temperature plays a big role in clotting, which is why it is regulated in trauma and surgery.","Examine the bleeding times and coags of every bleeding patient.","No known reason for the bleeding: think of an inherited coagulopathy (hemophilia, von Willebrand syndrome) or an acquired one (a medication, chemo).","Corrected with vitamin K or a factor VII drug, usually as a one-time dose. Given quite a bit in trauma and post-op recovery.","What is concerning: perfusion to the vital organs. No urine output in 6 or 7 hours matters more than pale, cool skin, which is an expected finding.","Medications and the Bleeding Patient","Always know what medications the patient is on.","Bright red blood in the sputum from an ET tube, on a heparin drip: does the drip need to stop?","GI bleed with a history of atrial fibrillation: is the patient on warfarin or apixaban?","Known to be bleeding after surgery, with an enoxaparin order: question it. It is sometimes still given, because the risk of DVT is so high.","Why it matters: active bleeding can lead to hypovolemic shock, which is low blood volume shock.","What Can Affect Coagulation","Alcohol, poisons, substances — All can affect bleeding times and coagulation. High levels of ethanol make the blood thinner.","Blood clots (clotting disorders) — A history of blood clots is a huge risk for more, and a reason to worry about a clotting disorder.","Liver disease — The liver makes a lot of the clotting factors, so bleeding times are prolonged.","Active bleeding, especially with a lot of loss — Affects the coagulation cascade. Plasma and clotting factors often need replacing as well.","Cardiac bypass — A lot of blood thinner is given to go on bypass. Blood touching what it normally does not can develop a coagulopathy. The longer on bypass, the more risk.","Splenectomy — Higher risk for bleeding issues.","Radiation, for cancer or from exposure — Bone marrow is irradiated or suppressed, and it makes the platelets and RBCs. Very high risk.","Previous transfusions — A lot of transfusions means a lot more antibodies, and can also alter coagulation.","Infections and inflammatory states — Inflammatory responses can disrupt coagulation, as in DIC. See below."]},{"s":"Labs","a":"labs","k":["Labs","CBC and H&H — Can be deceptive. The same H&H can belong to a patient who is talking and to one who is unresponsive, so H&H and CBC are trended, not read as one value. H&H may not reflect the anemia accurately, because plasma is lost equally…","Can be deceptive. The same H&H can belong to a patient who is talking and to one who is unresponsive, so H&H and CBC are trended, not read as one value.","H&H may not reflect the anemia accurately, because plasma is lost equally.","As bleeding continues, fluid shifts intravascularly and dilutes the remaining blood cells.","IV fluid resuscitation dilutes further. It can make the hematocrit seem lower than it is, so look at the hemoglobin as well.","Full extent of an acute bleed cannot be evaluated for at least 48 to 72 hours.","Reticulocyte count — Becomes elevated within several days, about as quickly as the kidneys can help.","Becomes elevated within several days, about as quickly as the kidneys can help.","Bleeding time (coags) — PT and PTT. INR, but not generally in an acute bleed. Fibrinogen, a clotting product.","PT and PTT. INR, but not generally in an acute bleed.","Fibrinogen, a clotting product.","TEG (thromboelastogram) — Used to examine coagulopathy: the shape of the tracing tells which part of the clotting cascade is abnormal.","Used to examine coagulopathy: the shape of the tracing tells which part of the clotting cascade is abnormal."]},{"s":"Interventions for the Critically Ill Bleeding Patient","a":"bleeding-interventions","k":["Interventions for the Critically Ill Bleeding Patient","Assessment — Oxygenation, cardiac output, level of consciousness. Skin, urine, stool and secretions, for evidence of active bleeding. Mucous membranes and pulmonary secretions too. Dressings: bleeding into them can be insidious, and they ma…","Oxygenation, cardiac output, level of consciousness.","Skin, urine, stool and secretions, for evidence of active bleeding. Mucous membranes and pulmonary secretions too.","Dressings: bleeding into them can be insidious, and they may need to be weighed.","Transfuse — What products do they need? A TEG can show a need for something other than packed cells, or whether packed cells are needed at all.","What products do they need? A TEG can show a need for something other than packed cells, or whether packed cells are needed at all.","Fluid resuscitation — A bleeding patient is volume depleted and eventually becomes hypotensive. Blood is a finite resource, so fluid is given in addition.","A bleeding patient is volume depleted and eventually becomes hypotensive.","Blood is a finite resource, so fluid is given in addition.","Monitoring labs — Monitored closely.","Monitored closely.","Anticoagulants off — Off when appropriate: no heparin drip while actively bleeding. Enoxaparin may still be needed, depending on the situation.","Off when appropriate: no heparin drip while actively bleeding.","Enoxaparin may still be needed, depending on the situation.","Bleeding precautions — Depend more on the platelet count than on the H&H. A low H&H means the patient is actively bleeding, not necessarily at higher risk for bleeding.","Depend more on the platelet count than on the H&H.","A low H&H means the patient is actively bleeding, not necessarily at higher risk for bleeding.","Limit invasive procedures — Active bleeding or altered coagulation times: not the best time to place a long-term port. A central line that is needed still goes in. Expect the site to bleed, and be ready for it.","Active bleeding or altered coagulation times: not the best time to place a long-term port.","A central line that is needed still goes in. Expect the site to bleed, and be ready for it."]},{"s":"Blood Products","a":"blood-products","k":["Blood Products","Whole blood — RBCs, WBCs, platelets and plasma. — Severe trauma and blood loss. Typically reserved for trauma, the ICU and the ED. Research is finding it very beneficial, so that may change. — As fast as the patient can tolerate, for severe…","Severe trauma and blood loss.","Typically reserved for trauma, the ICU and the ED. Research is finding it very beneficial, so that may change.","Packed red blood cells (PRBCs) — Concentrated RBCs, separated from the platelets and plasma. — Anemia, GI bleed, trauma or surgery. Primarily what is replaced in a patient who is actively bleeding. — 2 to 4 hours on average.","Anemia, GI bleed, trauma or surgery.","Primarily what is replaced in a patient who is actively bleeding.","Platelets — Cell fragments for blood clotting. — Low platelets, leukemia, chemotherapy. Given only when the count is very low: usually under 50,000, 40,000 or even 20,000, depending on active bleeding. — 30 to 60 minutes, faster if needed.","Low platelets, leukemia, chemotherapy.","Given only when the count is very low: usually under 50,000, 40,000 or even 20,000, depending on active bleeding.","Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) — Liquid portion of blood. Contains water, electrolytes, proteins and antibodies. — Clotting disorders (DIC, TTP), severe blood loss. Replaces coagulation factors. — 1 to 4 hours on average.","Clotting disorders (DIC, TTP), severe blood loss.","Replaces coagulation factors.","Albumin — Plasma protein. No clotting factors. — Shock, burns, ascites. Increases blood volume: pulls fluid into the vascular space. — Individualized rate. Check hospital policy.","Shock, burns, ascites.","Increases blood volume: pulls fluid into the vascular space.","Cryoprecipitate (cryo) — Derived from plasma. Has some clotting factors. — Helps stop bleeding.","Helps stop bleeding.","In practice, platelets, plasma, cryo and albumin are often run wide open, unless the patient is at really high risk for pulmonary edema. PRBCs are different: start slow, typically over 2 hours, and done within 4.","What each product is, compatibility and giving PRBCs are taught in the Transfusions review"]},{"s":"Mass Transfusion","a":"mass-transfusion","k":["Mass Transfusion","1:1:1:1 ratio. One PRBC : one FFP : one platelet : one cryo. A mass transfusion is never just PRBCs.","Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP)","For a patient who is bleeding to death. Rapid transfusion, by the hospital's or the unit's protocol.","Ordered by an MD, in coordination with a doctor from the blood bank.","Blood does not have to be cross-matched initially.","Why the ratio: PRBCs alone fall out of wherever the patient is bleeding. FFP, platelets and cryo help clot the bleed while the PRBCs fill the tank.","Fibrinogen may be especially important in obstetric hemorrhage. Supplemented with fibrinogen concentrates or cryoprecipitate. These patients usually receive tranexamic acid as well.","A Level 1 or a Belmont gives a whole unit of blood in minutes, and warms it.","Most products are very cold, and a hypothermic patient's blood does not clot as effectively. Warm the patient, give warm products, replace clotting factors, replace volume."]},{"s":"Thrombocytopenia and HIT","a":"thrombocytopenia","k":["Thrombocytopenia and HIT","Platelet count — Thrombocytopenia is low platelets: technically under 150,000. Under 30,000 is critically low, and a risk for a spontaneous bleed. Transfusion usually waits until the count is much more critically low.","Thrombocytopenia is low platelets: technically under 150,000.","Under 30,000 is critically low, and a risk for a spontaneous bleed.","Transfusion usually waits until the count is much more critically low.","Causes — Usually a bone marrow issue: chemo, radiation, a bone marrow cancer. Increased destruction of platelets, or their sequestration or abnormal distribution. Also cardiac bypass surgery, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), sepsis, …","Usually a bone marrow issue: chemo, radiation, a bone marrow cancer.","Increased destruction of platelets, or their sequestration or abnormal distribution.","Also cardiac bypass surgery, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), sepsis, cancers.","Identify the cause and address it. HIT: stop the heparin.","Skin assessment — Petechiae, purpura, ecchymosis. Oozing mucous membranes: bleeding from the nose, mouth and lips.","Petechiae, purpura, ecchymosis.","Oozing mucous membranes: bleeding from the nose, mouth and lips.","Where to look — Wounds, stool, urine, nose and pulmonary secretions: where a bleed is most likely to be evident.","Wounds, stool, urine, nose and pulmonary secretions: where a bleed is most likely to be evident.","Bleeding precautions — Needed most of the time, especially with a really low count.","Needed most of the time, especially with a really low count.","Evaluate meds — Done by the team and pharmacy: nothing given should make a low count even lower. A medication that lowers platelets may need to stop, but sometimes it is necessary. Some antibiotics, antidysrhythmics such as amiodarone, and …","Done by the team and pharmacy: nothing given should make a low count even lower. A medication that lowers platelets may need to stop, but sometimes it is necessary.","Some antibiotics, antidysrhythmics such as amiodarone, and NSAIDs can cause thrombocytopenia. Avoided in these patients, and a possible cause when none is known.","Labs — Serotonin release assay (SRA): detects platelet activation in the presence of antibodies, so it tells whether this is HIT. Technically challenging, and not easy to obtain. Platelets trending down on a heparin drip: HIT is assumed. Dr…","Serotonin release assay (SRA): detects platelet activation in the presence of antibodies, so it tells whether this is HIT. Technically challenging, and not easy to obtain.","Platelets trending down on a heparin drip: HIT is assumed.","Dropping platelets: off anticoagulation, usually, especially when the count is really low.","Coags, H&H and CBC are monitored.","Thrombocytopenic Precautions","Toothettes for oral care.","No flossing.","Electric shaver only.","Gentle nose blowing and coughing.","Avoid invasive procedures: enemas, rectal thermometers, suppositories, needle sticks.","No tourniquets.","No tampons.","Assess fall risk.","No aspirin or aspirin products.","Avoid constipation.","Needle sticks are often not avoidable at a higher level of care. A central line or an arterial line saves frequent sticks, but those lines are at high risk of bleeding and oozing. Pressure dressings, or special dressings for oozing areas, g…"]},{"s":"DIC: Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation","a":"dic","k":["DIC: Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation","DIC is an emergency. The patient is having microemboli (small clots) and is also bleeding. A very dangerous scenario.","Trigger","Vessel injury from a disease state, tissue injury or a foreign body in the bloodstream. Trauma, burns and sepsis are the most common.","Microvascular Clotting","Exaggerated coagulation. Uncontrolled microclots lodge in the small vessels: organ ischemia and necrosis.","Clotting Factors Depleted","The body also senses the clots and tries to thin the blood, but the clots have already lodged.","Bleeding","Really prolonged bleeding time. Any area that can bleed will bleed.","Clinical Picture and Diagnosis of DIC","An organ dies when microclots clog its circulation: liver, kidneys, lungs, heart.","Skin, lungs and kidneys are most commonly affected.","Manifests as thrombophlebitis, pulmonary embolism, stroke, GI bleeding and renal failure, often all at once.","Skin manifestations: sometimes only on the hands, the feet and the tips of the toes.","Generally acute and life threatening. A chronic low-grade DIC exists.","Diagnosis: by risk factors, presentation and lab values (coags).","Nursing Interventions for DIC","Goal: preserve organ function.","Correct hypotension, hypoxemia, acidosis — Hypotension: low volume. Hypoxemia: active blood loss means fewer circulating RBCs. Acidosis: tissue ischemia related to organ death.","Hypotension: low volume.","Hypoxemia: active blood loss means fewer circulating RBCs.","Acidosis: tissue ischemia related to organ death.","Treat the underlying cause — Trauma or sepsis: try to reverse it.","Trauma or sepsis: try to reverse it.","Replace clotting factors and platelets — Platelets are the highest priority: they provide the platelet plug at the beginning of the clotting cascade. FFP replaces fibrinogen and other clotting factors.","Platelets are the highest priority: they provide the platelet plug at the beginning of the clotting cascade.","FFP replaces fibrinogen and other clotting factors.","Heparin — A potent thrombin inhibitor. In very low doses it blocks the clotting processes that initiate DIC and prevents further clotting. Counterintuitive in a patient who might be actively bleeding.","A potent thrombin inhibitor. In very low doses it blocks the clotting processes that initiate DIC and prevents further clotting.","Counterintuitive in a patient who might be actively bleeding.","Treat pain and address comfort — Very painful: organs and tissues are dying from the microclots. Pain is treated whether the patient is awake or sedated.","Very painful: organs and tissues are dying from the microclots.","Pain is treated whether the patient is awake or sedated.","Monitor labs — Monitored very closely.","Monitored very closely."]},{"s":"Care of the Immunocompromised Patient","a":"immunocompromised-patient","k":["Care of the Immunocompromised Patient","What it is — WBCs are either decreased or non-functioning. Usually asymptomatic until an infection. The patient may not look very sick, but a lot could go wrong. Congenital or acquired.","WBCs are either decreased or non-functioning.","Usually asymptomatic until an infection. The patient may not look very sick, but a lot could go wrong.","Congenital or acquired.","Who — Organ transplant, bone marrow transplant, chemo, AIDS.","Organ transplant, bone marrow transplant, chemo, AIDS.","Labs — CBC: the white blood cell count shows leukopenia. CD4 counts, for a solid organ transplant or HIV and AIDS.","CBC: the white blood cell count shows leukopenia.","CD4 counts, for a solid organ transplant or HIV and AIDS.","Infection — Watch for opportunistic infections, and for infections in general. No normal white blood cell response, so the patient cannot mount a response to an infection. Temperature: usually at the bottom of the list of vital signs, but n…","Watch for opportunistic infections, and for infections in general.","No normal white blood cell response, so the patient cannot mount a response to an infection.","Temperature: usually at the bottom of the list of vital signs, but not here. A very low white count makes a temp very concerning.","Infection and sepsis are the primary cause of death.","Precautions — Protective isolation or neutropenic precautions. Masks worn all the time. A HEPA filter room may be needed. Dietary restrictions, such as no raw fruits and vegetables.","Protective isolation or neutropenic precautions.","Masks worn all the time. A HEPA filter room may be needed.","Dietary restrictions, such as no raw fruits and vegetables.","Teaching for home — Wash fruits and vegetables. No raw fish or sushi. Cook meat thoroughly.","Wash fruits and vegetables. No raw fish or sushi. Cook meat thoroughly."]}]},{"f":"day1-acute-pancreatitis.html","t":"Acute Pancreatitis","secs":[{"s":"Acute Pancreatitis","a":"","k":["Day 1 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"Causes and Pathophysiology","a":"causes-and-pathophysiology","k":["Causes and Pathophysiology","Acute inflammatory disease of the pancreas. Most common causes: alcohol or biliary disease.","Causes of acute pancreatitis: I GET SMASHED. I: Idiopathic. GET: Gallstones, Ethanol (alcohol), Trauma. SMASHED: Steroid use, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion stings, Hypercalcemia and hypertriglyceridemia, Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancrea…","I: Idiopathic.","GET: Gallstones, Ethanol (alcohol), Trauma.","SMASHED: Steroid use, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion stings, Hypercalcemia and hypertriglyceridemia, Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), Drugs and medications.","How Acute Pancreatitis Occurs","Enzymes Leak","Pancreatic enzymes leak from inside the pancreas into other pancreatic tissue.","Autodigestion","The pancreas starts digesting itself, and its cells necrotize.","Inflammatory Mediators Released","Dying cells set off the inflammatory response and the complement cascade. Protective, but here it can make things worse.","Systemic Inflammatory State","Mediators in the bloodstream injure the vessels and further organ systems, usually the liver and kidneys. Starts localized, then can accelerate to systemic."]},{"s":"Signs and Symptoms","a":"signs-symptoms-and-severity","k":["Signs and Symptoms","Signs and Symptoms of Acute Pancreatitis","Pain: epigastric, mild to severe. Can radiate to the flank, the back or both. Typically constant, dull and aching, and worse lying supine.","Abdominal guarding and distension.","Cullen's sign and Grey Turner's sign.","Nausea and vomiting: heavy.","Dehydration: quick, because appetite is lost.","Fever, tachycardia, jaundice.","Restlessness."]},{"s":"Severity","a":"mild-and-severe","k":["Severity","Mild","Most cases. Self-limiting: the patient self-corrects.","Mild abdominal pain, with elevated amylase and lipase.","Treatment: bowel rest, IV hydration and pain management.","Severe","What typically leads to critical care.","Requires: close hemodynamic monitoring (blood pressure, heart rate, oxygen), nutritional support, infection control and pharmacologic support.","High risk for dysfunction of other organs. See Systemic Complications below."]},{"s":"Labs","a":"labs","k":["Labs","Lipase — Most diagnostic. Most indicative of pancreatitis and of its severity. Trended.","Amylase — Not as specific to the pancreas as lipase. Both are used, and both are trended.","Albumin — Trended to look at fluid volume status.","Electrolytes — Potassium, magnesium and calcium. Hypocalcemia can point to severe pancreatitis. A low calcium within 48 hours is a cue that the patient is at very high risk for severe problems.","CRP — An inflammatory marker that may also be checked."]},{"s":"Treatment of Acute Pancreatitis","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment of Acute Pancreatitis","Maintain respiratory function — Acute pancreatitis is one of the biggest risk factors for ARDS, because of the systemic inflammatory response.","Acute pancreatitis is one of the biggest risk factors for ARDS, because of the systemic inflammatory response.","Fluid replacement — Huge risk for hypovolemic shock. Fluid collects in the retroperitoneal space and cavity, and can sequester as much as one third of the plasma volume. Shock and hypovolemia are the primary cause of death. Usually replaced…","Huge risk for hypovolemic shock. Fluid collects in the retroperitoneal space and cavity, and can sequester as much as one third of the plasma volume.","Shock and hypovolemia are the primary cause of death.","Usually replaced with colloids, such as albumin, which pull volume back into the vascular system. LR replaces volume as well.","Electrolyte replacement — Potassium, calcium and magnesium: monitored, and replaced as needed.","Potassium, calcium and magnesium: monitored, and replaced as needed.","Glucose — Can cause hyperglycemia. Pancreatitis that is severe enough can cause hypoglycemia instead. Close monitoring. An insulin drip or a dextrose drip may be needed, depending on severity.","Can cause hyperglycemia. Pancreatitis that is severe enough can cause hypoglycemia instead.","Close monitoring. An insulin drip or a dextrose drip may be needed, depending on severity.","Pain management — Quite a bit is needed. Keep a close eye on it.","Quite a bit is needed. Keep a close eye on it.","Abdominal compartment syndrome — High risk, because fluid shifts into the abdominal compartment. Treated with surgical decompression.","High risk, because fluid shifts into the abdominal compartment.","Treated with surgical decompression.","DIC and hemorrhage — High risk for both. Hemorrhage: especially in hemorrhagic pancreatitis. Necrotizing pancreatitis or a pseudocyst can also develop.","High risk for both.","Hemorrhage: especially in hemorrhagic pancreatitis. Necrotizing pancreatitis or a pseudocyst can also develop.","ARDS is taught in Respiratory and Progressive Issues in Critical Care","Abdominal compartment syndrome is taught in GI Critical Care Issues","Minimize Pancreatic Stimulation","Rest the GI Tract","For every case, mild or severe.","Why: the pancreas senses eating and releases its enzymes, such as trypsin, to digest the food. Rest turns that off, so no food or drink (NPO).","Duration: depends on the patient. Usually until hemodynamically stable, with pancreatic enzymes trending down.","Nutrition: severe cases usually need TPN.","NG tube placement may be needed.","Drug therapy, often needed: antacids, PPIs, anticholinergics."]},{"s":"Systemic Complications","a":"complications","k":["Systemic Complications","Severe acute pancreatitis can affect every organ system. Assess astutely, trend the findings, and keep a close eye on fluid status and electrolyte status.","Pulmonary — Hypoxemia, insufficiency or failure, pleural effusions, ARDS. A really big impact on oxygenation.","Cardiovascular — Pericardial effusion, from the fluid volume shifting.","Hypovolemic shock — From the fluid shifting. See Fluid Replacement above.","Sepsis — Higher risk of infection with a necrotizing, hemorrhagic pancreas. Can look like peritonitis or sepsis.","Renal — Acute kidney injury, from the fluid shifting.","Neurologic — Decreased LOC. Severe cases usually end up paralyzed and sedated on a ventilator until the pancreas settles.","Hematologic — Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).","Metabolic — Hyperglycemia. See Glucose above.","DIC is taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Prevent and Treat Complications","Early recognition and treatment — With known severe acute pancreatitis, trend the respiratory assessments and all the electrolytes.","With known severe acute pancreatitis, trend the respiratory assessments and all the electrolytes.","Close multi-system monitoring — Urine output, liver enzymes and the respiratory system: high risk for AKI and ARDS. Electrolytes, glucose, insulin. Pulmonary hygiene, at a high level. Cardiac monitoring. Antibiotic therapy.","Urine output, liver enzymes and the respiratory system: high risk for AKI and ARDS.","Electrolytes, glucose, insulin.","Pulmonary hygiene, at a high level.","Cardiac monitoring.","Antibiotic therapy.","Drainage of abscess material — CT-guided drain placement. Fluid culture and gram stain.","CT-guided drain placement.","Fluid culture and gram stain.","Debridement of the pancreas — Surgical. Necrotic tissue, abscess and fluid collections.","Surgical.","Necrotic tissue, abscess and fluid collections.","Partial pancreas resection — May be needed, in addition to resting the gut.","May be needed, in addition to resting the gut."]}]},{"f":"day2-cardiovascular.html","t":"Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","a":"","k":["Day 2 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Normal function of the heart: what the heart is responsible for. What low cardiac output means, and what it looks like.","Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, atherosclerosis: what each means, and why it matters.","Coronary artery disease (CAD), and STEMI and NSTEMI care: what is going on in CAD, its risk factors, why it matters so much, and how atherosclerosis relates to it.","Heart failure: chronic heart failure, left-sided versus right-sided.","Valve disease: the valve disorders from earlier classes.","Cardiac rhythm abnormalities: basic rhythm identification.","Pharmacology for heart failure, CAD and hypertension: the drug matrices. What beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and digoxin do.","Cardiovascular diagnostics: what an echo and a TEE (transesophageal echo) each show, and why one over the other. The labs: troponin, BMP, and the electrolytes that matter in cardiovascular disease.","Post-cardiac cath care: checking the access site and the perfusion in the hand.","Pleural chest tubes: the chest tubes that sit around the lungs."]},{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS)","a":"acs","k":["Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS)","Hypertension, hyperlipidemia and atherosclerosis are risk factors for every other cardiovascular condition.","From Atherosclerosis to an MI","Endothelial Injury","Atherosclerosis is an inflammatory disease. The lining of the vessels is damaged.","Fatty Plaques","Fatty plaques and complex lesions form in the vessels. Related to hypertension, smoking, diabetes and hyperlipidemia.","Plaque Rupture","A ruptured plaque interacts with the blood and can cause a thrombus.","Coronary Artery Occluded","Chest pain or an MI, depending on how big the occlusion is, where it is, and whether collateral circulation surrounds it.","What ACS Is","The acute version of CAD. CAD can lead to ACS: the patients coming to the hospital with chest pain, usually related to CAD.","A problem with myocardial oxygen supply and demand. A plaque or thrombus blocks a coronary artery. The area distal to it gets no oxygen or nutrients and starts dying: ischemia of the myocardium.","Decrease demand: rest, which is why stable angina resolves. Increase supply: a vasodilator opens the coronary vessels, giving blood more space around the plaque. Ultimately the blockage usually needs fixing.","Collateral circulation: sometimes very effective and sometimes not, depending on where it is trying to feed. Not everyone has it.","An acute MI falls under ACS. Classification is no longer based on EKG changes. STEMI versus NSTEMI is still heard, but is becoming antiquated.","Stable Angina","Relieved with rest.","Associated with CAD, but not as concerning as unstable.","Example: chest pain while mowing the yard that resolves after sitting down for a few minutes.","Unstable Angina","Does not resolve with rest. Can respond to nitrates. Ends up in the ED.","Increased risk of an acute MI.","Usually gets a cardiac cath, to evaluate the CAD and whether it needs an intervention.","Complications of ACS","Dysrhythmias: very common. Dying cells include those that conduct the heart's electrical impulses.","Heart failure: as more cells die.","Cardiogenic shock.","Thromboembolism, rupture of a portion of the heart, pericarditis: much more common with a true acute MI than with ACS alone.","Treatment Goals for ACS","Establish reperfusion: remove or bypass the plaque, or place a stent.","Reduce infarct size.","Prevent and treat complications: heart failure, dysrhythmias, cardiogenic shock.","Provide support and education."]},{"s":"Immediate Treatment of an MI","a":"mi-immediate-treatment","k":["Immediate Treatment of an MI","MONA-TAS: Morphine, Oxygen, Nitroglycerin, Aspirin, Thrombolytics, Anticoagulants, Sedatives. It replaced MONA-B: beta blockers are no longer given.","Morphine — Reduces pain and anxiety. A vasodilator. Reduces heart workload by reducing preload and afterload, so reduces myocardial oxygen demand.","Oxygen — Improves oxygenation of the ischemic heart tissue, so improves myocardial oxygen supply. 2 to 4 L/min by nasal cannula.","Nitroglycerin — Sublingual, for ACS, MI and angina. A potent vasodilator: opens the coronary arteries to get blood flow around the blockage.","Aspirin — Antiplatelet: decreases platelet aggregation around the clot. Inhibits COX-1, so no TXA2 forms. Chewed: a baby aspirin or a 325 mg aspirin.","Thrombolytics — Given to some MI patients, such as tPA. Dissolve the thrombus and minimize the size of the infarct.","Anticoagulants — Given to some MI patients, to keep the clot from getting bigger and blocking the coronary arteries. An IV heparin drip is very common.","Sedatives — Generally diazepam. Rests a patient made very anxious by the MI, and reduces oxygen demand on the heart."]},{"s":"Interventions for ACS: Cardiac Cath and CABG","a":"acs-interventions","k":["Interventions for ACS: Cardiac Cath and CABG","Cardiac Cath (Left-Sided)","What it is: where almost every MI goes first. Visualizes the coronary arteries to see the blockage. Through the radial or femoral artery, now almost always radial, for an easier recovery.","Chosen when: vessels are partly occluded, even several, such as 70 to 90%. A stent pushes the fatty plaque against the vessel wall. Balloon angioplasty is less common now that stents are so effective.","Bleeding: high risk at the site. Pressure goes on the artery, often from a device that slowly releases air. Check that the hand is still perfused.","Rhythm issues: the heart was irritated and is already ischemic: a perfect storm. On tele, watched very closely.","AKI: contrast dye is nephrotoxic. Watch urine output, BUN and creatinine.","Overall: very safe.","Teaching: stents need long-term anticoagulation, such as apixaban or warfarin, so blood flows freely through them.","Bypass Surgery (CABG)","What it is: coronary artery bypass grafting. Open heart surgery: the sternum is opened. A graft from the saphenous vein of the leg or the internal mammary artery is attached before the blockage and distal to it. Minimally invasive CABG exis…","Chosen when: the disease is too severe to stent, such as two or three vessels 100% occluded.","Bleeding: high risk. Blood must be very thin to go on bypass. Bleeding from the anastomosis sites and the chest wall. Chest tubes are monitored closely.","Rhythm issues: high risk. The heart was handled and has had an MI. AFib and PVCs are very common. Continuous tele, one nurse to one patient.","Also at risk for: acute heart failure, pericarditis, pericardial effusion, cardiac tamponade.","Overall: very invasive and painful, and very successful. Most complications come from the surgery itself, opening the chest and going on bypass, not from the grafts.","Post-cath care of the access site and the limb is taught in the Cardiovascular review"]},{"s":"Post-Op Cardiac Surgery Considerations","a":"post-op-cardiac-surgery","k":["Post-Op Cardiac Surgery Considerations","Chest tubes — Mediastinal chest tubes, not the pleural tubes around the lungs. Blood has no place to sit, so even a slow ooze must drain. Bright red at first. Serous drainage too, from the inflammation. Check color, rate and amount: every 1…","Mediastinal chest tubes, not the pleural tubes around the lungs. Blood has no place to sit, so even a slow ooze must drain.","Bright red at first. Serous drainage too, from the inflammation.","Check color, rate and amount: every 15 minutes for the first hour, every 30 minutes for the next few hours, then every 1 to 2 hours.","A sudden dump: watch the blood pressure.","Temporary pacers — Placed because the heart was handled and is at risk of ischemia. The patient can turn bradycardic.","Placed because the heart was handled and is at risk of ischemia. The patient can turn bradycardic.","Hypotension and hypovolemia — A lot of blood is lost on bypass and in surgery. Fluid resuscitation, blood transfusions, vasopressors to raise the blood pressure, positive inotropes.","A lot of blood is lost on bypass and in surgery.","Fluid resuscitation, blood transfusions, vasopressors to raise the blood pressure, positive inotropes.","Invasive lines — A big central line, usually an arterial line, and a couple of peripherals for the fluid resuscitation.","A big central line, usually an arterial line, and a couple of peripherals for the fluid resuscitation.","Hemodynamic monitoring — Cardiac output, cardiac index, PA pressures, SvO2 (mixed venous saturation), with the blood pressure: is the heart working effectively after surgery? Not at 100% right out of surgery, as if still under anesthesia. C…","Cardiac output, cardiac index, PA pressures, SvO2 (mixed venous saturation), with the blood pressure: is the heart working effectively after surgery?","Not at 100% right out of surgery, as if still under anesthesia. Closer to normal after about 2 hours.","Labs — H&H, kidney labs, electrolytes, bleeding times. Electrolyte imbalances are likely after so much fluid. Bleeding times, because the blood was very thin in the OR.","H&H, kidney labs, electrolytes, bleeding times.","Electrolyte imbalances are likely after so much fluid. Bleeding times, because the blood was very thin in the OR.","Urine output — One of the best indicators that the heart is working: the kidneys take a lot of cardiac output. Goal: 30 mL an hour.","One of the best indicators that the heart is working: the kidneys take a lot of cardiac output.","Goal: 30 mL an hour.","Pain — Often a lot. Balanced against getting the patient extubated.","Often a lot. Balanced against getting the patient extubated.","Extubation and respiratory management — Out of the OR intubated and sedated. Extubate, with ventilator management if needed.","Out of the OR intubated and sedated. Extubate, with ventilator management if needed.","Wound management — The sternotomy: closed, not bleeding much, not opening, not hot, not red.","The sternotomy: closed, not bleeding much, not opening, not hot, not red.","Temporary pacers and pacer wires are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review"]},{"s":"Cardiac Tamponade","a":"cardiac-tamponade","k":["Cardiac Tamponade","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. Blood accumulates in the pericardial space and constricts the heart from beating effectively. It can progress to circulatory shock and cardiac arrest.","Most common after a CABG or any open heart surgery. Also seen after a cardiac cath.","Signs After Cardiac Surgery","Hypotension and tachycardia: hard to see after surgery, when some of both is expected. Did it happen suddenly?","Abrupt stop in chest tube output, with the blood pressure dropping.","Muffled heart sounds: as if through a wall or through water. They can stop being heard at all.","JVD can occur.","H&H drop with no obvious sign of bleeding. H&H is trended every 1 to 2 hours after cardiac surgery.","Example: steady chest tube output, then none for the past hour. Or a hemoglobin of 14, then suddenly 10, with no big dump from the chest tubes.","Signs Without Cardiac Surgery","Chest pain, dyspnea, fainting or lightheadedness, anxiety, restlessness.","These tell little after cardiac surgery: sedation, intubation, and chest pain and dyspnea from the surgery hide them.","Beck's triad: low blood pressure, distended jugular veins (JVD), muffled heart sounds. Not everyone with cardiac tamponade shows all three. Tachycardia and shortness of breath may appear too.","Pericardial Effusion","Pericardial fluid builds up slowly over time.","The pericardium stretches out to hold bigger volumes of fluid without compressing the heart.","Symptoms: chest pain, shortness of breath, compression of nearby structures.","Acute Pericardial Tamponade","Sudden fluid accumulation: fluid, blood or air.","The pericardium cannot adjust, so the pressure inside the sac rises dramatically.","Causes: chest trauma, a ruptured aorta, a ruptured ventricle after a heart attack.","Treating Cardiac Tamponade","Blocked chest tube: the surgeons may \"milk\" it, to clear a clot and let the blood drain.","True tamponade after cardiac surgery: usually back to the OR, to open the chest and find the bleed. A pericardial window and a new chest tube may go in.","Not from cardiac surgery: pericardiocentesis (needle drainage of the blood) or a pericardial window."]},{"s":"Heart Failure in the ICU and Progressive Care","a":"acute-heart-failure","k":["Heart Failure in the ICU and Progressive Care","New Onset","Sudden: the heart abruptly stops working effectively.","After a big MI, or after cardiac surgery.","Likely to have heart failure for the rest of their life.","Exacerbation of Chronic HF","Progressively worse until more symptomatic, then to the ED.","Example: a few days of really salty food.","In the ICU, it is both sides. Left-sided failure is the more common, related to hypertension and diabetes. But the acutely ill patient shows pulmonary and body symptoms together, at the extreme.","What Heart Failure Looks Like in the ICU","Crackles — Fluid overloaded: the patient sounds wet. — Diuretics","Severe edema — The right side backs up the same way, into the body's veins: fluid in the tissues. 3+ or 4+ pitting edema, ascites. — Diuretics","Dysrhythmias — Reduced cardiac output reduces coronary artery flow, and the cells that conduct the heart's impulses die: an oxygen supply issue. — Antidysrhythmics, to control the rhythm and rate","SOB — The biggest thing seen, with fatigue. Pulmonary edema, the main thing the patient comes in with: the left ventricle cannot pump effectively, so blood backs up into the lungs and fluid is pushed into the alveoli. Not enough cardiac out…","Low BP — Cardiogenic shock: a floppy muscle that cannot generate the blood pressure. — Positive inotropes, vasopressors","Fatigue — Not enough cardiac output. — Positive inotropes","Treating Acute Heart Failure","Keep the patient dry: less fluid means less cardiac workload.","Ultrafiltration — Pulls fluid off, to keep the patient dry.","Mechanical circulatory support — An LVAD or ECMO. See below.","Biventricular pacing — Makes the left and right ventricles contract together. A really weak heart's ventricles sometimes do not, which is why extra heart sounds are often heard in acute HF.","Sedation — Decreases oxygen demand, so the heart does not work as hard.","Four Pillars of Heart Failure Medication","ACE inhibitor or ARB.","Aldosterone antagonist, such as spironolactone. Also a potassium-sparing diuretic, but given in HF for its aldosterone action.","Beta blocker.","SGLT2 inhibitor.","All four: started in the hospital, and taken for life.","Positive Inotropes","Milrinone (a phosphodiesterase inhibitor) and dobutamine (a beta agonist), IV. Cardiac glycosides such as digoxin are positive inotropes too.","Stronger heart muscle contraction raises stroke volume, which raises cardiac output.","Help the low blood pressure and the fatigue.","Milrinone is the more frequent. A patient can even go home on it.","IV Diuretics","Furosemide, bumetanide.","Get the fluid off: less pulmonary edema, less pitting edema.","Less workload, so the heart works less hard.","For symptom management and pulmonary congestion. They do not prolong life, but are often the primary treatment in acute HF.","Mechanical Circulatory Support","LVAD","For a left ventricle so sick from chronic or acute heart failure.","Pulls the blood out and bypasses it back to the aorta.","Some baseline function is needed, but the device works as the left ventricle.","VA ECMO (Veno-Arterial)","Acute support.","Takes the blood out, oxygenates it, and puts it back in.","The heart still needs to pump, but ECMO effectively does the work and creates the cardiac output.","Raising the blood pressure in acute HF: not fluid resuscitation. Vasopressors and positive inotropes make the heart work harder and constrict the vessels."]},{"s":"Pericarditis","a":"pericarditis","k":["Pericarditis","What it is — Inflammation of the pericardium.","Inflammation of the pericardium.","Causes — Often a consequence of an MI or open heart surgery. Also chest trauma, infection, radiation therapy and cancer (two big ones), a chronic inflammatory condition such as lupus.","Often a consequence of an MI or open heart surgery.","Also chest trauma, infection, radiation therapy and cancer (two big ones), a chronic inflammatory condition such as lupus.","Presentation — Very painful. Mimics MI pain, so an MI is ruled out: troponin, maybe a bedside echo. Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety.","Very painful. Mimics MI pain, so an MI is ruled out: troponin, maybe a bedside echo.","Dyspnea, fatigue, anxiety.","Findings — Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark diagnostic key. A scratchy sound with each heartbeat, one that can actually be heard. EKG changes: ST elevation. Increased white cell count and sed rate: an inflammatory problem.","Pericardial friction rub: the hallmark diagnostic key. A scratchy sound with each heartbeat, one that can actually be heard.","EKG changes: ST elevation.","Increased white cell count and sed rate: an inflammatory problem.","Diagnosis — An echo: a TTE (transthoracic echo), the normal bedside echo. After cardiac surgery an MI can also occur, so know what else is going on, and listen for the friction rub.","An echo: a TTE (transthoracic echo), the normal bedside echo.","After cardiac surgery an MI can also occur, so know what else is going on, and listen for the friction rub.","Treatment — Pretty easy to treat: anti-inflammatories. Ibuprofen, colchicine, and most of the time ketorolac for a big one. Can cause a tamponade if not monitored closely: treat it once it is noticed.","Pretty easy to treat: anti-inflammatories. Ibuprofen, colchicine, and most of the time ketorolac for a big one.","Can cause a tamponade if not monitored closely: treat it once it is noticed."]},{"s":"Valvular Disease and Infective Endocarditis","a":"valves-and-endocarditis","k":["Valvular Disease and Infective Endocarditis","Valvular Disease","Two most common: aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation. Others exist.","Can also be congenital.","Treatment: valve replacement, aortic or mitral. Open heart surgery.","After a valve replacement: essentially the same post-op risks and care as after a CABG.","Valve problems, their causes and their treatment are taught in the Heart Disease review","Infective Endocarditis","Causes — IV drug use: dirty needles put bacteria in the bloodstream. A growing problem in the United States. Sepsis.","IV drug use: dirty needles put bacteria in the bloodstream. A growing problem in the United States.","Sepsis.","Vegetations on heart valves — Bacteria lodge on the delicate valve tissue and damage it.","Bacteria lodge on the delicate valve tissue and damage it.","Which valve — Tricuspid: very common with IV drug use. It is the first valve the infected blood meets. Mitral: also one of the more common, and the more common with rheumatic heart disease or a congenital cause.","Tricuspid: very common with IV drug use. It is the first valve the infected blood meets.","Mitral: also one of the more common, and the more common with rheumatic heart disease or a congenital cause.","Presentation — A really sick infection: high temp, chills, diaphoresis, feeling like they are dying. Heart failure signs and symptoms: low blood pressure, poor perfusion. Dysrhythmias.","A really sick infection: high temp, chills, diaphoresis, feeling like they are dying.","Heart failure signs and symptoms: low blood pressure, poor perfusion.","Dysrhythmias.","Treatment — Valves not too damaged: continuous IV antibiotics for 2 to 6 weeks, watching the valve leaflets. Often valve replacement.","Valves not too damaged: continuous IV antibiotics for 2 to 6 weeks, watching the valve leaflets.","Often valve replacement.","Lifelong risk — Of it happening again. Prophylactic antibiotics before certain procedures.","Of it happening again. Prophylactic antibiotics before certain procedures."]},{"s":"Aortic Aneurysm and Aortic Dissection","a":"aortic-aneurysm-and-dissection","k":["Aortic Aneurysm and Aortic Dissection","Aortic Aneurysm","What it is: the aortic wall becomes very weak and a portion blows out. A false aneurysm is a complete tear in the arterial wall.","Where: the thoracic aorta (ascending, arch, descending) or the abdomen. AAA is the most common type.","Causes: atherosclerosis, the most common, damages the wall. Degeneration of elastin and collagen.","Risk factors: male, older, smoking or a history of it: the three huge ones. Also hyperlipidemia, hypertension, diabetes.","Symptoms: usually asymptomatic until it ruptures. Sometimes back or abdominal pain with a AAA, if it restricts blood flow or presses on something.","Diagnosis: CT angiogram.","Treatment: the goal is to avoid rupture. By size: watched, or surgically repaired. Smoking cessation.","Ruptured: very hypovolemic, chest pain, diaphoretic, looks like they are going to die. A big rupture is usually fatal.","Aortic Dissection","What it is: a tear in the intimal layer creates a false lumen. The vessel is still intact, but blood goes down the wrong side, between its layers.","Where: anywhere along the aorta.","Symptoms: none until it is too late, usually stage 3. Then sudden severe chest or back pain: tearing, ripping or sharp, like being pulled apart from the inside.","Pulse deficit, and different blood pressures on the extremities, because blood is flowing into the false lumen.","Treatment: a life-threatening emergency. Control the BP, HR and pain, then emergent surgery: a Dacron graft. A lot of blood replacement.","Stages of Aortic Dissection","Stage 1: Rupture of Intima","A tear in the intimal layer. Blood starts going into a false lumen.","Stage 2: Dissection of Media","The layers pull apart further, and the false lumen extends.","Stage 3: Rupture of Vessel","The outer layer is so thin and weak that it ruptures: full-blown dissection and rupture.","After a Rupture or a Repair","Always to the ICU.","Blood flow is lost below the rupture or the dissection, to the kidneys, liver and gut, for a significant time. Dead gut and bowel, lower extremity perfusion issues, severe AKI and CKD, liver damage, pancreatic issues.","Post-op care is like after a CABG, with chest tubes. Pacer wires are less common.","Monitor for organ damage: BUN and creatinine, AST and ALT, urine output, jaundice and bilirubin.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day2-rhythms-pacemakers.html","t":"Rhythms & Pacemaker Review","secs":[{"s":"Rhythms & Pacemaker Review","a":"","k":["Day 2 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Rhythm interpretation: sinus rhythm, sinus arrhythmia, sinus tach, sinus brady, atrial fibrillation (AFib), atrial flutter, PACs (premature atrial contractions), PVCs (premature ventricular contractions), ventricular tachycardia (VTach), to…","Common causes of dysrhythmias: and caring for the patients who have them.","Cardiac output: a basic understanding. What the rhythms do to it.","Treatment for dysrhythmias: the pharmacology, electrolytes, other nursing interventions.","Amiodarone, beta blockers (metoprolol, labetalol), adenosine, atropine: what each drug is, from the drug matrices.","Pacemakers and ICDs (implantable cardioverter defibrillators): their restrictions."]},{"s":"Causes Beyond the Heart","a":"dysrhythmia-causes","k":["Causes Beyond the Heart","Many causes of dysrhythmias are not cardiac. A sick heart is more likely to get rhythm problems, after an MI, ACS or cardiac surgery, but a whole host of other conditions cause them too.","Sinus tach — Fever? A big cause. Treat it with acetaminophen? Fluid volume deficit? Tachycardia is its number one sign. Is the patient dry? Give fluid?","Fever? A big cause. Treat it with acetaminophen?","Fluid volume deficit? Tachycardia is its number one sign. Is the patient dry? Give fluid?","Sinus brady — A beta blocker overdose?","A beta blocker overdose?","PACs, PVCs, short runs of VTach — Electrolytes? Often the cause, especially with PVCs and short runs. First: is the blood pressure okay, and is the patient tolerating it? Then a full CMP: potassium, magnesium, calcium.","Electrolytes? Often the cause, especially with PVCs and short runs.","First: is the blood pressure okay, and is the patient tolerating it? Then a full CMP: potassium, magnesium, calcium.","The causes of each rhythm, cardiac and not, are taught in the Dysrhythmias review"]},{"s":"Rhythms and Cardiac Output","a":"rhythms-and-cardiac-output","k":["Rhythms and Cardiac Output","Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. The cardiac rhythm is a big indicator of it. Cardiac output drives perfusion, which gets oxygen and nutrients to the cells and every organ system.","Three Pillars of Stroke Volume","Contractility — Raises stroke volume.","Preload — Fluid status: how much fluid is in the left ventricle before it contracts, which depends on its refilling. Raises stroke volume.","Afterload — How hard the heart has to push. Raising it lowers stroke volume, and too high or too low, it affects cardiac output. Measured as SVR (systemic vascular resistance). At the bedside, mostly think blood pressure.","Contractility, and how to raise it, is taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is taught in Hemodynamics","All rhythms decrease cardiac output: think about severity. A PAC every couple of beats is not as significant as a PVC every other beat, or VFib.","What each rhythm does to cardiac output is taught in the Dysrhythmias review","Rhythms and the Other Organ Systems","How Other Systems Affect the Rhythm","Hypoxemia: severely impacts the cardiac tissue and can precipitate rhythm changes.","Kidney disease, chronic or acute: raises the risk of electrolyte abnormalities. A hyperkalemic patient has a lot of rhythm issues.","Uremia and azotemia can also cause rhythm issues.","How Lost Cardiac Output Affects Other Systems","Respiratory: a patient with a baseline respiratory issue who goes into AFib with RVR (rapid ventricular rate) loses cardiac output. The work of breathing gets so much worse.","Kidneys: with an AKI, perfusion to the kidneys falls further, and they get worse."]},{"s":"AV Blocks","a":"av-blocks","k":["AV Blocks","What an AV Block Means","Delayed or completely blocked conduction from the SA node to the AV (atrioventricular) node. Also called heart block.","A big cause of bradycardia: the biggest concern. A second- or third-degree block puts the patient at high risk for bradycardia, with compromised cardiac output.","Related to age or ischemic disease: seen in post-MI heart failure, or just developing with age.","Treated with a pacemaker. No medications speed up the heart rate and fix a heart block.","The Four Degrees","1st Degree","The lowest degree.","2nd Degree, Type I","Also called Mobitz I.","2nd Degree, Type II","Also called Mobitz II.","3rd Degree","Complete heart block.","The higher the degree, the more severe: the more impact on cardiac output, and the more symptomatic bradycardia."]},{"s":"Pacemakers and ICDs","a":"pacemakers-and-icds","k":["Pacemakers and ICDs","Pacemaker","Helps the heart maintain a normal rhythm, from being too slow.","Sends low-energy pulses to the heart that make it beat.","Not placed for a consistent sinus tach.","Permanent or temporary.","Defibrillator (ICD)","Prevents or stops a dangerous arrhythmia leading to cardiac arrest.","Recognizes a dangerous rhythm and sends a high-energy shock to the heart.","Can serve as a pacemaker: one device can do both. Some patients have a pacemaker alone.","Permanent only. The temporary version is pads hooked to the Zoll machine.","Who Gets a Pacemaker or ICD","Damaged heart tissue and a lethal rhythm in the past: usually after a severe MI or severe ischemic or coronary artery disease. Examples: a third-degree heart block, or a run of VTach that needed CPR.","An underlying rhythm condition, such as Wolff-Parkinson-White, or a genetic condition that raises the risk of dysrhythmias.","Heart failure past a certain stage of progression: an ICD and pacemaker, for the high risk of a lethal rhythm.","AV block: a pacemaker keeps the rhythm from being too slow.","Batteries, Generators and Leads","Internal batteries and generators: most patients will at some point need a generator or battery exchange.","Leads sit in the cardiac tissue of the right atrium and right ventricle, sometimes the left ventricle, and send low-impulse shocks into it. Sometimes a lead needs replacing.","Newer devices limit or remove the need for generator and battery exchanges.","Restrictions of Pacemakers and ICDs","MRI: some are MRI safe, probably all the permanent ones.","Wave interference: no work with large machinery, such as crane operating or construction, and no work as a mechanic. Can be life-altering.","Airport: fine. Walk through the metal detector, not the screener.","More restrictions, and what to do when an ICD fires, are taught in the Dysrhythmias review","Pacing and Malfunctions","When a Pacemaker Paces","The rate is programmed: set by the doctor for the cardiac output needed, such as 70.","Senses the patient's own beat in the programmed time: does not pace.","No beat sensed in the programmed time: generates an electrical current and a beat.","Not felt by the patient. Not painful, unlike being defibrillated.","AAI — The atria only — An atrial contraction only: a P wave.","VVI — The ventricles only — A big QRS complex. Wide after a paced beat, which is normal for a paced ventricular beat.","DDD — Both: dual pacing — The atrial and the ventricular contraction: two pacer marks.","Failure to pace — Not pacing appropriately.","Failure to capture — A pacer spike, but no contraction generated behind it.","Failure to sense — Cannot sense what the patient is doing underneath: whether there are atrial or ventricular contractions.","Temporary Pacemakers","Transvenous Pacer","When: a symptomatic bradycardia that needs treating. Example: sinus brady at 40 with a low blood pressure, the cause not yet found.","How: threaded in through the internal jugular into the right atrium and right ventricle.","Pacer Wires","When: after cardiac surgery.","How: sewn into the myocardium. Removed after a number of days.","The Pacer Box","For temporary pacers. Sets the rate, the sensitivity and the mode: atrium, ventricle or dual.","Settings follow the order set, with some leeway for malfunctions.","Check the box, and evaluate continuously whether the pacer is still needed.","Keep pacer wires and transvenous pacers secure. Otherwise, it can be life-threatening."]},{"s":"Managing Deadly Rhythms","a":"deadly-rhythms","k":["Managing Deadly Rhythms","First: what does the patient look like? A rhythm seen on tele, or a call from central monitoring: go in and look at the patient immediately.","Evaluating the Patient","Level of consciousness: the first thing seen.","A set of vitals and an assessment: pale? Breathing normally? Diaphoretic? Blood pressure? Urine in the last hour?","Is the rhythm real? A patient who does not match the monitor: check the EKG leads.","Pulse: check it if the patient does not look great. A patient talking to you needs no carotid check.","No pulse felt, or a low level of consciousness — Call for help first: call the code. Then the airway and compressions.","Call for help first: call the code.","Then the airway and compressions.","Fast rhythm, conscious, with a blood pressure — Vagal maneuvers. PRN metoprolol, if ordered: often 5 mg IV push. Call the doctor. Adenosine for SVT. Next-level antidysrhythmics: amiodarone, lidocaine. Examples: VTach with a blood pressure, …","Vagal maneuvers.","PRN metoprolol, if ordered: often 5 mg IV push. Call the doctor.","Adenosine for SVT.","Next-level antidysrhythmics: amiodarone, lidocaine.","Examples: VTach with a blood pressure, or AFib with RVR or SVT at 150 to 170, the patient feeling lightheaded.","Sinus brady — Atropine. Not responsive to atropine, or even if it is: is a transvenous pacer needed?","Atropine.","Not responsive to atropine, or even if it is: is a transvenous pacer needed?","No adenosine or atropine without an EKG that shows the rhythm, and a doctor's order."]},{"s":"Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support (ACLS)","a":"acls","k":["Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support (ACLS)","A code in the hospital is run with ACLS, not the BLS used in the community. A team sport: never done alone.","Airway — Respiratory therapy or anesthesia.","Push meds — Pushes the emergency meds, such as epinephrine.","Time taker — CPR still runs in 2-minute cycles.","Compressions — Rotate in and out.","Recorder — Writes down everything done.","Team lead — Usually the MD.","Primary RN — The information person. Does none of these roles once the team is there. May do CPR until someone takes over, then steps back to inform. A student stays with the primary RN. Example: where the IVs are.","Epinephrine in a Code","The only known drug to stimulate or restore a cardiac rhythm.","1 mg IV push every 3 to 5 minutes, during CPR.","An alpha-1 adrenergic agonist.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day2-acute-kidney-injury.html","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","secs":[{"s":"Acute Kidney Injury","a":"","k":["Day 2 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Oliguria and azotemia: what each means.","BUN and creatinine: the big labs. What they mean, and what affects them.","Function of the kidneys: what the kidneys are responsible for.","Acid-base balance, blood pressure regulation, RBC production: the kidneys' part in each.","Pre-renal, intrarenal and post-renal causes: the three kinds of cause of AKI.","The renal diet: the dietary considerations for a patient with kidney problems.","Intermittent hemodialysis (IHD): how it works, and its complications, disequilibrium syndrome among them.","Chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease: why each manifestation of kidney disease happens. They are the same in AKI and CKD."]},{"s":"The Kidneys in Critical Illness","a":"kidneys-in-critical-illness","k":["The Kidneys in Critical Illness","The kidneys are the primary regulator of the body's internal environment: fluid and electrolyte balance, and metabolic waste and toxin elimination. The excreter.","Why Kidney Injury Is So Common in the Critically Ill","They take 20 to 25% of cardiac output: highly affected by changes in cardiac output and blood pressure. Tons of tiny vessels respond to them.","They filter the ICU's nephrotoxic meds: antibiotics, contrast dye, high doses. Poorly perfused, and asked to work harder.","Dehydration: as a main driver of fluid and electrolyte balance, they are highly affected by fluid balance.","What Fails When the Kidneys Fail","Potassium — Primarily responsible for excreting potassium. — Potassium builds up. Be very careful with it.","Blood pressure regulation — A blood pressure regulator: home of the RAAS (renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system). — Alterations in blood pressure regulation.","RBC production — Secrete erythropoietin. — No erythropoietin: anemia. A longer-term effect.","The kidneys' part in acid-base balance is taught in the Renal review","The RAAS, step by step, is taught in the Renal review"]},{"s":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","a":"aki","k":["Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","A sudden decline in kidney function that causes fluid, electrolyte and acid-base imbalances.","Azotemia — The buildup of nitrogenous waste products. Seen in the BUN and creatinine.","Oliguria — Lack of urine output. Normal output is about 0.5 mL/kg/hr: typically 30 mL/hr for an average person, down to 25 for a really small person.","Causes","Usually multifactorial: most often hypoperfusion plus nephron injury, so pre-renal plus intrarenal. Sepsis is the most common cause in the critically ill. Knowing the likely cause shows how to correct it.","Pre-renal — Hypoperfusion: a sudden and severe drop in blood pressure (shock), or interrupted blood flow to the kidneys from severe injury or illness. Very common in septic shock. Examples: very dehydrated, a lot of blood lost, low cardiac …","Hypoperfusion: a sudden and severe drop in blood pressure (shock), or interrupted blood flow to the kidneys from severe injury or illness.","Very common in septic shock.","Examples: very dehydrated, a lot of blood lost, low cardiac output from heart failure.","Intrarenal — Direct nephron injury: direct damage to the kidneys by inflammation, toxins, drugs, infection or reduced blood supply. Toxins and drugs are the big ones: the nephrotoxic meds. Reduced blood supply over time, from prolonged hype…","Direct nephron injury: direct damage to the kidneys by inflammation, toxins, drugs, infection or reduced blood supply.","Toxins and drugs are the big ones: the nephrotoxic meds.","Reduced blood supply over time, from prolonged hypertension. Not sudden, severe hypoperfusion.","Examples: glomerulonephritis from lupus or a virus, pyelonephritis, hypertensive glomerulonephropathy.","Post-renal — Tubular obstruction: sudden obstruction of urine flow. Not usually the cause in the critically ill, but can happen. Examples: an enlarged prostate, kidney stones, a bladder tumor, injury.","Tubular obstruction: sudden obstruction of urine flow.","Not usually the cause in the critically ill, but can happen.","Examples: an enlarged prostate, kidney stones, a bladder tumor, injury.","The three kinds of cause, and the most common, are taught in the Renal review","Onset and Prognosis","Onset","Rapid: hours to days.","Several hours of really poor perfusion and the kidneys take an immediate hit: no urine output, and a bump in BUN and creatinine.","That is an AKI, even when it needs less intervention and never becomes months of care or CKD.","Examples: a lot of blood lost in surgery or trauma, acute heart failure.","Prognosis","Reversible. Rehydrated and the problem solved, the kidneys can be repaired.","Keys: early identification and treatment.","Signals to Monitor Closely and Notify","A risk: a lot of blood lost in the OR, a big long surgery, a very nephrotoxic antibiotic, or several procedures with contrast dye while very dehydrated.","And low urine output for several hours.","Then: monitor the urine output and the BUN and creatinine closely, and mention them to the team.","Assessment","Blood pressure — Changes are common in AKI. Monitor closely. Pre-renal: watch closely for hypotension, with tachycardia. Intrarenal, such as acute tubular necrosis: hypertension for a short time, from RAAS malfunction.","Changes are common in AKI. Monitor closely.","Pre-renal: watch closely for hypotension, with tachycardia.","Intrarenal, such as acute tubular necrosis: hypertension for a short time, from RAAS malfunction.","Respirations — May change to compensate for the acid-base imbalance. The respiratory system responds very quickly. Severe AKI, very acidotic from a lack of bicarb: breathing off CO2, so hyperventilation may be seen.","May change to compensate for the acid-base imbalance. The respiratory system responds very quickly.","Severe AKI, very acidotic from a lack of bicarb: breathing off CO2, so hyperventilation may be seen.","Uremia or azotemia — Signs and symptoms: malaise, fatigue, disorientation, drowsiness. Super vague: the same as the low perfusion and sepsis that put patients at risk. Hard to pin on AKI unless the BUN and creatinine are through the roof wi…","Signs and symptoms: malaise, fatigue, disorientation, drowsiness.","Super vague: the same as the low perfusion and sepsis that put patients at risk. Hard to pin on AKI unless the BUN and creatinine are through the roof with nothing else going on.","Always look for them."]},{"s":"AKI Interventions","a":"aki-interventions","k":["AKI Interventions","No medication fixes the kidneys. Care is supportive: flush the system and improve cardiac output, so the kidneys can reverse and repair themselves.","Address the cause — Meds contributing? A team talk: is the drug appropriate? Switch it, lower the dose, monitor peak and trough levels? Hypoperfusion? Vasopressors to tighten the vessels, for better perfusion and blood pressure to the kidne…","Meds contributing? A team talk: is the drug appropriate? Switch it, lower the dose, monitor peak and trough levels?","Hypoperfusion? Vasopressors to tighten the vessels, for better perfusion and blood pressure to the kidneys. Hydrate.","Diuretics — Furosemide, bumetanide: increase the urine output. Help with the buildup of electrolytes: flush the potassium out.","Furosemide, bumetanide: increase the urine output.","Help with the buildup of electrolytes: flush the potassium out.","IV fluid replacement — Flush the kidneys: a lot of fluid and perfusion, to clear the filter. Many times, if the patient can tolerate it. Crystalloid: isotonic saline (normal saline) or LR. Pre-renal causes mainly. Helps intrarenal causes to…","Flush the kidneys: a lot of fluid and perfusion, to clear the filter. Many times, if the patient can tolerate it.","Crystalloid: isotonic saline (normal saline) or LR.","Pre-renal causes mainly. Helps intrarenal causes too: flushes out the nephrotoxic meds.","Dietary management — Longer-term cases: the dietary considerations for kidney problems.","Longer-term cases: the dietary considerations for kidney problems.","Electrolytes — Monitor them.","Monitor them."]},{"s":"Renal Replacement Therapy","a":"renal-replacement-therapy","k":["Renal Replacement Therapy","The primary treatment for AKI: dialysis. Used when the other interventions are not working, or the AKI is getting more severe.","Acidosis — An acid-base imbalance, typically acidotic, that needs correcting.","Hyperkalemia — The electrolytes on the labs.","Volume overload — Crackles, pulmonary edema, 2+ to 4+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.","Uremia — Rising BUN and creatinine. Level of consciousness changes that cannot be put down to anything else.","Low urine output alone is not an indication. The indications are a combination of things.","How Dialysis Works","Removes toxins and fluid through a semipermeable membrane. Works toward equilibrium, pulling solutes out of the blood: what the kidneys should be doing.","Corrects uremia, azotemia and acidosis, and pulls fluid off.","Settings: the rates of infusion, how much it pulls off, and what it pulls off. Managed by the renal or nephrology team.","The nurse monitors the settings, with a basic understanding of what is going on.","Monitor the indication for improvement, such as hyperkalemia and volume overload. BUN and creatinine should improve. Urine output may or may not.","Vascular Access","Temporary","Most common for AKI. A trialysis port, in the internal jugular.","Semi-Permanent","Tunneled catheters. The subclavian is kept for long-term or semi-permanent access.","Permanent","AV fistula.","A trialysis port is for dialysis only. The red and blue ports connect to the dialysis machine and nothing else. The purple port takes other access only occasionally, with the team's approval."]},{"s":"Intermittent or Continuous Dialysis","a":"ihd-or-crrt","k":["Intermittent or Continuous Dialysis","Intermittent Hemodialysis (IHD)","Patient: hemodynamically stable, typically on no drips for blood pressure. Can even be ventilated, with a lot going on.","Pace: 3 to 4 hours at a time. Intense and harsh on the body.","Where: the dialysis unit, or sometimes the bedside.","Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT)","Patient: fragile, such as with sepsis, a very unstable blood pressure, or a lot of blood lost. Often chosen for AKI for this reason.","Pace: continuous. Much more gentle: the rate can be slowed down or sped up, with much more nuanced control.","Where: the ICU only.","Provides: ultrafiltration and diffusion, continuously. Does the work of the kidneys while the body heals.","Indications: the same as IHD.","Is the Patient a Candidate for IHD?","Blood pressure stable with no IV meds? Chronic PO meds do not count.","Oxygen pretty stable on whatever device?","Tolerating the usual things?","If not: probably not a candidate for IHD.","Types of CRRT","Slow continuous ultrafiltration (SCUF)","Continuous venovenous hemofiltration (CVVH)","Continuous venovenous hemodialysis (CVVHD)","Continuous venovenous hemodiafiltration (CVVHDF)","IHD Complications","Hypotension — The biggest and most concerning. Very common. Cause: usually pre-existing intravascular hypovolemia. Fluid is pulled off the vascular system of a patient already a little too dry, and the preload falls too low. Prolonged hypot…","The biggest and most concerning. Very common.","Cause: usually pre-existing intravascular hypovolemia. Fluid is pulled off the vascular system of a patient already a little too dry, and the preload falls too low.","Prolonged hypotension cuts kidney perfusion further, and can damage the kidneys more.","Dysrhythmias — Cause: usually potassium imbalances. Commonly, too much potassium pulled off: hypokalemia. Replacing potassium with baseline kidney impairment is a delicate balance.","Cause: usually potassium imbalances. Commonly, too much potassium pulled off: hypokalemia.","Replacing potassium with baseline kidney impairment is a delicate balance.","Muscle cramping — Cause: ischemia to the skeletal muscles from aggressive fluid removal, or electrolyte imbalances. More related to chronic renal failure.","Cause: ischemia to the skeletal muscles from aggressive fluid removal, or electrolyte imbalances.","More related to chronic renal failure.","Decreased PaO2 — Usually not an issue unless critically ill. A ventilated patient not doing well with oxygenation may not be a good candidate for IHD.","Usually not an issue unless critically ill.","A ventilated patient not doing well with oxygenation may not be a good candidate for IHD.","Infection — Cause: the big temporary dialysis port attracts bacteria.","Cause: the big temporary dialysis port attracts bacteria.","Disequilibrium syndrome and the other complications of hemodialysis are taught in the Renal review","IHD Nursing Responsibilities","Caring for a Patient on IHD","Reassess the patient: tolerating IHD on Monday does not mean Wednesday. Fragility can come on very quickly: vitals, labs and oxygen change as the cause worsens or the meds change.","Communicate with the team if the patient may not tolerate it today. They can pull less fluid off, or go slower.","Labs before and after dialysis: often in the order, but clarify.","No water-soluble meds before dialysis: often antihypertensives and sedation meds.","An antihypertensive before dialysis: the blood pressure drops, then dialysis pulls more fluid off.","Drugs dialyzed out, and drugs that build up to toxic levels, are taught in the Renal review"]},{"s":"CRRT Nursing Responsibilities","a":"crrt-nursing","k":["CRRT Nursing Responsibilities","Know the parameters — The programmed parameters, the dialysate and the solutions match the orders. Know the mode, and what it is doing to the patient.","The programmed parameters, the dialysate and the solutions match the orders.","Know the mode, and what it is doing to the patient.","Intake and output — The most important: extremely careful, usually hourly. Each hour: fluid pulled off by CRRT, urine output, IV fluids given. A lot goes in: usually continuous IV fluids and continuous tube feeding through a Dobhoff tube, m…","The most important: extremely careful, usually hourly.","Each hour: fluid pulled off by CRRT, urine output, IV fluids given.","A lot goes in: usually continuous IV fluids and continuous tube feeding through a Dobhoff tube, maybe free water.","Chart it closely: is enough coming off? Fluid removal is one of the big things CRRT does.","Hemodynamic monitoring — An arterial line in the vast majority, for close blood pressure monitoring. Patient declining: slow the fluid removal, or the CRRT rate. Stable enough to need no arterial line: probably ready to move to IHD soon.","An arterial line in the vast majority, for close blood pressure monitoring.","Patient declining: slow the fluid removal, or the CRRT rate.","Stable enough to need no arterial line: probably ready to move to IHD soon.","Temperature — The blood comes back cold: it leaves the body through an external machine. The machines have heaters, but patients are often internally cold. Often need a Bair Hugger, and usually hourly temps.","The blood comes back cold: it leaves the body through an external machine. The machines have heaters, but patients are often internally cold.","Often need a Bair Hugger, and usually hourly temps.","Filter clotting — Assess the filter for clotting. A normal filter is all red blood. A clot shows first at the top. A clotted filter means that machine is done. Usually on a blood thinner: enoxaparin, maybe a heparin drip. Not a requirement,…","Assess the filter for clotting. A normal filter is all red blood. A clot shows first at the top. A clotted filter means that machine is done.","Usually on a blood thinner: enoxaparin, maybe a heparin drip. Not a requirement, but consistent: immobile patients at higher risk for DVT.","Labs — Very close: BUN and creatinine and an electrolyte panel, usually every 8 hours, to keep a nice equilibrium.","Very close: BUN and creatinine and an electrolyte panel, usually every 8 hours, to keep a nice equilibrium."]},{"s":"Long-Term Outlook","a":"long-term-outlook","k":["Long-Term Outlook","After an AKI","Kidneys can recover: CRRT, or even a while on IHD, does not mean lifelong dialysis. Corrected quickly enough, the kidneys can be saved.","Some develop chronic renal disease: lifelong IHD, or a kidney transplant.","AKI on Top of CKD","Much higher risk for a further AKI with CKD or end-stage renal disease.","Shows as a bump in BUN and creatinine from baseline. May need CRRT for a time.","Harder to spot: urine output may be lower than healthy kidneys make, or none at all on dialysis at home.","Monitor closely: BUN and creatinine, electrolytes, symptoms.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day2-nutrition.html","t":"Nutrition","secs":[{"s":"Nutrition","a":"","k":["Day 2 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"Nutrition: The Forgotten Concept","a":"forgotten-concept","k":["Nutrition: The Forgotten Concept","Goal for nutrition in the critically ill: preserve lean mass. Breaking down lean muscle mass puts the patient in a catabolic state, with a lot of adverse reactions.","When to start thinking about it — Once airway, breathing and circulation are maintained. Blood pressure stabilized, even on pressors. PaO2 at 55 or 60, even ventilated.","Once airway, breathing and circulation are maintained.","Blood pressure stabilized, even on pressors. PaO2 at 55 or 60, even ventilated.","Who is responsible — Nurse and multidisciplinary teams. With several teams caring for one patient, nutrition can get passed off. Nurse's part: make sure it is brought up. Are we thinking about feeding this patient?","Nurse and multidisciplinary teams. With several teams caring for one patient, nutrition can get passed off.","Nurse's part: make sure it is brought up. Are we thinking about feeding this patient?","Why it matters — Malnutrition is very common in hospitalized patients. Associated with increased readmissions, length of stay, healthcare costs and mortality.","Malnutrition is very common in hospitalized patients.","Associated with increased readmissions, length of stay, healthcare costs and mortality.","Catabolic Stress State","Critical Illness","A stress response, such as major surgery or major trauma. Metabolic demands are already high.","Lean Mass Broken Down","Altered protein and energy metabolism. The risk of malnutrition rises.","An inflammatory response and increased metabolic demands. A malnourished, catabolic state furthers the inflammatory response and the metabolic need.","Nutrition Screening","Goal: within 48 hours of admission, especially to the ICU. Very important for a full evaluation.","Dietary or nutrition usually involved: they document nutrition parameters, determine malnourishment risk factors, identify deficits and establish needs.","Involve the medical team. Psychosocial and socioeconomic factors influence nutritional status too.","Baseline nutrition is a huge risk factor. A nutritionally healthy patient has more reserve.","Not just low body weight: some of the most malnourished patients are morbidly obese. Body weight and BMI are not a great indicator."]},{"s":"Assessment","a":"nutrition-assessment","k":["Assessment","GI motility — Assess bowel sounds: present? Hypoactive, hyperactive or absent? Is there a reason, and how is it being addressed?","Assess bowel sounds: present? Hypoactive, hyperactive or absent? Is there a reason, and how is it being addressed?","Medications — Hemodynamically unstable patients often need blood pressure support via IV meds. Vasopressors shunt blood away from the gut: the vessels clamp down, and the gut is one of the first areas affected. The kidneys, heart, lungs and…","Hemodynamically unstable patients often need blood pressure support via IV meds.","Vasopressors shunt blood away from the gut: the vessels clamp down, and the gut is one of the first areas affected. The kidneys, heart, lungs and brain matter more. A very high risk of lower GI motility.","On pressors for several days, no bowel sounds: a concern.","Anesthesia and opioids slow the gut. Opioids every 4 hours? Frequent anesthesia?","Surgery or trauma — GI surgery or trauma affects the gut directly. A lot of blood lost: blood is shunted away from the GI system to more important organs, so the gut is at higher risk.","GI surgery or trauma affects the gut directly.","A lot of blood lost: blood is shunted away from the GI system to more important organs, so the gut is at higher risk.","Fluid status — Hypovolemic states impact nutritional status. Able to take PO?","Hypovolemic states impact nutritional status.","Able to take PO?","Wounds — Wound healing depends on nutrition, and a wound adds nutritional demand. Correct and prevent nutritional deficits in patients with wounds. Examples: an infected wound, a pressure ulcer, an infected surgical wound. Not a normal surg…","Wound healing depends on nutrition, and a wound adds nutritional demand.","Correct and prevent nutritional deficits in patients with wounds.","Examples: an infected wound, a pressure ulcer, an infected surgical wound. Not a normal surgical incision.","Age — Frailty: physiologic reserve decreases with age. The older the adult, the greater the concern for nutritional status.","Frailty: physiologic reserve decreases with age. The older the adult, the greater the concern for nutritional status.","Pregnancy — Alters nutritional needs quite intensely. Assess and address them.","Alters nutritional needs quite intensely. Assess and address them.","Nutrition Labs","Albumin — Longer term: about the last 3 months. Low means malnourishment is more likely.","Prealbumin — About the last 72 hours. Trend it when nutrition is a worry: once feeding starts, it should rise.","Triglycerides — What the baseline diet is like. Low: not a lot of good fats on board.","Sodium, potassium, magnesium, phosphate — Typically low in chronically malnourished patients. Monitor them.","Glucose — Hypoglycemia is a big deal. Chronic hyperglycemia shows a risk to watch for too."]},{"s":"Early Interventions and Assessments","a":"early-interventions-and-assessments","k":["Early Interventions and Assessments","Dietary or nutrition consult — Screeners that bedside nurses complete may trigger an automatic consult. Needed but not triggered? Press the team, and ask about placing a Dobhoff tube. Example: 24 hours in the ICU, on a couple of pressors, n…","Screeners that bedside nurses complete may trigger an automatic consult.","Needed but not triggered? Press the team, and ask about placing a Dobhoff tube.","Example: 24 hours in the ICU, on a couple of pressors, not eating for several days.","Close dietary monitoring — Taking PO but still critically ill or hemodynamically unstable: monitor closely. Chart: fluid intake, the percent of meals eaten, appetite. Appetite is often lost for a long time after major trauma or surgery. Edu…","Taking PO but still critically ill or hemodynamically unstable: monitor closely.","Chart: fluid intake, the percent of meals eaten, appetite.","Appetite is often lost for a long time after major trauma or surgery. Educate: without better intake, the patient gets sicker, stays longer, or needs a feeding tube through the nose.","24-hour calorie count — A high-level intervention. May be required to make sure the metabolic demands are met.","A high-level intervention. May be required to make sure the metabolic demands are met.","Protein and vitamin supplements — Common in critically ill patients. Juven: for wound healing. Amino acids and some electrolytes. Very common with a pressure ulcer. Beneprotein: instant protein powder, for any patient in a catabolic stress …","Common in critically ill patients.","Juven: for wound healing. Amino acids and some electrolytes. Very common with a pressure ulcer.","Beneprotein: instant protein powder, for any patient in a catabolic stress state. Helps wound healing and healing in general."]},{"s":"Feed the Gut When It Works","a":"feed-the-gut","k":["Feed the Gut When It Works","Feed the gut when it works. If the gut is working, that is where the nutrition goes.","How Do You Know the Gut Works?","Bowel movements.","Bowel sounds.","When Is the Gut Not the Place to Feed?","An interrupted system: a major bowel surgery, a bowel resection, an esophagectomy, part of the stomach removed.","On very high-dose pressors: not a great candidate for enteral nutrition. Blood is shunted away from the gut, so its blood supply is poor.","No good blood supply to the gut, no feeding it: it can cause ischemia, vomiting and nausea. The feed just sits there.","A very high lactate: the gut is likely not well perfused. Lactate rises in anaerobic metabolism. Not an indicator alone, since bowel sounds may be fine, but a cue to check."]},{"s":"Enteral Nutrition","a":"enteral-nutrition","k":["Enteral Nutrition","Start within 24 to 48 hours of admission if the patient is not taking PO. An earlier start is associated with less pneumonia, lower mortality, a shorter ICU length of stay, and GI tract integrity maintained long term.","Modalities","G tube — Stomach — A PEG is usually the choice when the stomach is available.","A PEG is usually the choice when the stomach is available.","NG tube — Stomach — Least favorite way to feed, especially longer term. Often to wall suction for decompression: no feeding at the same time.","Least favorite way to feed, especially longer term.","Often to wall suction for decompression: no feeding at the same time.","J tube — Jejunum, in the small intestine — For stomach or esophageal problems, long-term issues, when the stomach is not available.","For stomach or esophageal problems, long-term issues, when the stomach is not available.","Dobhoff tube — Post-pyloric: always the goal — Usually the best friend: very good for meds and tube feeding.","Usually the best friend: very good for meds and tube feeding.","Feeding Rates","Trophic — First level: may be what starts in the first 24 hours.","First level: may be what starts in the first 24 hours.","Targeted hourly rate — A set hourly rate for the assigned tube feed. Problem: does not account for stopping and starting for procedures and meds, so a lot of nutritional support can be lost.","A set hourly rate for the assigned tube feed.","Problem: does not account for stopping and starting for procedures and meds, so a lot of nutritional support can be lost.","Volume-based feeding — A daily volume goal. Compensates for the stops and is more flexible. The trend, and done more already in peds. Hourly rate set by the nurse from how much has gone in since midnight, how long the feed will be off for p…","A daily volume goal. Compensates for the stops and is more flexible. The trend, and done more already in peds.","Hourly rate set by the nurse from how much has gone in since midnight, how long the feed will be off for procedures, and so how much is left to give by the end of the shift.","More responsibility on the nurse, better for the patient long term.","Tube Care","Skin — Check the skin around every tube, especially in the critically ill. Pressure ulcers under Dobhoff tubes are common, and not always preventable. PEGs and J tubes: irritation, or skin breakdown under the securement device?","Check the skin around every tube, especially in the critically ill.","Pressure ulcers under Dobhoff tubes are common, and not always preventable.","PEGs and J tubes: irritation, or skin breakdown under the securement device?","Flushing — Don't lose the access: the tube is often how the meds go in too. Flush and maintain it frequently. At least every 8 hours, most often every 4, with meds every 4 hours. Behind every med. 30 mL each time is the goal. Less with a fl…","Don't lose the access: the tube is often how the meds go in too. Flush and maintain it frequently.","At least every 8 hours, most often every 4, with meds every 4 hours. Behind every med.","30 mL each time is the goal. Less with a fluid restriction.","Meds — Where is the tube located? Will it affect administration? Some meds need the stomach to be absorbed appropriately. Everyone, pharmacy included, knows how the patient is fed: a J tube or a PEG. No extended-release meds down these tube…","Where is the tube located? Will it affect administration? Some meds need the stomach to be absorbed appropriately. Everyone, pharmacy included, knows how the patient is fed: a J tube or a PEG.","No extended-release meds down these tubes.","More than one tube, such as a PEG and a J tube: clarify which is for what.","Position — Head of bed 30 degrees or higher, if able. Flat is not a contraindication to feeding through a Dobhoff tube. Be more aware of the aspiration risk. Flat, feeding into an NG tube is probably not the best bet.","Head of bed 30 degrees or higher, if able.","Flat is not a contraindication to feeding through a Dobhoff tube. Be more aware of the aspiration risk.","Flat, feeding into an NG tube is probably not the best bet.","Placement — Check the markings on an NG or Dobhoff tube: at the same mark, not advanced, not pulled out.","Check the markings on an NG or Dobhoff tube: at the same mark, not advanced, not pulled out.","Orders — Some patients are strict NPO. Some may have ice chips, or even be encouraged to eat and drink with a Dobhoff tube. Know the orders and why: a swallowing issue, or malnutrition?","Some patients are strict NPO. Some may have ice chips, or even be encouraged to eat and drink with a Dobhoff tube.","Know the orders and why: a swallowing issue, or malnutrition?","Tolerance","Signs of Intolerance","Abdominal distension, constipation, diarrhea, emesis, nausea.","Most often: nausea and diarrhea.","Gastric residuals: no longer recommended. Never with a Dobhoff tube: it is too small, and the access is lost. Only possible with an NG tube.","When the Feed Is Not Tolerated","Diarrhea, such as every hour after the feed was increased: talk to dietary and get the team involved. A different kind of tube feed? Bulk up with protein and fiber?","Constipation: add a stool softener.","Motility: meds such as metoclopramide may be added.","Tube feeds come in different types: use the order appropriately. Not tolerating it? Ask for a different kind, and keep working toward the goal volume to prevent malnutrition."]},{"s":"Parenteral Nutrition","a":"parenteral-nutrition","k":["Parenteral Nutrition","For patients who cannot take enteral nutrition: protein, fat, minerals, vitamins and carbohydrates via IV.","TPN — Total parenteral nutrition: the big yellow bag. More common, and lower fluid volume. Peripheral parenteral nutrition is not indicated for adults. Highly concentrated: high osmolality, lots of dextrose. Still a lot of fluid volume: tak…","Total parenteral nutrition: the big yellow bag. More common, and lower fluid volume.","Peripheral parenteral nutrition is not indicated for adults.","Highly concentrated: high osmolality, lots of dextrose.","Still a lot of fluid volume: take it into account when volume status is a worry, such as on CRRT.","Blood sugars — Monitor closely: lots of dextrose.","Monitor closely: lots of dextrose.","Line and tubing — A PICC line or central line is preferred. Change the tubing every 24 hours, per protocol: dextrose attracts bacteria.","A PICC line or central line is preferred.","Change the tubing every 24 hours, per protocol: dextrose attracts bacteria.","Lipids — Usually given with TPN: fatty acids, the white bag. Careful with propofol, a fat-soluble med. Lipids given with it can affect its absorption, and whether it works.","Usually given with TPN: fatty acids, the white bag.","Careful with propofol, a fat-soluble med. Lipids given with it can affect its absorption, and whether it works.","Labs — Glucose, triglycerides, electrolytes: monitor closely.","Glucose, triglycerides, electrolytes: monitor closely.","Stopping — Titrate off slowly, as with a corticosteroid. Refeeding syndrome is likely: work with pharmacy and nutrition on a plan.","Titrate off slowly, as with a corticosteroid.","Refeeding syndrome is likely: work with pharmacy and nutrition on a plan.","Infection — High risk: one of the reasons TPN is so dangerous and avoided. Bacteria are attracted to the dextrose. Monitor the line for signs and symptoms of infection. Clean ports, clean lines.","High risk: one of the reasons TPN is so dangerous and avoided. Bacteria are attracted to the dextrose.","Monitor the line for signs and symptoms of infection. Clean ports, clean lines.","TPN runs on its own dedicated line. Nothing is Y'd into it.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day3-gastrointestinal.html","t":"GI Critical Care Issues","secs":[{"s":"GI Critical Care Issues","a":"","k":["Day 3 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","GI anatomy and physiology: understanding what is going on leads the interventions when these issues occur in a critical care patient.","IBS, inflammatory bowel disease, GI bleeds and ulcers, peptic ulcer disease: the bowel diseases.","Chronic pancreatitis: applied to acute pancreatitis, the kind that lands a patient in the ICU.","Liver failure: liver disease and liver failure, and how their complications land a patient in the ICU. Portal hypertension and its complications.","How age affects the GI system: the changes that come with getting older, such as taste, smell and appetite."]},{"s":"GI System: Things to Consider","a":"gi-system","k":["GI System: Things to Consider","Neural and hormonal factors — Parasympathetic, cholinergic stimulation drives GI secretion and motility, and stimulates the pancreas and gallbladder. Sight and smell stimulate gastric secretions. Easily influenced by problems elsewhere in t…","Largest endocrine organ — Secretes hormones and enzymes to digest food and absorb nutrients and vitamins. Also affects other body systems.","Blood supply — The largest share of cardiac output, about 33%, across the intestines, liver, pancreas and gallbladder.","Age — The older the patient, the more susceptible the GI system is to damage.","Assessment — Two big indicators: distension and bowel sounds. Bowel sounds also show perfusion to the gut. Hypoactive or absent with no meds or anesthesia to explain it: a perfusion problem, usually with distension and rigidity.","When the body needs blood flow, it cuts off the GI system first. In hemorrhage or any big perfusion event, blood is diverted to the heart, lungs and brain, and the gut takes the brunt.","How age affects the GI system is taught in the GI review"]},{"s":"GI Bleeding","a":"gi-bleeding","k":["GI Bleeding","Peptic ulcer disease — A history of a peptic or duodenal ulcer puts the patient at very high risk of another, especially on an anticoagulant.","Stress-related mucosal bleeding (stress ulcers) — From homeostatic stress: chronic disease or a very acutely ill state. Critically ill patients are at high risk.","Mallory-Weiss tear — Arterial hemorrhage from longitudinal tears in the gastroesophageal mucosa. After long, aggressive or projectile vomiting.","Esophageal or gastric varices — A critical care emergency. See Variceal Bleeding below.","Peptic ulcer disease and the upper GI bleed are taught in the GI review","Who Is at Risk for a GI Bleed","Critically ill: high risk, for stress ulcers.","Anticoagulated: a heparin drip, long-term warfarin or apixaban, heparin or enoxaparin injections. Vastly increases the risk, on top of the critical illness.","History to assess: a blood coagulopathy or known bleeding issue, anticoagulants at home, liver disease, cancer and chemo drugs, drug and alcohol use.","Prophylaxis in Critical Care","Why: to prevent stress ulcers in high-risk critically ill patients.","H2 receptor antagonists are used a lot. Pantoprazole is a PPI, and is also used.","Conflicting evidence on PPIs in the critically ill. Most of the time, pantoprazole is given.","Assessing for a GI Bleed","Bowel sounds — In a severe bleed: hyperactive during the bleed, from the bowel's sensitivity to blood. Hypoactive or absent if the bowel begins dying from lack of perfusion.","Stool and emesis — Bright red: actively bleeding. Dark or black (melena): an older bleed.","Secretions and drainage — Dark output from an NG tube to low wall suction: an old upper GI bleed. Red drainage anywhere, wounds included.","Labs — Trend the H&H.","Color — Getting paler. Seems simple, but incredibly indicative.","Vital signs — A GI bleed causes a fluid volume deficit. Tachycardia is the first sign, as compensation: the heart rate creeps into the 90s and low 100s. Then a dropping blood pressure, a rising respiratory rate, and a dropping SpO2 with no …","Urine output — Drops with the fluid volume deficit, as the kidneys lose perfusion."]},{"s":"Management of a Severe GI Bleed","a":"severe-gi-bleed","k":["Management of a Severe GI Bleed","Hemodynamically unstable: the primary goal is to stabilize the vital signs.","Continuous vital signs — An arterial line, or a blood pressure cuff every 5 to 10 minutes, at minimum every 15. Watch the tele: EKG changes from lack of blood flow in a very severe bleed.","An arterial line, or a blood pressure cuff every 5 to 10 minutes, at minimum every 15.","Watch the tele: EKG changes from lack of blood flow in a very severe bleed.","Fluid resuscitation — Isotonic IV fluids, such as normal saline or LR.","Isotonic IV fluids, such as normal saline or LR.","Blood transfusions — Guided by the hemoglobin at first. The hematocrit may not change for several hours in an acute massive bleed, and says little after fluid resuscitation.","Guided by the hemoglobin at first.","The hematocrit may not change for several hours in an acute massive bleed, and says little after fluid resuscitation.","Vasopressors — Fill the tank first: fluid resuscitated, with an acceptable hemoglobin, before meds to raise the blood pressure.","Fill the tank first: fluid resuscitated, with an acceptable hemoglobin, before meds to raise the blood pressure.","Labs — H&H every 1 to 2 hours. Electrolytes. BUN, creatinine, LFTs: is the bleed cutting perfusion to the kidneys and liver? Lactate: rises in an acidotic state, when too few RBCs carry oxygen and nutrients. Clotting profiles: a big bleed u…","H&H every 1 to 2 hours. Electrolytes.","BUN, creatinine, LFTs: is the bleed cutting perfusion to the kidneys and liver?","Lactate: rises in an acidotic state, when too few RBCs carry oxygen and nutrients.","Clotting profiles: a big bleed uses up clotting factors, and FFP may be needed.","ABGs, trended.","Gastric lavage — An NG tube to low wall suction in a severe bleed, especially an upper one. Lavage flushes the system: is it actively bleeding, old blood, or stopped? Also cleans it out quickly before surgery.","An NG tube to low wall suction in a severe bleed, especially an upper one.","Lavage flushes the system: is it actively bleeding, old blood, or stopped? Also cleans it out quickly before surgery.","Medications — H2 blockers, PPIs, sucralfate. Usually a continuous IV pantoprazole drip.","H2 blockers, PPIs, sucralfate.","Usually a continuous IV pantoprazole drip.","Clotting factors lost in a big bleed are taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Vasopressors after fluid resuscitation are taught in Shock, Sepsis, MODS","Acute Versus Chronic GI Bleed","Acute GI Bleed: Symptoms","Weakness, shortness of breath, angina, syncope.","Hypotension, tachycardia.","Anemia.","May also show fatigue, weakness and pallor, but these usually come with a chronic bleed.","Chronic GI Bleed: Symptoms","Fatigue, weakness, pale appearance.","Syncopal events.","An unexplained increase in BUN or K+ can mean blood is being metabolized in the GI tract.","Both look like anemia, not like a GI problem. Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain may be there, but they are not the signs that point to the bleed."]},{"s":"GI Bleed Interventions","a":"bleed-interventions","k":["GI Bleed Interventions","Most GI bleeds resolve on their own, through platelet plugs. An intervention is needed when a bleed cannot be controlled: very severe, on anticoagulants, or a third or fourth bleeding ulcer.","Endoscopy","Used for: the treatment of choice for an upper GI bleed, in the stomach or duodenum.","When: once hemodynamically stable, or within 24 hours if the patient cannot be stabilized. Often at the bedside.","Procedure: sclerotherapy injects the bleeding ulcer with a necrotizing therapy. Endoclipping clips it with hemoclips, and decreases rebleeding.","Nurse: maintain the airway and monitor the vital signs.","Surgical Therapy","Used for: usually massive rebleeding, such as a dropping H&H and bright red blood the day after an endoclip.","Procedure: in the OR. May need a gastric resection, to remove the areas of the peptic ulcers.","Nurse: fluid and electrolyte balance, drains, and pain, a big risk with any GI surgery."]},{"s":"Variceal Bleeding","a":"variceal-bleeding","k":["Variceal Bleeding","Usually from end-stage liver disease or acute liver failure. Portal hypertension causes the varices.","Bleeding esophageal or gastric varices are a critical care emergency. The patient rapidly becomes hemodynamically unstable and quickly goes into hypovolemic shock. Without a history of liver issues or cirrhosis, the cause is often unknown a…","Priorities in a Variceal Bleed","Maintain the airway. Esophageal and gastric bleeding can really impact it.","Hemodynamic stability. Usually multiple transfusions and IV fluid resuscitation.","Gastric lavage, to identify what is going on.","Diagnose through endoscopy, at the bedside, to see what the varices are doing.","Treating a Variceal Bleed","Octreotide IV or somatostatin — Stop the bleeding. Decrease splanchnic blood flow and reduce portal pressure. Octreotide: a continuous IV drip, very safe, often given to stabilize before definitive treatment. Sometimes given for an upper GI…","Vasopressin — Lowers portal pressure through vasoconstriction of the splanchnic arteriolar bed, so the bleeding slows. A drip, especially in the unit.","Endoscopy — Usually needed eventually. Sclerotherapy right into the varices, or band ligation.","TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) — Non-surgical. For recurrent bleeding after sclerotherapy. A shunt between the hepatic and portal veins lowers the portal hypertension that causes the varices. Not a permanent fix: used …","Esophagogastric tamponade (Sengstaken-Blakemore tube) — Presses on the varices: holding pressure on a wound, internally. Needs the right traction, tight against the esophageal or gastric wall. Other tubes exist.","Portocaval shunt — Surgery. Diverts blood around the blocked portal system. Decreases bleeding risk, but does not improve survival.","Never clamp an esophagogastric tamponade tube. The pressure is lost, and the varices start bleeding again."]},{"s":"Ischemic Bowel Disease","a":"ischemic-bowel-disease","k":["Ischemic Bowel Disease","A vascular condition: the bowel wall does not receive oxygenated blood, and that part of the bowel dies. In the small intestine it is mesenteric ischemia. In the colon it is ischemic colitis.","Causes of Ischemic Bowel Disease","Low blood pressure: the gut and skin take the hit first. Watch the bowel closely after a significant hypoperfusion event.","A blood clot or blockage in an artery.","A twisted or enlarged bowel.","Lupus and sickle cell anemia.","Risk Factors for Ischemic Bowel Disease","Atherosclerosis: fatty plaque can build up in the gut's arteries.","Age over 50.","Smoking: damages the lining of the vessels.","Heart failure and AFib: cardiac output issues.","Medications, mainly hormonal birth control: clots in the bowel.","Blood clotting disorders.","Cocaine and methamphetamine use.","High-dose vasopressors, especially norepinephrine: clamp off the gut's arterial supply and shift it to vital areas. On prolonged high doses, monitor for ischemic bowel disease.","Diagnosed by colonoscopy, or a barium contrast study or CT with contrast. Not seen on an X-ray: the problem is perfusion and arterial flow.","Signs and Symptoms of Ischemic Bowel Disease","Abdominal pain, especially after eating: the gut cannot work. Pain at baseline too, as the gut dies.","Frequent, urgent need to have a bowel movement.","Loose stools or diarrhea.","Nausea and vomiting.","Bloody stool with dark red clots: depends on the cause. Bloody stool points first to a lower GI bleed, with ischemic bowel second on the list.","Fever: the inflammatory response to dead gut.","Labs: increasing lactate, from the acidotic state ischemia or infarction causes. WBC altered, usually elevated.","Trend the lactate, very frequently. A lactate that keeps rising, not stabilizing and not coming down, means continuing cell death in the gut.","Bowel rest — Not eating. Drinking depends on the severity.","Fluid resuscitation — A lot of fluid shifting: often intravascularly depleted.","Surgery — Removes the blockage or the infarcted area, cutting well into the healthy pink bowel, because the infarction can spread. Often ends in a colostomy, temporary or permanent, depending on how much gut is removed.","Treat the cause — A clotting disorder, hormonal birth control, atherosclerosis, AFib or heart failure, a perfusion event."]},{"s":"Bowel Obstruction","a":"bowel-obstruction","k":["Bowel Obstruction","Small Bowel Obstruction (SBO)","What it is: occlusion of the intestinal lumen. Mechanical: something physically blocks it, such as a tumor, adhesion or hernia. Functional: no peristalsis, a paralytic ileus. See below.","Diagnosis: an X-ray (KUB) or a CT of the abdomen and pelvis. The X-ray shows dilated loops of small bowel and a collapsed colon. Partial or complete: complete is more severe.","Treatment: usually surgery to repair it. Before surgery, if not hemodynamically stable: NPO, fluid resuscitation, an NG tube. Electrolytes. Meds such as metoclopramide. Avoid opioids and anesthesia when possible, especially in a functional …","Large Bowel Obstruction","What it is: usually a neoplasm, such as colon or rectal cancer. Also diverticulitis, strictures (as in ulcerative colitis), a severe fecal impaction.","Diagnosis: the same as an SBO.","Treatment: the same as an SBO, depending on the cause: removing the neoplasm, fluid resuscitation, perhaps bowel rest.","Signs of a Small Bowel Obstruction","Hallmark sign: abdominal distension. Can become a tympanic abdomen.","Cramping periumbilical pain in waves, with periods of comfort.","Tachycardia.","Cannot tolerate PO intake.","Large-volume bilious vomiting.","High-pitched, tinkling bowel sounds.","Cannot pass flatus or gas. Constipation.","Electrolyte imbalances: the small bowel absorbs nutrients and vitamins.","Paralytic Ileus","What Paralytic Ileus Is","A functional obstruction: intestinal peristalsis is lost, with no mechanical obstruction.","Common in the ICU, usually from opioids and anesthesia. Bowel regimens such as senna and docusate are given freely in the ICU to prevent it.","Same symptoms as a mechanical SBO, except absent to hypoactive bowel sounds: no peristalsis is occurring.","Other causes: muscle or nerve damage after surgery, higher risk after GI surgery than after cardiac or respiratory surgery. Intestinal ischemia or shock, a very high risk with ischemic bowel disease. A GI infection or peritonitis. Whenever …","Treating Paralytic Ileus","Pain management: it can be very painful. Acetaminophen or NSAIDs in place of a higher-dose opioid, if appropriate.","Motility: moving increases peristalsis, though these patients often don't feel like moving. Avoid opioids and other meds that slow GI motility when possible.","Bowel rest, IV fluids, electrolyte management.","NG decompression.","Aggressive bowel regimen: a lot of enemas, to get the gut to wake up."]},{"s":"Abdominal Compartment Syndrome","a":"abdominal-compartment-syndrome","k":["Abdominal Compartment Syndrome","Intra-Abdominal Hypertension","Fluid builds up in the abdominal compartment and presses on the abdominal organs. The intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) rises.","Organ Ischemia","IAP 16 to 20 mmHg. Impaired blood flow to multiple organs.","Multiple Organ Dysfunction","IAP above 20 mmHg. Tissue damage and organ failure.","Causes of Abdominal Compartment Syndrome","Massive fluid volume resuscitation: one of the big ones. Trauma patients who lost a lot of blood, as in a motor vehicle crash, stabbing or gunshot wound. Shock.","Third spacing: severe albumin deficiency, as in liver disease. Severe cardiac output issues, as in heart failure.","Abdominal surgery and trauma: a leak, or bleeding into the abdominal compartment.","A severe ileus or bowel obstruction, and ischemic bowel disease especially.","A ruptured aortic aneurysm.","Pancreatitis: third spacing, and trouble keeping fluid in the vascular space.","Acute pancreatitis is taught in Acute Pancreatitis","Recognizing Abdominal Compartment Syndrome","Hugely distended, possibly rigid: hard to the touch from the fluid, not hard like peritonitis.","Measured as bladder pressure: a catheter in the bladder gives the IAP. Measurements taken directly in the abdomen exist, but are not used often.","Supine, HOB flat — Completely flat. Raising the head, even reverse Trendelenburg, increases abdominal pressure. One of the rare times a patient stays flat, along with some neuro injuries.","Sedation and paralytics — Usually needed. Very painful, and pain or fighting the ventilator increases abdominal pressure. Usually ventilated, on a lot of IV pain meds.","Surgical decompression — Usually, when severe. The abdomen is left open for a while, then closed slowly over time.","Antibiotics — An open abdomen risks peritonitis and sepsis. Bacteria translocating out of the gut put an already very ill patient at very high risk of septic shock.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day3-liver-failure.html","t":"Hepatic Failure","secs":[{"s":"Hepatic Failure","a":"","k":["Day 3 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Liver failure: a great understanding of what is going on in the liver. Portal hypertension and its complications, such as its effect on cardiac output.","Hepatic encephalopathy: reviewed below, for the patient in the ICU."]},{"s":"Chronic Liver Disease and Acute Liver Failure","a":"chronic-and-acute","k":["Chronic Liver Disease and Acute Liver Failure","Chronic Liver Disease","Leads to: acute liver failure, through an exacerbation, as heart failure exacerbates or chronic pancreatitis turns acute.","End-stage liver disease: 50 to 100% mortality without a liver transplant.","Acute Liver Failure","What it is: catastrophic damage to an otherwise healthy liver.","Leads to: coagulation abnormalities and encephalopathy, both below. Can lead to chronic liver disease.","The ICU patient is in acute liver failure: truly acute, or chronic liver disease that has worsened.","Causes of Acute Liver Failure","Acetaminophen overdose: the biggest. Failure can come 48 hours to 8 weeks later, especially with binge drinking. No acetaminophen for a hangover.","Binge drinking and hepatotoxic drugs.","A virus, such as hepatitis.","Vascular, an acute loss of perfusion: a shock state, heart failure, an obstruction, a severe hypoperfusion episode.","Pregnancy: HELLP syndrome or preeclampsia."]},{"s":"Sequelae of Liver Disease","a":"sequelae","k":["Sequelae of Liver Disease","What the Liver Does","Metabolism of carbs, fats and proteins.","Makes clotting factors, and removes active clotting factors.","Vitamin and iron metabolism.","Detoxification.","Glucose levels: stores unused glucose, to release when needed.","Portal hypertension: can be seen in acute liver failure, though more associated with chronic liver disease. A concern either way. Its effect on cardiac output is below.","Impaired metabolic processes: the liver is the most complex organ in the body, and liver failure disrupts every function above.","Impaired bile formation and flow.","What portal hypertension is, and its complications, are taught in the GI review","Portal Hypertension and Cardiac Output","Vascular Underfilling","Blood cannot be returned effectively to the right atrium: a decreased fluid volume status.","Hyperdynamic Cardiac Output","Compensation: an overzealous cardiac output.","Cardiohepatic Syndrome","Severe liver failure can cause acute, severe heart failure."]},{"s":"Signs and Complications of Acute Liver Failure","a":"signs-and-complications","k":["Signs and Complications of Acute Liver Failure","Signs of Fulminant Hepatic Failure (Acute Liver Failure)","Pointing to neurologic status, from the lost detoxification: chills, convulsions, decreased level of consciousness and coma, hyperexcitability, insomnia, irritability.","Jaundice, lethargy, nausea and vomiting, sudden onset of high fever.","Complications of Acute Liver Failure","Hepatic encephalopathy and cerebral edema: the biggest. They explain the neurologic signs above. See below.","Coagulopathy: see below.","Hypoglycemia and metabolic abnormalities: without the liver's glucose stores, hypoglycemia is more likely.","Infection: a higher risk.","Cardiopulmonary abnormalities: the hyperdynamic state, above.","Acute kidney injury."]},{"s":"Hepatic Encephalopathy","a":"hepatic-encephalopathy","k":["Hepatic Encephalopathy","What Hepatic Encephalopathy Is","Altered neurologic status caused by a buildup of circulating toxins of hepatic origin, such as ammonia.","Occurs in acute and chronic liver failure.","Exact mechanism unclear: the liver is supposed to take these toxins out, and does not.","Graded 1 to 4, least to most severe. Grades 3 and 4: a lot of long-term sequelae, and worse outcomes.","Hepatic encephalopathy: think neurologic status, seizures and the airway. Once confused, lethargic or obtunded, can the patient maintain the airway? Hepatic encephalopathy also increases the risk of seizures.","Measuring Hepatic Encephalopathy: Ammonia","No exact lab test for hepatic encephalopathy. It is measured with ammonia.","Shows the level of hepatic encephalopathy, not how severe the liver failure is.","High levels: see Cerebral Edema below.","What causes hepatic encephalopathy, and what rising ammonia does, are taught in the GI review","Treating Hepatic Encephalopathy","Lactulose, primarily, titrated to bowel movements and neurologic status.","Goal: 2 to 4 bowel movements a day, and baseline or improved neurologic function.","Other meds that can help: rifaximin, neomycin, metronidazole.","Used in the acute patient. Chronic patients usually take lactulose daily."]},{"s":"Cerebral Edema","a":"cerebral-edema","k":["Cerebral Edema","Cerebral edema is a life-threatening complication of acute liver failure, and the reason hepatic encephalopathy is so concerning.","What Raises the Risk of Cerebral Edema","Hepatic encephalopathy grade: up to 35% of patients at grade 3, up to 80% at grade 4.","Ammonia above 150: increased risk.","Ammonia above 200 mcg/dL: associated with cerebral edema and brain herniation.","Increased ICP is what happens with cerebral edema. Very close, focused and frequent neurologic assessments, watching for sudden symptoms.","Signs of Increased ICP","Pupillary changes.","Level of consciousness.","Decreased motor response.","Change in breathing pattern.","Increased ICP is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP"]},{"s":"Coagulopathy","a":"coagulopathy","k":["Coagulopathy","Coagulopathy in Acute Liver Failure","INR above 1.5: very common.","Thrombocytopenia: platelets below 150,000.","Decreased production of clotting factors: II, V, VII, IX, X. The liver makes some of the clotting factors, so bleeding times are prolonged.","Decreased circulating fibrinogen.","High risk for bleeding: from falls, GI bleeds, variceal bleeding. The body cannot self-correct.","Coagulopathy is taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Variceal bleeding is taught in GI Critical Care Issues","Vitamin K — 5 to 10 mg IV. Often needed, as IV infusions. The liver uses it to make clotting factors II, VII, IX and X.","5 to 10 mg IV. Often needed, as IV infusions.","The liver uses it to make clotting factors II, VII, IX and X.","Platelet transfusion — Often needed. Below 150,000 is low, but platelets are not replaced until very low. Below 10,000/mm3. Below 50,000/mm3 for an invasive procedure.","Often needed. Below 150,000 is low, but platelets are not replaced until very low.","Below 10,000/mm3.","Below 50,000/mm3 for an invasive procedure.","Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) — Bleeding, with an INR above 1.5. INR above 5 to 7: given no matter what, for the very high bleeding risk.","Bleeding, with an INR above 1.5.","INR above 5 to 7: given no matter what, for the very high bleeding risk.","Packed red blood cells — Actively bleeding.","Actively bleeding.","Blood products are taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care"]},{"s":"Interventions and Treatment","a":"interventions","k":["Interventions and Treatment","Assessment — Focused neurologic assessments, for hepatic encephalopathy. Abdominal assessments.","Labs — Ammonia, albumin, bilirubin, coags, liver enzymes.","Supportive therapy — Hemodynamic instability and decreased perfusion to organs. Nutrition.","Bleeding — Monitor bleeding and bleeding risk.","Ascites and encephalopathy — Manage both. Monitor fluid status: albumin production decreases, and fluid third spaces.","A liver biopsy is very dangerous in liver failure, for the high risk of bleeding. Done when the cause is unknown, to show why the patient is in acute liver failure. Bleeding is a huge issue, and hard to control.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day3-neuro-assessment-icp.html","t":"Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP","secs":[{"s":"Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP","a":"","k":["Day 3 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Neurologic assessment: level of consciousness, sensory and motor function, memory, reflexes, respiratory functioning.","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS): eyes, motor and verbal responses.","Stroke: from pathopharm and med-surg."]},{"s":"Neurologic Assessment","a":"neuro-assessment","k":["Neurologic Assessment","The most important focused assessment: neurologic function controls so many of the other body systems. Two parts: brain function and spinal function.","Level of consciousness — The first thing, on walking into the room. Alert to comatose, or paralyzed and sedated.","The first thing, on walking into the room.","Alert to comatose, or paralyzed and sedated.","Glasgow Coma Scale — Takes the level of consciousness to the next level: eyes, motor and verbal responses. See below.","Takes the level of consciousness to the next level: eyes, motor and verbal responses. See below.","Sensory and motor function — Shows brain function and spinal function.","Shows brain function and spinal function.","Memory and reflexes — Higher levels of neurologic function. Memory: not tested on every visit, but often by neuro ICU and stroke nurses. Reflexes: show spinal and brain function. Tested a lot when brain function may be decreased or the brai…","Higher levels of neurologic function.","Memory: not tested on every visit, but often by neuro ICU and stroke nurses.","Reflexes: show spinal and brain function. Tested a lot when brain function may be decreased or the brain injured.","Respiratory functioning — Neurologic status controls and maintains the airway, above all. Drowsy, close to obtunded, not responding to voice: can the patient maintain the airway? Breathing patterns: see below.","Neurologic status controls and maintains the airway, above all.","Drowsy, close to obtunded, not responding to voice: can the patient maintain the airway?","Breathing patterns: see below.","The levels of consciousness and the focused neuro assessment are taught in the Neuro review","Know the neurologic baseline. Gentle, nuanced changes can mean changes in the brain's arterial supply and perfusion. Get the history: a patient who takes a sleeping pill may normally wake confused.","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","Obeys (6) — Squeezes the hands, wiggles the toes, on a verbal command.","Localizes pain (5) — Shakes the hand when the nail bed is squeezed.","Flexion-withdrawal (4) — Moves the hand a little.","Total: 3 to 15. The lowest GCS is 3.","The full Glasgow Coma Scale, eyes, motor and verbal, is taught in the Neuro review","Scoring the Sedated or Intubated Patient","Paralyzed and sedated on purpose: GCS 3. Comment in the GCS box that the patient is paralyzed and sedated.","Sedation vacation (sedation break): sedation titrated down or paused, usually once a day, for a neuro status. Neurologic issues can be hidden in paralyzed and sedated patients.","Intubated or trached: no verbal response, so the score carries a T: 10T, 11T. Eyes open and following commands, but not talking.","Baseline: dementia can lower the GCS. Disoriented but conversing: GCS 14.","GCS scores and airway protection are taught in Respiratory and Progressive Issues in Critical Care","Posturing","Decorticate Posturing","Muscles: all turn in.","Arms: bent toward the center of the body. Curled wrists, balled hands against the chest.","Legs: rigid and extended. Toes pointed and turned in.","GCS motor score: 3, flexion-abnormal.","Decerebrate Posturing","Muscles: all turn out.","Arms: straight, tense and parallel to the body. Flexed wrists, curled fingers.","GCS motor score: 2, extension.","Decorticate is to the core. Decerebrate is away from the core. Either one indicates a significant neurological injury: inappropriate brain function.","Pupils and Breathing Patterns","Pupil Checks","Crucial in high-risk patients, especially the sedated, who may not obey commands, and for whom a sedation vacation may not be possible.","Any change is a neurologic issue: one or both pupils blown, or not reactive to light.","Compare with the baseline: reactive to light earlier in the night, and not now, is a big deal.","Time is brain cells: decompression or another intervention may help, but only once the change is noticed. Check neuro status together at bedside report.","One pupil a different size from the other is an immediate stroke alert. It can mean pressure on one side of the brain.","Breathing Patterns in Neurologic Disorders","Abnormal patterns show where in the brain the lesion is. All are very abnormal.","These patients are usually intubated, so the pattern is seen with the ventilator paused, or when care is withdrawn and the patient extubated.","Withdrawal of care: reassure the family the patient is comfortable. The pattern is the result of what is going on physiologically.","Examples: Cheyne-Stokes respiration, apneustic breathing, cluster breathing."]},{"s":"Increased Intracranial Pressure (ICP)","a":"increased-icp","k":["Increased Intracranial Pressure (ICP)","0 to 15 mmHg","Normal ICP.","Above 20","Increased.","Above 20, Over 5 Minutes","Life-threatening: brain tissue may be dying, or ischemia occurring.","Can result from: head injury, bleeding in the brain, tumors, infections, extra fluid in the brain, stroke.","Three Components of Cranial Pressure","What Makes Up Cranial Pressure","Cerebral tissue: the brain itself.","Blood: arterial and venous flow, perfusing and draining the brain.","Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF): held in the ventricles.","Three water balloons in a bucket. When one expands, the other two are compressed. The skull cannot give the way other areas of the body can, so the end point is herniation, which can kill the patient.","Causes of Increased ICP","Cerebral edema — Brain tissue swollen: increased brain volume. Two types, with different mechanisms: cytotoxic and vasogenic.","Brain tissue swollen: increased brain volume.","Two types, with different mechanisms: cytotoxic and vasogenic.","Increased cerebral blood volume — Loss of autoregulation: from decreased cerebral oxygenation, high metabolic demands, or obstructed outflow. More blood to the brain helps a healthy person under stress. With raised ICP, the aim is less bloo…","Loss of autoregulation: from decreased cerebral oxygenation, high metabolic demands, or obstructed outflow.","More blood to the brain helps a healthy person under stress. With raised ICP, the aim is less blood volume, to cut the brain's demand and the ICP.","Raised in the ICU by: suctioning, turning, pain, vital sign changes, head hyperflexion or extension during oral or trach care.","Increased CSF — Hydrocephalus: more CSF on board, from an obstruction such as a tumor, or blocked absorption after a subarachnoid hemorrhage or infection. One of the easier causes to control.","Hydrocephalus: more CSF on board, from an obstruction such as a tumor, or blocked absorption after a subarachnoid hemorrhage or infection.","One of the easier causes to control.","Signs of Increased ICP","Increased ICP is a medical emergency. When the ICP rises above the mean arterial pressure (MAP), the brain can no longer receive oxygen.","Vital Sign Changes","Cushing's triad: bradycardia, irregular respirations and a widened pulse pressure.","Other Symptoms, If Awake","Significant headache: one of the big ones.","Vomiting, blurred vision.","Lethargy, weakness.","Changes in behavior."]},{"s":"ICP Monitoring and Cerebral Perfusion","a":"icp-monitoring","k":["ICP Monitoring and Cerebral Perfusion","ICP Monitoring: What It Is For","Correlates objective data with the clinical picture to look at cerebral perfusion. The aim is a perfused brain and a stable neurologic status, not the number itself.","Usually for patients with a GCS of 3 to 8.","Assesses response to therapy: is the ICP decreasing after part of the skull is removed?","Classified by location of the device or type of transducer: epidural, ventriculostomy and others.","Ventricular devices are the most common: a catheter into a ventricle, connected to a drainage bag. Drains CSF in a controlled manner and monitors ICP.","Shows the ICP waveform.","Read with the other assessments of perfusion: level of consciousness, nerve function, pupil changes.","Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP)","CPP = MAP − ICP. Cerebral perfusion is how much blood the brain is getting.","CPP Targets","Normal: 60 to 100.","Goal for neurological injuries: above 70, for enough blood flow to heal, bringing oxygenated, nutritious blood to the injured area.","Usually needs a higher MAP than normal, or the ICP kept as low as possible.","Example: an ICP of 20 needs a MAP of 90 for a CPP of 70."]},{"s":"Managing Increased ICP","a":"managing-icp","k":["Managing Increased ICP","Goals: ICP below 20, CPP above 70. Keep the MAP up.","Monitoring and Support for High ICP","Monitor and assess neurologic status: often sedated, so pupils, reflexes, posturing, response to pain and vital signs, with the ICP.","Maintain the airway, provide ventilation and oxygenation, decrease the brain's metabolic demands. Usually established early.","Usually sedated and paralyzed, to let the brain rest, with high MAPs for perfusion.","Positioning — Head of bed at least 30°: promotes cerebral blood flow and CSF drainage. Head neutral and midline, propped up, not sinking forward. BP may drop as the head of the bed rises: a delicate balance with the CPP and ICP.","Head of bed at least 30°: promotes cerebral blood flow and CSF drainage.","Head neutral and midline, propped up, not sinking forward.","BP may drop as the head of the bed rises: a delicate balance with the CPP and ICP.","Turning — Evaluate how the ICP responds. Goal: back to baseline within 5 minutes. Brain perfusion is more important than skin. If the ICP stays up, turn very gently.","Evaluate how the ICP responds. Goal: back to baseline within 5 minutes.","Brain perfusion is more important than skin. If the ICP stays up, turn very gently.","Endotracheal suctioning — Only when necessary: rhonchi, not clear lungs. Preoxygenate at 100%. Hypoxemia elevates ICP, and suctioning can increase it. 10 seconds at a time, the whole procedure within 1 minute.","Only when necessary: rhonchi, not clear lungs.","Preoxygenate at 100%. Hypoxemia elevates ICP, and suctioning can increase it.","10 seconds at a time, the whole procedure within 1 minute.","Hygiene — Bed baths: as with turning, the ICP back to baseline within 5 minutes.","Bed baths: as with turning, the ICP back to baseline within 5 minutes.","Family — Family presence and gentle touch decrease ICP. Teach the family to keep a calm, restorative environment, with the TV quiet.","Family presence and gentle touch decrease ICP.","Teach the family to keep a calm, restorative environment, with the TV quiet.","Medical Management of Increased ICP and CPP","Oxygenation and ventilation — PaO2 above 80, for maximum oxygen delivery to the brain. Normally 60 is acceptable. PaO2 below 50 increases ICP, so these patients are typically sedated and intubated. PEEP kept low: it can increase ICP. Tidal …","PaO2 above 80, for maximum oxygen delivery to the brain. Normally 60 is acceptable.","PaO2 below 50 increases ICP, so these patients are typically sedated and intubated.","PEEP kept low: it can increase ICP. Tidal volume and FiO2 are maximized instead.","Hyperventilation: lowers CO2, which can help increase CPP. Once first line, now less common. Used for an immediate need, such as active herniation.","Diuretics — Loop and osmotic diuretics: decrease cerebral blood volume. Mannitol: an osmotic diuretic, working in minutes. Watch for hypotension and electrolyte problems. Stopping mannitol suddenly can cause a big increase in ICP. Often giv…","Loop and osmotic diuretics: decrease cerebral blood volume.","Mannitol: an osmotic diuretic, working in minutes. Watch for hypotension and electrolyte problems.","Stopping mannitol suddenly can cause a big increase in ICP.","Often given with vasopressors, to keep the MAP up while the patient diureses.","Fluid administration — Maintain and optimize the MAP. Normal saline or hypertonic fluids at times, to avoid cerebral edema. Strict I&O. Serum osmolality below 320.","Maintain and optimize the MAP.","Normal saline or hypertonic fluids at times, to avoid cerebral edema.","Strict I&O.","Serum osmolality below 320.","BP management — MAP usually 70 to 90: whatever keeps the CPP consistently above 70. Systolic below about 160: above 160 is a higher risk of stroke.","MAP usually 70 to 90: whatever keeps the CPP consistently above 70.","Systolic below about 160: above 160 is a higher risk of stroke.","PEEP is taught in Oxygen Therapy and Mechanical Ventilation","Vasoactive medications are taught in Shock, Sepsis, MODS","Reducing Metabolic Demands","Temperature control — No fever: fever greatly increases metabolic demand. It can follow a TBI or spinal cord injury. Cooling blankets, cooling pads, acetaminophen. Targeted temperature management: cooled to about 34 to 35°C for about 24 hou…","No fever: fever greatly increases metabolic demand. It can follow a TBI or spinal cord injury.","Cooling blankets, cooling pads, acetaminophen.","Targeted temperature management: cooled to about 34 to 35°C for about 24 hours, then rewarmed gradually.","Sedation — Reduces pain, agitation, restlessness and resistance to mechanical ventilation, which all increase ICP. Sedatives can lower BP: keep the BP up.","Reduces pain, agitation, restlessness and resistance to mechanical ventilation, which all increase ICP.","Sedatives can lower BP: keep the BP up.","Avoiding seizures — Seizures greatly increase ICP, and brain-injured patients are prone to them. Often a prophylactic anti-seizure drug.","Seizures greatly increase ICP, and brain-injured patients are prone to them.","Often a prophylactic anti-seizure drug.","Paralytics (neuromuscular blockade) — A step beyond sedation.","A step beyond sedation.","Surgical intervention — Craniectomy: part of the skull taken out, to open up the bucket. May be needed to address the cause of the increased ICP.","Craniectomy: part of the skull taken out, to open up the bucket.","May be needed to address the cause of the increased ICP."]},{"s":"Psychosocial Impact of Traumatic Neurological Injury","a":"psychosocial-impact","k":["Psychosocial Impact of Traumatic Neurological Injury","Supporting the Patient and Family","Sudden and unexpected: no one wakes up expecting a stroke, a car accident or a fall.","Life-changing: may be paralyzed for good, or have lifelong or temporary neurologic impairment.","Support the psychosocial needs of the patient and the family.","Keep the room's energy and mood conducive to healing: patience, encouragement, calm energy.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day3-brain-injury-stroke.html","t":"Brain Injury and Stroke","secs":[{"s":"Brain Injury and Stroke","a":"","k":["Day 3 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"Things to Bring Forward","a":"bring-forward","k":["Things to Bring Forward","Earlier Content This Topic Builds On","Stroke: most of it is review.","FAST stroke scale: for recognizing a stroke early.","Diabetes insipidus (DI) and syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH): endocrine complications of TBI, from pathopharm and med-surg.","Seizure precautions: from fundamentals."]},{"s":"Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)","a":"tbi","k":["Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)","TBI: What It Is and Who Gets It","Mechanical disruption of brain tissue from an external impact or injury to the head. A catch-all term for anything that damages the brain.","Leading cause of trauma-related death in people younger than 45.","Highest risk: males, children under 4, young adults 15 to 19.","Leading cause: falls. Then unknown or other, struck by or against, motor vehicle crashes, assaults.","Survival depends on prompt treatment of the primary and secondary injuries.","Three Mechanisms of TBI","Acceleration and deceleration — Acceleration: a moving blunt object strikes a stationary head, transferring injury to the brain along a linear path. Deceleration: the brain rebounds and is injured on the opposite side. Two impacts, two area…","Acceleration: a moving blunt object strikes a stationary head, transferring injury to the brain along a linear path.","Deceleration: the brain rebounds and is injured on the opposite side.","Two impacts, two areas of the brain: the primary impact and the secondary one from the rebound.","Example: a passenger's head hits the dashboard, then the back of the seat.","Rotational — The force transfers its energy to the brain in a non-linear fashion. The head rotates on its axis. The brain and its cells twist: shearing of the nerve cells. Example: a boxer hit in the face, the head turning sharply.","The force transfers its energy to the brain in a non-linear fashion. The head rotates on its axis.","The brain and its cells twist: shearing of the nerve cells.","Example: a boxer hit in the face, the head turning sharply.","Penetrating — A foreign object invades the intracranial space. Can affect multiple areas of the brain: it may go all the way through. Think comprehensively about what to expect. Examples: a bullet, a knife, a falling object.","A foreign object invades the intracranial space.","Can affect multiple areas of the brain: it may go all the way through. Think comprehensively about what to expect.","Examples: a bullet, a knife, a falling object.","Pathophysiology of TBI","Skull Fractures","Often with a scalp laceration. Very mild to extremely severe, open or closed.","The scalp is very vascular and bleeds a lot: scalp bleeding is not a great indicator of severity.","Not common: the skull has a really high compressive strength and is somewhat elastic.","When one happens, a significant risk of a true TBI: brain tissue compromised.","Basilar Skull Fracture","A linear (straight-line) fracture at the base of the skull.","Leads to meningitis more quickly than other open fractures.","Watch for CSF leaks from the ears and nose: a dural tear, a significant risk.","Basilar skull fracture, known or suspected: no NG tubes, no suctioning through the nose, nothing in the ear. The base of the skull is compromised, so an NG tube could go into the brain.","Primary Brain Injury","What happened: concussion, contusion, penetrating injuries, diffuse axonal injury.","Little can be done for it, unless there is an active bleed or a skull fracture to repair.","Secondary Brain Injury","A biochemical consequence of the primary injury, in response to it.","Ischemia, inflammation, cerebral swelling, neuronal death.","Hematomas: epidural, subdural, intracerebral.","Inflammatory response, leading to further cerebral edema. Increased vascular permeability, a bad sign anywhere: cells are dying.","Free radicals overproduced: they further damage brain cells and decrease cerebral perfusion.","Decreased cerebral perfusion is the primary driver of secondary injury.","Treatment aims to prevent the secondary brain injury. There is no medication that stops cerebral edema from a concussion.","TBI Management","GCS as a guide — Used very frequently: is the GCS the same, worse or better? Often driven by the motor and eye responses: a severe TBI is often intubated.","Used very frequently: is the GCS the same, worse or better?","Often driven by the motor and eye responses: a severe TBI is often intubated.","Airway and oxygenation — The priority. GCS below 8 or 9: can the patient maintain the airway? Prevent hypoxia: oxygenating, as well as maintaining the airway, prevents further secondary damage to the brain.","The priority.","GCS below 8 or 9: can the patient maintain the airway?","Prevent hypoxia: oxygenating, as well as maintaining the airway, prevents further secondary damage to the brain.","Monitoring — CPP, ICP and hemodynamics, crucial throughout. Increased ICP is a result of TBI. The worry is the CPP: is the brain perfused adequately?","CPP, ICP and hemodynamics, crucial throughout.","Increased ICP is a result of TBI. The worry is the CPP: is the brain perfused adequately?","Increased ICP — Managed the same way as any increased ICP: the secondary injury causes cerebral edema, with no place in the skull for the swelling to go.","Managed the same way as any increased ICP: the secondary injury causes cerebral edema, with no place in the skull for the swelling to go.","Temperature — Prevent fever: always. Hypothermia may improve outcomes: targeted temperature management cools the patient. Cold slows the cells down, and the overwhelming inflammatory response with them. Not well studied or understood.","Prevent fever: always.","Hypothermia may improve outcomes: targeted temperature management cools the patient. Cold slows the cells down, and the overwhelming inflammatory response with them.","Not well studied or understood.","Nutritional support — Crucial in severe TBI. Hypermetabolism is a common response to TBI: higher nutritional needs.","Crucial in severe TBI.","Hypermetabolism is a common response to TBI: higher nutritional needs.","Surgical intervention — Depends on the severity and the ICP. Fracture repair. Burr holes for subdural hematomas: help decrease the ICP. Craniectomy: a resection of the skull, to prevent herniation. Gives the swelling somewhere to go.","Depends on the severity and the ICP.","Fracture repair.","Burr holes for subdural hematomas: help decrease the ICP.","Craniectomy: a resection of the skull, to prevent herniation. Gives the swelling somewhere to go.","The Glasgow Coma Scale is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP","Managing increased ICP, temperature control included, is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP","TBI Complications","Endocrine: DI, SIADH, cerebral salt wasting (CSW) — Controlled by the pituitary gland, in the brain: damage there puts the patient at risk. CSW: very similar to SIADH. Monitor urine output with all three: it shows what is going on, or wheth…","Controlled by the pituitary gland, in the brain: damage there puts the patient at risk.","CSW: very similar to SIADH.","Monitor urine output with all three: it shows what is going on, or whether one is developing.","Seizures — Higher risk with any TBI. Monitor for seizures. Often a prophylactic seizure medication. See Seizures below.","Higher risk with any TBI. Monitor for seizures.","Often a prophylactic seizure medication. See Seizures below.","Long-term cognitive defects — Personality changes, difficulty with some motor skills: areas of the brain severely damaged, ischemic and necrotic. Often irreversible. Not a guarantee, but a definite risk.","Personality changes, difficulty with some motor skills: areas of the brain severely damaged, ischemic and necrotic.","Often irreversible. Not a guarantee, but a definite risk.","Herniation and brain death — The most concerning complications. See below.","The most concerning complications. See below.","DI and SIADH are taught in the Neuro review","Brain Herniation","A catastrophic consequence of increased ICP. Cerebral edema or a subdural hematoma leaves the brain nowhere else to go, so it moves.","Brain tissue shifts from an area of high pressure to low pressure. The direction depends on the type and location of injury.","Restricts blood flow: neurons begin dying very quickly.","Recovery very unlikely. The outcome depends on where the herniation occurs, and how long the ICP was elevated.","Downward herniation is the most damaging: into the area of the brain that controls breathing and blood flow.","Brain Death and Organ Donation","Brain Death","Permanent and complete loss of all brain function, in both the cerebrum and the brainstem.","Primarily loss of the brainstem: it controls breathing, heart rate, blood pressure and level of consciousness.","Caused by loss of blood flow to the brain: a heart attack, stroke, bleeding in the brain, head injury.","Not brain dead does not mean recovery: a patient with some reflexes, or decerebrate posturing, may never come off the ventilator. Brain death is not needed for extubation.","Three Conditions to Declare Brain Death","Persistent coma: no eye, verbal or movement response to painful stimuli. A GCS of 3.","No brainstem reflexes: no eye response to light, no cough response to throat suction.","Cannot breathe without assistance: the ventilator is paused to see whether any breaths come spontaneously. No breathing for 10 minutes without mechanical ventilation.","Organ Donation: The Nurse's Role","Brain death often leads to organ donation, if the family is inclined.","The nurse never brings up organ donation, and never answers questions about it. Leave it all to the organ donation team.","The team reaches out: a persistently low GCS triggers an organ donation consult, or the doctors put one in.","Many sensitive conversations: not the bedside RN's responsibility."]},{"s":"Stroke","a":"stroke","k":["Stroke","Stroke: What Happens","Blood supply to the brain disturbed, by occlusion or hemorrhage.","Brain cells survive about 3 to 4 minutes without oxygen.","Very early treatment aims to save the penumbra, preserving and promoting cerebral blood flow.","Time is brain cells, just as time is heart cells in an MI.","Metabolic Disturbances as Neurons Die","Lactic acidosis.","Release of the neurotransmitter glutamate.","ATP depletion.","Sodium and calcium into the cells.","Cytotoxic cerebral edema.","Mitochondrial failure.","Two dangers, as in TBI: the cells that are dying, and secondary cerebral edema from the secondary injury.","Ischemic vs Hemorrhagic Stroke","Ischemic Stroke","87% of strokes.","Large artery atherosclerosis: the biggest risk factor. Stenosis of the large arteries of the head and neck: a cholesterol plaque, or a thrombus superimposed on the plaque.","Cardioembolic: low flow states or blood in a cardiac chamber. An embolism dislodges to a brain vessel.","AFib: the best example. Stagnant blood in the atria can dislodge straight to the brain, so AFib patients are at high risk and on blood thinners.","Lacunar: small vessel occlusive disease. Chronic: thickened arterial walls, decreased blood flow. Very high rates of recurrence.","Hemorrhagic Stroke","Intracerebral hemorrhage: from uncontrolled hypertension, the most common cause.","Ruptured cerebral aneurysm: occasionally.","AV malformation: congenital. More prone to subarachnoid hemorrhages.","Treated vastly differently, but the consequences of both are pretty similar.","Stroke Assessment","Signs and Symptoms of Stroke","Weakness or numbness of one side of the body: face, arm, leg, or any combination.","Slurred speech, or an inability to comprehend what is being said.","Visual disturbance: transient loss of vision in one or both eyes, double vision, a visual field deficit.","Dizziness, incoordination, ataxia, vertigo.","Sudden-onset severe headache: \"worst headache of my life\".","Assessing a Stroke, in Order","Recognize it early, for either type: the FAST stroke scale. Call for help, or take the family member to the ED.","A stat CT scan without contrast, the second the patient reaches the ED, or presents on the floor. It shows hemorrhagic or ischemic, which decides what is done next.","Monitor severity: the NIH stroke scale (NIHSS), the GCS and level of consciousness. Trend them.","The FAST stroke scale and the nursing assessment are taught in the Neuro review","NIHSS: specific to stroke abnormalities. The higher the score, the more severe the stroke. A score trending down means brain function is improving.","0 — No stroke","1 to 4 — Minor stroke","5 to 15 — Moderate stroke","16 to 20 — Moderate to severe stroke","21 to 42 — Severe stroke","Stroke Management","Ischemic Stroke Management","Bust the clot.","Thrombolysis (tPA): first-line, for a candidate. Most patients get it.","Not a candidate: BP not well controlled (extremely high), ICP already increased, or herniated.","For non-candidates: BP control, ICP control, lower-dose anticoagulants, supportive care.","Interventional radiology: a brain cath, like a heart cath. One option, less frequent.","Hemorrhagic Stroke Management","BP control: primarily, keeping the BP controlled or managed.","Surgical intervention, usually: neurosurgery to repair whatever is bleeding.","Anyone who gets tPA is at high risk for bleeding.","A severe stroke can affect the airway, breathing and circulation, and raise the ICP. It then needs ICP management."]},{"s":"Seizures","a":"seizures","k":["Seizures","A complication of TBI, stroke, really any brain damage. Seen a lot in neurologic injury patients.","Seizure precautions — Needed by many of these patients. Include an Ambu bag in the room, hooked up to oxygen.","Needed by many of these patients.","Include an Ambu bag in the room, hooked up to oxygen.","EEG monitoring — Continuous, or a snapshot. For a decreased neurologic status with no clear cause. Shows brain activity and seizure activity.","Continuous, or a snapshot.","For a decreased neurologic status with no clear cause.","Shows brain activity and seizure activity.","Status epilepticus — Often requires IV benzodiazepines (benzos).","Often requires IV benzodiazepines (benzos).","Maintain airway — If seizing, or at very high risk. Maintain the airway after the seizure.","If seizing, or at very high risk.","Maintain the airway after the seizure.","Maintain vascular access — An IV, to give IV benzodiazepines if seizing begins.","An IV, to give IV benzodiazepines if seizing begins.","IV medications — Several, usually. Given continuously, preventatively, or as treatment. Example: levetiracetam.","Several, usually. Given continuously, preventatively, or as treatment.","Example: levetiracetam.","Seizure precautions, first aid during a seizure and status epilepticus are taught in the Neuro review"]},{"s":"Meningitis","a":"meningitis","k":["Meningitis","Bacterial meningitis is a neurological emergency. IV antibiotics STAT.","What it is — Infection of the pia mater and arachnoid layers of the meninges, and the CSF in the subarachnoid space. Most meningitis is viral. Bacterial: a very high risk of neurologic problems.","Infection of the pia mater and arachnoid layers of the meninges, and the CSF in the subarachnoid space.","Most meningitis is viral. Bacterial: a very high risk of neurologic problems.","How it spreads — Usually from a nearby local infection: an open skull fracture, an abscess on the skull or in the brain. An eye abscess or infection is a big one: it can travel to the brain.","Usually from a nearby local infection: an open skull fracture, an abscess on the skull or in the brain.","An eye abscess or infection is a big one: it can travel to the brain.","Signs and symptoms — Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days. Fever, throbbing headache, nuchal rigidity.","Headache, stiff neck and vomiting, over 1 to 2 days.","Fever, throbbing headache, nuchal rigidity.","Diagnosis — Spinal tap: a sample of the CSF. WBCs in the CSF indicate bacterial meningitis. There should be none. A stiff neck, a throbbing head and vomiting: essentially a guaranteed spinal tap.","Spinal tap: a sample of the CSF.","WBCs in the CSF indicate bacterial meningitis. There should be none.","A stiff neck, a throbbing head and vomiting: essentially a guaranteed spinal tap.","Nursing care — Droplet precautions, seizure precautions, temperature management, ICP monitoring. Can be very severe: the inflammation can cause cerebral edema and increased ICP. Monitor closely.","Droplet precautions, seizure precautions, temperature management, ICP monitoring.","Can be very severe: the inflammation can cause cerebral edema and increased ICP. Monitor closely.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day3-spinal-cord-injury.html","t":"Spinal Cord Injury","secs":[{"s":"Spinal Cord Injury","a":"","k":["Day 3 · Topic 5"]},{"s":"Spinal Cord Injury (SCI)","a":"sci","k":["Spinal Cord Injury (SCI)","Who Gets SCI, and How","Most common at ages 16 to 30.","Causes: motor vehicle accidents, falls, acts of violence (gunshots, stabbings), sports injuries, diving accidents.","TBI and SCI often go hand in hand.","The level of injury decides the long-term effects to anticipate. C4 and C6 injuries: tetraplegia. T6 and L1 injuries: paraplegia."]},{"s":"Pathophysiology of SCI","a":"sci-pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology of SCI","What Happens to the Cord","With or without injury to the vertebrae, which house the spinal cord.","Complex, multifaceted biochemical changes in the cord: the secondary injury.","As in TBI and stroke, the biochemical inflammatory response on top of the primary injury causes much of the damage.","Spinal Cord Edema","From the inflammatory response. Compresses the tissue and the blood vessels. Can ascend or descend from the level of injury.","Microscopic Hemorrhages","Vascular changes: bleeding into the gray and white matter. The edema worsens.","Ischemia","Blood flow decreases further.","Axonal Degeneration","The nerves fail to conduct: the permanent loss of function.","Secondary injury in TBI is taught in Brain Injury and Stroke","Two Types of Shock","Spinal Shock","Electrical silence of the cord below the level of injury: complete loss of motor, sensory and reflex activity, at the level of injury and below.","Begins within minutes of the injury. Can last up to 6 weeks.","The permanence of the injury is not known until it resolves: the secondary injury can still spread up or down.","Resolution: usually signaled by the return of deep tendon reflexes. Rarely, motor and sensory function return.","Neurogenic Shock","Disruption of autonomic function below the level of injury: the pathways are damaged.","Sympathetic input lost: vasodilation, a distributive shock.","Manifests as hypotension, bradycardia, hypothermia.","Can occur during spinal shock. Duration variable.","Resolution: signaled by the return of sympathetic tone.","Not always: depends on whether the lesion is complete or incomplete. More likely with an injury at T6 or higher.","Distributive shock is taught in Shock, Sepsis, MODS","Complete vs Incomplete Lesion","Complete — Total, permanent loss of sensory and motor function.","Incomplete — Some motor and sensory function spared.","The lesion type reflects the severity of the injury."]},{"s":"Assessment of SCI","a":"sci-assessment","k":["Assessment of SCI","Airway and Respiratory: The First Priority","Why Breathing Is at Risk","An injury at the cervical or thoracic level can affect the diaphragm.","No nerve stimulation to the diaphragm: no independent breathing.","Paralysis of the diaphragm: intubation and mechanical ventilation.","C1 to C3","Ventilator dependent.","C4 or C5","Will need a phrenic nerve pacemaker.","C5 to C6","Typically intact diaphragmatic breathing. Varying impairment of the intercostal and abdominal muscles.","T6 and above: very high risk for breathing issues. An incomplete lesion above T6 varies: watch these patients very closely for airway and respiratory problems.","Assessing the Other Systems","Neurologic — Sensory, motor and reflex function.","Sensory, motor and reflex function.","Temperature management — T6 and higher: unable to regulate body temperature. Febrile or hypothermic: more likely to have prolonged issues. Monitor closely and continuously, especially in the first several days. Example: a continuous bladder…","T6 and higher: unable to regulate body temperature.","Febrile or hypothermic: more likely to have prolonged issues.","Monitor closely and continuously, especially in the first several days.","Example: a continuous bladder temperature.","Hemodynamics — At risk from neurogenic shock, especially T6 and higher. A significant SCI: in the ICU at least 7 days after the injury, for early detection and management of an unstable heart rate and BP. Likely bradycardic: from hypothermi…","At risk from neurogenic shock, especially T6 and higher.","A significant SCI: in the ICU at least 7 days after the injury, for early detection and management of an unstable heart rate and BP.","Likely bradycardic: from hypothermia, hypoxia or neurogenic shock.","Bowel and bladder function — Function lost: spinal shock leads to atony of the bowel and bladder. High risk for urinary retention. High risk for paralytic ileus.","Function lost: spinal shock leads to atony of the bowel and bladder.","High risk for urinary retention.","High risk for paralytic ileus.","Skin — High risk of pressure ulcers and skin deterioration: motor and sensory function lost, impaired circulation, immobility.","High risk of pressure ulcers and skin deterioration: motor and sensory function lost, impaired circulation, immobility.","Psychosocial — A life-changing, unexpected, horrible event: much psychosocial and psychological support needed. Reach out to get these patients the support they need, and provide it yourself as the bedside nurse.","A life-changing, unexpected, horrible event: much psychosocial and psychological support needed.","Reach out to get these patients the support they need, and provide it yourself as the bedside nurse.","Paralytic ileus is taught in GI Critical Care Issues","Psychosocial support after a neurological injury is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP"]},{"s":"Interventions for SCI","a":"sci-interventions","k":["Interventions for SCI","Anytime the brain or nerves are involved: maintain a patent airway and respiratory function.","Skin assessment — Thorough skin assessments, for the pressure ulcer risk above.","Thorough skin assessments, for the pressure ulcer risk above.","Immobilization — Usually immobilized until cleared by the neurosurgery team. Unstable vertebral fracture: surgery, usually within 24 hours. Stable fracture: may need a halo vest, so the patient is not moving and further damaging the spinal …","Usually immobilized until cleared by the neurosurgery team.","Unstable vertebral fracture: surgery, usually within 24 hours.","Stable fracture: may need a halo vest, so the patient is not moving and further damaging the spinal cord.","Perfusion — Goal MAP 85 to 90, for about 7 days: a higher goal, for spinal cord perfusion. Minimize systolic hypotension during this time: it can cause further damage and secondary injury.","Goal MAP 85 to 90, for about 7 days: a higher goal, for spinal cord perfusion.","Minimize systolic hypotension during this time: it can cause further damage and secondary injury.","Bowel and bladder regimen — Often an NG tube and abdominal assessments. Bladder: often long-term catheters, or a bladder program. Bowel: maintain bowel programs, with enemas.","Often an NG tube and abdominal assessments.","Bladder: often long-term catheters, or a bladder program.","Bowel: maintain bowel programs, with enemas.","Heparin and VTE prophylaxis — Venous stasis: limbs not moving, so a very high risk for DVT and PE.","Venous stasis: limbs not moving, so a very high risk for DVT and PE.","Nutrition — A hypermetabolic state: prompt nutritional support. Very important, as in TBI.","A hypermetabolic state: prompt nutritional support. Very important, as in TBI.","Preventing DVTs and PEs is taught in the Respiratory Diseases review"]},{"s":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","a":"autonomic-dysreflexia","k":["Autonomic Dysreflexia","What it is — A chronic complication of SCI. An exaggerated response from the sympathetic nervous system (SNS): the SNS is completely activated, and the parasympathetic too. The body's protective response.","A chronic complication of SCI.","An exaggerated response from the sympathetic nervous system (SNS): the SNS is completely activated, and the parasympathetic too.","The body's protective response.","Who and when — An injury at T6 or above. Typically after the initial spinal shock resolves.","An injury at T6 or above.","Typically after the initial spinal shock resolves.","Triggers — Usually a problem the patient cannot sense. Bladder: a kinked catheter, a very distended bladder. Bowel: fecal impaction. Skin: clothes too tight, wrinkled bed sheets. Temperature not controlled adequately.","Usually a problem the patient cannot sense.","Bladder: a kinked catheter, a very distended bladder.","Bowel: fecal impaction.","Skin: clothes too tight, wrinkled bed sheets.","Temperature not controlled adequately.","Signs and symptoms — Pounding headache. Bradycardia. Elevated, uncontrolled BP. Diaphoresis and flushing above the level of injury. High levels of anxiety.","Pounding headache.","Bradycardia.","Elevated, uncontrolled BP.","Diaphoresis and flushing above the level of injury.","High levels of anxiety.","Treatment — Fix whatever is causing it, and it can resolve. Supportive care.","Fix whatever is causing it, and it can resolve.","Supportive care.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"day4-hemodynamics.html","t":"Hemodynamics","secs":[{"s":"Hemodynamics","a":"","k":["Day 4 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"What Hemodynamics Are","a":"what-are-hemodynamics","k":["What Hemodynamics Are","Hemodynamics: the forces involved in the flow of blood as it circulates through the cardiovascular system. They control oxygen delivery and respond to oxygen demand.","Think adequate perfusion and oxygenation. Judging stability asks: does the patient have both, and how do I know?"]},{"s":"Assessing Hemodynamic Stability","a":"assessing-stability","k":["Assessing Hemodynamic Stability","Look at the Patient: Signs of Perfusion and Oxygenation","Level of consciousness: is there perfusion and oxygenation to the brain?","Breathing: deep or shallow, rapid or slow? An increased work of breathing?","Color: warm, pink and dry, or pale, really pale, green, blue? Look at the face and hands.","Hands and feet: peripheral perfusion, so not always a great indicator. A face doing okay with very pale hands and feet still says something about perfusion and oxygenation.","Warmth: warm, cool or cold? Diaphoretic or clammy?","Pulses: weak, thready or strong? Appropriate for the scenario?","Heart rate — 60 to 100, at a regular rate? Frequent PVCs or PACs? Four-beat runs of VT?","60 to 100, at a regular rate?","Frequent PVCs or PACs? Four-beat runs of VT?","Blood pressure — A great non-invasive indicator of perfusion. The ICU uses the MAP (mean arterial pressure) a lot: it tells what the tissues are getting perfused. Wide or narrow pulse pressures affect it. Typical goal: MAP above 60. Some un…","A great non-invasive indicator of perfusion.","The ICU uses the MAP (mean arterial pressure) a lot: it tells what the tissues are getting perfused. Wide or narrow pulse pressures affect it.","Typical goal: MAP above 60. Some units also keep the systolic above 100.","SpO2 and respiratory rate — Read against the supplemental oxygen. Example: 94% on 4 L an hour ago, 90% on 6 L now, respirations 16 then 24. Not terrible yet, but trending toward hemodynamically unstable.","Read against the supplemental oxygen.","Example: 94% on 4 L an hour ago, 90% on 6 L now, respirations 16 then 24. Not terrible yet, but trending toward hemodynamically unstable.","ABG — Arguably non-invasive: a snapshot by arterial puncture, usually drawn by respiratory therapy. Shows whether oxygen supply and demand are evening out. More acidotic means anaerobic metabolism: not a good sign.","Arguably non-invasive: a snapshot by arterial puncture, usually drawn by respiratory therapy.","Shows whether oxygen supply and demand are evening out. More acidotic means anaerobic metabolism: not a good sign.","Trend it. Patient condition in the ICU can change moment to moment, so the heart rate, blood pressure and SpO2 are read as trends."]},{"s":"Cardiac Output","a":"cardiac-output","k":["Cardiac Output","Heart Rate","Set by: nerves and hormones.","More than 60 to 100: a rate of 150, 120 or 40 each plays into cardiac output differently. What is the rhythm?","AFib loses the atrial kick: about 20% of cardiac output, even rate controlled at 90. A very common dysrhythmia in the critically ill.","Frequent PVCs decrease cardiac output.","Stroke Volume","Preload: the blood volume.","Afterload: the vascular resistance, usually called SVR (systemic vascular resistance), because it can be measured.","Both are measured through invasive monitoring.","Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume, and the three pillars of stroke volume, are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review"]},{"s":"Invasive Monitoring","a":"invasive-monitoring","k":["Invasive Monitoring","Why invasive: heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory rate are sometimes not enough to show what is happening at the vascular and tissue level. Invasive lines measure closer to the heart.","Central line (central venous catheter) — In a vena cava, just outside the right atrium. Venous circulation. — Lots of access: a triple lumen, the common one, gives three ports for meds. Some hemodynamic monitoring: the CVP.","Lots of access: a triple lumen, the common one, gives three ports for meds.","Some hemodynamic monitoring: the CVP.","Pulmonary artery (PA) catheter — A central line through the right atrium and right ventricle, into the pulmonary artery. Technically venous circulation. — How the heart is working, and the pulmonary vasculature. Meds can go through some of …","How the heart is working, and the pulmonary vasculature.","Meds can go through some of its ports, not all.","Arterial line — In an artery. — Blood pressure and frequent ABGs. Not for drug infusion.","Blood pressure and frequent ABGs.","Not for drug infusion.","Nothing is ever infused into an arterial line. It sits in an artery, and meds never go into an artery.","Managing the Equipment","Pressure tubing — Required whenever a line is transduced. Never regular IV tubing: the two are not interchangeable. Less flexible, so more reliable monitoring.","Required whenever a line is transduced. Never regular IV tubing: the two are not interchangeable.","Less flexible, so more reliable monitoring.","Transducer — Translates intravascular pressure changes into waveforms and numbers.","Translates intravascular pressure changes into waveforms and numbers.","Flush system — Normal saline: maintains patency. Flush whenever the line is used or set up again, and with every assessment, about every 4 hours.","Normal saline: maintains patency.","Flush whenever the line is used or set up again, and with every assessment, about every 4 hours.","Location — The phlebostatic axis: the fourth intercostal space at the midpoint of the chest. Roughly level with the armpit and the heart. Too high or too low gives wrong numbers. If the lines suddenly read differently and inconsistently, ch…","The phlebostatic axis: the fourth intercostal space at the midpoint of the chest. Roughly level with the armpit and the heart.","Too high or too low gives wrong numbers. If the lines suddenly read differently and inconsistently, check the level.","Zeroing the transducer — Every shift, at the start. Open it to atmospheric pressure and zero it. Then close it, so no air gets in the system, and flush. Keeps the lines patent and the numbers accurate.","Every shift, at the start.","Open it to atmospheric pressure and zero it. Then close it, so no air gets in the system, and flush.","Keeps the lines patent and the numbers accurate."]},{"s":"Arterial Line","a":"arterial-line","k":["Arterial Line","Site — Radial (thumb side) whenever possible. Femoral less often. Ulnar (pinky side) occasionally, but not preferred: much more likely to lose perfusion to the hand.","Radial (thumb side) whenever possible. Femoral less often.","Ulnar (pinky side) occasionally, but not preferred: much more likely to lose perfusion to the hand.","Continuous BP monitoring — Blood pressure second to second, more accurate than a cuff. Who gets one: unstable blood pressure, a risk of blood pressure issues such as after a big surgery, and IV blood pressure medications. IV medications act…","Blood pressure second to second, more accurate than a cuff.","Who gets one: unstable blood pressure, a risk of blood pressure issues such as after a big surgery, and IV blood pressure medications.","IV medications act in minutes. A cuff cycled every 1 to 2 minutes does not give accurate readings.","Frequent ABGs — An oxygen supply and demand issue leads to anaerobic metabolism and acidosis. ABGs as often as every hour or every 30 minutes, then every 2 hours as the patient stabilizes. Gives the arterial oxygen, the arterial CO2 and the…","An oxygen supply and demand issue leads to anaerobic metabolism and acidosis. ABGs as often as every hour or every 30 minutes, then every 2 hours as the patient stabilizes.","Gives the arterial oxygen, the arterial CO2 and the pH.","Easy access for other labs too, as is any central line.","Complications and Removal","Lost perfusion below the insertion — Check below the catheter: cap refill, warm, pink, consistent with the patient's other skin tone. Radial or femoral alike. Rare, but always a risk.","Check below the catheter: cap refill, warm, pink, consistent with the patient's other skin tone. Radial or femoral alike.","Rare, but always a risk.","Bleeding — A likely spot to ooze and bleed in a patient at high risk, such as with DIC or a coagulation issue. An artery bleeds much more than a venous site. Watch closely. A pressure dressing or a clotting product on the site may be needed…","A likely spot to ooze and bleed in a patient at high risk, such as with DIC or a coagulation issue.","An artery bleeds much more than a venous site. Watch closely. A pressure dressing or a clotting product on the site may be needed.","Kinks — A weird reading, or a sudden zero: make sure the patient is not coding, then check for a kink. Secure it better, or change the dressing. Insertion is painful: avoid losing the line.","A weird reading, or a sudden zero: make sure the patient is not coding, then check for a kink.","Secure it better, or change the dressing. Insertion is painful: avoid losing the line.","Before Removing an Arterial Line","Check the bleeding risk: low platelets, a high INR, an elevated anti-Xa on a heparin drip. Any of these: high risk of bleeding at the site.","Hold pressure until it stops: about 5 minutes by policy. The patient may hold it, while watched."]},{"s":"Central Venous Pressure (CVP)","a":"central-venous-pressure","k":["Central Venous Pressure (CVP)","CVP: What It Tells You","Transduced from a central line, whose long tip sits right outside the right atrium.","An indicator of central blood volume: a preload, or fluid volume, indicator. Low: low fluid volume. High: fluid volume excess. See the RAP below.","Normal: 2 to 8 mmHg.","General information on cardiac output, venous return and fluid status. Has a waveform too.","Can be very inaccurate: if not transduced properly, if a lot is going on, if a lot of fluid runs through that line. Trend it and read it with the clinical picture.","Central Line Concerns","Infection (CLABSI) — A big entry point for bacteria. An infection from a central line is already in the bloodstream: a worry for sepsis.","A big entry point for bacteria.","An infection from a central line is already in the bloodstream: a worry for sepsis.","Pneumothorax — A risk at insertion: the lung can be punctured. After insertion: breath sounds and a chest X-ray.","A risk at insertion: the lung can be punctured.","After insertion: breath sounds and a chest X-ray.","Hematoma — Bleeding at the site, under or around it. Worse with a coagulopathy. The bigger the line, the bigger the bleeding risk. Multi-lumen central lines are smaller. An introducer is larger.","Bleeding at the site, under or around it. Worse with a coagulopathy.","The bigger the line, the bigger the bleeding risk. Multi-lumen central lines are smaller. An introducer is larger."]},{"s":"Pulmonary Artery (PA) Catheter","a":"pa-catheter","k":["Pulmonary Artery (PA) Catheter","PA Catheter: What It Gives","Formerly called a Swan-Ganz.","The most invasive hemodynamic monitoring, and the best measure of what is going on with the heart.","Several ports: some infuse, but most only transduce pressures.","Measures: the RAP, cardiac output and cardiac index, PA pressures, SvO2. Each is below.","Also gives: the SVR, a stroke volume estimate, and the most accurate temperature, right at the heart.","Right Atrial Pressure (RAP) and CVP","The RAP directly measures right ventricular preload: it sits in the right atrium. Normal: 2 to 8 mmHg, the same as the CVP, which is the indirect and less accurate reading.","Increased RAP or CVP","Means: fluid volume excess, or increased pulmonary vascular resistance.","Seen in: heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, cardiac tamponade, extreme fluid resuscitation.","Treat: restrict fluid and sodium, diuretics such as furosemide, vasodilating medications. Vessels that are too tight can look like fluid overload, and vasodilating lowers the RAP.","Decreased RAP or CVP","Means: fluid volume deficit, absolute or relative.","Absolute: no fluid. Over-diuresis, acute hemorrhage, a lot of vomiting and diarrhea, burns.","Relative: fluid in the body, but not in the vasculature. Third spacing such as ascites or peripheral edema, vasodilation from septic shock. It still does not help cardiac output or perfusion, so is sometimes treated the same.","Treat: IV fluids, blood transfusions, vasoconstricting medications to tighten the vessels and use the fluid there.","PA Pressures and Wedge Pressure","PA pressures are read like a blood pressure: PA systolic over PA diastolic. They measure left and right heart pressures.","PA systolic (PAS) — 20 to 30 mmHg — Peak pressure of the right ventricle ejecting its stroke volume: the pressure needed to open the pulmonic valve. Speaks more to the pulmonary system.","PA diastolic (PAD) — 4 to 12 mmHg — Resistance of the pulmonary vasculature, with the pulmonic valve closed and the tricuspid valve open.","Wedge pressure (PAOP) — 8 to 12 mmHg — The left ventricle's function and fluid status. Indirect, but the most accurate measure of them. Also called the pulmonary artery occlusive pressure, or the pulmonary artery or capillary wedge pressure…","Elevated PA systolic — Increased right ventricle afterload or pulmonary vascular resistance: lung problems — Pulmonary hypertension, lung disease, severe hypoxemia","Elevated PA diastolic — The left side of the heart not functioning well — Left-sided heart failure, myocardial infarction","Low PA diastolic — Inadequate venous return: low preload — Shock (very vasodilated), dehydration, fluid volume deficit","PA systolic speaks to the lungs. PA diastolic speaks to the left heart.","Cardiac Output and Cardiac Index","Cardiac output (CO) — 4 to 8 L/min — The amount of blood pumped out by the ventricle every minute. Continuous cardiac output monitoring (CCO) through the PA catheter is the best measurement of it.","Cardiac index (CI) — 2 to 6 L/min. Goal: above 2. — Cardiac output individualized to body surface area. Usually the one used.","Cardiac output is more than preload or stroke volume: it takes in volume status, contractility (the squeeze) and afterload together."]},{"s":"Systemic Vascular Resistance (SVR)","a":"svr","k":["Systemic Vascular Resistance (SVR)","SVR: What It Measures","The resistance the left ventricle must overcome to open the aortic valve and eject blood into the aorta: the afterload measurement.","Not blood pressure: vessel resistance. Are the vessels open, clamped down tight, or appropriate?","Normal: 800 to 1200.","SVR = (MAP − CVP) ÷ CO × 80. The MAP is the blood pressure, the CVP the fluid status, the CO the squeeze.","Elevated SVR","Ask: is it a positive or a negative compensation?","Hypovolemia, in a \"healthy\" patient: positive. Narrowed vessels move the fluid there out to the peripheral tissues more effectively, as in anyone dehydrated. Treat the cause: give fluid.","Cardiac failure: negative. Compensatory too, but the heart fights a higher afterload, which worsens the failure. Give meds to decrease the SVR.","Decreased SVR","Systemic vasodilation: the vessels are wide open.","Medications: vasodilators, given on purpose or not.","Shock states, which vasodilate the vessels.","Treat the underlying cause: BALANCE. Why is the SVR altered? Is the patient fluid overloaded, systemically vasodilated, or in cardiac failure?"]},{"s":"Mixed Venous Oxygen Saturation (SvO2)","a":"svo2","k":["Mixed Venous Oxygen Saturation (SvO2)","SvO2: What It Measures","Oxygen saturation in the pulmonary artery: the average venous oxygen saturation of the whole body, so \"mixed venous\".","The pulmonary artery is the last stop before hemoglobin is resaturated in the lungs, so the lowest oxygen level. SvO2 is the oxygen that was not used.","Normal: 60 to 75%, not near zero. The tissues use only about 25 to 40% of the oxygen in the blood.","Shows oxygen supply and demand.","ScvO2: essentially the same, from a central line, with slightly different normals.","Higher SvO2 (Above 75%)","Increased O2 supply: the supplemental oxygen is known already, so not usually the reason.","Decreased O2 demand: usually. Sedated and paralyzed, the tissues are not taking up oxygen.","Inability to extract O2 at the tissues: especially in the critically ill. In acidosis, such as a pH of 7.1, the cells cannot extract oxygen as well.","Lower SvO2","Decreased O2 supply: low hemoglobin (check the H&H), low cardiac output, low SaO2.","Increased O2 consumption: the most common in the critically ill. Infection, trauma, shivering after anesthesia, agitation and restlessness.","Example: a cardiac index of 1.5 gives an SvO2 of 40 to 50%. Without the cardiac output to compensate, the tissues take more oxygen."]}]},{"f":"day4-shock-sepsis-mods.html","t":"Shock, Sepsis, MODS","secs":[{"s":"Shock, Sepsis, MODS","a":"","k":["Day 4 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Microcirculation: The Key to Cell Life","a":"microcirculation","k":["Microcirculation: The Key to Cell Life","The Capillary Bed and Cell Survival","Where: the portion of the vascular bed between arterioles and venules. The most significant portion of circulation for cell survival: cells line up next to the capillaries.","Its jobs: delivers oxygen and nutrients to cells, removes waste products, regulates blood volume.","Constricts or dilates selectively to send blood to cells in need: the gut's capillaries shut down when the brain, lungs and heart need more. Where shunting to the vital organs happens.","The capillaries themselves do not contract: a precapillary sphincter regulates the flow.","The exchange is controlled by blood pressure, SVR and cardiac output. All three matter because they serve this transfer of oxygen and nutrients to the cells.","Lost in shock: critically ill patients can lose the ability to perfuse at the capillary level."]},{"s":"From Insult to MODS","a":"insult-to-mods","k":["From Insult to MODS","SIRS or Sepsis","The precursors. An insult sets off a widespread inflammatory response.","Shock","Perfusion is lost at the capillary level. The cells lose oxygen and nutrients.","MODS","The shock state progresses until organs fail. See below."]},{"s":"SIRS and Sepsis","a":"sirs-and-sepsis","k":["SIRS and Sepsis","What Goes Wrong in SIRS and Sepsis","Widespread inflammation in a severe disease process, set off by an insult.","Negative feedback loop lost: no balance between the pro-inflammatory and anti-inflammatory signals.","Increased capillary permeability: inflammatory mediators open the endothelial walls. Fluid shifts from the intravascular space into the cellular space: a relative hypovolemia.","Other mediators: microvascular clotting, impaired fibrinolysis, widespread vasodilation.","SIRS (Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome)","Trigger: an insult that is not an infection: trauma, burns, pancreatitis. Also a big MI (usually through ischemia), an inflammatory disease process, or a combination.","Anyone can have a SIRS response. Many patients meet its criteria, so a pro-inflammatory state is common.","A cue: not everyone goes on to sepsis or shock, but the patient is at risk for worse.","Sepsis","Trigger: a known or suspected source of infection: bacteria, fungi, parasites, viruses, other.","Positive blood cultures are not required.","SIRS plus an infection is sepsis. The presentation is the same: an overactive inflammatory response. Only the trigger differs."]},{"s":"What Shock Is","a":"what-is-shock","k":["What Shock Is","Shock begins when the cardiovascular system fails to function properly because of at least one of four essential circulatory components.","The Four Essential Circulatory Components","Blood volume","Myocardial contractility","Blood flow","Vascular resistance","Shock: The Clinical Picture","Compensation first: when one component fails, the others usually compensate. Shock follows when the compensatory mechanisms fail and perfusion is lost.","Not a single clinical entity: no shock criteria, as there are for SIRS and sepsis. Read the whole picture of the patient.","A life-threatening response to altered circulation that leads to a lack of tissue perfusion.","Any or all cells can be affected. End result: organ dysfunction or death. With enough lost perfusion, the organ itself dies.","Lack of Perfusion","Lack of perfusion is a lack of oxygen to the cells.","Cells Switch Metabolism","The cells begin to go to anaerobic metabolism.","Tissue Acidosis","Its by-product, lactic acid, builds up. Too acidotic: death.","All shock is an imbalance of oxygen demand and supply: cellular demand outweighs supply. Not only a blood pressure or cardiac perfusion problem."]},{"s":"Stages of Shock","a":"stages-of-shock","k":["Stages of Shock","Stage 1: Initiation","Subclinical hypoperfusion, and altered oxygen delivery and consumption at the tissues. No obvious signs, though monitored hemodynamics may change.","Stage 2: Compensatory","Sustained reduction in tissue perfusion. Neural, endocrine and chemical mechanisms kick in.","Stage 3: Progressive","Increasing tissue hypoperfusion, lactic acidosis, failure of the sodium-potassium pump. The big clinical downturn.","Stage 4: Refractory","Severe tissue hypoxia, with ischemia and necrosis. Treatment stops working.","An increased respiratory rate is often the first sign of shock. Count it. Trend it with the heart rate and SpO2: respirations and heart rate creeping up, SpO2 creeping down.","Compensatory Mechanisms","Neural (the SNS response) — Heart rate and contractility increase. Vasoconstriction, bronchodilation. Blood redistributed to the vital organs.","Endocrine — The renin-angiotensin system (RAS), ADH and glucocorticoids: the body holds on to water and sodium and vasoconstricts, to raise blood pressure and perfusion.","Chemical — The liver releases stored glycogen and blood sugar rises, to feed the cells that need it. Sugar is the cell's energy source.","Compensation is not good news: it answers an inappropriate hypoperfusion, so cells somewhere are dying.","Progressive Stage: What You See","Blood pressure: low, with a narrow pulse pressure.","Breathing: tachypnea.","Skin: cold, clammy, mottled.","Neuro: lethargy, altered level of consciousness, even coma.","Kidneys: anuria. Increased BUN and creatinine.","Labs: hyperglycemia, increased potassium, acidosis.","The truly shocked patient in the ICU. Still reversible: fixing the cause can stop it here.","Refractory Stage: What You See","Tissues: severe hypoxia with ischemia and necrosis. Worsening acidosis.","Lungs and blood: ARDS, acute respiratory failure, DIC.","Resistant hypotension: fluid resuscitation and blood pressure meds are not working.","Organs: acute kidney injury, liver injury, cerebral or myocardial damage.","Higher mortality, lower chance of recovery: the goal is to prevent this stage."]},{"s":"Shock States","a":"shock-states","k":["Shock States","Cardiogenic — Impaired oxygenation: the heart fails as a pump to deliver oxygenated blood.","Hypovolemic — Inadequate cardiac output from decreased intravascular volume.","Distributive — A relative hypovolemia from excessive vasodilation.","Obstructive — Impaired oxygenation or perfusion from a mechanical barrier to blood flow."]},{"s":"Clinical Findings in Shock","a":"shock-findings","k":["Clinical Findings in Shock","Vital signs — Low blood pressure, increased respiratory rate. Heart rate up or down, by the severity and the type of shock.","Low blood pressure, increased respiratory rate.","Heart rate up or down, by the severity and the type of shock.","Urine output — Low: less perfusion to the kidneys.","Low: less perfusion to the kidneys.","Level of consciousness — Altered: anywhere from lethargy to comatose.","Altered: anywhere from lethargy to comatose.","Color — Shows perfusion to the skin, especially central color: around the lips and face.","Shows perfusion to the skin, especially central color: around the lips and face.","Arterial pH — Acidotic, from anaerobic metabolism.","Acidotic, from anaerobic metabolism.","Lactate — Lactic acid is the by-product of anaerobic metabolism, so lactate is one of the best trenders of shock and perfusion. An ABG with a lactate every hour in the seriously shocky patient. Expect a peak, then a downward trend as treatm…","Lactic acid is the by-product of anaerobic metabolism, so lactate is one of the best trenders of shock and perfusion.","An ABG with a lactate every hour in the seriously shocky patient. Expect a peak, then a downward trend as treatment works.","SvO2 — Really decreased in severe shock. Trend it: a mixed venous sample, or continuous cardiac output monitoring.","Really decreased in severe shock. Trend it: a mixed venous sample, or continuous cardiac output monitoring.","Organ function — BUN, creatinine, liver enzymes: kidney and liver function. Lost perfusion to the organs is why shock is so concerning.","BUN, creatinine, liver enzymes: kidney and liver function.","Lost perfusion to the organs is why shock is so concerning.","SvO2 is taught in Hemodynamics"]},{"s":"Treating Shock","a":"shock-treatment","k":["Treating Shock","Optimize Oxygen Delivery","Supplemental oxygen: almost every patient. It often helps little unless the problem is like ARDS, but maximize it.","IV fluids: volume to perfuse with. Crystalloid: usually LR, or normal saline. Colloid when third spacing is a concern: albumin. Blood is a colloid too, but it replaces blood rather than pulling volume back from the tissues.","Inotropic medications: positive inotropes such as milrinone and dobutamine give the heart more squeeze.","Vasoactive medications: tighten up the blood pressure, so perfusion reaches the peripheral tissues.","Decrease Oxygen Consumption","Decrease the total work of the body: movement, pain and anxiety, temperature.","Sedation, pain control, neuromuscular blocking agents: the biggest way. The patient is put to sleep (not a restorative sleep), which severely decreases demand.","Maintain normothermia: critical. A fever is a big increase in metabolic demand.","Maintain normal blood sugars: they may be scary high or low. An insulin drip or a dextrose drip. Poor glycemic control varies oxygen consumption."]},{"s":"Cardiogenic Shock","a":"cardiogenic-shock","k":["Cardiogenic Shock","Cause: usually a serious MI or end-stage heart failure. Also myocarditis or severe dysrhythmias. The heart muscle is damaged or stunned.","Hemodynamic — Hypotension: low MAP. Low cardiac output, cardiac index, stroke volume: the heart is not pumping effectively. High PA pressures, wedge pressure, SVR: the body clamps down to raise the blood pressure. Low urine output.","Hypotension: low MAP.","Low cardiac output, cardiac index, stroke volume: the heart is not pumping effectively.","High PA pressures, wedge pressure, SVR: the body clamps down to raise the blood pressure.","Low urine output.","Fluid volume overload — Pulmonary edema, edema, JVD.","Pulmonary edema, edema, JVD.","Neurologic — Decreased level of consciousness.","Decreased level of consciousness.","ABG — Respiratory acidosis, hypoxemia.","Respiratory acidosis, hypoxemia.","Cardiogenic Shock: Treatment Goals","Improve contractility: positive inotropes such as milrinone and dobutamine. Or mechanical circulatory support: LVAD, Impella, ECMO, balloon pump.","Optimize preload: maybe a little fluid, but never overload a heart failure patient.","Reduce afterload: control the blood pressure.","Prevent and treat dysrhythmias: the irritated or stunned heart is at higher risk.","LVAD and ECMO are taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues"]},{"s":"Hypovolemic Shock","a":"hypovolemic-shock","k":["Hypovolemic Shock","External blood loss — GI hemorrhage, surgery, trauma","External loss of fluid — Diarrhea and vomiting, diuresis, burns","Internal sequestration of blood or fluid — Ascites, third spacing, internal bleeding such as a retroperitoneal bleed. That blood cannot be pulled back into the vessels, as third-spaced fluid can.","Usually an absolute loss: the blood or fluid is gone. Third spacing hypovolemic shock can be trended, and does not usually come on so quickly.","Hypovolemic Shock: Hemodynamic Effects","Low blood pressure, cardiac output and index, urine output: with no volume there is no preload, and cardiac output falls.","MAP below 60.","Heart rate high, initially: a fluid volume deficit in the vessels.","The unique feature, a very high SVR: the vessels clamp down to push what little fluid there is out to the peripheral tissues.","Hypovolemic Shock: Assessments","Altered LOC: agitation, confusion, then coma.","Pulmonary: early, tachypnea and hyperventilation. Late, labored and shallow.","Pulse: weak and thready.","Skin: cool and clammy.","Urine: dark, concentrated, low.","Hypovolemic Shock: Treatment Goal","Fluid resuscitate and improve the blood pressure.","Still not doing well once fluid resuscitated: vasopressors.","Usual targets: hemoglobin around 7 if the cause is blood loss. MAP around 60."]},{"s":"Distributive Shock","a":"distributive-shock","k":["Distributive Shock","What Happens in Distributive Shock","Widespread vasodilation: also called vasogenic shock.","Decreased venous return to the right atrium: the vessels are so open that fluid flows into the capillary space and stays there.","Why relative hypovolemia: by the time the volume reaches the capillaries, there is no blood pressure behind it.","Not really a fluid volume problem: fluids refill the tank, but the vessels stay open.","Decreased stroke volume, cardiac output, blood pressure and tissue perfusion. Hypoxia.","Three types: septic, the most common (below), neurogenic, and anaphylactic. Treat anaphylactic shock with epinephrine.","Neurogenic shock is taught in Spinal Cord Injury"]},{"s":"Sepsis and Septic Shock","a":"septic-shock","k":["Sepsis and Septic Shock","Sepsis — A systemic response to infection. Meets at least two SIRS criteria.","A systemic response to infection.","Meets at least two SIRS criteria.","Severe sepsis — Sepsis plus at least one: acute organ dysfunction, hypoperfusion or hypotension (lactic acidosis, oliguria), acute LOC alteration.","Sepsis plus at least one: acute organ dysfunction, hypoperfusion or hypotension (lactic acidosis, oliguria), acute LOC alteration.","Septic shock — Persistent sepsis-induced hypotension and hypoperfusion despite adequate fluid resuscitation. Plus multiple organ dysfunctions (at least two) and a persistent shock state.","Persistent sepsis-induced hypotension and hypoperfusion despite adequate fluid resuscitation.","Plus multiple organ dysfunctions (at least two) and a persistent shock state.","Early Septic Shock","Increased respiratory rate: the first sign.","Changes in LOC: confusion, agitation.","Fever or hypothermia: temperature dysregulation.","Progressive Septic Shock","Fluid shifts: third spacing and edema. The vessels are open, and intravascular volume moves into the capillary space.","Lab trends: rising WBC. Blood cultures positive or negative.","Misdistribution of circulation: blood shunted from the gut to the vital organs. Diminished bowel sounds. Oliguria, from less perfusion to the kidneys.","Tissue ischemia and necrosis: watch the gut, skin and extremities. Ischemic bowel can follow.","Coagulopathy: bleeding and oozing from insertion sites, altered platelet counts, prolonged bleeding times.","Why Septic Shock Clots and Bleeds","A thrombotic and fibrinolytic response to infection: mediators released from bacteria and WBCs.","They stimulate an unregulated procoagulant state, and platelet aggregation and adhesion: microcirculatory clots.","Clots and bleeding in the peripheral tissues: perfusion there is lost, and patients can lose hands and feet.","Particular to septic shock, though possible in other shock. Seen quite often in severe septic shock.","No specific treatment: treat the sepsis early, and keep perfusion and the MAP up."]},{"s":"Obstructive Shock","a":"obstructive-shock","k":["Obstructive Shock","PE (pulmonary embolism) — Alveolar dead space, so a ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Increased pulmonary vascular resistance. Fix: dissolve the PE, or severe shock follows.","Alveolar dead space, so a ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Increased pulmonary vascular resistance.","Fix: dissolve the PE, or severe shock follows.","Tension pneumothorax — Increased pleural pressure impedes venous return: blood cannot return to the right side of the heart. A barrier to O2 delivery. Fix: a chest tube or needle decompression.","Increased pleural pressure impedes venous return: blood cannot return to the right side of the heart. A barrier to O2 delivery.","Fix: a chest tube or needle decompression.","Cardiac tamponade — Pressure building around the heart impairs ventricular filling and decreases cardiac output. The heart is constricted so much the left ventricle cannot squeeze out. Fix: relieve the tamponade.","Pressure building around the heart impairs ventricular filling and decreases cardiac output. The heart is constricted so much the left ventricle cannot squeeze out.","Fix: relieve the tamponade.","Obstructive shock: fix the obstruction."]},{"s":"Hemodynamics of Shock","a":"shock-hemodynamics","k":["Hemodynamics of Shock","Shock is where invasive hemodynamic monitoring comes in: preload, afterload and cardiac output, with the assessment, show whether the interventions are improving perfusion.","Hypovolemic — ↓ Primary — ↓ — ↑ — IV fluids","Cardiogenic — ↑ — ↓ Primary — ↑ — Inotropes, revascularization","Distributive (septic, neurogenic) — ↓ — ↓ — ↓ Primary — Vasopressors, IV fluids","Primary: the abnormality that starts that shock state.","Wedge pressure, cardiac output and SVR are taught in Hemodynamics"]},{"s":"Multiple Organ Dysfunction Syndrome (MODS)","a":"mods","k":["Multiple Organ Dysfunction Syndrome (MODS)","MODS: What It Is","Dysfunction of two or more organ systems, as the shock state progresses.","Considered chronically critically ill.","Mortality: about 50% with two organ systems failing, 100% at five.","No magic treatment for MODS: prevent shock, and identify and treat it quickly.","Who Is at Risk for MODS","Systemic infection, particularly gram-negative sepsis","Trauma","Multiple blood transfusions","Aspiration pneumonia","Large volume replacement with crystalloid","Extensive burns","End-organ failure: end-stage liver or renal disease already has one organ failing.","Acute pancreatitis","Hypovolemia","Cardiogenic shock","HIV","Look for Severe Organ Dysfunction","Monitor: urine output, respiratory function, bowel sounds and the gut, liver enzymes.","Titrate and assess every organ's function.","MODS: Treatment Goals","Identify at-risk patients: who is in shock, and whose shock is not improving quickly with the interventions.","Prevent progression: more interventions, before the organ dysfunction progresses.","How many organs are involved, and to what degree? — Trend each one: urine output, BUN and creatinine, liver enzymes, bowel sounds hypoactive or absent. Less urine with a BUN and creatinine that are not horrible is less risk than no urine ou…","Trend each one: urine output, BUN and creatinine, liver enzymes, bowel sounds hypoactive or absent.","Less urine with a BUN and creatinine that are not horrible is less risk than no urine output at all.","How severe is the illness or injury? — The more severe, the higher the risk of MODS.","The more severe, the higher the risk of MODS.","What is the patient's underlying organ reserve? — Age and comorbidity apply here. Long-term chronic disease, such as years of hypertension or diabetes, may already have compromised kidney, liver and gut function.","Age and comorbidity apply here.","Long-term chronic disease, such as years of hypertension or diabetes, may already have compromised kidney, liver and gut function.","How rapidly was effective treatment started? — Noticed and fixed quickly, in the right order? The tank filled? Antibiotics on board very quickly in sepsis?","Noticed and fixed quickly, in the right order? The tank filled? Antibiotics on board very quickly in sepsis?","How many later injuries and complications, and how severe? — Example: a nephrotoxic drug given in a fluid volume deficit, a double punch to the kidneys.","Example: a nephrotoxic drug given in a fluid volume deficit, a double punch to the kidneys."]}]},{"f":"review-respiratory.html","t":"Respiratory","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Oxygen Delivery Devices","a":"oxygen-delivery-devices","k":["Oxygen Delivery Devices","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 1 – Oxygenation.","Oxygen Therapy Basics","FiO2: room air is 21%, and every liter added raises it.","Titration: an order can give a range and a target, such as 2 to 4 L/min by nasal cannula PRN to keep SpO2 88 to 92%. Go up a liter at a time until the saturation is in the window.","Safety: oxygen is highly flammable. Teach no smoking, no candles, no open flames, and post an oxygen-in-use sign.","Low flow: nasal cannula, simple face mask, partial rebreather, non-rebreather. High flow: Venturi mask.","Nasal cannula — 2 to 6 L/min, 24 to 44% — Best for: stable patients with low oxygen needs, including long-term use. Safe and well tolerated. Skin breakdown on the cheeks, behind the ears and at the nose. Humidify above 4 L/min.","Best for: stable patients with low oxygen needs, including long-term use.","Safe and well tolerated.","Skin breakdown on the cheeks, behind the ears and at the nose.","Humidify above 4 L/min.","Simple face mask — 6 to 12 L/min, 35 to 50% — Best for: short periods and transport. Poor choice for claustrophobic patients. Higher risk of aspiration: no eating with the mask on. Assess the fit and the skin under it.","Best for: short periods and transport.","Poor choice for claustrophobic patients.","Higher risk of aspiration: no eating with the mask on.","Assess the fit and the skin under it.","Partial rebreather mask — 6 to 11 L/min, 60 to 75% — Best for: short periods of dyspnea or other increased oxygen needs. Patient rebreathes up to one third of exhaled air, which helps humidification. Keep the reservoir bag partially inflate…","Best for: short periods of dyspnea or other increased oxygen needs.","Patient rebreathes up to one third of exhaled air, which helps humidification.","Keep the reservoir bag partially inflated. Flat and collapsed means no air in the bag.","No flaps over the exhalation ports.","Non-rebreather mask — 10 to 15 L/min, 80 to 95% — Best for: a patient in critical need of oxygen, the steps before intubation. One-way valve lets the patient inhale the maximum O2 concentration. Two exhalation ports with flaps keep exhaled …","Best for: a patient in critical need of oxygen, the steps before intubation.","One-way valve lets the patient inhale the maximum O2 concentration.","Two exhalation ports with flaps keep exhaled air from being rebreathed.","Venturi mask — 4 to 12 L/min, 24 to 60% — Best for: patients who need highly regulated oxygen concentrations, as in chronic lung disease. Delivers a precise oxygen concentration with humidity. Not preferable for long periods.","Best for: patients who need highly regulated oxygen concentrations, as in chronic lung disease.","Delivers a precise oxygen concentration with humidity.","Not preferable for long periods.","Partial rebreather and non-rebreather masks: watch for aspiration, and assess at least hourly.","Other Oxygen Devices","Face tent (aerosol mask): fits loosely around the face and neck. 24 to 100% oxygen with relatively high humidity. Seen a lot post-op.","High-flow nasal cannula: up to 100% oxygen, very precise. For critical patients.","Nasal cannula with ETCO2 monitor: measures the concentration of CO2 the patient exhales.","CPAP and BiPAP","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 1 – Obstructive Sleep Apnea.","Positive airway pressure pushes air into the airway and helps it stay open on inhalation and exhalation.","CPAP","Continuous positive airway pressure: the same setting the whole time.","For spontaneously breathing patients, to improve oxygenation.","Does not augment ventilation. It only maintains upper airway patency.","Mainstay for obstructive sleep apnea. Simplest to use, works well, cheapest.","Noninvasive BiPAP","Two pressures: one during inhalation and a lower one during exhalation, so air is easier to push out.","Works well in COPD, where the problem is exhaling.","Also used for nocturnal ventilation in neuromuscular disease, chest wall deformity, obstructive sleep apnea and COPD.","To prevent intubation: usually one of the last steps before intubating and sending the patient to the ICU."]},{"s":"Respiratory Assessment","a":"respiratory-assessment","k":["Respiratory Assessment","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 1 – Oxygenation.","Areas to Assess","Now: cough, shortness of breath, dyspnea, breath sounds, pain.","History: pulmonary, smoking, medication use, environmental and occupational exposures.","Normal rate: 12 to 20 breaths per minute.","A prolonged increased rate is a cue that something is wrong. A rate above 27 is linked with an increased risk of cardiac arrest.","Alterations in breathing pattern: bradypnea, tachypnea, hyperpnea, apnea, hyperventilation, hypoventilation, Cheyne-Stokes respiration, Kussmaul's respiration.","Breath Sounds","Vesicular — Low — Most of the normal lung","Bronchovesicular — Medium — Mainstream bronchi","Bronchial (tracheal) — High — Trachea","Crackles (rales) — Sounds like: fine to coarse bubbly sounds, due to air passing through fluid or collapsed small airways. Caused by: fluid overload, illnesses that increase fluid.","Sounds like: fine to coarse bubbly sounds, due to air passing through fluid or collapsed small airways.","Caused by: fluid overload, illnesses that increase fluid.","Wheezes — Sounds like: high-pitched whistling, due to narrowed, obstructed airways. Caused by: asthma, inflammatory responses, allergic reactions. Chart whether inspiratory, expiratory or both.","Sounds like: high-pitched whistling, due to narrowed, obstructed airways.","Caused by: asthma, inflammatory responses, allergic reactions.","Chart whether inspiratory, expiratory or both.","Rhonchi — Sounds like: loud, low-pitched rumbling that sounds junky, due to fluid or mucus in the airways. Caused by: conditions that leave secretions in the larger airways, such as bronchitis, pneumonia, COPD. Can resolve with coughing.","Sounds like: loud, low-pitched rumbling that sounds junky, due to fluid or mucus in the airways.","Caused by: conditions that leave secretions in the larger airways, such as bronchitis, pneumonia, COPD.","Can resolve with coughing.","Stridor — Sounds like: harsh, high-pitched crowing on inspiration, due to a narrowed or obstructed upper airway. Caused by: choking. Also heard in children.","Sounds like: harsh, high-pitched crowing on inspiration, due to a narrowed or obstructed upper airway.","Caused by: choking. Also heard in children.","Pleural friction rub — Sounds like: dry grating or creaking, due to the two layers of the pleura rubbing together. Caused by: an inflamed pleural space.","Sounds like: dry grating or creaking, due to the two layers of the pleura rubbing together.","Caused by: an inflamed pleural space.","Oxygen Saturation","SpO2 and SaO2","Oxygen saturation measures diffusion and perfusion.","SpO2: peripheral, read by a probe using light transmission.","SaO2: the arterial measurement.","Normal: 95 to 100%. A provider can prescribe a different limit, as for a patient with lung disease.","Interference with light transmission: patient motion, nail polish (remove it), jaundice, intravascular dyes. Change the probe if the reading is in doubt.","Interference with arterial pulsation: cool fingers and hypothermia, peripheral vascular disease, vasoconstrictors, decreased cardiac output, edema, a tight probe.","Always look at the patient. Do the cues match the number?","Abnormal Findings and What to Do","Dyspnea — Treat the underlying condition first: asthma, pneumonia, heart failure. Sit the patient up. The thoracic cavity expands and the lungs expand better. Oxygen therapy, which can improve gas exchange. Medications: bronchodilators, inh…","Treat the underlying condition first: asthma, pneumonia, heart failure.","Sit the patient up. The thoracic cavity expands and the lungs expand better.","Oxygen therapy, which can improve gas exchange.","Medications: bronchodilators, inhaled steroids, mucolytics, and anti-anxiety medication because anxiety makes dyspnea worse.","Blocked airway — A patent airway is the nursing priority in a patient in distress who still has a pulse. Open it with a head tilt and chin lift. Use a jaw thrust if a neck injury is a concern. Keep an oral airway at the bedside for a patien…","A patent airway is the nursing priority in a patient in distress who still has a pulse.","Open it with a head tilt and chin lift. Use a jaw thrust if a neck injury is a concern.","Keep an oral airway at the bedside for a patient at risk of losing patency.","Rhonchi, or secretions in the airway — Encourage coughing every 2 hours: the most effective way to move secretions, and better than suctioning. Mobilize, hydrate to thin the secretions, and humidify. Medications only supplement these. Thick…","Encourage coughing every 2 hours: the most effective way to move secretions, and better than suctioning.","Mobilize, hydrate to thin the secretions, and humidify. Medications only supplement these.","Thick secretions: chest physiotherapy, then coughing and deep breathing. It needs an order.","Weak or ineffective cough — Suction when the patient cannot clear secretions by coughing or chest physiotherapy. Sterile procedure in hospital. Keep it under 10 seconds in total, and consider pre-oxygenating.","Suction when the patient cannot clear secretions by coughing or chest physiotherapy.","Sterile procedure in hospital. Keep it under 10 seconds in total, and consider pre-oxygenating.","Pain with coughing or deep breathing — Manage the pain and help the patient splint, so coughing and deep breathing are possible.","Manage the pain and help the patient splint, so coughing and deep breathing are possible.","Risk of atelectasis — Incentive spirometer: lung expansion through deep breathing. Prevents or treats atelectasis. Every post-op patient should have one. Deep breathing moves air to the lower lobes and helps start a cough. Change position o…","Incentive spirometer: lung expansion through deep breathing. Prevents or treats atelectasis. Every post-op patient should have one.","Deep breathing moves air to the lower lobes and helps start a cough.","Change position often. Upright is best, and bedridden patients are repositioned frequently."]},{"s":"Respiratory Diagnostics","a":"respiratory-diagnostics","k":["Respiratory Diagnostics","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 1 – Respiratory Labs and Diagnostics.","Chest x-ray (CXR) — Two-dimensional picture of the lung. Most common views: PA and lateral. — Have the patient remove metal between the neck and the waist, such as a bra or necklaces.","Two-dimensional picture of the lung.","Most common views: PA and lateral.","Have the patient remove metal between the neck and the waist, such as a bra or necklaces.","CT scan — Shows structures in cross-section. Usually preferred over MRI for the lung. With or without contrast, by provider preference. Contrast is injected IV and makes specific tissues, organs and blood vessels stand out. — Explain it fir…","Shows structures in cross-section. Usually preferred over MRI for the lung.","With or without contrast, by provider preference.","Contrast is injected IV and makes specific tissues, organs and blood vessels stand out.","Explain it first: a hard table, and a scanner that revolves around the body with a loud clicking noise.","Sedation may be needed to lie still, or for claustrophobia.","With contrast: check creatinine and BUN. Contrast is nephrotoxic, so report an elevated creatinine to the provider.","With contrast: ask about iodine allergy. The contrast is iodine-based and anaphylaxis is possible. Shellfish allergy is listed with it, though research no longer links the two.","Warn of a warm flush all over the body when the contrast goes in. It is expected.","Force fluids afterward to flush the contrast through the kidneys.","Thoracentesis — The provider puts a large-bore needle into the pleural space. Done to obtain fluid for diagnosis, remove pleural fluid, or instill medication. — Position upright with the elbows on the overbed table, which opens the intercos…","The provider puts a large-bore needle into the pleural space.","Done to obtain fluid for diagnosis, remove pleural fluid, or instill medication.","Position upright with the elbows on the overbed table, which opens the intercostal spaces.","Instruct the patient to stay still and not talk.","After: chest x-ray per order, in case the pleura was nicked.","After: assess for hypoxia and pneumothorax. Neither is expected."]},{"s":"Tracheostomy","a":"tracheostomy","k":["Tracheostomy","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 1 – Tracheostomy and Med Surg I Week 1 – Tracheostomy.","Shiley","Plastic. Seen most in the hospital. A fresh post-op trach is a Shiley, even one that will be long-term.","Inner cannula: disposable. At trach care it is thrown away and replaced.","Cuff: has one.","Jackson","Metal. Long-term: a patient who keeps a trach long enough is switched from a Shiley to a Jackson.","Inner cannula: reusable. At trach care it is cleaned with solution and a small scrub brush.","Cuff: none.","Cuff Inflated","When: the patient is mechanically ventilated, or the provider ordered it, such as with meals.","Why: a snug fit in the trachea prevents aspiration and helps the ventilator give stronger breaths, with no air leaking around it.","Cuff Deflated","When: typically on the floor (med-surg), so usually deflated at the initial head-to-toe assessment there.","To deflate: Suction the mouth first. Secretions resting on the cuff would be aspirated once it deflates. Deflate the cuff. Suction the trachea afterward.","Suction the mouth first. Secretions resting on the cuff would be aspirated once it deflates.","Deflate the cuff.","Suction the trachea afterward.","Respiratory therapy usually inflates and deflates the cuff: how much air it takes depends on the trach.","Assessment — Type and size. A Shiley is usually a 6 or an 8. The 6 is smaller. Cuff: inflated or deflated? The pilot balloon shows which. Discomfort or pain. A new trach can still be tender. Oxygenating appropriately? The whole respiratory …","Type and size. A Shiley is usually a 6 or an 8. The 6 is smaller.","Cuff: inflated or deflated? The pilot balloon shows which.","Discomfort or pain. A new trach can still be tender.","Oxygenating appropriately? The whole respiratory assessment applies, breath sounds included.","Ties intact, and the obturator and spare trachs in the room: see below.","Trach care — Usually every 12 hours. The inner cannula is replaced or cleaned, by type: see above.","Usually every 12 hours. The inner cannula is replaced or cleaned, by type: see above.","Suctioning — Indication: secretions the patient cannot clear. They can plug the trach. As often as indicated, which may be more often than trach care. Assess for the indications. Sterile: the trach is direct access to the lungs, a risk for …","Indication: secretions the patient cannot clear. They can plug the trach.","As often as indicated, which may be more often than trach care. Assess for the indications.","Sterile: the trach is direct access to the lungs, a risk for infection.","Technique: see Weak or ineffective cough, above.","Communication — Passy-Muir speaking valve: helps the patient speak. The cuff is deflated while it is in use. Not in any respiratory distress. Some patients do not tolerate it: it can bring anxiety or a feeling of respiratory distress. Needs…","Passy-Muir speaking valve: helps the patient speak. The cuff is deflated while it is in use.","Not in any respiratory distress. Some patients do not tolerate it: it can bring anxiety or a feeling of respiratory distress.","Needs the provider's OK. Speech and respiratory therapy usually help with the first use.","If the Trach Comes Out","Accidental decannulation: most often while the patient coughs, or at transition times. A cough during a tie change can push the whole trach out.","Keep at the bedside: the obturator, taped to the bed or wall at all times, whatever the type. Two spare trachs: the patient's size, and one smaller in case of edema or swelling.","Take out the inner cannula and put the obturator into the outer cannula.","Extend the neck and open the tissue. Insert the outer cannula with the obturator in it, holding both at the flange so the obturator cannot back out.","Remove the obturator immediately. Left in, it obstructs the airway. Put the inner cannula back.","Check bilateral breath sounds.","Secure the trach: ties back on.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-respiratory-diseases.html","t":"Respiratory Diseases","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory Diseases","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)","a":"copd","k":["Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 1 – COPD.","COPD is emphysema, chronic bronchitis, or most often both. The problem is getting air out of the body, not getting it in.","Emphysema","Alveolar damage. The alveoli are overinflated and stretched, and work less well.","Impaired gas exchange at the alveoli.","Chronic Bronchitis","Excessive secretion production is the key feature.","Barrel chest — Increased anteroposterior diameter from hyperinflation. Closer to 1:1 than the expected 2:1.","Breath sounds — Decreased. Often wheezing, rales or rhonchi as well, especially in an exacerbation.","Prolonged expiration — Exhaling is not passive. The air has to be pushed out.","Pursed-lip breathing — Advanced disease. Compensatory: it creates positive pressure between the lips and the alveoli.","Neck vein distention, peripheral edema — Advanced disease. May indicate pulmonary hypertension, which makes blood back up.","Cachexia — Advanced disease. Excessively malnourished and extremely thin, because so many calories go to breathing.","Chest x-ray — Widened AP diameter, so the chest looks boxy, and a flattened diaphragm.","Nursing Implications","Smoking cessation: the single most effective and cost-effective intervention to reduce the risk of COPD and its progression.","Inhaler therapy: the mainstay of self-management. Long-acting beta-agonists (the mainstay) and long-acting muscarinic antagonists bronchodilate. Inhaled corticosteroids reduce inflammation.","Oxygen: target O2 sat 88 to 92%, lower than for the average patient.","Positioning and breathing: tripod position, which opens up the thorax, and pursed-lip breathing.","Acute Exacerbation of COPD","Worsening of dyspnea, cough or sputum beyond the patient's typical day-to-day variation, requiring a change in the treatment plan. Most common cause: a respiratory infection, such as pneumonia on top of the COPD.","Management in Hospital","Oxygen is the mainstay when hypoxemia is present. Keep SpO2 above 90%. Some providers accept 88%: the floor is provider preference.","Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation, such as BiPAP, when hypercapnic.","Bronchodilators by metered-dose inhaler or wet nebulizer.","Chest physiotherapy, usually by respiratory therapy.","Possibly antibiotics, if the cause is bacterial.","Never withhold oxygen from a hypoxemic patient, even one who retains CO2. Avoid high saturations instead: 99% is too high for a patient with COPD.","CO2 Narcosis and the CO2 Retainer","Normal stimulus to breathe: an elevated CO2 level.","CO2 retainer: after years of COPD the body has adapted to retained CO2, and a low oxygen level becomes the stimulus to breathe.","CO2 narcosis: in theory, a high percentage of oxygen (6 L/min, saturating at 99%) takes away that drive to breathe."]},{"s":"Asthma","a":"asthma","k":["Asthma","For further study, see Pediatrics Week 2 – Chronic Respiratory.","Chronic inflammatory airway disorder, also called reactive airway disease.","Pathophysiology","Type 1 hypersensitivity immune response, IgE mediated.","Mast cells release histamine and leukotrienes. Result: diffuse obstructive and restrictive airway disease.","Three causes of the narrowing: inflammation and edema of the mucous membranes, tenacious secretions from the mucous glands, and spasm of the bronchial smooth muscle.","Signs and Symptoms","Dyspnea: inflammation, bronchoconstriction and mucus narrow the airway and raise airway resistance.","Expiratory wheezing: turbulent airflow through narrowed bronchioles during expiration, when airways naturally become smaller.","Cough: irritation and mucus stimulate the cough receptors. Hacking and nonproductive at onset, from early bronchospasm. Later rattling and productive of clear sputum, as mucus production rises.","Prolonged expiratory phase: bronchoconstriction delays airflow out of the lungs.","Respiratory distress: nasal flaring, cyanosis, intercostal retractions. Severe airway obstruction leads to hypoxia and accessory muscle use.","Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR)","Three zones interpret the PEFR. Each is a percent of the personal best, established over 2 to 3 weeks while the asthma is stable.","Green — 80 to 100% — Probably okay. Continue activities and controller medication. No rescue medication needed.","Yellow — 50 to 79% — Take the rescue medication, a bronchodilator. Continue controller medication.","Red — Below 50% — Take the bronchodilator immediately, then seek emergency care.","Management","Trigger Control","Triggers: pollen, exercise, cold air, pets, stress, anger, pollution, strong odors, smoke, dust, chemical fumes, bugs in the home, fungus spores.","Eliminating the triggers is a main way of controlling asthma. Secondhand smoke is a big one.","Controllers (Preventer Medications)","Corticosteroids: inhaled beclomethasone, budesonide and fluticasone, and prednisone. The inhaled ones can cause thrush, so rinse the mouth after each dose.","Long-acting beta-2 agonists: salmeterol, and fluticasone with salmeterol. Added when the inhaled corticosteroid alone does not control the asthma. Not for acute episodes.","Leukotriene inhibitor: montelukast. Helps with the mucus and the edema.","Mast cell stabilizer: cromolyn.","Relievers (Rescue Medications)","Short-acting beta-2 agonists (bronchodilators): albuterol, levalbuterol. For wheezing or coughing on the spot.","Side effects: nausea, shakiness, anxiety, tachycardia, tachypnea.","Magnesium sulfate: in the ED or ICU, for moderate to severe asthma. Relaxes the muscles, decreases inflammation, and improves pulmonary function and peak flow.","Bronchodilator first, then the inhaled corticosteroid. The bronchodilator opens the airways, so the corticosteroid can reach deeper into the lungs. Given the other way round, the narrowed airways keep the medication from depositing.","Asthma Exacerbation","Progressively worsening shortness of breath, cough, wheezing or chest tightness, alone or in combination.","What Happens in the Airways","Air is trapped behind airways narrowed or occluded by bronchospasm, mucosal edema and mucus plugging.","Hyperinflation: functional residual capacity rises. It keeps the airways open and permits gas exchange.","Hypoxemia from the mismatch of ventilation and perfusion: carbon dioxide tension rises and oxygen tension falls.","Nursing Interventions","High Fowler's: upright for maximum chest expansion.","Oxygen: supplemental as needed, with continuous pulse oximetry.","Control panic with a calm approach. Sit beside the patient and breathe together, using the diaphragm to pull air in and expel it.","Rescue drugs: usually by nebulizer, sometimes continuously for a while. Then transition to the regular medications."]},{"s":"Pneumonia","a":"pneumonia","k":["Pneumonia","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 1 – Pneumonia.","Acute infection of the lung parenchyma. Secretions that sit in the lower lungs are the perfect breeding ground, so a patient who cannot expectorate them is at risk.","Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)","Patient has not been hospitalized or lived in a long-term care facility within 14 days of the onset of symptoms.","Hospital-Acquired Pneumonia (HAP)","Begins 48 hours or longer after admission, and was not present at admission.","In non-intubated patients.","Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP): the type of HAP in an intubated patient.","Viral — Most common type. May be mild and self-limiting, or cause life-threatening problems. Cough: nonproductive or scanty. No antibiotics.","Most common type.","May be mild and self-limiting, or cause life-threatening problems.","Cough: nonproductive or scanty.","No antibiotics.","Bacterial — Usually more serious. May require hospitalization. Cough: productive. Purulent sputum that is green, rusty, or looks like red currant jelly. Treated with antibiotics.","Usually more serious. May require hospitalization.","Cough: productive. Purulent sputum that is green, rusty, or looks like red currant jelly.","Treated with antibiotics.","Aspiration — Abnormal entry of material from the mouth or stomach into the trachea and lungs. Risk factors: decreased level of consciousness, dysphagia, NG tubes. May be silent: no vomiting and no cough.","Abnormal entry of material from the mouth or stomach into the trachea and lungs.","Risk factors: decreased level of consciousness, dysphagia, NG tubes.","May be silent: no vomiting and no cough.","Opportunistic — In immunocompromised patients.","In immunocompromised patients.","Clinical Manifestations","Fever, chills, cough, malaise, dyspnea, fatigue, myalgia.","Chest pain with inspiration and coughing, so the patient avoids deep breaths.","Elderly: new-onset confusion. Often one of the first signs of any infection.","Severe cases: respiratory distress, then respiratory failure.","Chest x-ray — Infiltrates.","Infiltrates.","WBC with differential — Leukocytosis with bacterial infection. Shift to the left: indicates an acute bacterial infection.","Leukocytosis with bacterial infection.","Shift to the left: indicates an acute bacterial infection.","Sputum culture and sensitivity (C&S) — Positive when bacterial: identifies the specific bacteria and the antibiotics that will kill it. No bacteria when viral.","Positive when bacterial: identifies the specific bacteria and the antibiotics that will kill it.","No bacteria when viral.","Acute Interventions","Oxygen: supplemental as ordered, to keep the pulse oximetry above 95%.","Secretions: pulmonary toilet and chest physiotherapy, to loosen them so the patient can expectorate them.","Increase fluids (IV or PO) to thin the secretions, unless contraindicated, as in severe heart failure.","Ambulation, with energy conservation: bundle the care, and still get the patient up and moving.","Drug therapy: antibiotics if bacterial, analgesics for pleuritic pain, antipyretics for fever."]},{"s":"Venous Thromboembolism (VTE)","a":"venous-thromboembolism","k":["Venous Thromboembolism (VTE)","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 2 – Cardiovascular, Med Surg II Week 2 – Chronic Venous Insufficiency and Med Surg I Week 3 – Post-op.","VTE is DVT and PE together. A deep vein thrombus is a stationary clot, most often in the legs. The danger is the clot dislodging, traveling to the lungs and wedging there as a pulmonary embolism, which can kill.","Who Is at Risk","Virchow's triad: venous stasis, endothelial damage, hypercoagulability of the blood. The three together form the clot.","Venous stasis — Blood pooling in the veins. — Advanced age. Bed rest and prolonged immobility. Surgery. Heart failure. A fractured leg or hip. A long trip without adequate exercise. Obesity. Pregnancy: the belly impedes venous return. Varic…","Advanced age.","Bed rest and prolonged immobility.","Surgery.","Heart failure.","A fractured leg or hip.","A long trip without adequate exercise.","Obesity.","Pregnancy: the belly impedes venous return.","Varicose veins.","Endothelial damage — Damage to the lining of the vessels. — Caustic or hypertonic IV fluids and drugs. An IV insertion site. IV drug abuse. Fractures of the pelvis, hip or leg, and dislocations. Trauma.","Caustic or hypertonic IV fluids and drugs.","An IV insertion site.","IV drug abuse.","Fractures of the pelvis, hip or leg, and dislocations.","Trauma.","Hypercoagulability — Thickened blood. — Dehydration or malnutrition. Blood dyscrasias, such as a platelet disorder. Oral contraceptives. Pregnancy. Cancer. Tobacco use. High altitudes.","Dehydration or malnutrition.","Blood dyscrasias, such as a platelet disorder.","Oral contraceptives.","Pregnancy.","Cancer.","Tobacco use.","High altitudes.","A history of DVT or VTE puts the patient at higher risk of another.","Preventing DVT and PE","Early ambulation — Out of bed as soon as it is allowed. In bed the blood pools, because nothing works the muscle pump. Cannot ambulate: turn every 2 hours.","Out of bed as soon as it is allowed. In bed the blood pools, because nothing works the muscle pump.","Cannot ambulate: turn every 2 hours.","Leg exercises — Calf pumping: dorsiflex, plantar flex and circumduct the ankles every hour while awake. Flex and extend the feet, hips and knees every 2 to 4 hours while awake. Both mimic the skeletal muscle pump and promote venous return.","Calf pumping: dorsiflex, plantar flex and circumduct the ankles every hour while awake.","Flex and extend the feet, hips and knees every 2 to 4 hours while awake.","Both mimic the skeletal muscle pump and promote venous return.","Sequential compression devices (SCDs) — Sleeves on the calves that squeeze and release intermittently, mimicking the skeletal muscle pump of walking. Worn in bed. Off to ambulate, and back on when the patient returns to bed. Used more often…","Sleeves on the calves that squeeze and release intermittently, mimicking the skeletal muscle pump of walking.","Worn in bed. Off to ambulate, and back on when the patient returns to bed.","Used more often than TED hose.","TED hose — Thromboembolic deterrent stockings. They promote venous return, not arterial circulation, and decrease swelling and venous stasis. The size has to be right. A poor fit can cause a pressure ulcer.","Thromboembolic deterrent stockings. They promote venous return, not arterial circulation, and decrease swelling and venous stasis.","The size has to be right. A poor fit can cause a pressure ulcer.","Hydration — Promote it. Dehydration makes the blood more concentrated.","Promote it. Dehydration makes the blood more concentrated.","Anticoagulation — An at-risk patient will probably be on enoxaparin or heparin. Puts the patient at risk for bleeding.","An at-risk patient will probably be on enoxaparin or heparin.","Puts the patient at risk for bleeding.","IVC filter (Greenfield filter) — A filter placed in the inferior vena cava, for a patient at extremely high risk who keeps forming clots. Catches a clot before it reaches the lungs. The clot still forms.","A filter placed in the inferior vena cava, for a patient at extremely high risk who keeps forming clots.","Catches a clot before it reaches the lungs. The clot still forms."]}]},{"f":"review-chest-tubes.html","t":"Chest Tubes","secs":[{"s":"Chest Tubes","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 3"]},{"s":"The Drainage System","a":"chest-drainage-system","k":["The Drainage System","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 2 Ticket to Clinical – Chest Tubes.","A chest drainage system gets air and fluid out of the pleural space and lets no atmospheric air back in.","1. Collection chamber — Accepts air or fluid through the extension tubing attached to the patient's chest tube. When it fills, change to a new setup. No provider order is needed.","Accepts air or fluid through the extension tubing attached to the patient's chest tube.","When it fills, change to a new setup. No provider order is needed.","2. Water-seal chamber — One-way valve: air leaves the pleural space and cannot flow back into the patient. Filled with sterile water to the 2 cm mark before first use. Add more as it evaporates.","One-way valve: air leaves the pleural space and cannot flow back into the patient.","Filled with sterile water to the 2 cm mark before first use. Add more as it evaporates.","3. Suction chamber — Regulates the negative suction pressure on the intrapleural space. Amount of suction is the number dialed in. The provider orders it and the nurse sets the dial. Most are at 20 cm. Visual alert shows the suction is work…","Regulates the negative suction pressure on the intrapleural space.","Amount of suction is the number dialed in. The provider orders it and the nurse sets the dial. Most are at 20 cm.","Visual alert shows the suction is working.","Turning the wall suction up further does not increase the pressure.","New order, such as 20 to 25 cm: turn the dial, then raise the wall suction until the visual alert comes back.","To Suction","The second extension tubing runs from the air vent to wall suction.","Nearly everyone starts on suction.","No walking in the hall while on wall suction.","To Underwater Seal (UWS)","The air vent is open to air. No suction.","Turn off the wall suction and take the tubing off the air vent.","Tolerated well for a period of time, by respiratory status and chest x-ray: the provider discontinues the chest tube.","Walking in the hall is fine with two people. One carries the system below the level of the chest.","Never occlude or clamp the air vent. It is either open to air or connected to wall suction. Suction turned off with the tubing left on traps the air, and the backed-up pressure causes a tension pneumothorax."]},{"s":"Assessment and Management","a":"chest-tube-assessment-management","k":["Assessment and Management","The Patient","Out of bed unless contraindicated, with aggressive pulmonary toilet.","Head of bed up (Fowler's). Ventilation is better than supine.","Keep the collection system below the level of the chest. Raised above it, the drainage runs back into the pleural space.","Range of motion for the affected shoulder: active, or passive if necessary.","Teach: never disconnect the chest tube, as for a trip to the bathroom.","Insertion site — Dressing occlusive, dry and intact. Reinforce or change it per hospital policy. Bleeding on the dressing: mark the edge with a pen and the date, initials and time (DIT). Notify the provider if it increases. Palpate around t…","Dressing occlusive, dry and intact. Reinforce or change it per hospital policy.","Bleeding on the dressing: mark the edge with a pen and the date, initials and time (DIT). Notify the provider if it increases.","Palpate around the dressing for subcutaneous emphysema. Typically reabsorbs on its own with time. Mark the edge with a pen and DIT, and notify the provider. Trend it, and notify again if it increases.","Extension tubing — Connections intact. A straight shot to the collection chamber with no excessive dependent loops. Tubing that loops down makes the drainage work against gravity.","Connections intact.","A straight shot to the collection chamber with no excessive dependent loops. Tubing that loops down makes the drainage work against gravity.","Collection chamber — Routinely mark the level on the outside of the chamber with the time and date, and count it as output on the I&O. Assess the volume and the appearance, such as serosanguineous. Expected: drainage that decreases over tim…","Routinely mark the level on the outside of the chamber with the time and date, and count it as output on the I&O.","Assess the volume and the appearance, such as serosanguineous.","Expected: drainage that decreases over time. It increases when the patient first gets up and walks.","Not expected: a sudden large output after very little. Take vital signs and notify the provider.","Water-seal chamber — Water at the 2 cm mark. Abnormal bubbling is a sign of a leak in the system.","Water at the 2 cm mark.","Abnormal bubbling is a sign of a leak in the system.","Suction chamber — Visual indicator in view, and the dial set at the prescribed level.","Visual indicator in view, and the dial set at the prescribed level."]},{"s":"Tidaling and Bubbling","a":"tidaling-and-bubbling","k":["Tidaling and Bubbling","Both are read in the water-seal chamber.","Tidaling","The water bobs up and down as the patient breathes.","Reflects the pressure changes in the pleural space.","Expected when the chest tube is to UWS. It shows a straight shot from the water seal to the pleural space.","No Tidaling Means One of Three Things","The lungs have re-expanded, and the chest tube is about ready to come out.","A kink or obstruction in the tubing.","Suction is on.","Intermittent Bubbling","Normal and expected when a pneumothorax is present. It is the air getting out.","Appears on exhalation, coughing or sneezing, which raise intrathoracic pressure.","Constant Bubbling","Never normal. Ask whether there is a leak in the system.","Gently apply a padded clamp on the drainage tubing, close to the occlusive dressing.","Bubbling stops: the leak is at the exit site or inside the patient. Notify the provider.","Bubbling continues: the leak is between the clamp and the drainage system. Check the connections or replace the system."]},{"s":"Clamping and Accidents","a":"clamping-and-accidents","k":["Clamping and Accidents","Never clamp a chest tube for transport, or when the tubing is accidentally disconnected. Clamping risks a pneumothorax.","Whole chest tube pulled out of the chest — Cover the exit site with an occlusive dressing at once, such as sterile petroleum (Vaseline) gauze on a 4x4. Atmospheric air entering the hole causes a tension pneumothorax.","Tubing becomes disconnected — Put the end of the tube in a bottle of sterile saline to recreate the underwater seal, then get a new setup and reconnect the patient."]},{"s":"Chest Tube Removal","a":"chest-tube-removal","k":["Chest Tube Removal","Steps of Removal","Chest x-ray shows the lung has re-expanded, and fluid drainage has ceased.","Nurse: premedicate with an analgesic, teach and support the patient.","Provider: cuts the sutures and applies sterile petroleum gauze.","Patient takes a deep breath and bears down, which keeps atmospheric air from being sucked into the chest. The provider removes the tube.","An airtight dressing is applied. Mark any drainage with DIT, and reinforce or change the dressing per protocol."]}]},{"f":"review-abgs.html","t":"ABGs","secs":[{"s":"ABGs","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 4"]},{"s":"The ABG Values","a":"abg-values","k":["The ABG Values","For further study, see Pathopharm II Week 2 – Arterial Blood Gas and Fundamentals II Week 1 – Oxygenation.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — Balance of H+ (hydrogen ions). — Acid — Base","Balance of H+ (hydrogen ions).","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 mmHg — Respiratory parameter: changes with the lungs. Partial pressure of CO2 in the artery. Carbonic acid dissolves into CO2 and H2O. — Base — Acid","Respiratory parameter: changes with the lungs.","Partial pressure of CO2 in the artery. Carbonic acid dissolves into CO2 and H2O.","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 mEq/L — Metabolic parameter: changes with the kidneys. Calculated on the ABG, not measured. Serum CO2 on a BMP is the same value, with the same normal. — Acid — Base","Metabolic parameter: changes with the kidneys.","Calculated on the ABG, not measured.","Serum CO2 on a BMP is the same value, with the same normal.","PaO2 — 80 to 100 mmHg — Oxygenation parameter: partial pressure of oxygen in the artery. — Hypoxemia — Hyperoxemia","Oxygenation parameter: partial pressure of oxygen in the artery."]},{"s":"The Four Disorders","a":"four-disorders","k":["The Four Disorders","Respiratory acidosis — ↓ Below 7.35 — PaCO2 ↑ above 45: carbonic acid excess. Hypoventilation retains CO2. — HCO3 ↑, by the kidneys","Respiratory alkalosis — ↑ Above 7.45 — PaCO2 ↓ below 35: carbonic acid deficit. Hyperventilation blows off CO2. — HCO3 ↓, by the kidneys","Metabolic acidosis — ↓ Below 7.35 — HCO3 ↓ below 24: bicarbonate deficit. Excess acids are added, or HCO3 is lost. — PaCO2 ↓, by the lungs","Metabolic alkalosis — ↑ Above 7.45 — HCO3 ↑ above 29: too much HCO3, or not enough carbonic acid. — PaCO2 ↑, by the lungs","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory Opposite: pH ↑ and PaCO2 ↓ is alkalosis. pH ↓ and PaCO2 ↑ is acidosis. Metabolic Equal: pH ↑ and HCO3 ↑ is alkalosis. pH ↓ and HCO3 ↓ is acidosis.","Respiratory Opposite: pH ↑ and PaCO2 ↓ is alkalosis. pH ↓ and PaCO2 ↑ is acidosis.","Metabolic Equal: pH ↑ and HCO3 ↑ is alkalosis. pH ↓ and HCO3 ↓ is acidosis.","Common Causes","Respiratory acidosis — Cardiopulmonary arrest. Head injury. Narcotics or sedatives: an overdose, or too much given. Anesthesia, and after it while the drugs are metabolized. Pulmonary disorders: acute asthma, COPD exacerbation, pneumonia, r…","Cardiopulmonary arrest.","Head injury.","Narcotics or sedatives: an overdose, or too much given.","Anesthesia, and after it while the drugs are metabolized.","Pulmonary disorders: acute asthma, COPD exacerbation, pneumonia, respiratory failure, atelectasis.","Pain: shallow breathing, no deep breaths because it hurts.","Abdominal distension.","Airway obstruction.","Chest wall deformities.","Neuromuscular problems: muscles too weak to ventilate, as in ALS or Guillain-Barré.","Respiratory alkalosis — Hyperventilation: acute anxiety, a panic attack, fear. Increased metabolic demands: fever, infection, sepsis. Medications that increase the breathing rate. Hypoxia. PE or lung disease. CNS lesions: head injuries, bra…","Hyperventilation: acute anxiety, a panic attack, fear.","Increased metabolic demands: fever, infection, sepsis.","Medications that increase the breathing rate.","Hypoxia.","PE or lung disease.","CNS lesions: head injuries, brain lesions.","Ventilator settings: rate set too high, or tidal volume too big.","Metabolic acidosis — Renal failure: the kidneys cannot excrete H+. Fistulas: HCO3 lost with the fluid and electrolytes. Diabetes, type 1: DKA. Ketones are acids. Lactic acidosis: lactate produced when the cells lack oxygen. Prolonged diarrh…","Renal failure: the kidneys cannot excrete H+.","Fistulas: HCO3 lost with the fluid and electrolytes.","Diabetes, type 1: DKA. Ketones are acids.","Lactic acidosis: lactate produced when the cells lack oxygen.","Prolonged diarrhea: the HCO3 released into the intestines to neutralize stomach acid is lost.","Starvation: body fat broken down, causing ketosis.","Medication overdose: aspirin.","Shock and cardiac arrest: lactic acid from tissues without enough oxygen.","Metabolic alkalosis — Too much base: excess baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) or antacids. Prolonged vomiting: stomach acid (hydrochloric acid) is lost, and H+ with it. NG tube: prolonged suctioning pulls hydrochloric acid out of the stomach…","Too much base: excess baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) or antacids.","Prolonged vomiting: stomach acid (hydrochloric acid) is lost, and H+ with it.","NG tube: prolonged suctioning pulls hydrochloric acid out of the stomach.","Diuretics, especially loop diuretics: H+ lost in the urine.","Clinical Manifestations","Respiratory acidosis — CNS: depression. Headache, restlessness, confusion, drowsiness, dizziness, disorientation. Decreased LOC, stupor, coma. Muscle weakness, hyperreflexia. Breathing: hypoventilation, slow, or rapid and shallow. Dyspnea, …","CNS: depression. Headache, restlessness, confusion, drowsiness, dizziness, disorientation. Decreased LOC, stupor, coma. Muscle weakness, hyperreflexia.","Breathing: hypoventilation, slow, or rapid and shallow. Dyspnea, respiratory distress: the patient cannot catch their breath. Hypoxia.","Cardiovascular: tachycardia, dysrhythmias. Decreased BP with vasodilation.","Potassium: hyperkalemia.","Respiratory alkalosis — CNS: over-excitability. Light-headedness, syncope. Confusion, blurred vision, lethargy. Paresthesias of the hands and feet, perioral. Hyperactive reflexes, seizures, coma. Breathing: deep and rapid: tachypnea, hyperv…","CNS: over-excitability. Light-headedness, syncope. Confusion, blurred vision, lethargy. Paresthesias of the hands and feet, perioral. Hyperactive reflexes, seizures, coma.","Breathing: deep and rapid: tachypnea, hyperventilation. Once the patient passes out, breathing regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Cardiovascular: tachycardia. Decreased or normal BP.","Potassium: hypokalemia.","GI: nausea, vomiting.","Metabolic acidosis — CNS: lethargy, drowsiness, confusion, headache. Tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching. Paresthesias of the fingers or toes, perioral. Breathing: Kussmaul respirations (DKA): the lungs' compensatory hyperventilation, …","CNS: lethargy, drowsiness, confusion, headache. Tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching. Paresthesias of the fingers or toes, perioral.","Breathing: Kussmaul respirations (DKA): the lungs' compensatory hyperventilation, blowing off CO2. Fruity odor breath (DKA).","Cardiovascular: hypotension. Warm, flushed skin from vasodilation.","Potassium: hyperkalemia: H+ moves into the cells and K+ comes out.","GI: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.","Metabolic alkalosis — CNS: over-excitability. Restlessness followed by lethargy. Confusion, decreased LOC, dizziness, irritability. Tremors, muscle cramps. Paresthesias of the fingers and toes, perioral. Coma. Breathing: respiratory depress…","CNS: over-excitability. Restlessness followed by lethargy. Confusion, decreased LOC, dizziness, irritability. Tremors, muscle cramps. Paresthesias of the fingers and toes, perioral. Coma.","Breathing: respiratory depression: compensatory hypoventilation, holding on to CO2.","Cardiovascular: dysrhythmias, tachycardia.","Potassium: hypokalemia: H+ shifts out of the cells to buffer the alkalosis, and K+ moves in."]},{"s":"Compensation","a":"compensation","k":["Compensation","Compensation brings the pH back toward normal without correcting the underlying cause. The system not involved in the imbalance does it.","The Lungs: Respiratory Compensation","Compensate for: a metabolic problem.","How: hyperventilation blows off CO2 to correct an acidosis. Hypoventilation conserves CO2 to correct an alkalosis.","Speed: rapid, minutes to hours.","How long: cannot keep it up for long.","The Kidneys: Metabolic Compensation","Compensate for: a respiratory problem.","How: retain (reabsorb) HCO3 to correct an acidosis. Excrete HCO3 to correct an alkalosis. Also retain or excrete H+.","Speed: slow, hours to days.","How long: can keep it up for longer, if functioning properly.","Acute Respiratory Acidosis","pH low, PaCO2 high.","HCO3 normal: no time for the kidneys to compensate.","Examples: respiratory arrest.","Chronic Respiratory Acidosis","PaCO2 high: CO2 retained over time.","HCO3 high: the kidneys have had time to compensate. pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","Never a corrected ABG: the CO2 and the HCO3 stay high.","Breathing worsens, as with an infection: PaCO2 rises, HCO3 has not had time to follow, and the pH drops.","Examples: COPD."]},{"s":"Interpreting an ABG","a":"interpreting-abgs","k":["Interpreting an ABG","1. pH — Acid (below 7.35) or base (above 7.45)? Normal, with PaCO2 or HCO3 abnormal: compensation has occurred. Decide which way it leans: 7.40 is neutral. Below 7.40 leans acid, above 7.40 leans base. Examples: 7.36 and 7.38 lean acid, 7.4…","Acid (below 7.35) or base (above 7.45)?","Normal, with PaCO2 or HCO3 abnormal: compensation has occurred. Decide which way it leans: 7.40 is neutral. Below 7.40 leans acid, above 7.40 leans base.","Examples: 7.36 and 7.38 lean acid, 7.45 leans base.","2. PaCO2 — Acid (above 45), base (below 35) or normal? Abnormal: a respiratory problem. Moves opposite to the pH: one up, one down.","Acid (above 45), base (below 35) or normal?","Abnormal: a respiratory problem. Moves opposite to the pH: one up, one down.","3. HCO3 — Acid (below 24), base (above 29) or normal? Abnormal: a metabolic problem. Moves with the pH: both up or both down.","Acid (below 24), base (above 29) or normal?","Abnormal: a metabolic problem. Moves with the pH: both up or both down.","4. Compensation — Is the body restoring the pH by altering the buffer system not involved in the imbalance? If so, that value moves in the same direction as the other component: both up or both down. One reads acid, the other base.","Is the body restoring the pH by altering the buffer system not involved in the imbalance?","If so, that value moves in the same direction as the other component: both up or both down. One reads acid, the other base.","The pH points the way. The value that matches the pH, both acid or both base, names the problem. With a normal pH, match the way it leans."]},{"s":"The Four Levels of Compensation","a":"levels-of-compensation","k":["The Four Levels of Compensation","Uncompensated","pH abnormal. PaCO2 or HCO3 abnormal, not both.","Partially Compensated","pH abnormal. PaCO2 and HCO3 abnormal: the body has started to pull the pH back.","Fully Compensated","pH normal. PaCO2 and HCO3 abnormal, but balanced. Neutralized, not corrected.","Corrected","pH normal. All values back to normal after the imbalance."]},{"s":"Practice ABGs","a":"practice-abgs","k":["Practice ABGs","7.25, 56, 24 — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis. pH acid, PaCO2 acid, HCO3 normal.","7.35, 56, 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis. pH normal, leaning acid. PaCO2 acid, HCO3 base.","7.60, 25, 24 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. pH base, PaCO2 base, HCO3 normal.","7.45, 30, 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis. pH normal, leaning base. PaCO2 base, HCO3 acid.","7.26, 40, 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis. pH acid, PaCO2 normal, HCO3 acid.","7.35, 33, 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis. pH normal, leaning acid. PaCO2 base, HCO3 acid.","7.45, 49, 30 — Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis. pH normal, leaning base. PaCO2 acid, HCO3 base.","7.30, 52, 30 — Partially compensated respiratory acidosis. pH acid, PaCO2 acid, HCO3 base.","7.60, 31, 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. pH base, PaCO2 base, HCO3 normal.","7.30, 29, 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis. pH acid, PaCO2 base, HCO3 acid.","7.52, 48, 39 — Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis. pH base, PaCO2 acid, HCO3 base.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-hematology.html","t":"Hematology","secs":[{"s":"Hematology","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 5"]},{"s":"The CBC and the H&H","a":"cbc-and-hh","k":["The CBC and the H&H","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 2 – Hematology Labs and Diagnostics.","Hemoglobin (Hgb) — Men 14 to 18 g/dL. Women 12 to 16 g/dL. — The iron-containing pigment of the RBC.","The iron-containing pigment of the RBC.","Hematocrit (Hct, \"the crit\") — Men 42 to 52%. Women 37 to 47%. — The percentage of total blood volume made up by the RBCs. With the hemoglobin, the H&H: used far more in the hospital than the RBC count.","The percentage of total blood volume made up by the RBCs.","With the hemoglobin, the H&H: used far more in the hospital than the RBC count.","WBC count — 5,000 to 10,000 — With a differential (\"CBC w/ diff\"): each type of WBC as a percentage of the total. Tells what may be causing a raised count. See below.","With a differential (\"CBC w/ diff\"): each type of WBC as a percentage of the total.","Tells what may be causing a raised count. See below.","Platelet count — 150,000 to 300,000 or 400,000, depending on the source — A really low count is thrombocytopenia, a reason for bleeding precautions. See below.","A really low count is thrombocytopenia, a reason for bleeding precautions. See below.","Hemoglobin and Hematocrit","Note trends, more so than stand-alone values. An H&H of 9.2 and 28.1 two days after surgery, up from 8.1 and 24.6 the day before, is trending upward: keep an eye on it.","Low Hematocrit","True low: anemia. Anemia is a low hematocrit, with a cause behind it.","Causes: bleeding, bone marrow failure, dietary deficiency, renal disease.","False low: fluid retention, as in heart failure or kidney failure, adds plasma. The percentage drops without anemia.","High Hematocrit","True high: polycythemia.","False high: dehydration leaves little plasma. The percentage rises without polycythemia.","The bus analogy: RBCs and oxygen. RBC: the bus. Without the buses, oxygen cannot get where it belongs. Hgb: the seats. Iron: what the seats are made of. Oxygen: the passengers. O2 sat: how full the buses are, about 95% or higher. Hct: how m…","RBC: the bus. Without the buses, oxygen cannot get where it belongs.","Hgb: the seats.","Iron: what the seats are made of.","Oxygen: the passengers.","O2 sat: how full the buses are, about 95% or higher.","Hct: how much of the highway, the blood, is made up of buses.","The WBC Differential","The five WBCs: Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas. Never: neutrophils, 40 to 70%. Let: lymphocytes, 20 to 25%. Monkeys: monocytes, 3 to 8%. Eat: eosinophils, 2 to 4%. Bananas: basophils, under 1%.","Never: neutrophils, 40 to 70%.","Let: lymphocytes, 20 to 25%.","Monkeys: monocytes, 3 to 8%.","Eat: eosinophils, 2 to 4%.","Bananas: basophils, under 1%.","When the Neutrophils Rise","The most plentiful WBC, and the first to arrive at an acute bacterial infection.","Their percentage goes up and the others go down: the differential always adds up to 100%.","The mature neutrophils (segs) respond, and the baby neutrophils (bands) are released too: a shift to the left, a sign of the onset of an acute bacterial infection."]},{"s":"Anemia Signs and Symptoms","a":"anemia-signs","k":["Anemia Signs and Symptoms","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 2 – Anemia.","The severity of the anemia, and how fast the blood is lost, determine the degree of symptoms.","Mild anemia — 10 to 14 g/dL — No to mild symptoms, with activity.","Moderate anemia — 6 to 10 g/dL — An increase in cardiopulmonary symptoms. May occur at rest.","Severe anemia — Under 6 g/dL — Many body systems involved. Symptoms at rest. Can be life-threatening with other comorbidities, which hospital patients more often than not have.","Sudden against slow loss is taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Why Each Sign Appears","Oxygen to muscles — ↓ — Weakness.","Energy production — ↓ — Fatigue.","Peripheral circulation, to the skin — Redistributed to the vital organs, the heart and lungs (compensatory) — Pallor: palms, nailbeds, face, conjunctiva.","Cardiac output — ↑ at first (compensatory) — ↑ HR, palpitations: a thumping in the chest.","Secretion of erythropoietin — ↑ — Bone pain where the red marrow is: sternum, iliac crest.","Cardiac muscle — Hypoxia — Angina, MI, heart failure: potentially life-threatening.","Overall oxygenation — Hypoxia — Dyspnea, ↑ RR.","The general signs and symptoms in critical care are taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care"]},{"s":"Heparin Drips","a":"heparin-drips","k":["Heparin Drips","Heparin Drip: Labs and Adverse Reactions","Labs: the aPTT or the anti-Xa, by protocol, about 6 hours after the drip starts and after each rate change. The rate is titrated to the result. The platelets too, for HIT.","Bleeding: the main adverse reaction. Gums, urine, stool, wounds, new bruising, a falling H&H. Report it at once: the drip may need to stop. Antidote: protamine sulfate.","HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia): the platelets fall by half or more, usually 5 to 10 days in, and new clots can form.","HIT, its lab and stopping the heparin, are taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care"]},{"s":"Oral Anticoagulants","a":"oral-anticoagulants","k":["Oral Anticoagulants","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Valvular Problems and Med Surg II Week 2 – Atrial Dysrhythmias.","Warfarin","Used for: a mechanical valve, for life. AFib, long term, when treatment fails.","Labs: the INR, monitored regularly. Normal 0.8 to 1.1. Therapeutic with a prosthetic valve: 2.5 to 3.5. Thin, but not too thin: any higher risks bleeding.","Antidote: vitamin K, for any signs of bleeding. FFP can also be given.","Diet: vitamin K reverses warfarin. Foods loaded with it, such as kale greens, are eaten consistently, never binged.","Bleeding: an electric razor, not a straight edge. Report any bleeding or excessive bleeding. See below for bleeding precautions.","The Newer Oral Anticoagulants","Used for: non-valvular AFib, as alternatives to warfarin.","Labs: no routine lab testing, the advantage. Creatinine checked before giving: contraindicated with impaired renal function.","Dosing: dabigatran and apixaban twice daily. Rivaroxaban and edoxaban once daily.","Drawbacks: cost, and more than once-daily dosing."]},{"s":"Bleeding Precautions","a":"bleeding-precautions","k":["Bleeding Precautions","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 7 – Cirrhosis and Med Surg I Week 7 – Cancer.","For a patient at risk of bleeding: a high prothrombin time, as in cirrhosis, where the liver lacks prothrombin, or a really low platelet count (thrombocytopenia).","What Bleeding Precautions Include","Monitor the platelets, PT and PTT. With thrombocytopenia, serious bleeding may not be apparent until the platelet count is under 50,000.","Assess the oral cavity: the gums are one of the first places bleeding shows.","Monitor for ecchymosis, purpura and petechiae.","Protect from falls: a fall with a prolonged bleeding time risks a head injury and a subarachnoid hemorrhage.","No aspirin, an antiplatelet that only increases the bleeding. No alcohol, spicy foods or bulky foods.","No injections: the patient may bleed considerably. Pressure on any bleeding for 5 minutes, and considerably longer than usual after an IM injection.","No vigorous nose blowing or straining with BMs: bearing down raises intrathoracic pressure, which can rupture esophageal varices. Stool softeners.","Soft toothbrush. No rectal temperatures or enemas.","Teach the patient all of these, especially when going home on them.","Thrombocytopenic precautions in critical care are taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-transfusions.html","t":"Transfusions","secs":[{"s":"Transfusions","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 6"]},{"s":"Transfusions and Blood Products","a":"transfusions","k":["Transfusions and Blood Products","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 2 – Blood Components.","The Blood Products","Packed red blood cells (PRBCs) — RBCs only: no plasma, WBCs or platelets. About 250 to 350 mL. — Severe anemia. — ABO and Rh compatibility, by a type and crossmatch. See below for giving PRBCs.","RBCs only: no plasma, WBCs or platelets.","About 250 to 350 mL.","Severe anemia.","ABO and Rh compatibility, by a type and crossmatch.","See below for giving PRBCs.","Platelets — Prepared from fresh whole blood. About 30 to 60 mL. — Bleeding caused by thrombocytopenia. See below for the count. Examples: chemotherapy. — ABO compatibility not a factor. Agitate the bag periodically: platelets clump. As fast…","Prepared from fresh whole blood.","About 30 to 60 mL.","Bleeding caused by thrombocytopenia. See below for the count.","Examples: chemotherapy.","ABO compatibility not a factor.","Agitate the bag periodically: platelets clump.","As fast as tolerated.","Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) — The liquid portion of whole blood, frozen. About 250 mL. Rich in clotting factors, such as prothrombin. No platelets. — Bleeding from a lack of clotting factors. Rarely, a fluid volume expander. Examples: liver i…","The liquid portion of whole blood, frozen. About 250 mL.","Rich in clotting factors, such as prothrombin. No platelets.","Bleeding from a lack of clotting factors.","Rarely, a fluid volume expander.","Examples: liver issues, hemophilia, von Willebrand's.","ABO compatibility required.","Used within 24 hours of thawing.","Albumin — A colloid, prepared from plasma: a plasma volume expander. Pulls fluid into the vascular space and keeps it there. 5%, equal to plasma, or 25%, hypertonic. — Low albumin: fluid shifts out to the third space, and the patient become…","A colloid, prepared from plasma: a plasma volume expander.","Pulls fluid into the vascular space and keeps it there.","5%, equal to plasma, or 25%, hypertonic.","Low albumin: fluid shifts out to the third space, and the patient becomes very edematous.","25% for a patient in a really bad way.","Examples: a liver patient.","Compatibility not a factor.","When to give platelets, by the count and active bleeding, is taught in Hematologic Issues in Critical Care","Platelets are not clotting factors. Both enhance clotting, but not in the same way. Thrombocytopenia: give platelets. A lack of clotting factors: give FFP, the plasma.","Blood Type and Compatibility","O− — O−","O+ — O−, O+","A− — O−, A−","A+ — O−, O+, A−, A+","B− — O−, B−","B+ — O−, O+, B−, B+","AB− — O−, A−, B−, AB−","AB+ — Every type","What Makes Blood Compatible","Two sets of antigens: ABO (A, B, both or neither) and Rh (present or absent).","A recipient can receive only antigens they already have. One they lack causes a hemolytic reaction.","O negative: universal donor. No A, B or Rh antigen, so it can go to anyone.","AB positive: universal recipient. Every antigen already, so it can receive any blood.","When PRBCs Are Given","Know the H&H before hanging PRBCs. The guidelines, for a hemodynamically stable patient without active bleeding:","Under 6 g/dL — Recommended, except in exceptional circumstances.","6 to 7 g/dL — Generally likely. Is the patient symptomatic: BP low, HR high from compensating?","7 to 8 g/dL — May be appropriate, after evaluating the clinical status: orthopedic or cardiac surgery, stable cardiovascular disease.","8 to 10 g/dL — Generally not. Considered for some: symptomatic anemia, ongoing bleeding, acute coronary syndrome with ischemia, hematology and oncology patients with severe thrombocytopenia at risk of bleeding.","Over 10 g/dL — Generally not, except in exceptional circumstances.","Giving PRBCs","Type and crossmatch within the last 72 hours. Premedicate as ordered: acetaminophen for a fever risk, diphenhydramine for an itching risk.","IV 20 gauge or larger, 18 ideal: a smaller gauge can break down the RBCs. Y tubing with a filter, primed with NS. NS only, never dextrose or LR: they can hemolyze the cells, and LR's calcium can cause clots.","Start it, or take it back to the blood bank. Never left on the floor or in the medication refrigerator. The final identity check is done at the bedside, by two RNs.","Teach the patient to report anything unusual at once: chills, SOB, itching, back pain. Cold at the IV site is expected. Baseline vital signs, by the RN.","Start slowly: 1 to 2 mL/min (60 to 120 mL/hr). The RN stays with the patient for the first 15 to 30 minutes, per protocol.","Vital signs. No reaction: increase the rate, to infuse in about 2 hours. With a risk for fluid volume excess, such as an older patient with heart failure or kidney failure, stretch it toward the 4 hours.","Vital signs.","The longest blood can ever hang: after that, bacterial proliferation. Not in by then: take it down, document, and let the provider know. Never rush the rest in.","NS through the tubing, for every last drop. Know the orders: furosemide between units with a risk for fluid volume excess, another unit, a repeat H&H."]},{"s":"Transfusion Reactions","a":"transfusion-reactions","k":["Transfusion Reactions","When a Reaction Is Suspected","Stop the transfusion, always first, to assess what is going on.","New tubing primed with NS, at the same IV site. Not the saline on the blood tubing, which is still full of blood.","Assess the patient. Notify the provider and the blood bank.","Recheck the ID tags and numbers.","Monitor vital signs and urine output: a hemolytic reaction can shut the kidneys down.","Treat the symptoms, per the provider's orders.","Save the blood bag and tubing for the blood bank, which rechecks everything.","Complete the transfusion reaction report form. Collect specimens to evaluate for hemolysis.","Acute hemolytic (AHTR) — Incompatible blood: the wrong blood. The most life-threatening. — Immediate onset. Facial flushing, fever with or without chills, headache. Low back pain, from the kidneys in acute renal failure. Also abdominal, che…","Immediate onset.","Facial flushing, fever with or without chills, headache.","Low back pain, from the kidneys in acute renal failure. Also abdominal, chest, flank pain.","Blood in the urine (hemoglobinuria).","Dyspnea, tachypnea.","Hypotension. Cardiac arrest, death.","Treat the shock: keep the BP up with IV colloids, such as albumin.","Blood samples from the site.","First voided urine. A Foley to monitor urine output.","Send the unit, tubing and filter to the lab.","Febrile non-hemolytic (FNHTR) — Fever develops during the infusion, not from hemolysis. — Sudden chills and fever, headache, flushing, anxiety, vomiting, muscle pain. Not immediate: the temperature may rise a degree over the hour. No low ba…","Sudden chills and fever, headache, flushing, anxiety, vomiting, muscle pain.","Not immediate: the temperature may rise a degree over the hour.","No low back pain, no blood in the urine, no severe hemodynamic compromise. A sharp drop in BP points to a hemolytic reaction.","Antipyretic: acetaminophen.","Restart only if the provider orders it.","Mild allergic — The most common. Sensitization to donor WBCs, platelets or plasma proteins. — Flushing, itching, urticaria (hives). — Antihistamine (diphenhydramine) or corticosteroid, as ordered. Mild and transient: may restart slowly, wit…","Flushing, itching, urticaria (hives).","Antihistamine (diphenhydramine) or corticosteroid, as ordered.","Mild and transient: may restart slowly, with the provider's order.","Anaphylactic and severe allergic — Sensitization to donor plasma proteins. — Anxiety, urticaria, dyspnea and wheezing. Progressing to cyanosis, bronchospasm, hypotension, shock and cardiac arrest. — Treated as a code: CPR, oxygen, epinephri…","Anxiety, urticaria, dyspnea and wheezing.","Progressing to cyanosis, bronchospasm, hypotension, shock and cardiac arrest.","Treated as a code: CPR, oxygen, epinephrine.","Also antihistamines, corticosteroids, β2 agonists.","Bacterial or sepsis — Bacteria in the blood, as when it hangs too long. — Rapid onset of chills and high fever. Vomiting and diarrhea. Marked hypotension. — Blood culture. Return the blood bag to the lab. Treat the septicemia: antibiotics, …","Rapid onset of chills and high fever.","Vomiting and diarrhea.","Marked hypotension.","Blood culture. Return the blood bag to the lab.","Treat the septicemia: antibiotics, IV fluids, vasopressors.","Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) — Too much blood volume: the patient cannot handle the excess fluid. — Cough and dyspnea. Pulmonary congestion: crackles at the bases. Headache, increased vital signs, distended neck veins.…","Cough and dyspnea.","Pulmonary congestion: crackles at the bases.","Headache, increased vital signs, distended neck veins.","Slow or stop the infusion.","Diuretics (furosemide), oxygen, morphine: they ease the workload on the heart.","Stat chest x-ray.","Only TACO may be slowed in place of stopped. Only a febrile or a mild allergic reaction may restart, and only on the provider's order."]}]},{"f":"review-cardiovascular.html","t":"Cardiovascular","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 7"]},{"s":"Anatomy and Physiology of the Heart","a":"heart-anatomy","k":["Anatomy and Physiology of the Heart","Chambers — Two atria on top receive the blood. Two ventricles below pump it out. The right side pumps to the lungs, the left side to the body. The left ventricle has the thickest wall.","Two atria on top receive the blood. Two ventricles below pump it out.","The right side pumps to the lungs, the left side to the body. The left ventricle has the thickest wall.","Blood flow — Right side, to the lungs: the superior and inferior vena cava, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonic valve, pulmonary artery. Left side, to the body: the pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventr…","Right side, to the lungs: the superior and inferior vena cava, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonic valve, pulmonary artery.","Left side, to the body: the pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta.","Heart sounds — S1: the tricuspid and mitral valves close, as the ventricles contract. S2: the pulmonic and aortic valves close, as the ventricles relax.","S1: the tricuspid and mitral valves close, as the ventricles contract.","S2: the pulmonic and aortic valves close, as the ventricles relax.","Conduction system — The SA node, the heart's pacemaker, fires: the atria contract. The AV node holds the impulse briefly, then passes it down the bundle of His, the right and left bundle branches and the Purkinje fibers: the ventricles cont…","The SA node, the heart's pacemaker, fires: the atria contract.","The AV node holds the impulse briefly, then passes it down the bundle of His, the right and left bundle branches and the Purkinje fibers: the ventricles contract.","Systole: the ventricles contract and eject the blood. Diastole: they relax and refill.","What the valves do, and valve disease, are taught in the Heart Disease review","How the conduction shows on the EKG is taught in the Dysrhythmias review"]},{"s":"Normal Function of the Heart","a":"heart-function","k":["Normal Function of the Heart","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 2 – Cardiovascular.","Perfusion is the heart's job: the passage of fluid through the circulatory or lymphatic system to an organ or tissue. It gets oxygen to the cells, where they do their work. Without perfusion, tissues die.","Cardiac output (CO) — The volume of blood pumped by the heart in one minute. CO = stroke volume × heart rate. Altered by anything that alters the heart rate, the stroke volume or the contractility. Examples: medications, disease processes, …","The volume of blood pumped by the heart in one minute.","CO = stroke volume × heart rate.","Altered by anything that alters the heart rate, the stroke volume or the contractility.","Examples: medications, disease processes, activity.","Stroke volume — The blood ejected from the left ventricle each time it pumps, in mL.","The blood ejected from the left ventricle each time it pumps, in mL.","Myocardial contractility — How hard the heart muscle contracts, regardless of the stretch.","How hard the heart muscle contracts, regardless of the stretch.","Blood pressure — The force the blood exerts against the vessel walls. Must be adequate to maintain tissue perfusion during activity and rest. Normal: 120/80, always read against the patient's baseline. 90/60 can be normal for a patient with…","The force the blood exerts against the vessel walls. Must be adequate to maintain tissue perfusion during activity and rest.","Normal: 120/80, always read against the patient's baseline. 90/60 can be normal for a patient with no symptoms.","Preload and afterload, with contractility the three pillars of stroke volume, are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review","Low Cardiac Output","What Low Cardiac Output Looks Like","Blood pressure and heart rate: the indirect measures of cardiac output at the bedside, why vital signs are vital. Measured directly by invasive procedures, in the ICU.","A rising heart rate: when the stroke volume falls, as with a low blood volume, the heart rate goes up to maintain cardiac output. A compensatory mechanism.","Poor perfusion to the brain: dizziness, passing out, confusion.","Poor perfusion to the periphery: pale toes, decreased pulses, sluggish cap refill.","A low pulse pressure: a failing heart without a strong systolic ejection, or hypovolemia.","Measuring cardiac output in the ICU, with its normal values, is taught in Hemodynamics","Ejection Fraction","The percentage of the blood in the left ventricle ejected with each contraction: how effective the pump is. The left ventricle is the powerhouse chamber. Measured by an echo, below.","Reduced","40% or less: heart failure. A weakened heart muscle, as in cardiomyopathy, cannot eject as much as it should. Symptoms may show even at rest. Very low: may need a heart transplant.","Borderline","41 to 49%. Symptoms may show during activity.","Normal","55 to 70%: a little over half the blood with every contraction. Usually comfortable during activity."]},{"s":"Hypertension, Hyperlipidemia and Atherosclerosis","a":"htn-hyperlipidemia-atherosclerosis","k":["Hypertension, Hyperlipidemia and Atherosclerosis","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 2 – Cardiovascular and Med Surg II Week 1 – Coronary Artery Disease and Acute Coronary Syndrome.","Hypertension — High blood pressure: the pressure in the arteries is higher than it should be. Broken into stages. See below. Often asymptomatic: a silent disease, \"the silent killer\". It goes unnoticed until complications occur. — Changes t…","High blood pressure: the pressure in the arteries is higher than it should be.","Broken into stages. See below.","Often asymptomatic: a silent disease, \"the silent killer\". It goes unnoticed until complications occur.","Changes the arterial walls, which increases the peripheral vascular resistance (PVR). Over a long time it wears the heart out: heart failure.","Left untreated, decreases perfusion. Complications: cardiovascular disease, MI, heart failure, stroke, peripheral vascular disease, renal disease, retinal disease and vision loss.","Hyperlipidemia — Too many lipids in the blood: cholesterol and triglycerides. Both are made in the body and taken in with food. The liver makes cholesterol. Targets: total cholesterol under 200 mg/dL. LDL, the bad, under 130 mg/dL. HDL, the…","Too many lipids in the blood: cholesterol and triglycerides. Both are made in the body and taken in with food. The liver makes cholesterol.","Targets: total cholesterol under 200 mg/dL. LDL, the bad, under 130 mg/dL. HDL, the good, over 45 mg/dL for men and over 55 mg/dL for women.","Forms hard deposits inside the arteries, building up on the arterial walls: atherosclerosis.","Atherosclerosis — Hardening of the arteries: the walls narrow and become less elastic. — Perfusion cannot get past it. In the heart: coronary artery disease (CAD), a risk for an MI. Atherosclerosis is responsible for almost all CAD. In the …","Hardening of the arteries: the walls narrow and become less elastic.","Perfusion cannot get past it.","In the heart: coronary artery disease (CAD), a risk for an MI. Atherosclerosis is responsible for almost all CAD.","In the periphery: peripheral arterial disease (PAD), sometimes called PVD.","The Stages of Hypertension","Normal — Under 120 — and under 80","Elevated — 120 to 129 — and under 80","Stage 1 hypertension — 130 to 139 — or 80 to 89","Stage 2 hypertension — 140 or higher — or 90 or higher","Hypertensive crisis — Higher than 180 — and/or higher than 120","Hypertensive crisis: notify the provider immediately, or the patient goes to the emergency room."]},{"s":"Cardiovascular Diagnostics","a":"cv-diagnostics","k":["Cardiovascular Diagnostics","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Cardiovascular Labs and Diagnostics and Med Surg II Week 1 – Coronary Artery Disease and Acute Coronary Syndrome.","Echo Versus TEE","Echocardiogram (Echo)","How: sound waves create a moving picture of the heart.","Shows: the anterior heart. The valves and the chambers, in diastole and systole. Measures the ejection fraction, very important in heart failure.","Noninvasive: no radiation, no prep, painless. Done at the bedside or as an outpatient.","Transesophageal Echo (TEE)","How: a probe with an ultrasound transducer goes down the throat into the esophagus, its end near the heart.","Shows: the posterior heart, with improved pictures and more information than a plain echo, depending on the patient's needs.","Invasive: done in the vascular lab. An IV, local anesthetic or conscious sedation. NPO for solid food 6 hours before, so the patient does not vomit. Dentures removed.","Contrast dye may be used: check the BUN and creatinine, and force fluids after. The dye can be nephrotoxic.","After: NPO until the gag reflex returns. A temporary sore throat is common.","The Labs","Troponin — The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Heart cells die in an MI, and the troponin rises. Elevated: think MI. More sensitive and specific than CK-MB, which shows any muscle damage, as after a motor vehicle crash. Tells…","The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Heart cells die in an MI, and the troponin rises. Elevated: think MI.","More sensitive and specific than CK-MB, which shows any muscle damage, as after a motor vehicle crash.","Tells cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain, such as GI.","Rises 2 to 3 hours after an MI. Back to baseline in 10 to 14 days.","Drawn serially: at first, then 3 and 6 hours later, then occasionally daily for 3 to 5 days, per the provider.","Not raised by unstable angina: how an MI is told from it.","BMP — Basic metabolic panel: the routine blood chemistry panel. Includes: sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate (CO2), BUN, creatinine, glucose and calcium. Not magnesium, which is ordered on its own.","Basic metabolic panel: the routine blood chemistry panel.","Includes: sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate (CO2), BUN, creatinine, glucose and calcium. Not magnesium, which is ordered on its own.","Electrolytes — Potassium, calcium and magnesium, in particular. Imbalances are often the root cause of dysrhythmias: watched closely in any patient with a cardiac issue or a risk for dysrhythmias. Potassium: too high or too low brings EKG c…","Potassium, calcium and magnesium, in particular.","Imbalances are often the root cause of dysrhythmias: watched closely in any patient with a cardiac issue or a risk for dysrhythmias.","Potassium: too high or too low brings EKG changes and ventricular dysrhythmias, which can be life-threatening. Telemetry until it is back in range.","Calcium: affects the heart's action potential, so an imbalance can change the EKG.","Magnesium: stabilizes cardiac muscle cells. Too little: a faster pulse, a higher BP, dysrhythmias, torsades above all. Too much: IV calcium gluconate opposes its effect on the heart.","Electrolytes as the cause of a rhythm are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review"]},{"s":"Post-Cardiac Cath Care","a":"post-cath-care","k":["Post-Cardiac Cath Care","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Cardiovascular Labs and Diagnostics.","Caring for the Access Site","The site: an artery, in the groin (femoral), the radial or the brachial. Arteries are high-pressure vessels.","Watch for bleeding: a heparin drip is used during the procedure, to decrease the risk of clots.","Before the sheath is pulled: the heparin is stopped and the clotting time closely monitored.","Pressure held once the sheath is out, usually for about 20 minutes, far longer than for an IV.","Femoral site: bed rest for at least 6 to 8 hours, with the affected leg straight and the HOB below 30 degrees, so the vessel does not bleed.","Inspect the puncture site closely for bleeding and a hematoma.","Perfusion past the site: pulses, color and sensation. Assess the circulation of the extremity used, distal to where the vessel was entered, frequently and per agency protocol. For a radial site, that is the hand.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-heart-disease.html","t":"Heart Disease","secs":[{"s":"Heart Disease","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 8"]},{"s":"Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)","a":"cad","k":["Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Coronary Artery Disease and Acute Coronary Syndrome.","What Is Going On in CAD","One disease, three names: coronary artery disease, coronary heart disease, ischemic heart disease.","The coronary arteries perfuse the heart: they get oxygen to the heart muscle. A blocked artery is a problem for the muscle it feeds.","Why it matters: the left main and the left anterior descending (LAD) perfuse the left ventricle, the powerhouse chamber. A complete blockage there is the \"widow maker\": often fatal before the patient reaches the hospital.","Atherosclerosis, responsible for almost all CAD, is taught in the Cardiovascular review","How atherosclerosis leads to an MI is taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","Risk Factors","Non-Modifiable","Age: the risk increases significantly with each decade of life.","Gender: men are at greater risk.","Family history: especially of premature disease, in younger people.","Modifiable","The four most firmly established: elevated serum lipids, hypertension, tobacco use, physical inactivity.","Hypertension: untreated, the pressure in the arteries damages them over time, and they harden. Often silent.","Tobacco: the risk is proportional to the number of cigarettes smoked.","Also: obesity, higher for the \"apple\" shape than the \"pear\". Diabetes mellitus, which needs tight glucose control. Depression and stress.","From Stable Angina to a STEMI","Stable Angina","Chronic ischemic heart disease. Stable for years and predictable: the patient lives with it.","Unstable Angina","ACS. An incomplete occlusion, with no heart damage. An emergency: it precedes an MI.","NSTEMI","ACS: an MI. An incomplete occlusion, but heart cells die. The smaller infarct, with better outcomes.","STEMI","ACS: an MI. The larger, more extensive infarct, with poor outcomes.","Stable versus unstable angina is taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","Time is of the essence. Heart cells stand about 20 minutes of ischemia before they start to die, and the full thickness of the muscle dies over 4 to 6 hours. Waiting costs heart muscle: activate EMS.","Recognizing an MI","Anginal Pain","Brought on by: exertion or stress.","Relieved by: rest or sublingual nitroglycerin.","Lasts: less than 15 minutes.","MI Pain","Brought on by: nothing. Often in the early morning.","Relieved by: opioids only.","Lasts: 20 minutes or more.","The hallmark: severe, immobilizing chest pain not relieved by rest, a change of position or nitrates.","Feels like: heaviness, pressure, tightness, burning, constriction, crushing. Ask about pressure, not only pain.","With: nausea and vomiting, diaphoresis, dyspnea, anxiety or fear, dysrhythmias.","Atypical Presentations","Who: women, older patients, patients with diabetes.","Women's anginal signs: fatigue, the most prominent. Shortness of air, indigestion, anxiety.","A silent MI: women and patients with diabetes may have no symptoms at all.","Telling Unstable Angina, NSTEMI and STEMI Apart","Unstable angina — No changes, or ST depression or deep T wave inversions. — Not elevated: no heart damage.","No changes, or ST depression or deep T wave inversions.","Not elevated: no heart damage.","NSTEMI — ST depression or normal. T wave inverted. QRS normal. — Elevated: heart damage.","ST depression or normal. T wave inverted. QRS normal.","Elevated: heart damage.","STEMI — ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads. T wave peaked, then inverted. QRS usually wide, developing over hours. — Elevated.","ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads.","T wave peaked, then inverted. QRS usually wide, developing over hours.","Elevated.","Troponin, when it rises and how it is drawn, is taught in the Cardiovascular review","STEMI and NSTEMI Care","First Care for Suspected ACS","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes of arrival. Repeated every 10 to 15 minutes while it is not diagnostic and suspicion remains: an EKG is a snapshot in time.","Continuous telemetry: for dysrhythmias, the most common complication of an MI, and ST changes. It monitors but cannot diagnose a STEMI. ST elevation on the monitor: get a 12-lead.","Upright, with oxygen by nasal cannula, to support ventilation.","Aspirin 325 mg, chewed and swallowed, for its antiplatelet effect.","Nitroglycerin 0.4 mg SL: one at a time, 5 minutes apart, up to 3, for persistent chest pain. Blood pressure first: it drops. Too low: morphine instead.","Morphine IV: for unacceptable, persistent discomfort. Decreases preload and afterload, easing the heart's workload, and helps the anxiety. Often better tolerated than nitroglycerin.","Baseline labs: cardiac markers such as troponin, electrolytes, H&H, possibly coags.","Heart and lung sounds: a new murmur, gallop or rub. Crackles may mean left-sided heart failure.","The drugs for an MI, MONA-TAS, are taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","Reperfusion: Three Strategies","Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) — STEMI or NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI. — Open within 90 minutes of ED arrival. — A cardiac cath first: locates the blockages and their severity, looks for collateral circu…","STEMI or NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI.","Open within 90 minutes of ED arrival.","A cardiac cath first: locates the blockages and their severity, looks for collateral circulation, evaluates the left ventricle.","Local anesthesia, walking soon after, 1 to 3 days in hospital. More are done than CABGs.","Thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) therapy — STEMI. — Start within 30 minutes of ED arrival. Most effective within 6 hours of the event. — Clot busters: dissolve the thrombus and restore blood flow. May be given during the cath. Monitor for bleedi…","STEMI.","Start within 30 minutes of ED arrival.","Most effective within 6 hours of the event.","Clot busters: dissolve the thrombus and restore blood flow. May be given during the cath.","Monitor for bleeding after.","Examples: tPA, reteplase.","Coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) — Diabetes and/or 3-vessel disease, or a blockage the catheter cannot reach. — Elective or emergency. — Open heart surgery: bypasses the blockage.","Diabetes and/or 3-vessel disease, or a blockage the catheter cannot reach.","Elective or emergency.","Open heart surgery: bypasses the blockage.","No thrombolytics with a history of intracranial hemorrhage, a recent stroke or abdominal surgery, any active bleeding (menses excepted), or abnormal coagulation studies.","Cardiac cath and CABG, what each is and when each is chosen, are taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","After a PCI","Watch for ischemia: chest pain, EKG changes, dysrhythmias, hemodynamic instability.","Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin with another antiplatelet, such as clopidogrel. IV heparin may be given during the procedure. A delicate balance: thin, but not so thin the patient bleeds out.","The sheath comes out 4 to 6 hours after the procedure.","Internal bleeding: the heart rate creeps up, compensating for the lost volume.","The access site and the perfusion past it, the same after a PCI as after a cath, are taught in the Cardiovascular review"]},{"s":"Heart Failure","a":"heart-failure","k":["Heart Failure","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Heart Failure.","Heart failure: inadequate pumping or filling of the heart.","Preload — Increased blood volume increases preload.","Afterload — Elevated, as by untreated hypertension: harder for the blood to get out of the heart.","Myocardial contractility — A damaged myocardium, as after an MI, cannot contract, whatever the fluid volume.","Heart rate — Too fast or too slow, for a long period.","Preload, afterload and contractility are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review","The ejection fraction, reduced in heart failure, is taught in the Cardiovascular review","Left-Sided Versus Right-Sided","Left-Sided Heart Failure","Blood backs up into: the left atrium and the pulmonary veins.","Cues: pulmonary. Pulmonary congestion and edema: crackles, rhonchi, wheezes, cough, tachypnea. Blood-tinged sputum, from damage at the alveoli.","Also: dyspnea on exertion or at rest, orthopnea, the tripod position. A raised heart rate. Restlessness, confusion, fatigue, cyanosis.","Advanced: paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND).","Right-Sided Heart Failure","Blood backs up into: the right atrium and the venous circulation, everywhere but the lungs.","Cues: the body. JVD first: the jugular vein sits just outside the right atrium. Never an expected finding.","Also: hepatomegaly, the liver tender in the RUQ, and an engorged spleen. Ascites. GI congestion: nausea, no appetite. Peripheral edema, settling with gravity in the ankles, and in the hands. Scrotal edema, which can be very painful. Fatigue…","Not cor pulmonale: right-sided failure that results from left-sided failure.","A closed circuit: left-sided failure eventually brings right-sided failure, and right brings left. Often both are present.","Chronic Versus Acute Decompensated","Chronic Heart Failure","Diagnosed: as an outpatient.","Course: periods of acutely or slowly worsening cardiac function. Can be insidious: the patient tells the doctor about fatigue.","Cause: often the damage from other cardiac events or disease, such as CAD or hypertension, whether or not it showed as an acute event.","Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF)","Diagnosed: as an inpatient.","Course: chronic heart failure's signs and symptoms worsen and need urgent therapy. Life-threatening: acute interventions. Acute: no time to compensate.","Presents with: shortness of air, from excess fluid and cardiac overload. Maybe 4+ edema and ascites, but not being able to breathe is what brings the patient in.","What heart failure looks like in the ICU is taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues"]},{"s":"Valve Disease","a":"valve-disease","k":["Valve Disease","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Valvular Problems.","The valves keep the blood moving one way: each lets the blood leave one chamber for the next, and slams shut when the next chamber contracts. The mitral and aortic valves, on the left, high-pressure side, take more of a beating: most repair…","Stenosis — Constriction or narrowing: \"the door won't open all the way\". Blood backs up. — Rheumatic heart disease.","Constriction or narrowing: \"the door won't open all the way\". Blood backs up.","Rheumatic heart disease.","Regurgitation — Incompetence or insufficiency: \"the door won't close all the way\". Blood backs up into the previous chamber. — Aging, infective endocarditis.","Incompetence or insufficiency: \"the door won't close all the way\". Blood backs up into the previous chamber.","Aging, infective endocarditis.","Prolapse — The leaflets \"buckle\". Often in younger people, and can improve with age. — Unknown.","The leaflets \"buckle\". Often in younger people, and can improve with age.","Unknown.","The two most common valve problems are taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","What Valve Disease Looks Like","Heart failure: the blood backs up. From the left side into the lungs, from the right into the periphery. Depends on the valve and the severity.","Classic: exertional dyspnea, weakness, fatigue, chest pain, a murmur, pulmonary or peripheral edema.","Treatment","Treating Valve Disease","Without surgery first: antibiotic prophylaxis, to prevent rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease and infective endocarditis. Sodium restriction. Drugs for the heart failure. Anticoagulants and antidysrhythmics.","Repair before replacement: commissurotomy, valvuloplasty (a balloon), annuloplasty (rings). Some are percutaneous, as is an aortic valve replacement.","Mechanical Valve","Lasts: more than 20 years. More durable.","Anticoagulation: lifelong. The body sees the mechanical parts as foreign, and clots form.","Sound: a click, sometimes heard at the bedside without a stethoscope.","Chosen for: a patient who will adhere to lifetime anticoagulation.","Biologic Valve","Lasts: 8 to 10 years. Less durable. Usually porcine: a pig valve.","Anticoagulation: none needed.","Sound: no click.","Chosen for: an older patient with a shorter life expectancy, or one unwilling or unable to adhere to anticoagulation.","After a Valve Replacement","Recovery: very similar to a CABG. Sometimes done with a CABG, in the same surgery.","A prosthetic valve: a medic alert bracelet or card. A metal valve: no MRI.","Post-op care after cardiac surgery is taught in Cardiovascular Critical Care Issues","Warfarin for a valve, the INR and its antidote, is taught in the Hematology review"]},{"s":"Pharmacology for Heart Failure, CAD and Hypertension","a":"cv-pharmacology","k":["Pharmacology for Heart Failure, CAD and Hypertension","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 1 – Coronary Artery Disease and Acute Coronary Syndrome, Med Surg II Week 2 – Heart Failure and Fundamentals II Week 2 – Cardiovascular.","Beta blockers — Slow the heart rate: less myocardial oxygen demand. Block adrenaline's effect on the heart: weaker contractions and a lower BP. — Heart failure: carvedilol, the prototype. CAD: relieve angina. The only class proven to preven…","Slow the heart rate: less myocardial oxygen demand.","Block adrenaline's effect on the heart: weaker contractions and a lower BP.","Heart failure: carvedilol, the prototype.","CAD: relieve angina. The only class proven to prevent re-infarction and improve survival after an MI.","Hypertension: with calcium channel blockers, the two most prevalent antihypertensives.","Examples: metoprolol, atenolol, propranolol, nadolol: the \"-olol\" drugs.","Bradycardia, hypotension, heart failure, AV block, bronchospasm, impotence.","Calcium channel blockers — Block calcium from entering heart and artery muscle cells: the arteries relax and widen, lowering the BP and the heart's workload. Verapamil and diltiazem also slow the heart rate. — CAD: relieve angina, usually g…","Block calcium from entering heart and artery muscle cells: the arteries relax and widen, lowering the BP and the heart's workload.","Verapamil and diltiazem also slow the heart rate.","CAD: relieve angina, usually given with a beta blocker.","Hypertension: with beta blockers, the two most prevalent antihypertensives.","Examples: amlodipine, verapamil, nifedipine, diltiazem.","Hypotension, peripheral edema, flushing, headache, constipation.","Digoxin — A negative chronotrope: slows the heart rate. A positive inotrope: increases contractility. Together, they enhance cardiac output. — Heart failure: a cardiac glycoside. Second line, for its risk of dysrhythmias, but still a classi…","A negative chronotrope: slows the heart rate.","A positive inotrope: increases contractility.","Together, they enhance cardiac output.","Heart failure: a cardiac glycoside. Second line, for its risk of dysrhythmias, but still a classic drug.","Digitoxicity: a very narrow therapeutic index, so it comes easily.","Hypokalemia makes it worse: monitor the potassium, especially with a potassium-losing diuretic.","Signs: bradycardia and serious dysrhythmias, headache, dizziness, confusion, nausea and vomiting, blurry or yellow vision, halos around lights.","Apical pulse for a full minute before giving it. Hold it below 60. Monitor the cardiac rhythm. Teach the patient to take their own pulse and record it daily.","Antidote: digoxin immune Fab, IV.","Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers: nobody stops them without seeing the provider first.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-dysrhythmias.html","t":"Dysrhythmias","secs":[{"s":"Dysrhythmias","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 9"]},{"s":"Reading a Rhythm Strip","a":"reading-a-strip","k":["Reading a Rhythm Strip","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Dysrhythmias.","P wave — Atrial depolarization: the atrial kick. A little bump.","PR interval — From the start of the P to the start of the QRS: the pause while the impulse goes through the AV node. 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. Longer: something is wrong with conduction between the atria and the ventricles.","QRS complex — Ventricular depolarization: a big spike, where cardiac output happens. Atrial repolarization is buried in it. 0.10 seconds or less, 0.12 in some sources: skinny. Wide is not normal.","ST segment — Back on the baseline (the isoelectric line). Elevated: may indicate an MI.","T wave — Ventricular repolarization. Peaked: may be a potassium imbalance.","QT interval — Under 0.38 seconds.","R to R interval — The tip of one R to the tip of the next. Equal intervals that \"march out\" make a regular rhythm.","ST elevation and troponin, telling a STEMI from an NSTEMI, are taught in the Heart Disease review","Measuring a Six-Second Strip","One small box: 0.04 seconds. Five small boxes, one big box: 0.20 seconds.","Six seconds: every strip on a test. The marks at the top are 3 seconds apart.","Heart rate: the QRSs in the six seconds, times 10.","Regular or not: march out the R to R intervals.","Two Ways to Sort Dysrhythmias","By Rate: Early, Slow, Fast","Premature complexes: early beats.","Bradydysrhythmias: a heart rate below 60.","Tachydysrhythmias: a heart rate above 100.","Why 60 and 100: the SA node fires 60 to 100 times a minute on its own.","By Site of Origin","Sinus: from the SA node.","Atrial: from the atria.","Ventricular: from the ventricles."]},{"s":"Rhythms and Cardiac Output","a":"cardiac-output-by-rhythm","k":["Rhythms and Cardiac Output","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Dysrhythmias.","Cardiac output, and what low cardiac output means, is taught in the Cardiovascular review","Bradydysrhythmias: Below 60","Myocardial oxygen demand: reduced, a good thing. Beta blockers slow the heart rate on purpose for it.","Coronary perfusion: the coronary arteries perfuse in diastole. A slow rate prolongs diastole: more perfusion time. Too slow: perfusion falls, and angina may start.","Cardiac output: may be tolerated if the BP is adequate. An athlete resting at 54 with a good BP goes about the day. Another patient at 54 is symptomatic.","Tachydysrhythmias: Above 100","Myocardial oxygen demand: raised by the extra workload, a bad thing. Worse in a compromised heart.","Coronary perfusion: a shorter diastole shortens perfusion time, and angina may start.","Cardiac output: may rise at first, with the BP: compensation. Faster or sustained, the ventricles cannot fill: stroke volume falls, and with it cardiac output and BP. 104 is usually tolerable. 120 or 130 cannot be sustained.","Either, if not tolerated, can lead to: myocardial ischemia or infarct, dysrhythmias, hypotension, heart failure.","Blood pressure is the indirect measure of cardiac output. A rate outside 60 to 100: go check on the patient, and take a set of vital signs.","Key Features of a Sustained Tachy- or Bradydysrhythmia","What Too Slow or Too Fast Looks Like","Heart: angina, from a lack of coronary perfusion. An S3 or S4, a gallop. Palpitations, with a tachycardia. Hypotension.","Brain, poorly perfused: restlessness, anxiety, confusion. Dizziness, syncope.","Lungs: shortness of air, tachypnea. Pulmonary crackles, a sign of left-sided heart failure. Orthopnea.","Periphery: a pulse deficit. JVD, a sign of right-sided heart failure. Pale, cool skin, diaphoresis, delayed cap refill. Weakness, fatigue.","Kidneys, poorly perfused: decreased urine output. Watch it closely.","GI: nausea and vomiting.","The Atria Versus the Ventricles","Atrial Dysrhythmias: the Ventricular Rate Matters","The atrial kick is lost in AFib, and inconsistent in flutter.","The ventricular rate is what matters: the left ventricle is the powerhouse chamber. The AV node, the gatekeeper, lets only some atrial impulses through.","Controlled: AFib with a ventricular rate of 90 is not ideal, but livable.","Too fast: no time for the ventricles to fill. Stroke volume and cardiac output fall.","PVCs","Typically do not perfuse. With bigeminy, only every other beat perfuses.","VTach","Seriously decreases cardiac output: too fast for the ventricles to fill. A pulse means it is perfusing.","VFib","No cardiac output: the ventricles only quiver. A deadly rhythm.","Untreated, each slips into the next, and the further it goes, the less well treatment works.","You can live with AFib. You cannot live with VFib."]},{"s":"The Eleven Rhythms","a":"the-rhythms","k":["The Eleven Rhythms","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Sinus Dysrhythmias, Med Surg II Week 2 – Atrial Dysrhythmias and Med Surg II Week 2 – Ventricular Dysrhythmias.","Sinus Rhythms","The Two Normal Sinus Rhythms","Normal sinus rhythm (NSR): the SA node fires 60 to 100, with normal conduction. A P before every QRS, all alike, and the R to R regular. Often just called \"sinus\".","Sinus arrhythmia: the same, but the R to R is not quite regular. Changes in intrathoracic pressure with breathing change the SA node's firing. A normal physiological phenomenon, seen in young, healthy people. Not treated: an assessment find…","Sinus bradycardia — The SA node fires below 60. A P before every QRS. — Vagal stimulation: carotid sinus massage (provider only), vomiting or gagging, Valsalva maneuvers, eyeball pressure, parasympathomimetic drugs. Digoxin toxicity. Hyperk…","The SA node fires below 60.","A P before every QRS.","Vagal stimulation: carotid sinus massage (provider only), vomiting or gagging, Valsalva maneuvers, eyeball pressure, parasympathomimetic drugs.","Digoxin toxicity.","Hyperkalemia: slows depolarization.","MI.","Normal in a well-conditioned athlete, and during sleep.","May be asymptomatic, but for the slow rate.","Symptomatic: lightheaded, nauseous, about to pass out, a low BP. The key features above.","Not symptomatic: monitor, and do not treat.","Symptomatic: IV atropine.","Still symptomatic: transcutaneous pacing, right away.","Persistent: a permanent pacemaker.","Sinus tachycardia — The SA node fires above 100, from vagal inhibition. A P before every QRS. — Physical activity, anxiety, pain, stress, fever. Anemia: the heart speeds up to circulate the red cells there are. Hypoxia. Compensating for a l…","The SA node fires above 100, from vagal inhibition.","Physical activity, anxiety, pain, stress, fever.","Anemia: the heart speeds up to circulate the red cells there are.","Hypoxia.","Compensating for a low BP: dehydration or hypovolemia, MI, heart failure. A low stroke volume raises the heart rate.","May be asymptomatic, but for the fast rate.","Not tolerated: palpitations, with the key features above.","Treat the cause: fluid replacement, an analgesic, an antipyretic, an anxiolytic such as a benzodiazepine.","Beta blockers reduce the rate and myocardial oxygen demand. The cause comes first.","Atrial Dysrhythmias","Premature atrialcontraction (PAC) — An ectopic focus in the atria fires before the SA node: one early beat. Equal R to R intervals, one early beat, then equal again. — Usually benign. Electrolytes: check them, especially magnesium, calcium …","An ectopic focus in the atria fires before the SA node: one early beat.","Equal R to R intervals, one early beat, then equal again.","Usually benign.","Electrolytes: check them, especially magnesium, calcium and potassium.","Stress, stimulants such as caffeine, atrial pathology.","Benign most of the time. In a hospital patient, it may be pathologic.","New, or more frequent: may be about to convert to AFib. Time can be of the essence.","Benign: nothing.","New or more frequent: check the electrolytes and tell the provider. The drugs given for AFib may prevent it.","Atrial fibrillation(AFib) — Many ectopic foci fire at once, and the atria quiver. No P waves: tiny, irregular \"f\" waves. Atrial rate above 400, ventricular rate 100 to 175. R to R irregularly irregular. — Underlying heart disease, almost an…","Many ectopic foci fire at once, and the atria quiver. No P waves: tiny, irregular \"f\" waves.","Atrial rate above 400, ventricular rate 100 to 175. R to R irregularly irregular.","Underlying heart disease, almost any.","Electrolyte imbalance, hypoxia.","Cardiac surgery: often postoperatively.","The most common dysrhythmia, more common with age.","Depends on the ventricular rate, how long the rhythm has lasted, and the cardiovascular status.","Onset usually fast: the key features of a tachydysrhythmia.","Clots: blood pools in the quivering atria. An embolus from the left atrium causes a stroke.","Rate control first, then rhythm control, and preventing an embolic stroke. See below.","Atrial flutter — One ectopic focus: recurring, regular sawtooth \"F\" waves, not P waves. Atrial rate may be above 250, the ventricular rate slower. R to R usually regular, sometimes irregular. — Underlying heart condition, any. Rare in a hea…","One ectopic focus: recurring, regular sawtooth \"F\" waves, not P waves.","Atrial rate may be above 250, the ventricular rate slower. R to R usually regular, sometimes irregular.","Underlying heart condition, any. Rare in a healthy heart, and less common than AFib.","Electrolyte imbalance: check them.","As with AFib: depends on the ventricular rate, how long, and the cardiovascular status. Onset usually fast.","At risk for emboli too.","As for AFib. See below.","Paroxysmalsupraventriculartachycardia (PSVT) — A run of repeated premature beats, from a focus anywhere above the bundle of His. Usually starts with a PAC, and stops as abruptly as it began. — In a normal heart: overexertion, emotional stre…","A run of repeated premature beats, from a focus anywhere above the bundle of His.","Usually starts with a PAC, and stops as abruptly as it began.","In a normal heart: overexertion, emotional stress, stimulants. Not terribly uncommon.","More serious: digoxin toxicity, heart disease.","Depends on how long it lasts and how fast the ventricular rate.","Too fast: the key features of a tachydysrhythmia.","Sustained SVT does not stop on its own: it needs treating.","On the monitor: vagal maneuvers, then adenosine, then cardioversion. See below.","Ventricular Dysrhythmias","Premature ventricularcontraction (PVC) — From an ectopic focus in the ventricles: early, with no normal P wave. Wide and distorted beside the normal QRS. Patterns: bigeminy, trigeminy, quadrigeminy. Unifocal or multifocal: multifocal is wor…","From an ectopic focus in the ventricles: early, with no normal P wave. Wide and distorted beside the normal QRS.","Patterns: bigeminy, trigeminy, quadrigeminy. Unifocal or multifocal: multifocal is worse.","Stimulants, electrolytes, hypoxia, fever, exercise, emotional stress, cardiovascular disease.","Isolated: can be benign. Chronic for some patients.","New, or more frequent: may be about to convert to VTach, a crisis. Watch closely, and tell the provider.","Treat the cause.","Drugs: beta blockers, lidocaine or amiodarone.","Ventriculartachycardia (VTach) — 3 or more PVCs in a row: an ectopic focus in the ventricles takes control, and the atria do not contract. Rate 150 to 200, usually regular. No P wave, no measurable PR. — MI, CAD, significant electrolyte abn…","3 or more PVCs in a row: an ectopic focus in the ventricles takes control, and the atria do not contract.","Rate 150 to 200, usually regular. No P wave, no measurable PR.","MI, CAD, significant electrolyte abnormalities, heart failure, drug toxicity.","Symptomatic very quickly, unless it converts back.","Pulse or pulseless: the first question. No pulse, no perfusion.","ACLS, by the pulse. Pulseless: defibrillation.","An antidysrhythmic: a beta blocker, calcium channel blocker or amiodarone.","Electrolytes: checked, and replaced at once.","Ventricularfibrillation (VFib) — Irregular waveforms of varying shapes and sizes. No QRSs. — Untreated VTach slips into VFib. — The ventricles just quiver: no effective contractions. Cardiac arrest, unconscious. — CPR, ACLS, defibrillation.…","Irregular waveforms of varying shapes and sizes.","No QRSs.","Untreated VTach slips into VFib.","The ventricles just quiver: no effective contractions. Cardiac arrest, unconscious.","CPR, ACLS, defibrillation. Call a code."]},{"s":"Treatment","a":"dysrhythmia-treatment","k":["Treatment","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Atrial Dysrhythmias and Med Surg II Week 2 – Ventricular Dysrhythmias.","Always check the electrolytes in a patient with a dysrhythmia. Imbalances cause PACs, AFib, flutter, PVCs and VTach. In VTach, replace them at once.","The electrolytes that matter, potassium, calcium and magnesium, are taught in the Cardiovascular review","AFib and Atrial Flutter","Three Goals of Treatment","Rate control, the ventricular rate: the priority. Beta blockers such as metoprolol, or calcium channel blockers such as diltiazem and verapamil. IV at first.","Rhythm control: amiodarone or dofetilide. IV at first.","Preventing an embolic stroke: warfarin, or another anticoagulant.","Stable, but Symptomatic","Slow the ventricular rate: an IV calcium channel blocker, beta blocker, digoxin or amiodarone.","Often a bolus and a drip: the bolus raises the drug level at once, and the drip keeps it there. A drip alone takes too long.","Unstable","Hemodynamically compromised: synchronized cardioversion. See below.","Emergent cardioversion: low-molecular-weight heparin or a heparin drip is started as well.","Before Cardioverting AFib","AFib for more than 48 hours: clots may have formed in the atria. Cardioversion restores the kick and throws the clot to the brain: a stroke.","Anticoagulation, usually warfarin: 3 to 6 weeks before cardioversion, and 3 to 4 weeks after.","A TEE may be done first, to make sure there are no clots in the atria.","Long-term anticoagulation when treatment fails, warfarin and the newer drugs, is taught in the Hematology review","Drugs often work, but fail often and can be toxic. Two treatments are not drugs:","Catheter Ablation","Invasive: destroys the irritable focus causing the dysrhythmia.","First: electrophysiology (EP) studies and mapping, to locate the focus.","Maze Procedure","Surgical: incisions in the atrium disrupt the re-entrant circuits.","Leaves one path from the SA node to the AV node, like a children's maze.","PSVT","Vagal Maneuvers","First, on the monitor.","Adenosine","If they fail: IV push.","Synchronized Cardioversion","If both fail, or the patient becomes hemodynamically unstable.","Vagal Maneuvers for PSVT","Valsalva, the most effective: bear down as for a bowel movement. Hold the breath 10 to 15 seconds, hard enough to distend the neck veins, then breathe.","Coughing: a ventilated patient can be suctioned to make them cough.","Carotid sinus massage: provider only. Never the nurse.","Diving reflex: the face in cold water. Rarely used now.","Cardioversion Versus Defibrillation","For: a hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachydysrhythmia: AFib, flutter, SVT. Needs an R wave.","Sync on: the shock lands on the R wave of the QRS. Unsynchronized, a shock on the wrong part of the complex can trigger a life-threatening dysrhythmia.","Energy: 50 to 100 joules at first, raised if needed: 50 to 200 in all.","The patient: usually elective, awake, and sedated first unless it is an emergency. A consent form, and the EKG monitor.","Pulseless after: the QRS is lost. Sync off, and defibrillate.","After: a patent airway and oxygen, vital signs and level of consciousness, watch for dysrhythmias, emotional support. Document whether it worked.","Defibrillation","For: VFib and pulseless VTach: no cardiac output. An emergency: call a code.","Sync off: there is no QRS to sense, so with the sync on it would never fire. It fires the moment the button is pushed.","Energy: more than cardioversion. Begins at 200 joules, up to 360.","The patient: unconscious, on the cardiac monitor.","All clear before any shock: no one touching the patient or the bed.","If it's VFib, you defib, and call a code."]},{"s":"The Drugs","a":"dysrhythmia-drugs","k":["The Drugs","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Sinus Dysrhythmias and Med Surg II Week 2 – Atrial Dysrhythmias.","Atropine — Vagolytic: blocks the vagus nerve, the main nerve of the parasympathetic nervous system. Speeds up the heart rate. An anticholinergic: with the vagus nerve's brake off, the SA node fires faster and impulses cross the AV node fast…","Vagolytic: blocks the vagus nerve, the main nerve of the parasympathetic nervous system.","Speeds up the heart rate.","An anticholinergic: with the vagus nerve's brake off, the SA node fires faster and impulses cross the AV node faster.","Symptomatic sinus bradycardia.","1 mg IV every 3 to 5 minutes, 3 mg max. Aim: a rate back in 60 to 100.","Not working: transcutaneous pacing.","Adenosine — Slows the heart rate. Briefly blocks conduction through the AV node, which most PSVT needs to keep going: the rhythm breaks, and the SA node takes back over. — PSVT, when vagal maneuvers fail. — IV push, very fast, then a rapid …","Slows the heart rate.","Briefly blocks conduction through the AV node, which most PSVT needs to keep going: the rhythm breaks, and the SA node takes back over.","PSVT, when vagal maneuvers fail.","IV push, very fast, then a rapid NS flush: a very short half-life. Often two syringes of saline, pushed together.","Onset: 10 to 40 seconds. Lasts 1 to 2 minutes.","A pause on the strip, a period of asystole. The patient may be aware of it: tell them first.","Beta blockers,such as metoprolol — Slow the heart rate, reducing myocardial oxygen demand. Slow conduction through the AV node: fewer of the atria's impulses reach the ventricles. Labetalol also blocks alpha receptors, so the arteries widen…","Slow the heart rate, reducing myocardial oxygen demand.","Slow conduction through the AV node: fewer of the atria's impulses reach the ventricles.","Labetalol also blocks alpha receptors, so the arteries widen too: mostly a blood pressure drug.","AFib and flutter: rate control.","Sinus tachycardia, once the cause is treated.","PVCs, and VTach.","IV at first for AFib and flutter, often a bolus and a drip.","Amiodarone — An antidysrhythmic: rhythm control. Prolongs repolarization: the heart's cells take longer before they can fire again, which suppresses fast rhythms from the atria and the ventricles. — AFib and flutter: rhythm control. Also sl…","An antidysrhythmic: rhythm control.","Prolongs repolarization: the heart's cells take longer before they can fire again, which suppresses fast rhythms from the atria and the ventricles.","AFib and flutter: rhythm control. Also slows the ventricular rate when the patient is stable but symptomatic.","Adenosine and atropine both start with A. Adenosine slows the heart rate. Atropine speeds it up. Do not mix them up.","Beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and digoxin, their examples and what to watch for, are taught in the Heart Disease review"]},{"s":"Pacing","a":"pacing","k":["Pacing","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 2 – Sinus Dysrhythmias.","Transcutaneous Pacing","When: symptomatic sinus bradycardia that atropine does not fix. Right here, right now.","How: pads on the patient, and the device paces the heart through the skin. It stands in for the SA node.","Permanent Pacemaker","When: a persistent bradycardia that responds to nothing else. The provider decides.","How: under the skin, usually just below the clavicle. Fires when the SA node is not cooperating.","Paces as needed: every sixth beat in one patient, every beat in another.","ICDs and Device Restrictions","When an ICD Fires","The patient feels the shock: a sudden, hard thump or kick in the chest.","One shock, and the patient feels well after: call the provider that day.","More than one shock, or chest pain, dizziness or shortness of breath: call 911.","Living With a Pacemaker or ICD","For the first few weeks: keep the arm on the device side below the shoulder, and lift nothing heavy with it, while the leads settle in.","Magnets and cell phones at least 6 inches from the device: no phone in a shirt pocket over it.","Carry the device ID card at all times.","Pacemakers and ICDs, who gets one, and the MRI, machinery and airport restrictions, are taught in Rhythms & Pacemaker Review","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-renal.html","t":"Renal","secs":[{"s":"Renal","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 10"]},{"s":"Oliguria","a":"oliguria","k":["Oliguria","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Acute Kidney Injury.","Oliguria: Decreased Urine Output","Under 30 mL/hr: the magic number, and the one preferred. Waiting to see 24 hours of output may let an AKI worsen significantly.","Under 400 mL in 24 hours: the other loose definition.","Next best action: notify the provider, unless the cause is something the nurse can troubleshoot, such as a kinked Foley.","In AKI: the oliguric phase, first of three: oliguric, diuretic, recovery. Begins about 1 day after a hypotensive event and lasts 1 to 3 weeks.","Then: fluid, potassium and wastes are retained. See why each manifestation happens, below."]},{"s":"BUN and Creatinine","a":"bun-and-creatinine","k":["BUN and Creatinine","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Urinary Labs and Diagnostics.","Creatinine — 0.5 to 1.2 mg/dL — The most reliable indicator of kidney function, of any test. More reliable than the BUN. Specific to the kidneys: the breakdown of muscle and protein metabolism, released at a consistent rate. — Not much else…","The most reliable indicator of kidney function, of any test. More reliable than the BUN.","Specific to the kidneys: the breakdown of muscle and protein metabolism, released at a consistent rate.","Not much else: elevated means the kidneys.","Low (below 0.5): muscle atrophy, as in a malnourished patient with little muscle to break down. Otherwise not significant.","BUN (blood urea nitrogen) — 10 to 20 mg/dL — Not specific to the kidneys. Nitrogen is the end product of protein breakdown. — High-nitrogen tube feedings, or a high-protein diet. A GI bleed: blood is protein-rich, and is digested in the GI …","Not specific to the kidneys.","Nitrogen is the end product of protein breakdown.","High-nitrogen tube feedings, or a high-protein diet.","A GI bleed: blood is protein-rich, and is digested in the GI tract.","Hydration status: very well hydrated, a false low. Dehydrated, a false high.","eGFR (estimated glomerular filtration rate) — 90 mL/min or above — Falls as kidney function is lost: it stages CKD. See below. — A calculation from the serum creatinine, with age, sex, weight and ethnicity. The same creatinine can put two p…","Falls as kidney function is lost: it stages CKD. See below.","A calculation from the serum creatinine, with age, sex, weight and ethnicity.","The same creatinine can put two patients in different stages of CKD.","An elevated BUN: check the creatinine. Both up: probably the kidneys. The BUN alone: look for another reason, such as a GI bleed with a falling H&H.","Azotemia","A raised BUN and creatinine: nitrogenous wastes building up in the blood. A lab finding.","With symptoms, it is called uremia. The two words are often used together."]},{"s":"Function of the Kidneys","a":"kidney-function","k":["Function of the Kidneys","What the Kidneys Do","Filter the blood: in each nephron, the kidney's working unit, the glomerulus filters the blood and the tubules take back what the body needs.","Excrete wastes and drugs: urea, creatinine and other wastes leave in the urine, with many drugs.","Balance fluid and electrolytes: keep or excrete water, sodium, potassium, calcium, phosphorus and magnesium, as the body needs.","Regulate the pH and the blood pressure, make erythropoietin, activate vitamin D: see below."]},{"s":"The Kidneys in Acid-Base Balance","a":"kidneys-acid-base","k":["The Kidneys in Acid-Base Balance","For further study, see Pathopharm II Week 2 – Acid-Base Imbalance.","What the Kidneys Do for the pH","The third line of defense, after the buffers and the lungs. The last to alter the pH, in hours to days: the long-term regulators.","The primary controller of bicarb (HCO3), the base: reabsorbed into the bloodstream, or excreted in the urine. Can also produce bicarb.","Eliminate large amounts of acid (hydrogen ions), by active transport. Not carbonic acid, which the lungs control.","The most effective regulator of pH, with normal kidney function.","Manage an acidosis better than an alkalosis: holding on to bicarb is easier for them than excreting it.","How the kidneys compensate for an acidosis or an alkalosis is taught in the ABGs review","If the kidneys fail, the pH balance fails. Patients in renal failure often take bicarb daily to keep a normal pH."]},{"s":"The Kidneys in Blood Pressure Regulation","a":"kidneys-bp-regulation","k":["The Kidneys in Blood Pressure Regulation","Low Blood Flow Sensed","The kidneys sense a low BP or low blood flow, and release renin.","Angiotensin II","Renin starts a chain that ends in angiotensin II, made by ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme). It constricts the arteries.","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II releases aldosterone from the adrenal glands: the kidneys keep sodium and water, and excrete potassium.","Blood Pressure Rises","Tighter arteries and more blood volume raise the BP."]},{"s":"The Kidneys in RBC Production","a":"kidneys-rbc-production","k":["The Kidneys in RBC Production","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Chronic Kidney Disease.","Erythropoietin","Produced by the kidneys, and necessary for RBC production.","End-stage kidney disease: decreased production, so the patient is quite severely anemic.","Activity intolerance: little energy, and a need for a lot of rest. Provide rest periods.","Anti-anemic agents, such as erythropoietin, are given. Monitor the H&H: past a certain hemoglobin, erythropoietin's risks outweigh its benefits."]},{"s":"Pre-Renal, Intrarenal and Post-Renal Causes","a":"aki-cause-categories","k":["Pre-Renal, Intrarenal and Post-Renal Causes","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Acute Kidney Injury.","Pre-renal — Blood flow to the kidneys — Decreased perfusion to the kidneys, from hypotension or hypovolemia. Related to decreased cardiac output. The most common cause of AKI. Hypotensive or hypovolemic for a period of time: an AKI may deve…","Decreased perfusion to the kidneys, from hypotension or hypovolemia. Related to decreased cardiac output.","The most common cause of AKI.","Hypotensive or hypovolemic for a period of time: an AKI may develop within the next day or two.","Intrarenal — Inside the kidneys — Exposure to a nephrotoxic agent. Most common cause: acute tubular necrosis (ATN).","Exposure to a nephrotoxic agent.","Most common cause: acute tubular necrosis (ATN).","Post-renal — The other side of the kidneys — Urine backs up from a problem past the kidneys. Example: a swollen prostate.","Urine backs up from a problem past the kidneys.","Example: a swollen prostate.","Each cause in the critically ill, with its examples, is taught in Acute Kidney Injury"]},{"s":"CKD and End-Stage Renal Disease","a":"ckd-and-esrd","k":["CKD and End-Stage Renal Disease","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Chronic Kidney Disease and Med Surg I Week 4 – Acute Kidney Injury.","The Stages of CKD","1 — 90 or above — Kidney damage with a normal or increased GFR. Asymptomatic.","2 — 60 to 89 — Mild decrease. Asymptomatic, possible hypertension. Most do not know they have CKD.","3A — 45 to 59 — Moderate decrease. Hypertension, otherwise asymptomatic. The critical point to catch patients.","3B — 30 to 44 — Moderate decrease, worse than 3A. Hypertension, otherwise asymptomatic.","4 — 15 to 29 — Severe decrease. Manifestations become apparent: diagnosis usually happens here. The kidneys' physiologic reserve hides the loss until then. Prepare for dialysis or a kidney transplant.","5 — Under 15, or on dialysis — Kidney failure, end-stage: uremic. Dialysis starts.","Why Each Manifestation Happens","Fluid volume excess (edema) — The kidneys cannot excrete fluid, so it is retained.","The kidneys cannot excrete fluid, so it is retained.","Hypertension — The kidneys cannot excrete enough sodium and water: the blood volume rises. Damaged, poorly perfused kidneys release more renin: the RAAS constricts the arteries. See above.","The kidneys cannot excrete enough sodium and water: the blood volume rises.","Damaged, poorly perfused kidneys release more renin: the RAAS constricts the arteries. See above.","Hyponatremia — Dilutional: from the retained fluid. Brings neurologic changes: disorientation and confusion.","Dilutional: from the retained fluid. Brings neurologic changes: disorientation and confusion.","Hyperkalemia — The kidneys excrete the majority of potassium. One of the first electrolytes to become abnormal. Above 6.0: cardiac dysrhythmias and muscle weakness. Can be life-threatening.","The kidneys excrete the majority of potassium. One of the first electrolytes to become abnormal.","Above 6.0: cardiac dysrhythmias and muscle weakness. Can be life-threatening.","Hyperphosphatemia, hypermagnesemia — The kidneys are the primary excretory organ for both. Phosphorus is inversely related to calcium: as it rises, calcium falls.","The kidneys are the primary excretory organ for both.","Phosphorus is inversely related to calcium: as it rises, calcium falls.","Metabolic acidosis — Acidic waste products accumulate, no longer excreted in the urine. The kidneys no longer regulate the pH. See above.","Acidic waste products accumulate, no longer excreted in the urine.","The kidneys no longer regulate the pH. See above.","Uremia — The body is no longer rid of wastes via the urine: nitrogenous products build up, and the BUN rises. Anorexia, itching, CNS changes such as confusion.","The body is no longer rid of wastes via the urine: nitrogenous products build up, and the BUN rises.","Anorexia, itching, CNS changes such as confusion.","Malnutrition — Not hungry, in a hypermetabolic state, on a very limited diet.","Not hungry, in a hypermetabolic state, on a very limited diet.","Anemia — Decreased production of erythropoietin. See above.","Decreased production of erythropoietin. See above.","Renal osteodystrophy — Decreased activation of vitamin D and decreased reabsorption of calcium: hypocalcemia. Brittle, porous bones: a risk for injury and breaks. Usually on calcium and vitamin D supplements.","Decreased activation of vitamin D and decreased reabsorption of calcium: hypocalcemia.","Brittle, porous bones: a risk for injury and breaks. Usually on calcium and vitamin D supplements.","Infection — Immunosuppression: toxic accumulation, immune cell dysfunction, chronic inflammation and dialysis-related stress.","Immunosuppression: toxic accumulation, immune cell dysfunction, chronic inflammation and dialysis-related stress."]},{"s":"The Renal Diet","a":"renal-diet","k":["The Renal Diet","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Chronic Kidney Disease.","The renal diet: low protein, low potassium, low sodium, possibly low magnesium and phosphate, and typically a fluid restriction.","Protein — Restricted: BUN and creatinine are end products of protein metabolism, and cannot be urinated out. A catch-22: the patient is often severely malnourished. Low before dialysis. Higher once on dialysis, which filters out the impurit…","Restricted: BUN and creatinine are end products of protein metabolism, and cannot be urinated out.","A catch-22: the patient is often severely malnourished.","Low before dialysis. Higher once on dialysis, which filters out the impurities, and the diet is more liberal.","Sodium, potassium, phosphate — Restricted. The actual restrictions are individualized.","Restricted. The actual restrictions are individualized.","Fluid — Required when oliguric. Sometimes the day's intake is set by yesterday's urine output.","Required when oliguric.","Sometimes the day's intake is set by yesterday's urine output.","Supplements — High calorie, high protein, low potassium and phosphorus, slow-release carbohydrates: renal shakes and tube feedings. The high protein is for a patient on dialysis.","High calorie, high protein, low potassium and phosphorus, slow-release carbohydrates: renal shakes and tube feedings.","The high protein is for a patient on dialysis."]},{"s":"Intermittent Hemodialysis","a":"intermittent-hemodialysis","k":["Intermittent Hemodialysis","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 4 – Chronic Kidney Disease.","How Hemodialysis Works","Diffusion","Moves: particles.","From: an area of higher to lower concentration.","Osmosis","Moves: water.","From: an area of lower to higher concentration.","The goal of both: equal concentrations on either side of a semipermeable membrane. Dialysis is all about the membrane.","What Happens in Hemodialysis","Blood is pulled out through the access, run through the dialyzer, and put back. The filtering, the GFR, takes place in the dialyzer.","The dialyzer is a semipermeable membrane outside the body. Substances move from the blood through it into the dialysate.","The nephrologist sets the dialysate from the labs, by how much shifting is wanted. Less potassium than the blood, for one, so potassium diffuses out.","Intermittent: 3 days a week. In end-stage disease, likely for the rest of their life, unless they get a transplant.","Advantages of Hemodialysis","Rapid removal of fluid, urea and creatinine, and potassium.","Disadvantages of Hemodialysis","Needs vascular access, and may need heparin.","Stringent diet and fluid restrictions: dialysis is only 3 days a week. Between sessions, a patient who makes no urine can feel miserable.","Hypotension during dialysis.","Vascular access for dialysis is taught in Acute Kidney Injury","Complications","Complications of Hemodialysis","Disequilibrium syndrome: unique to hemodialysis. Most often in the first few treatments: the body is not used to so much fluid pulled off and put on so quickly. Disorientation, seizures, headache, agitation, nausea and vomiting.","Decreased cardiac output: hemodialysis brings big shifts, which are hard on the heart.","Also: cardiac dysrhythmias, infection, air embolism.","Hypotension, dysrhythmias and infection on IHD, with their causes, are taught in Acute Kidney Injury","Nursing Care","The access limb: no blood pressures, injections or IV sticks. A dialysis catheter is not an IV access: no IV fluids or medications through it.","Drugs and Hemodialysis","Drugs build up to toxic levels: not excreted, they circulate in their active form for prolonged periods. Doses may need altering: renal dosing.","Some drugs are dialyzed out across the membrane, leaving subtherapeutic levels. Know which, and give them after dialysis.","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-gi.html","t":"GI","secs":[{"s":"GI","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 11"]},{"s":"GI Anatomy and Physiology","a":"gi-anatomy-physiology","k":["GI Anatomy and Physiology","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 3 – Nutrition.","The GI system's three main functions: transportation, digestion and absorption.","The Path Through the GI Tract","Esophagus, then the esophageal sphincter.","Stomach: a very acidic environment.","Pyloric sphincter.","Small intestine: duodenum, jejunum, ileum. The duodenum sits just past the pyloric sphincter, the ileum just before the large intestine. Nutrients are absorbed here.","Large intestine (colon): ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid. Water is reabsorbed here, not nutrients.","Rectum, then anus.","Esophagus — Peristalsis, waves of muscle contraction, carries food to the stomach. The lower esophageal sphincter keeps stomach contents from flowing back up.","Peristalsis, waves of muscle contraction, carries food to the stomach.","The lower esophageal sphincter keeps stomach contents from flowing back up.","Stomach — Stores and churns food with hydrochloric acid and pepsin, which begin breaking down protein. A mucus layer protects its lining from its own acid. Empties slowly into the duodenum.","Stores and churns food with hydrochloric acid and pepsin, which begin breaking down protein.","A mucus layer protects its lining from its own acid. Empties slowly into the duodenum.","Small intestine — Most digestion happens here: bile and pancreatic enzymes enter the duodenum. The terminal ileum also absorbs vitamin B12 and bile salts.","Most digestion happens here: bile and pancreatic enzymes enter the duodenum.","The terminal ileum also absorbs vitamin B12 and bile salts.","Large intestine — Absorbs electrolytes along with the water, and forms and stores stool.","Absorbs electrolytes along with the water, and forms and stores stool.","Liver — The portal vein brings it the blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas and spleen. The liver processes what was absorbed before it reaches the rest of the body. Makes bile, which digests fat.","The portal vein brings it the blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas and spleen. The liver processes what was absorbed before it reaches the rest of the body.","Makes bile, which digests fat.","Gallbladder — Stores and concentrates bile, and releases it into the duodenum after a fatty meal.","Stores and concentrates bile, and releases it into the duodenum after a fatty meal.","Pancreas — Exocrine: releases amylase (carbohydrates), lipase (fats) and protease (proteins) into the duodenum, with bicarbonate to neutralize the stomach acid. Endocrine: releases insulin and glucagon into the blood.","Exocrine: releases amylase (carbohydrates), lipase (fats) and protease (proteins) into the duodenum, with bicarbonate to neutralize the stomach acid.","Endocrine: releases insulin and glucagon into the blood.","What the liver does is taught in Hepatic Failure"]},{"s":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","a":"inflammatory-bowel-disease","k":["Inflammatory Bowel Disease","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 6 – Inflammatory Bowel Disease.","What Crohn's Disease and Ulcerative Colitis Share","Chronic inflammation of the intestine, with periods of remission and exacerbation (flares).","Cause unknown. Autoimmune. No cure, except removing the whole colon in ulcerative colitis.","Signs and symptoms: diarrhea, weight loss, abdominal pain, fever, fatigue.","Complications: hemorrhage, strictures, perforation. Sometimes surgery.","Treatment: medication to decrease the inflammation and maintain remission, and surgery.","Crohn's Disease","Where: anywhere from mouth to anus. Most often the terminal ileum and the colon.","Pattern: skip lesions, irregular. Deep ulcerations with a cobblestone appearance.","Depth: transmural.","Rectal bleeding: sometimes.","Malabsorption and nutritional deficiencies: common. More of a small intestine disease.","Complications: perianal abscesses and fistulas, strictures, perforation: the inflammation involves the entire bowel wall.","Cancer risk: increased for small intestinal cancer. Colorectal too, but less than with ulcerative colitis.","Surgery: for complications such as a stricture.","Ulcerative Colitis","Where: the large intestine only. Starts in the rectum and spreads up the colon.","Pattern: uniform and continuous.","Depth: submucosa or mucosa. Not as deep.","Rectal bleeding: common.","Malabsorption and nutritional deficiencies: minimal. The colon absorbs water, not nutrients.","Complications: perforation, from toxic megacolon.","Cancer risk: increased colorectal cancer after 10 years of disease.","Surgery: curative. With the colon out, the disease is gone.","Goals of Treatment in a Flare","Rest the bowel: NPO, with IV fluids to hydrate.","Control the inflammation, with medication.","Combat infection, if any.","Correct malnutrition: severe disease often leaves the patient exceptionally malnourished.","Alleviate stress: stress makes it worse.","Relieve symptoms and improve quality of life, with drug therapy."]},{"s":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","a":"irritable-bowel-syndrome","k":["Irritable Bowel Syndrome","Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)","A functional bowel disorder: chronic abdominal pain with a change in bowel habits, and no visible inflammation or damage to the bowel.","Not IBD: no inflammation, ulceration or bleeding, and no added risk of colon cancer.","Types, by the main stool pattern: constipation (IBS-C), diarrhea (IBS-D), or mixed (IBS-M).","Signs: cramping abdominal pain, often eased by a bowel movement. Bloating, gas, mucus in the stool. Often triggered by certain foods or stress.","Red flags point elsewhere, such as to IBD or cancer: rectal bleeding, weight loss, anemia, fever, onset after age 50.","Managed with diet changes, stress management, and drugs for the main symptom."]},{"s":"Peptic Ulcer Disease","a":"peptic-ulcer-disease","k":["Peptic Ulcer Disease","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 6 – Peptic Ulcer Disease.","Peptic ulcer disease (PUD): erosion of the GI mucosa from the digestive action of hydrochloric acid and pepsin.","Causes of PUD","H. pylori: triggers inflammation.","Drugs: NSAIDs above all, such as ibuprofen and naproxen.","H. pylori with NSAIDs: double trouble, a greater risk of an ulcer.","Also: alcohol, smoking, family history.","Stress aggravates an existing ulcer. Not shown to cause one.","Gastric — Most often in the antrum of the stomach.","Duodenal — Just past the pyloric sphincter. 80% of ulcers. 90 to 95% have H. pylori.","Acute — Through fewer layers of the wall. Short duration: resolves quickly.","Chronic — 4 times more common. Deeper, more severe and long-term.","Diagnosing PUD","EGD: the most accurate diagnostic procedure.","H. pylori, non-invasive: a urea breath test and a stool test. Urea is a byproduct of the bacteria's metabolism.","H. pylori, invasive: biopsy of the antral mucosa, tested for urease. The gold standard.","CBC: the H&H, for anemia. Is it bleeding, or has it been?","Stool for occult blood. See Upper GI Bleed below.","Complications of PUD","The complications of PUD: HOP. HOP: Hemorrhage, Obstruction, Perforation.","HOP: Hemorrhage, Obstruction, Perforation.","Hemorrhage — The most common complication. Watch the vital signs' trend: a heart rate creeping up through the day. Gastric aspirate: a change in its amount or redness. Anything attached to the patient is part of the assessment. Pain may dec…","The most common complication.","Watch the vital signs' trend: a heart rate creeping up through the day.","Gastric aspirate: a change in its amount or redness. Anything attached to the patient is part of the assessment.","Pain may decrease at first: the blood neutralizes the gastric acid.","NG to low wall suction, to decompress the stomach. Otherwise as for an upper GI bleed, below.","Obstruction — Can happen at any time. Most likely with an ulcer close to the pylorus. Gradual onset of symptoms. NG to low wall suction until it resolves, irrigated per protocol.","Can happen at any time. Most likely with an ulcer close to the pylorus.","Gradual onset of symptoms.","NG to low wall suction until it resolves, irrigated per protocol.","Perforation — The most lethal complication: gastric contents reach the peritoneum. Sudden and dramatic. In the first 2 hours: sudden, severe upper abdominal pain, spreading throughout the abdomen, radiating to the back, not relieved by food…","The most lethal complication: gastric contents reach the peritoneum.","Sudden and dramatic. In the first 2 hours: sudden, severe upper abdominal pain, spreading throughout the abdomen, radiating to the back, not relieved by food or antacids.","Rigid, board-like abdomen. Rebound tenderness.","Respirations shallow and rapid. Pulse weak and tachycardic. Bowel sounds may be absent, after listening 5 minutes.","Bacterial peritonitis within 6 to 12 hours.","Collaborative Care for PUD","Adequate rest: lowers the stress response.","Smoking cessation.","Diet: no special diet. Individualized.","Stop aspirin and NSAIDs for 4 to 6 weeks. If aspirin must continue, give it with a PPI.","Drugs, a vital part of treatment: antibiotics if H. pylori, a PPI (omeprazole, esomeprazole), and sucralfate, a cytoprotective agent."]},{"s":"Upper GI Bleed","a":"upper-gi-bleed","k":["Upper GI Bleed","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 6 – Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed.","An upper GI bleed's onset: slow and insidious, or sudden.","Bright red hematemesis — Blood in the vomit, seen with the naked eye (\"frank\" or \"gross\"): a recent or ongoing bleed.","Coffee-ground hematemesis — The blood has been partly digested: an older bleed, or a significant bleed has stopped. Very malodorous.","Melena — Black, tarry, malodorous stool: a transit time over 8 hours. Helps the provider find where the bleed began.","Occult blood — Not apparent by appearance. Found by a positive guaiac test, never an expected finding.","Causes of an Upper GI Bleed","Stomach and duodenal ulcers: about 50% of upper GI bleeds. H. pylori and NSAIDs are the two most common causes of a bleeding ulcer.","Drug-induced ulcers: NSAIDs, salicylates such as aspirin, steroids. Many are over the counter.","Stress-related mucosal disorder (SRMD): a mucosal injury in 70 to 90% of ICU patients. So ICU patients are typically on a PPI, IV pantoprazole.","Esophageal varices, from portal hypertension. See Liver Failure below.","Vital signs — Closely: the baseline, and the trend. Orthostatic hypotension points to hypovolemia.","Closely: the baseline, and the trend.","Orthostatic hypotension points to hypovolemia.","Signs of hypovolemia — Rapid blood loss can bring hypovolemic shock. A rising heart rate, increased thirst, cold clammy skin, restlessness. Always investigate restlessness.","Rapid blood loss can bring hypovolemic shock.","A rising heart rate, increased thirst, cold clammy skin, restlessness. Always investigate restlessness.","Abdomen — Tense, rigid, board-like: a gut perforation or peritonitis, with distension and guarding.","Tense, rigid, board-like: a gut perforation or peritonitis, with distension and guarding.","Labs — H&H and BUN. An elevated BUN is not always the kidneys: a GI bleed raises it too.","H&H and BUN. An elevated BUN is not always the kidneys: a GI bleed raises it too.","IV access — Large bore, 18-gauge preferred. Anticipate PRBCs, and at least fluid resuscitation.","Large bore, 18-gauge preferred. Anticipate PRBCs, and at least fluid resuscitation.","NG lavage — Washes the blood out of the stomach. Effectiveness questionable.","Washes the blood out of the stomach. Effectiveness questionable.","Emesis and stool — Assess them, and check for occult blood.","Assess them, and check for occult blood.","Endoscopy — Hemostasis is the first-line management: the provider finds the bleed and stops it, as by cautery. No other intervention helps until then.","Hemostasis is the first-line management: the provider finds the bleed and stops it, as by cautery. No other intervention helps until then.","Assessing and managing a GI bleed in the ICU is taught in GI Critical Care Issues"]},{"s":"Chronic Pancreatitis","a":"chronic-pancreatitis","k":["Chronic Pancreatitis","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 7 – Pancreatitis.","Chronic pancreatitis: progressive and destructive, with remissions and flares. Caused by inflammation and fibrosis of the pancreatic tissue.","Signs of Chronic Pancreatitis","Intense abdominal pain. Less tenderness than in acute pancreatitis.","A mass: suspect a pseudocyst, or an abscess within the pancreas that needs prompt surgical drainage.","Ascites, with respiratory compromise from it.","Steatorrhea: the pancreatic enzymes are not working.","Dark urine: from increased bilirubin. The whole hepatobiliary tree is interconnected.","Pain — Opioids are used, cautiously: some may increase the pain by causing spasms.","Opioids are used, cautiously: some may increase the pain by causing spasms.","Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT) — The standard of care, to prevent malnutrition, malabsorption and excessive weight loss. Usually pancrelipase: amylase, lipase and protease. Record the number and consistency of stools each day.…","The standard of care, to prevent malnutrition, malabsorption and excessive weight loss.","Usually pancrelipase: amylase, lipase and protease.","Record the number and consistency of stools each day. Goal: fewer, and less fatty.","Giving the enzymes — With the first bite of every meal and snack, when the body would secrete them. Swallowed whole, not chewed. Wipe any off the lips: enzymes break things down. Not mixed into protein foods. Enteric-coated forms not crushe…","With the first bite of every meal and snack, when the body would secrete them.","Swallowed whole, not chewed. Wipe any off the lips: enzymes break things down.","Not mixed into protein foods. Enteric-coated forms not crushed.","Sometimes with an antacid or H2 blocker: a low pH inactivates them.","Nutrition — Weight loss can be significant. Sometimes a candidate for TPN. Eating by mouth: may need 4,000 to 6,000 calories a day to maintain weight.","Weight loss can be significant. Sometimes a candidate for TPN.","Eating by mouth: may need 4,000 to 6,000 calories a day to maintain weight.","Preventing exacerbations — Avoid what makes the symptoms worse: alcohol, with a referral to a self-help group, caffeinated drinks, nicotine. Meals: bland, low-fat, high-protein, high-carbohydrate. No gastric stimulants, such as spices. Smal…","Avoid what makes the symptoms worse: alcohol, with a referral to a self-help group, caffeinated drinks, nicotine.","Meals: bland, low-fat, high-protein, high-carbohydrate. No gastric stimulants, such as spices. Small, high-calorie meals and snacks.","Rest frequently until strength returns.","Acute pancreatitis, the kind that lands a patient in the ICU, is taught in Acute Pancreatitis"]},{"s":"Liver Failure: Cirrhosis","a":"liver-failure","k":["Liver Failure: Cirrhosis","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 7 – Cirrhosis.","What Is Going On in the Liver","Early Cirrhosis","Insidious onset.","Weight loss and weakness.","GI disturbances: anorexia, nausea and vomiting, flatulence, a change in bowel habits.","Hepatomegaly, with RUQ pain and a palpable liver.","Late Cirrhosis","Jaundice: often the first sign.","Low albumin, a protein the liver makes. Albumin has pulling power: without it, fluid shifts out of the vessels into the interstitium. Third spacing, and ascites.","Low prothrombin, a protein the liver makes and a precursor in the clotting cascade. Bleeding times increase, and so does the risk of bleeding.","Portal hypertension and its complications: see below.","Hepatic encephalopathy, with asterixis: see below.","Portal Hypertension and Its Complications","Portal hypertension: the blood coming into the liver through the portal system backs up, from congestion in the cirrhotic liver, as in heart failure. The pressure in the portal area rises, and the backed-up fluid engorges vessels.","Ascites — Fluid backs up into the peritoneum: third spacing. Low albumin aggravates it. Managing it: see below.","Fluid backs up into the peritoneum: third spacing.","Low albumin aggravates it. Managing it: see below.","Splenomegaly — The congestion enlarges the spleen, in the left upper quadrant. The spleen filters the blood, removing microbes and worn-out RBCs, and makes WBCs and antibodies.","The congestion enlarges the spleen, in the left upper quadrant.","The spleen filters the blood, removing microbes and worn-out RBCs, and makes WBCs and antibodies.","Spider angiomas and caput medusae — Engorged vessels. Caput medusae: dilated veins on the abdomen, like varicose veins.","Engorged vessels.","Caput medusae: dilated veins on the abdomen, like varicose veins.","Esophageal and anorectal varices — Engorged veins, like hemorrhoids. The esophagus sits close to the liver. Bleeding varices are very dangerous: the patient already lacks prothrombin. Prevent them, and keep them from bleeding.","Engorged veins, like hemorrhoids. The esophagus sits close to the liver.","Bleeding varices are very dangerous: the patient already lacks prothrombin. Prevent them, and keep them from bleeding.","Ascites, splenomegaly, varices, engorged vessels: all from the portal hypertension.","Portal hypertension's effect on cardiac output is taught in Hepatic Failure","Treating bleeding varices is taught in GI Critical Care Issues","Bleeding precautions, for a patient with varices, are taught in the Hematology review","Managing Ascites","Abdominal girth — Measured and trended. Daily weights are a more accurate indicator of fluid retention.","Measured and trended. Daily weights are a more accurate indicator of fluid retention.","Sodium and fluid restriction — Water follows salt: a low-sodium diet, and possibly a fluid restriction.","Water follows salt: a low-sodium diet, and possibly a fluid restriction.","Diuretics — Spironolactone, potassium-sparing, and a loop diuretic such as furosemide, potassium-losing. Often both. Assumes the kidneys are working well.","Spironolactone, potassium-sparing, and a loop diuretic such as furosemide, potassium-losing. Often both.","Assumes the kidneys are working well.","Paracentesis — Goal: relieve respiratory distress, once the ascites impairs breathing. Often repeated in cirrhosis: large-volume paracentesis.","Goal: relieve respiratory distress, once the ascites impairs breathing.","Often repeated in cirrhosis: large-volume paracentesis.","TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) — Non-surgical. A stent lowers the portal hypertension: controls long-term ascites and reduces variceal bleeding.","Non-surgical. A stent lowers the portal hypertension: controls long-term ascites and reduces variceal bleeding.","IV albumin — Raises the oncotic pressure: more pulling power into the vascular space.","Raises the oncotic pressure: more pulling power into the vascular space."]},{"s":"Hepatic Encephalopathy","a":"hepatic-encephalopathy","k":["Hepatic Encephalopathy","For further study, see Med Surg I Week 7 – Cirrhosis.","What Causes Hepatic Encephalopathy","A reversible neuropsychiatric manifestation of impaired liver function.","Primarily too much serum ammonia. Most ammonia forms when bacteria in the intestines break down protein.","A working liver converts ammonia to urea, excreted in the urine. A failing liver does not.","Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier: a CNS depressant, with a sedative effect.","What Rising Ammonia Does","Consciousness: slowed responses and confusion, then lethargy, semi-stupor, and hepatic coma.","Intellect: confusion, loss of place, inability to compute, loss of self, then no intellect.","Personality: depression, irritability, decreased inhibitions, then no personality or behavior at all.","Neuromuscular: asterixis, poor coordination, impaired handwriting, ataxia, then coma.","The goal: get the ammonia down, and keep the patient safe. Without that, the patient ends up comatose.","Protein — Restricted only with severe encephalopathy, at end stage: 20 to 40 g a day. Otherwise its benefits outweigh the ammonia its breakdown adds: the lesser of two evils. Calorie-dense food, especially carbohydrates.","Restricted only with severe encephalopathy, at end stage: 20 to 40 g a day.","Otherwise its benefits outweigh the ammonia its breakdown adds: the lesser of two evils.","Calorie-dense food, especially carbohydrates.","Control GI bleeding — Blood in the GI tract is nitrogen-rich, another source of protein: it drives up the ammonia.","Blood in the GI tract is nitrogen-rich, another source of protein: it drives up the ammonia.","Avoid constipation — Constipation increases the ammonia in the feces.","Constipation increases the ammonia in the feces.","Lactulose — The bowel movements remove the ammonia. Teach why: a patient who has already had a bowel movement today still needs the dose.","The bowel movements remove the ammonia.","Teach why: a patient who has already had a bowel movement today still needs the dose.","Neuro checks and safety — EMV (the Glasgow Coma Scale) regularly, for the sedative effect of the ammonia. Safety precautions: these patients are often confused.","EMV (the Glasgow Coma Scale) regularly, for the sedative effect of the ammonia.","Safety precautions: these patients are often confused.","Hepatic encephalopathy in the ICU, with its grades, the airway and lactulose's goal, is taught in Hepatic Failure"]},{"s":"How Age Affects the GI System","a":"gi-and-age","k":["How Age Affects the GI System","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 6 – Older Adults and Fundamentals II Week 4 – Bowel.","Mouth, taste and smell — Trouble chewing. Less saliva: a drier mouth. Taste buds atrophy and lose sensitivity: harder to tell salt, sweet, bitter and sour apart. Health conditions and medications can alter taste. Loss of smell. With taste a…","Trouble chewing. Less saliva: a drier mouth.","Taste buds atrophy and lose sensitivity: harder to tell salt, sweet, bitter and sour apart. Health conditions and medications can alter taste.","Loss of smell. With taste and smell dwindling, nutrition is a challenge.","Esophagus — Emptying slows.","Emptying slows.","Stomach — Less hydrochloric acid, which starts breaking down the food. Delayed gastric emptying: not hungry as quickly. Less tolerant of foods.","Less hydrochloric acid, which starts breaking down the food.","Delayed gastric emptying: not hungry as quickly.","Less tolerant of foods.","Intestines — Slower peristalsis. Impaired absorption, of vitamins too. Fewer digestive enzymes, including lipase for fat digestion.","Slower peristalsis.","Impaired absorption, of vitamins too.","Fewer digestive enzymes, including lipase for fat digestion.","Rectum and anus — Weakened sphincters. Less sensation to defecate.","Weakened sphincters.","Less sensation to defecate.","Abdomen — More fatty tissue in the trunk and abdomen: more protuberant.","More fatty tissue in the trunk and abdomen: more protuberant.","Why Older Adults Get Constipated","Lack of muscle tone, in the bowel and the abdomen.","Slowed peristalsis: more time for water to be reabsorbed.","Lack of exercise, too little fluid, too little fiber.","Too many dairy products, such as cheese.","Medications.","Why age matters in the critically ill is taught in GI Critical Care Issues","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]},{"f":"review-neuro.html","t":"Neuro","secs":[{"s":"Neuro","a":"","k":["Review · Topic 12"]},{"s":"Neurologic Assessment","a":"neuro-assessment","k":["Neurologic Assessment","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 5 – Neurosensory.","The basic assessment: general survey, level of consciousness, orientation. A focused assessment adds the rest.","General survey — How the patient acts, talks and looks. Symmetry, above all of the face. Speech: well paced and clear? Trouble finding words?","How the patient acts, talks and looks. Symmetry, above all of the face.","Speech: well paced and clear? Trouble finding words?","Level of consciousness — Orientation: person, place, time and situation (why in the hospital). Month and year can stand for the date. The levels, alert to comatose: below.","Orientation: person, place, time and situation (why in the hospital). Month and year can stand for the date.","The levels, alert to comatose: below.","Sensory function — Proprioception: the heel run down the opposite shin. Sharp and dull. Pupils: a late sign. Unequal in a patient who becomes unresponsive: an emergency, above all with a known head injury or a stroke.","Proprioception: the heel run down the opposite shin. Sharp and dull.","Pupils: a late sign. Unequal in a patient who becomes unresponsive: an emergency, above all with a known head injury or a stroke.","Motor function — Muscle strength: hand grasps, pedal pushes and pulls. Coordination: rapid alternating movements. The thumb touches each finger of the same hand, quickly.","Muscle strength: hand grasps, pedal pushes and pulls.","Coordination: rapid alternating movements. The thumb touches each finger of the same hand, quickly.","Memory and mental status — Memory: reminding the patient of the same thing again and again? Mood and behavior: violent? A flat affect? Ask the family whether this is the patient's normal. For a poor historian, the family answers the subject…","Memory: reminding the patient of the same thing again and again?","Mood and behavior: violent? A flat affect?","Ask the family whether this is the patient's normal. For a poor historian, the family answers the subjective questions.","Reflexes — The gag reflex: a later sign of a more significant neuro problem, like the pupils.","The gag reflex: a later sign of a more significant neuro problem, like the pupils.","Respiratory functioning — The airway first: is the patient neurologically intact enough to protect it? Swallowing: a suspected stroke gets nothing to drink, NPO. The risk is aspiration.","The airway first: is the patient neurologically intact enough to protect it?","Swallowing: a suspected stroke gets nothing to drink, NPO. The risk is aspiration.","A neurologic decline: rule out the 4 H's first, physiological causes that can be fixed. All four take a couple of minutes. Hypoxia: the oxygen saturation. Hypoglycemia: a blood sugar. Its signs can mimic a stroke. Hypotension: the blood pre…","Hypoxia: the oxygen saturation.","Hypoglycemia: a blood sugar. Its signs can mimic a stroke.","Hypotension: the blood pressure. A poorly perfused brain shows neurologic symptoms.","Hypoventilation: count the respirations.","Levels of Consciousness","Alert — Awake and easily aroused. Receptive and responsive.","Awake and easily aroused. Receptive and responsive.","Lethargic (somnolent) — Not fully alert: drifts off to sleep unless stimulated. Appears drowsy. Awakens to name, then responds appropriately, if slowly. Expected at times, like a patient woken in the night. Not 24 hours a day.","Not fully alert: drifts off to sleep unless stimulated. Appears drowsy.","Awakens to name, then responds appropriately, if slowly.","Expected at times, like a patient woken in the night. Not 24 hours a day.","Obtunded — Sleeps most of the time. Hard to arouse: needs a loud shout or a vigorous shake. Acts confused when aroused. Speech mumbled or incoherent. Needs constant stimulation to stay awake.","Sleeps most of the time. Hard to arouse: needs a loud shout or a vigorous shake.","Acts confused when aroused. Speech mumbled or incoherent.","Needs constant stimulation to stay awake.","Stupor (semi-comatose) — Cannot stay awake: the difference from obtunded. Spontaneously unconscious. Responds only to a vigorous shake, or really to pain. Groans and mumbles: no real words.","Cannot stay awake: the difference from obtunded. Spontaneously unconscious.","Responds only to a vigorous shake, or really to pain.","Groans and mumbles: no real words.","Comatose — Completely unconscious: no meaningful response to stimuli. Light coma: no purposeful movement, some reflex activity. Deep coma: no motor response.","Completely unconscious: no meaningful response to stimuli.","Light coma: no purposeful movement, some reflex activity.","Deep coma: no motor response.","Obtunded, stupor and coma are the more significant changes."]},{"s":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","a":"glasgow-coma-scale","k":["Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 5 – Neurosensory.","An objective assessment with a numeric value, also called EMV: eyes, motor, verbal. Scores run 3 to 15: a zero is not possible.","6 — — — Obeys commands — —","5 — — — Moves to localized pain — Oriented to time, place and person","4 — Spontaneously — Flexion withdrawal from pain — Confused","3 — To speech — Abnormal flexion (decorticate) — Inappropriate words","2 — To pain — Abnormal extension (decerebrate) — Incomprehensible sounds","1 — No response — No response — No response","Brain injury by GCS: severe 8 or less, moderate 9 to 12, mild 13 to 15.","Coma and airway protection by GCS score are taught in Respiratory and Progressive Issues in Critical Care","The GCS at the bedside, with posturing and the sedated or intubated patient, is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP"]},{"s":"Stroke","a":"stroke","k":["Stroke","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 6 – Cerebral Vascular Accident.","Also called a CVA (cerebral vascular accident), or a brain attack: acute stroke care is as urgent as a heart attack's.","Types of Stroke","Thrombotic (ischemic) — Atherosclerosis: a ruptured plaque forms a clot. A heart attack, in the brain. — Evolves over minutes to hours.","Embolic (ischemic) — An embolus, usually from the heart: AFib, heart valve disease, endocarditis. — Sudden: neurologic deficits develop rapidly.","Hemorrhagic — Vessel integrity interrupted, and bleeding: an intracranial bleed, an aneurysm, an AV malformation. Hypertension is the most common cause. — Sudden, then progresses over minutes to hours.","Thrombotic is the most common, more than half of all strokes, and embolic the second. Hemorrhagic has a very poor prognosis beside the other two.","Ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke in the ICU, lacunar stroke included, are taught in Brain Injury and Stroke","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","A transient episode of neurologic dysfunction from ischemia, with no acute infarction of the brain.","To a stroke as angina is to an MI: a warning, with no tissue death.","Symptoms usually last less than 1 hour.","No way to predict the outcome: a third have no other event, a third have more TIAs, a third progress to stroke.","Clinical Manifestations","They follow the location of the stroke, not whether it is ischemic or hemorrhagic. Weakness falls on the side opposite the stroke.","Motor function — Impaired mobility, breathing, swallowing and speech, gag reflex and self-care. Akinesia. Muscle tone: flaccid at first, then spastic, more often than not. Reflexes: lost at first, then hyperreflexive.","Impaired mobility, breathing, swallowing and speech, gag reflex and self-care. Akinesia.","Muscle tone: flaccid at first, then spastic, more often than not.","Reflexes: lost at first, then hyperreflexive.","Communication — Receptive, expressive or global aphasia (also called dysphasia), and dysarthria.","Receptive, expressive or global aphasia (also called dysphasia), and dysarthria.","Affect — Emotions hard to control: exaggerated and unpredictable. Laughing, then crying, within minutes or seconds.","Emotions hard to control: exaggerated and unpredictable. Laughing, then crying, within minutes or seconds.","Intellectual function — Impaired memory and judgment.","Impaired memory and judgment.","Spatial-perceptual — Homonymous hemianopsia: the patient ignores the side they cannot see, the food on that half of the plate included. Approach from the side they can see. May not recognize their own body parts. Also agnosia and apraxia.","Homonymous hemianopsia: the patient ignores the side they cannot see, the food on that half of the plate included. Approach from the side they can see.","May not recognize their own body parts.","Also agnosia and apraxia.","Elimination — Bowel and bladder changes, usually temporary.","Bowel and bladder changes, usually temporary.","Acute Care","Goals for both types: preserving life, preventing further brain damage, reducing disability. Most strokes are treatable, and the time of onset is critical information.","Ischemic Stroke","Manage: circulation, airway, breathing. Then the ICP, with interventions that improve venous drainage.","Monitor: a baseline neurologic assessment, then closely for a growing deficit. Many worsen in the first 24 to 48 hours.","BP: often elevated right after a stroke, perhaps a protective response that maintains cerebral perfusion. The provider sets specific BP guidelines.","Fluids: hydrate adequately, to promote perfusion and decrease further brain injury.","Clot buster: IV tPA (alteplase), the only drug approved for acute ischemic stroke. Started within 4.5 hours of onset (3 hours by FDA approval, 4.5 under certain criteria), after careful screening.","Endovascular: a mechanical thrombectomy with a stent retriever, or carotid angioplasty with a stent, to improve perfusion.","Once stable: anticoagulants and platelet inhibitors, to prevent further clots, and statins.","Examples: aspirin, ticlopidine, clopidogrel, dipyridamole.","Hemorrhagic Stroke","Manage: airway, breathing, circulation, and the ICP: often unstable.","Surgery, chosen by the cause: evacuating a hematoma, resection or radiosurgery, clipping or coiling an aneurysm.","Managing increased ICP is taught in Neuro Assessment and Increased ICP","Who is not a candidate for tPA, and its bleeding risk, are taught in Brain Injury and Stroke","Recognizing and Assessing a Stroke","FAST: a quick screen to recognize a stroke early, at home or on the floor. Any one sign is a possible stroke. Face: ask for a smile. One side droops. Arms: raise both. One drifts down, or will not rise. Speech: repeat a simple phrase. Slurr…","Face: ask for a smile. One side droops.","Arms: raise both. One drifts down, or will not rise.","Speech: repeat a simple phrase. Slurred, strange or absent.","Time: call for help at once, and note when the symptoms began.","Nursing Assessment in Acute Care","Primary assessment: cardiac, respiratory and neurologic status.","If stable, the history: symptom onset and duration above all, with its nature and changes. Similar symptoms before. A family history of stroke, aneurysm or cardiovascular disease.","Secondary assessment: a comprehensive neurologic exam. Level of consciousness, the NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS), cognition, motor abilities, cranial nerves, sensation, proprioception, cerebellar function (motor control), deep tendon reflexes.","The NIHSS: nurses do it all the time. Nurses on a neuro floor are NIHSS certified.","The stroke assessment in order, with the NIHSS scores, is taught in Brain Injury and Stroke"]},{"s":"DI and SIADH","a":"di-and-siadh","k":["DI and SIADH","For further study, see Med Surg II Week 4 – Antidiuretic Hormone Disorders.","ADH (antidiuretic hormone, or vasopressin), with thirst, is the major regulator of the body's water. It moves water only: potassium is not affected.","Concentrated or Low Volume","Plasma osmolality up, or circulating fluid volume down.","Thirst and ADH","Thirst: more fluid taken in. ADH secreted from the posterior pituitary: less water excreted.","Water Retained","Circulating fluid volume rises.","ADH and Thirst Fall","A negative feedback loop.","Sodium makes up most of the serum osmolality. Hypernatremic: cells shrink. Hyponatremic: cells swell. Either way, a confused patient.","SIADH vs DI","SIADH: Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH","ADH: overproduced. More water reabsorbed in the renal tubules.","Fluid volume: intravascular volume up, an excess. Cells swell as fluid shifts into them.","Serum: dilutional hyponatremia, and a decreased serum osmolality.","Cause: most often lung cancer cells that secrete ADH (ectopic hormone production).","Signs: those of hyponatremia, by how severe it is and how fast it came on. Muscle cramping, dyspnea, fatigue. Dulled sensorium, confusion, lethargy. Impaired taste, anorexia.","Nursing problems: excess fluid volume. Risk for injury from an altered mental status.","DI: Diabetes Insipidus","ADH: underproduced, or the kidneys do not respond to it. Less water reabsorbed.","Fluid volume: intravascular volume down, a deficit. Pure water lost: dehydrated and hemoconcentrated.","Serum: hypernatremia, and a serum osmolality of 300 or higher. It makes the patient thirsty.","Cause, neurogenic (central): head trauma. Abrupt, with the most severe signs. After head trauma, self-limiting: it resolves on its own, and the signs are treated meanwhile.","Cause, nephrogenic: the kidneys do not respond to ADH. Lithium is the most common drug cause. Less abrupt and less severe.","Signs: polyuria, 5 to 20 L a day, as dilute as water: a very low urine specific gravity and urine osmolality. Polydipsia. Fatigue from nocturia, and weakness.","Nursing problems: deficient fluid volume. Risk for injury from an altered mental status.","Flip too much against not enough: SIADH is a fluid volume excess, so DI is a deficit. Both risk injury, as brain cells swell or shrink.","SIADH and DI Care","Caring for SIADH","Goal: restore a normal fluid volume and osmolality.","Monitor: signs of volume overload, I&O, daily weights, mental status.","Mild: sodium below 125, mild symptoms. Fluid restriction only, 1,000 mL a day.","Severe: sodium below 120, or severe symptoms. 3% saline IV, slowly, and fluids restricted to 500 mL a day.","IV fluids: per order, the rate closely monitored.","Drugs: only for chronic SIADH, not first line. Sometimes diuretics, or demeclocycline, which blocks ADH's effects.","Caring for DI","Goal: maintain the fluid and electrolyte balance.","Monitor: I&O, daily weights. Labs: urine specific gravity (1.010 to 1.030), serum osmolality (270 to 290), serum sodium (135 to 145).","IV fluids.","Drugs: desmopressin, a synthetic ADH in very small doses: nasal spray, PO, IV, subQ. With fluids, the cornerstone of treatment for neurogenic DI.","Notify the provider: urine volume up with the specific gravity down. It is getting worse, and the desmopressin dose may need raising.","Hypertonic saline goes in slowly, over hours to days. Too fast, rapid fluid shifts shrink and swell the brain's cells: it can be fatal.","DI and SIADH after a TBI, with cerebral salt wasting and urine output, are taught in Brain Injury and Stroke"]},{"s":"Seizure Precautions","a":"seizure-precautions","k":["Seizure Precautions","For further study, see Fundamentals II Week 5 – Neurosensory and Med Surg II Week 6 – Seizure Disorder.","Seizure Precautions: In the Room","Suction, set up and working.","Oxygen.","Padded bed rails, where the agency's policy has them.","Why these patients: after a TBI, blood on the brain irritates the tissue, a risk for seizures.","The Ambu bag, IV access and EEG monitoring are taught in Brain Injury and Stroke","Airway — A patent airway, with suction handy. Breathing assistance after the seizure, as needed.","A patent airway, with suction handy.","Breathing assistance after the seizure, as needed.","Safety — Stay with the patient until the seizure ends. Nothing forced into the mouth: no tongue blade. No restraint, unless the movements put the patient in danger. Turn the head to the side if possible. Never force it.","Stay with the patient until the seizure ends.","Nothing forced into the mouth: no tongue blade.","No restraint, unless the movements put the patient in danger.","Turn the head to the side if possible. Never force it.","Monitor — VS, LOC, SpO2, GCS, pupil size and reactivity. Time the onset and duration. Document accurately. Cyanosis in a tonic-clonic seizure: usually self-limiting, but note it.","VS, LOC, SpO2, GCS, pupil size and reactivity.","Time the onset and duration. Document accurately.","Cyanosis in a tonic-clonic seizure: usually self-limiting, but note it.","Rest and support — Rest until fully awake. Then reassurance and support.","Rest until fully awake. Then reassurance and support.","Status epilepticus is a medical emergency: a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures over 30 minutes. Call the rapid response team. Airway first. IV lorazepam or diazepam is the drug of choice. A seizure over 10 minutes can caus…","Added from outside the course lectures."]}]}];
